ation excluded NYHA class I in advance. Those three quantities are no longer NEEDED, because the composite was sourced directly. || PREVIOUS NOTE PRESERVED: CALIBRATED, not measured. Originating model: Guyton 1972. The Frank-Starling and venous return relationships are E1; this linearised sensitivity around the operating point is a fitted constant. Second most consequential unmeasured number after the pressure natriuresis slope - together these two set the loop gain. || THE TEST THAT SHOULD SET THIS ROW HAS NOW BEEN RUN, AND IT GIVES A NUMBER: 2880 SHOULD BE ABOUT 554. 2026-09-02, from ADR 0013's falsifiable test - validation/ecf_salt_response_extract.py. This row sets dMAP/dV_ecf, which is 11.285 mmHg/L at the 70 kg reference and scales as 1/mass. van den Bosch 2021 (n = 70 healthy men, ECFV by iothalamate and blood pressure in the SAME subjects, intake verified by 24 h urinary sodium) gives 1.885 mmHg/L at 80.6 kg, which is 2.170 at the 70 kg reference and corresponds to G_vr = 554. THE MODEL IS 5.2x TOO STIFF ON PRESSURE PER UNIT VOLUME. This is measurable independently of G_pn: an 8x change in this row moves the salt-step PRESSURE response by 0.12% while moving the VOLUME response exactly inversely, so the two parameters are orthogonal and each is identified by its own human datum. DO NOT SIMPLY REFIT IT TO 554. This row is `calibrated` and HANDOVER section 4 item 1 is to REPLACE it with sourced venous compliance - Pmsf, right atrial pressure, stressed versus unstressed volume. 554 is the target that work now has to explain, not a value to enter; entering it would swap one fitted constant for another and lose the falsifiable test in the process. DECLARED CONFLICT: Heer 2000 and Heer 2009 report no body-mass or total-body-water rise on high sodium, which would reverse the sign of this discrepancy. See ADR 0013. || EVIDENCE BASE WIDENED THE SAME DAY, AND THE TARGET IS A RANGE, NOT 554. Two further sweeps (24 queries, 162 records) because the verdict rested on one cohort. The body-weight limb now has FOUR independent groups - van den Bosch 2021 (n=70, 0.729 kg/100 mmol), Rorije 2018 (Anesthesiology 2018;128(2):352-360, PMID 29206647, n=12, +2.5 kg 95% CI 1.7-3.2 with blood pressure UNCHANGED), Foo 1998 (Clin Sci 1998;95(2):157-164, PMID 9680497, n=18, +0.45 +/- 0.69 kg) and Heer 2000 (n=32, no increase, entered as zero and that is an interpretation of a null). n-weighted pooled 0.572 kg/100 mmol over n=132, or 0.755 excluding Heer. THE TRACER LIMB GIVES 0.553 L, so two independent methods agree to 4% and the pre-registered 1 kg = 1 L conversion is corroborated rather than assumed - prereg section 8 said it would be falsified if they diverged. Pairing the META-ANALYTIC pressure (1.70-2.30 mmHg/100 mmol) with the pooled volume gives 2.97-4.16 mmHg/L, so the model is 2.7-3.8x too stiff and G_vr's target is 758-1062; the within-subject van den Bosch pair gives 5.2x and 554 as the harshest reading. USE THE POOLED RANGE: the mechanistic volume studies are underpowered for the 2 mmHg the meta-analyses detect - Kirkendall 1976 (PMID 1249473, n=8, FOUR WEEKS per level, the closest protocol to this model's 30 days), Rorije 2018 and Taurio 2023 (PMID 36708156, n=510, the largest dataset found) all report NO blood pressure change at all, and taking that at face value would drive this row to zero. PART OF THE DISCREPANCY IS NOT THIS ROW: Cardiovascular.jl computes V_blood as V_plasma/(1-Hct) with Hct constant, so red cell volume expands with plasma over a 30 day salt step when red cell mass is fixed. dV_blood/dV_ecf should be f_pv = 0.211, not f_pv/(1-Hct) = 0.386 - a factor of 1.83 that would take the model ratio to 6.17 and leave this row needing only 1.5-2.8x, i.e. about 1000-1950. DIAGNOSED, NOT YET RUN. || TARGET REVISED 2026-09-02 AFTER THE RED CELL CORRECTION: 1012-1941, NOT 758-1062. Cardiovascular.jl stopped letting red cell volume expand with plasma, which moved dMAP/dV_ecf from 11.285 to 6.173 and closed 1.83x of the discrepancy on its own. The residual attributable to THIS row is therefore 1.5-2.1x rather than 2.7-5.2x. Required ratio 2.97-4.16 mmHg/L at the 70 kg reference gives 2880 * ratio / 6.173 = 1385-1941, or 1012 on the harshest within-subject reading. AND THE ENDPOINT IS NOW MEASURED: with G_pn = 51 (ADR 0013) AND this row near 1400 the model returns dMAP = 1.944 mmHg and dV_ecf = 0.648 L per 100 mmol/day at a ratio of 3.001, against human 1.70-2.30, 0.553-0.572 and 2.97-4.16 - inside all three at once. NEITHER CORRECTION ALONE LANDS: ADR 0013 alone gives the right pressure with dV 0.315 (1.8x too small), and this row alone gives the right ratio with dMAP 4.958 (2.5x too large). DO NOT ENTER 1400. It is illustrative. This row must be REPLACED by sourced venous compliance, and that table is the target the sourced relation has to EXPLAIN. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13. || THE HUMAN BAND THIS ROW IS COMPARED AGAINST MOVED 2026-09-16. ecf_salt_response_extract.py de-indexed van den Bosch's extracellular volume with ONE body surface area where the paper prints one per arm, understating the human expansion by 9 percent. The tracer limb goes 0.553 -> 0.602 L per 100 mmol/day and the human dMAP/dV_ecf band 2.97-4.16 -> 2.82-4.02 mmHg/L. THIS ROW'S VALUE IS UNCHANGED and was not refitted to the new band - that would be fitting a parameter to a target the same pass moved. ADR 0013 addendum, validation/ecf_deindex_prereg.md.
"""
const CV_VENOUS_RETURN_SENSITIVITY = 1400.0

"""Volume-keyed natriuretic gain [(mEq/day)/L] +/- 500-900 (range)
Source (tier B, derived): ROUTE B, ACUTE ISOTONIC EXPANSION, TWO HUMAN STUDIES. Lobo DN, Stanga Z, Simpson JA, Anderson JA, Rowlands BJ, Allison SP. Clin Sci (Lond) 2001;101(2):173-9. PMID 11473492. 10 healthy men, 2 L of 0.9% saline over 1 h, double-blind crossover: 95 mmol urinary sodium over the 6 h after infusion, and a 7.5% fall in haematocrit which gives the intravascular share of the load DIRECTLY with red cell mass fixed. || Jensen JM, Mose FH, Bech JN, Nielsen S, Pedersen EB. BMC Nephrol 2013;14:202. PMID 24067081. 23 healthy subjects, randomised crossover on a standardised diet, 0.9% saline 23 mL/kg: fractional sodium excretion rose 123%. || CROSS-CHECKED against ROUTE A, the chronic steady-state constraint built from the meta-analytic human pressure response and the pooled human ECF response already extracted in this repo. || CORROBORATING the timescale: Drummer C, Heer M, Baisch F, Blomqvist CG, Lang RE, Maass H, Gerzer R. Acta Physiol Scand Suppl 1992;604:101-11. PMID 1324562. 6 healthy men, 22 mL/kg 0.9% saline: sodium excretion rose 3-fold, under 15% of the load was excreted acutely, and excretion stayed elevated beyond 48 h - the excretion of an acute isotonic load takes days, which is what this model does.
Notes: RE-ESTIMATED 2026-09-03, 700 -> 585, AND THIS ROW'S OWN NOTE REQUIRED IT. It said: part of 700 compensates for a volume excursion 1.5-2.1x too large while CV.VENOUS_RETURN.SENSITIVITY is calibrated, so re-estimate when G_vr is sourced. G_vr was sourced in healthy humans on 2026-09-03 (2880 -> 1400, section 3.19), the caveat came due, and this is that re-estimation. NOT a new search - the same two human datasets, re-solved against corrected inputs. || THE PROCEDURE IS UNCHANGED AND THE IDENTIFICATION IS CLEAN. Two data, two parameters: the CHRONIC salt-step response fixes this gain, Lobo's 6 h ACUTE time course fixes RN.ANP.TAU. A first-order lag cannot move a steady state, so the two are separately identified - measured, not assumed: at G_anp 500/700/900 the chronic sensitivity is 2.275/1.720/1.382 regardless of tau, and 1/sensitivity is linear in the gain. || THE VALUE. Solved for a chronic salt sensitivity of 2.00 mmHg per 100 mmol/day, the CENTRE of the meta-analytic human 1.70-2.30 (Cutler 1997, He 2013, He 2002; k = 3). Verified by running rather than interpolating: 585 gives exactly 2.000. || WHAT IT NOW REPRODUCES, at G_pn = 8.4 and G_vr = 1400. Lobo 2001 (PMID 11473492): 566 mL and 95.1 mmol over the 6 h after 2 L of 0.9% saline, against a measured 563 and 95 - within 0.5 percent on both. READ THAT AS A FIT RESIDUAL AND NOT AS ACCURACY: RN.ANP.TAU WAS ESTIMATED AGAINST THIS SAME 6 H TIME COURSE, as the identification paragraph on this row says, so agreeing with it closely is what estimation is FOR rather than evidence that the model predicts anything. Corrected 2026-09-04, when HANDOVER was found claiming the four Lobo endpoints as validations in three places while section 3.21 said two paragraphs above that Lobo fixed the lag. The genuinely out-of-sample number is Jensen 2013, and it is a third low. What DOES survive here is an internal consistency check: the two Lobo endpoints imply the same lag independently, 0.171 d from the volume and 0.166 d from the sodium. Two endpoints of one protocol in the same subjects are not an independent test, and the acute limb of this model rests entirely on that one protocol class - Lobo and Jensen are both intravenous isotonic saline into healthy volunteers. Chronic salt sensitivity 2.000 against 1.70-2.30. dMAP/dV_ecf 3.000 mmHg/L against a measured human 2.97-4.16. || AND AN OUT-OF-SAMPLE CHECK THAT IS REPORTED RATHER THAN TUNED. Jensen 2013 (PMID 24067081) was NOT used in the estimation. The model predicts a peak fractional sodium excretion rise of 82.5% against the reported 123%. Inside the harness band and about a third low. That is the honest standing of this parameterisation on data it was not fitted to, and it is the sharpest remaining discrepancy in the sodium limb. || WHY THE GAIN FELL WHILE THE MODEL GOT BETTER. G_vr halving roughly doubled the model's volume excursion per unit intake, so a smaller gain now produces the same chronic natriuresis. 700 x 0.836 = 585, and the 0.836 is the correction the old note predicted would be needed. || SENSED VARIABLE, FORM AND EVIDENCE BASE ARE UNCHANGED - see the citation column and ADR 0010. The algebraic form was refuted before the lag was added and that finding stands. Uncertainty range 500-900 spans the chronic window 1.70-2.30. || TRAILING ZERO REMOVED 2026-09-09: 585.0 read as four significant figures against a range of 500-900. THE ROW IS EXEMPTED from the precision gate rather than rounded further, and the reason is that this is a SOLVED value, not a measured one - it is the gain that reproduces a chronic salt sensitivity of 2.00, so its digits are reproducible even though its CONFIDENCE is only as good as the 500-900 the target window permits. Those are different quantities and this row now says so. || THE OUT-OF-SAMPLE FIGURE ON THIS ROW IS SUPERSEDED TWICE OVER. CORRECTED 2026-09-17, HANDOVER section 3.45. This note says the model predicts a peak fractional sodium excretion rise of 82.5 percent against Jensen's reported 123, 'inside the harness band and about a third low'. FIRST, IT WAS STALE: the macula densa arm of ADR 0021 moved the endpoint on 2026-09-05 and nothing recorded it. SECOND, AND WORSE, THE COMPARISON WAS NOT LIKE FOR LIKE: it set a MODEL PEAK against Jensen's value in its FINAL SAMPLING PERIOD, and Jensen's series is monotone rising to that period - 1.26, 1.93, 2.35, 2.67, 2.80 - so the study never observed a peak at all. || MEASURED ON JENSEN'S OWN 210-240 MINUTE WINDOW the model is at 110.1 percent against a measured 122, inside a reported 2.80 +/- 0.75, and it tracks the whole observed time course. || THE FIRST CLAUSE OF THIS NOTE STANDS. Jensen was and remains held out of this row's estimation - the gain is solved for a chronic salt sensitivity of 2.00 and against nothing acute - so it is still the one place this parameterisation is tested rather than fitted. What is withdrawn is the characterisation of the result. || AND THE AGREEMENT IS NOT EVIDENCE FOR THIS ROW EITHER. This row already records that the zero-correlation upper bound on Jensen's ratio is -18 to +502 percent, so 110 against 122 is as uninformative as 79 was. THE GAIN WAS NOT RE-SOLVED IN THE CORRECTION and must not be re-solved against Jensen now that the two agree - that would convert the only out-of-sample datum into an estimation set. || WHAT IS ACTUALLY OPEN IS THE LATE TIME COURSE, AND NOTHING BOUNDS IT. The model's maximum falls at 375 minutes after infusion start; Jensen's protocol ends at 240 with its series still climbing. RN.ANP.TAU, this row's partner, was estimated against Lobo's 6 h CUMULATIVE endpoints, which fix the area under the curve and leave the peak time free. A human isotonic-saline study sampling past 6 h would test it; Drummer 1992, PMID 1324562, is the first place to look. || THE ACUTE AND CHRONIC LIMBS DISAGREE BY A FACTOR OF THREE, MEASURED 2026-09-17. HANDOVER section 3.46, validation/late_time_course_prereg.md branch L2. Drummer 1992 (PMID 1590419, abstract read in full; six healthy supine volunteers, 2 L of isotonic saline in 25 min, 48 h of collections plus a 48 h control) reports elevated body weight returning to baseline with an approximate half-life of 7 hours. The model gives 13.10 h. Scaling THIS GAIN alone by 3 reaches 7.10 h and drives the chronic salt sensitivity from 1.960 to 0.766 against the human 1.70-2.30 this row is solved against. Scaling both natriuretic gains by 3 gives 6.98 h and 0.681. || THIS ROW WAS NOT RE-SOLVED AND MUST NOT BE. Section 4 of the pre-registration forbade it and nothing moved. A gain chosen to fix the acute decay would break the chronic target this row exists to reproduce, and a gain split between the two would be a fitted compromise with no evidence behind the split. || WHAT THE MEASUREMENT IS EVIDENCE ABOUT IS THE FORM, NOT THE VALUE. A single linear volume-keyed gain cannot serve an acute decay that needs 3x what the chronic steady state permits; a saturating or multi-timescale path can. That is ADR 0010's original proposal, which it never built, and the record now carries this as its identifying experiment - replacing the acute-MAGNITUDE comparison that HANDOVER section 3.45 retired earlier the same day. The two constraints any such build must satisfy SIMULTANEOUSLY are the 7 h half-life and the 1.70-2.30 chronic window. || RENAMED 2026-09-18, CV.ANP.NATRIURETIC_GAIN -> CV.VOLUME.NATRIURETIC_GAIN, anp_label_prereg.md. NO NUMBER MOVED AND THE MODEL OUTPUT IS BIT-IDENTICAL - that was the pass's only test. FAILURE MODE #11: a name carrying a convention its value contradicts. The `name` field already read 'Volume-keyed natriuretic gain' and ADR 0010's own Decision already called it 'a lumped volume-keyed natriuretic term, algebraic in V_blood, with ANP as its named evidence base rather than as a state'. THE param_id DID NOT SAY THAT, and the param_id is what appears in code, in LedgerParams.jl and in the GUI. || AND 'ANP AS ITS NAMED EVIDENCE BASE' WAS ITSELF WRONG. This gain was estimated from WHOLE-BODY ISOTONIC EXPANSION - Lobo 2001 and Jensen 2013 - which measures the TOTAL natriuretic response to a volume load. ANP was the mechanism assumed to carry that total, not the thing measured. HANDOVER section 3.57 supplied the counter-evidence: Lohmeier's split-bladder dogs give a denervated/innervated sodium ratio near 0.56 at sustained elevated pressure, so RENAL SYMPATHETIC WITHDRAWAL carries a substantial share of exactly the response this gain was fitted to. The gain is not wrong. The attribution was. || WHAT THIS LUMP CONTAINS, NAMED AND NOT QUANTIFIED: atrial natriuretic peptide; renal sympathetic withdrawal via cardiopulmonary volume receptors; and whatever else volume expansion does to tubular sodium handling that neither of those covers. THE SHARE IS DELIBERATELY NOT SPLIT. Lohmeier's ratio implies a nerve share near 47 percent, but the authors report a latent impairment in sodium excretion from the denervated kidneys, making that an UPPER BOUND rather than an estimate; the protocol is ANG II hypertension and not a salt step; n = 5 and abstract only. The supportable statement is 'between zero and about a half, biased high', and a parameter with that interval buys nothing. Directive 1.14. || THE TRAP THIS RENAME EXISTS TO PREVENT: a future ANP infusion dose-response MUST NOT be added on top of this gain. This gain ALREADY CONTAINS ANP's contribution, along with the neural one. Sourcing an ANP-specific gain would require SPLITTING this row, not supplementing it. || OLD IDENTIFIER CV.ANP.NATRIURETIC_GAIN IS RETAINED IN THE NOTE TEXT ABOVE AND THROUGHOUT THIS LEDGER'S HISTORY, which is append-only and was not rewritten - searching the old name still finds the record.
"""
const CV_VOLUME_NATRIURETIC_GAIN = 585.0


