"""Basal endogenous glucose production, adult plateau [mg/kg/min]
Source (tier B, reported): Huidekoper HH, Ackermans MT, Ruiter AFC, Sauerwein HP, Wijburg FA. Endogenous glucose production from infancy to adulthood: a non-linear regression model. Arch Dis Child 2014;99(12):1082-1087. PMID 24996789.
Notes: THE ADULT PLATEAU of endogenous glucose production. Huidekoper 2014, PMID 24996789, ABSTRACT READ IN FULL on PubMed 2026-09-21. glucose_insulin_prereg.md section 9.4. || MEASURED BY [6,6-2H2] GLUCOSE DILUTION after an overnight fast during normoglycaemia, n = 40 healthy subjects aged 2.5-54.3 y, fitted as a one-phase exponential decay: EGP (mg/kg/min) = 6.50 * e^(-0.145 * age_y) + 1.93. || 1.93 IS THE ASYMPTOTE AND THAT IS WHY IT IS THE ENTERED VALUE. Beyond about 30 years the exponential term contributes under 2 percent, so EGP is 1.93 to 1.97 across the adult range. THIS MODEL HAS NO AGE DIMENSION, so the age term is not representable and the plateau is the only part of the regression it can consume. || THE COHORT IS PAEDIATRIC-WEIGHTED AND THAT IS THE LIMITATION. It is an Archives of Disease in Childhood paper whose purpose is paediatric fluid therapy; the adult end of its range is the sparse end. || NO DISPERSION IS PUBLISHED for the regression parameters in the abstract, so uncertainty_type is none and THAT IS AN EXTRACTION GAP RATHER THAN A PRECISION CLAIM - the same kind as Lorenz's SEMs. Retiring it needs the full text. || DIRECTIVE 1.12: section 2 listed '2 mg/kg/min' in advance. It is close, and it carries neither a cohort nor a functional form. || SCALING: PER KILOGRAM, so it multiplies body mass directly rather than a size factor. That is the paper's own normalisation and no conversion is applied.
"""
const GLU_EGP_BASAL = 1.93

"""Plasma insulin at half-maximal suppression of endogenous glucose production [pmol/L] +/- 46 (sd)
Source (tier A, reported): Rizza RA, Mandarino LJ, Gerich JE. Dose-response characteristics for effects of insulin on production and utilization of glucose in man. Am J Physiol 1981;240(6):E630-E639. PMID 7018254. 15 healthy subjects, graded 8 h insulin infusion, EGP measured isotopically. Half-maximal suppression of glucose production 29 +/- 2 microunits/ml, against 55 +/- 7 for half-maximal glucose utilization (P < 0.01). Corroborated on the form, NOT pooled, by Groop LC, Bonadonna RC, DelPrato S, Ratheiser K, Zyck K, Ferrannini E, DeFronzo RA. J Clin Invest 1989;84(1):205-213. PMID 2661589.
Notes: PLASMA INSULIN AT HALF-MAXIMAL SUPPRESSION OF ENDOGENOUS GLUCOSE PRODUCTION. Pre-registered in validation/egp_suppression_prereg.md, branch E1; ADR 0034. || WHY IT EXISTS. The model's EGP was a CONSTANT, so insulin could not suppress hepatic glucose output at all. Unrestrained EGP is the principal driver of FASTING hyperglycaemia in BOTH diabetes types, so a model with fixed EGP can only raise glucose by failing to DISPOSE of it - it represents the postprandial defect and not the fasting one. A missing term, not a wrong coefficient. HANDOVER section 4 named it the single biggest gap in the glucose axis. || THE SOURCE. Rizza RA, Mandarino LJ, Gerich JE. Dose-response characteristics for effects of insulin on production and utilization of glucose in man. Am J Physiol 1981;240(6):E630-E639. PMID 7018254. 15 healthy subjects, insulin infused 8 h at sequential rates from 0.2 to 5.0 mU/kg/min, 2 h per rate, glucose production and utilization measured isotopically with [3-3H]glucose. GRADED INFUSION IS WHY THIS SOURCE: a single-step clamp gives one point and cannot constrain a dose-response, which egp_suppression_prereg.md section 3 excluded before searching. || THE VALUE AND THE CONTRAST THAT MAKES IT MATTER. Half-maximal suppression of glucose PRODUCTION at 29 +/- 2 microunits/ml, against half-maximal glucose UTILIZATION at 55 +/- 7, significantly different at P < 0.01. PRODUCTION IS ROUGHLY TWICE AS INSULIN-SENSITIVE AS UTILIZATION, which is precisely why a model that suppresses neither cannot represent fasting hyperglycaemia. || UNIT CONVERSION, STATED BECAUSE IT IS LOAD-BEARING. 1 microunit/ml = 6.00 pmol/L for human insulin (1 IU = 0.0347 mg, MW 5808), so 29 microunits/ml = 174 pmol/L. The model carries insulin in pmol/L and its fasting value is 64.2 pmol/L = 10.7 microunits/ml, consistent with the NHANES row this model already uses. || DISPERSION. Rizza reports SEM. SD = 2 * sqrt(15) = 7.75 microunits/ml = 46 pmol/L, per pooling.md and thirst_prereg.md section 8.4 - pooling shrinks uncertainty in a mean and must not shrink population spread. || WHAT IS DERIVED FROM IT AND DELIBERATELY NOT STORED. The hyperbolic constant K_egp = I50 - 2*I_fast = 45.6 pmol/L and the zero-insulin production EGP_0 = 2.41 x basal are computed IN-COMPONENT from this row and the fasting insulin row. Storing them would be a precision claim and a drift risk - the ADR 0032 lesson about derived constants, applied on purpose. || AN OUT-OF-SAMPLE CHECK THE ANCHORS DID NOT GUARANTEE. The inversion implies EGP at ZERO insulin is 2.41 times basal. Uncontrolled type 1 EGP is reported elevated roughly two- to threefold, so the form lands inside the literature range at a point neither anchor constrained. egp_suppression_prereg.md section 7 test 4. || THE COST OF A HILL COEFFICIENT OF 1, DECLARED IN ADVANCE AND MEASURED. Rizza reports production 'completely suppressed' at approximately 60 microunits/ml; this hyperbolic gives 27 percent of basal there. IT UNDER-SUPPRESSES AT HIGH INSULIN AND THAT IS NOT TUNED AWAY - section 9 of the pre-registration names raising h to match a qualitative word as a way the pass fails. Only a digitised multi-point curve could justify h > 1. || CORROBORATION, INDEPENDENT AND NOT POOLED. Groop LC, Bonadonna RC, DelPrato S, Ratheiser K, Zyck K, Ferrannini E, DeFronzo RA. J Clin Invest 1989;84(1):205-213, PMID 2661589 - a DIFFERENT laboratory (Pisa-Yale), graded physiologic hyperinsulinaemia at +5, +15, +30, +70 and +200 microunits/ml in eight controls, reporting progressive suppression of hepatic glucose production. It confirms the DOSE-RESPONSE SHAPE; no I50 is extractable from its abstract, so it is corroboration on the FORM and is NOT pooled. || SINGLE-SOURCE for the value, stated as such; OPEN-QUESTIONS B21. || SCALING: none, a concentration.
"""
const GLU_EGP_INSULIN_I50 = 174.0

"""Fasting plasma insulin in healthy adults [pmol/L] +/- 41.9-103.2 (iqr)
Source (tier A, reported): NHANES 2007-2008, 2009-2010 and 2011-2012, extracted by validation/glucose_insulin_extract.py. n = 5,563 adults, the same population as GLU.PLASMA.FASTING.
Notes: WEIGHTED MEDIAN fasting plasma insulin, 9.25 uU/mL, in 5,563 healthy adults, from the same extraction and the same exclusions as GLU.PLASMA.FASTING. glucose_insulin_prereg.md sections 9.5 and 10. || IT WAS MEASURED ON 2026-09-21 AND DELIBERATELY NOT ENTERED THEN, because branch G2 had removed its consumer and a ledger row nothing reads is what directive 1.11 exists to prevent. ADR 0031 built the insulin split, so the consumer now exists and the row enters. || THE MEDIAN, NOT THE MEAN, AND THAT WAS FIXED BEFORE THE DATA WERE SEEN - section 10.4. Fasting insulin is right-skewed: the weighted mean is 11.87 uU/mL against a median of 9.25, 28 percent apart, so a mean would have described nobody typical. || AN INDEPENDENT CHECK NOBODY ARRANGED: Merovci 2021's 12 healthy subjects have a fasting insulin of 9 +/- 1 mU/L, which is 62 pmol/L against this row's 64.2 - a different decade, cohort and assay. || DISPERSION IS THE IQR, not a standard error; design strata and PSU are not used, so any standard error would be optimistic. || SCALING: none, it is a concentration.
"""
const GLU_INSULIN_FASTING = 64.2

"""Glucose increment at half-maximal insulin response [mmol/L]
Source (tier B, derived): Merovci A, Tripathy D, Chen X, Valdez I, Abdul-Ghani M, Solis-Herrera C, Basu S, DeFronzo RA. Effect of Mild Physiologic Hyperglycemia on Insulin Secretion, Insulin Clearance, and Insulin Sensitivity in Healthy Glucose-Tolerant Subjects. Diabetes 2021;70(1):204-213. PMID 33033064. FULL TEXT READ, supplied by the owner.
Notes: THE HALF-SATURATION of the plasma glucose to plasma insulin relation, 17.7 mmol/L above fasting, entered at two figures as 18. || THE SOURCE IS A TWO-STEP HYPERGLYCAEMIC CLAMP IN 12 HEALTHY SUBJECTS with no family history of type 2 diabetes, and what is taken is the BASELINE arm, before the 72-hour glucose infusion. The post-infusion arm is an intervention and glucose_insulin_prereg.md section 12.4 excludes it for setting a healthy relation. || THE PROTOCOL COMES FROM THE METHODS AND NOT THE ABSTRACT, and the difference matters: the abstract's '100 and 300 mg/dL' are INCREMENTS, while the methods give step one as 100 mg/dL above baseline and step two as 400 mg/dL ABSOLUTE. Second-phase insulin is an incremental AUC over 70-minute windows, so the plateau concentrations are those AUCs divided by 70 and added to fasting: 9 mU/L at 5.00 mmol/L, 42 at 10.55, 78 at 22.20. || AN EXACT TWO-POINT INVERSION, NOT A FIT. Two parameters from two increments reproduce both points identically, so there is NO goodness of fit and no residual - the form is interpolation between two measured points and extrapolation beyond them. || UNCERTAINTY IS LARGE AND IS WHY ONLY TWO FIGURES ARE ENTERED. The two AUCs carry SE of 21 and 24 percent, and the inversion is sensitive to their ratio, so these constants are known to tens of percent at best. Directive 1.13. || SE, NOT SD: the AUC dispersions are standard errors over n = 12, so thirst_prereg.md section 8.4's conversion applies to any population sampling of this relation. || SINGLE-SOURCE, AND IT SAYS SO. A second two-step hyperglycaemic clamp in healthy adults reporting both axes would pool with this one; OPEN-QUESTIONS B21 carries the debt. || WHY A SATURATING FORM AND NOT A LINE. Merovci's own two intervals give 5.98 mU/L per mmol/L and then 3.04 - the response HALVES across the range. A linear relation fitted to the first interval would overstate insulin at 22 mmol/L roughly twofold and would buffer hyperglycaemia that people do not buffer. || SCALING: none, a concentration increment.
"""
