Notes: AVAILABLE DIETARY CARBOHYDRATE - the glucose input the model did not have. Weighted MEDIAN of DR1TCARB minus DR1TFIBE, 224.7 g/day, IQR 167.7-301.9, in 5,337 adults. glucose_insulin_prereg.md, ADR 0029 amendment. || AVAILABLE, NOT TOTAL. Fibre is not absorbed as glucose and counting it would overstate appearance; the subtraction is arithmetic on two columns of the SAME recall, not an assumption. || WHY IT MATTERS: ADR 0029 ceilinged plasma glucose at (appearance + TmG)/GFR = 25.5 mmol/L because appearance was HEPATIC PRODUCTION ALONE. The model ate sodium and drank water and took in no glucose. With dietary carbohydrate the ceiling rises to about 34 mmol/L, which is severe hyperglycaemia rather than an artefact of a fasting-only model. || THE HEALTHY OPERATING POINT IS UNCHANGED BY CONSTRUCTION, because k_glu is DERIVED as appearance/G_fast and appearance now includes this term. A larger input gives a proportionally larger clearance and the same fasting glucose. || ENTERED TO THREE FIGURES, 225 g/day. The median of 5,337 would support four, but a 24-hour recall does not: it is a single day per person and it UNDER-REPORTS, so the fourth figure would be false precision on a biased instrument. Directive 1.13. || DISPERSION IS THE IQR AND IT IS WIDER THAN THE HABITUAL DISTRIBUTION. One recall per person means this spread is between-person variation PLUS within-person day-to-day variation. For a population model that matters: sampling individuals from this IQR would overstate how different people's habitual diets are. Stated rather than corrected, because correcting it needs a second recall day. || DIRECTION OF BIAS: dietary recall under-reports intake, so the true median is HIGHER and the true ceiling HIGHER still. The deviation is conservative for the model. || SCALING: MASS-like. Energy and carbohydrate intake track body size, so it carries mass_factor rather than the surface-like factor GFR uses.
