"""Exponent of renal potassium excretion in plasma potassium [dimensionless] +/- 11.9-24.7 (range)
Source (tier A, derived): Cappuccio FP, Buchanan LA, Ji C, Siani A, Miller MA. Systematic review and meta-analysis of randomised controlled trials on the effects of potassium supplements on serum potassium and creatinine. BMJ Open 2016;6(8):e011716. doi:10.1136/bmjopen-2016-011716. PMID 27566636. PMC5013341. OPEN ACCESS (CC BY-NC), READ IN FULL from the Europe PMC full text. 20 trials, 21 independent groups, 1216 participants, 12 countries, minimum four weeks of supplementation, and INTAKE VERIFIED BY 24-HOUR URINE COLLECTION - which validation/potassium_doseresponse_prereg.md section 2 requires and dietary recall does not satisfy. Table 1 reports urinary potassium AND plasma or serum potassium in both arms of every trial, which is the elasticity this row is the reciprocal of, measured twenty times over. Extracted in validation/potassium_doseresponse_extract.py.
Notes: RE-SOURCED 2026-09-06 UNDER validation/potassium_doseresponse_prereg.md, WHICH WAS COMMITTED AT 1698d2a BEFORE THE SEARCH WAS RUN. The value moved 17.71 -> 17.73, which is nothing, AND THAT IS NOT WHAT THIS PASS BOUGHT. || WHAT CHANGED IS THE DISPERSION. The row previously rested on six studies in ten subjects (Brunner 1970) whose per-study exponents were 16.31, 19.11, 23.45, 3.45, 13.26 and 41.68 - a TWELVEFOLD spread, and the weakest number in the potassium component. It now rests on 1216 participants with a 95 percent interval of 11.9 to 24.7, which excludes both ends of that spread. The pre-registration's section 8 fixed exactly that as the test of whether the pass was worth running. || THIS ROW IS AN ELASTICITY AND THAT IS WHY IT TRANSFERS. At this model's steady state 1/n_K is d ln(plasma potassium) / d ln(intake), which is dimensionless and survives differences in assay, cohort and country - the property section 3.26 established the hard way when a thyroid slope and a concentration turned out not to share a measurement scale. || THREE INDEPENDENT ROUTES AGREE TO 11 PERCENT: the pre-registered admissible subset of this paper gives 17.73, the full pooled meta-analysis 16.00, and Brunner's median 17.71. The two that carry a dispersion disagree by far less than either dispersion. || THE VALUE COMES FROM THE ADMISSIBLE SUBSET AND THE INTERVAL FROM THE FULL POOL, and the split is deliberate. Pre-registration section 2 excludes hypertensive cohorts and diuretics, which costs 16 of the 19 trials that report both quantities; three trials cannot carry a dispersion and 1216 participants can. Both come from this one paper and neither is a different study. || POOLED AS A RATIO OF SUMS, not as a mean of ratios. Two trials report IDENTICAL plasma potassium in both arms, so their exponent is infinite and no mean of exponents exists; one (Siani 1987) is NEGATIVE, plasma potassium falling on a supplement, and it is kept because discarding the inconvenient sign narrows a spread without evidence. || BRUNNER 1970 IS DEMOTED TO A COMPARISON and stays in the record. Its median was right; what it could not carry was an interval. || WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX: there is still no potassium adaptation in this model - OPEN-QUESTIONS B11 - and the pre-registration's section 7 forbade this pass from touching it. || THE ROW THE SUITE DEMANDED. Excretion was first written LINEAR in plasma potassium, which makes the steady-state concentration PROPORTIONAL to intake: doubling a normal diet gave 7.6 mmol/L, a lethal hyperkalaemia from an ordinary dietary variation, and ADR 0021's falsifiable test 3 caught it on its first run. || d ln(intake) / d ln(plasma potassium) at steady state, per admissible study: 16.31, 19.11, 23.45, 3.45, 13.26, 41.68. MEDIAN 17.71 entered, and the median rather than the mean because two studies sit at the ends of a twelvefold spread and a mean would follow them. || A TWELVEFOLD SPREAD, REPORTED. What every value agrees on is that the exponent is FAR above 1, which is the physiological content: renal potassium excretion adapts so steeply that a fortyfold change in intake moves plasma potassium by a third. || IT LUMPS THREE MECHANISMS INTO ONE EXPONENT, and this is where ADR 0021 decision 5 is amended. Excretion rises with aldosterone, with distal flow and with plasma potassium; every human study found moves all three together, so no gain separates from the others. Collapsing them onto plasma potassium is a LUMPING and not a claim that the other two do not exist - and it means aldosterone's effect on potassium is present in this number rather than absent from the model. || WHAT IT DISQUALIFIES: anything whose perturbed variable is one of the three separately. Spironolactone, a potassium-sparing diuretic, primary aldosteronism and a pure flow change are all outside this model, because the model cannot tell them apart from a change in plasma potassium. || INDEPENDENTLY CORROBORATED IN DIRECTION, NOT IN MAGNITUDE. Hene 1988 (PMID 3199680), six healthy males on fixed sodium and potassium intake, reports 'a steep positive relation between plasma K and urine K' - which is this exponent's qualitative claim, from a different group, and the reason a value far above 1 is not an artefact of Brunner's six studies. No number could be taken: the paper is not open access and only its abstract was read. || WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER IS ADAPTATION. Rabelink 1990 (PMID 2266680) found renin and aldosterone back at baseline by day 20 of a 400 mmol/day load with kaliuresis maintained; a fixed exponent holds aldosterone at 2.80 times baseline for ever. ADR 0021 amendment A7 disagreement 2. || PRECISION CORRECTED 2026-09-09. The value carried more significant figures than its own reported interval supports, which tools/ledger_to_julia.py now refuses. Rounding it changes no claim - the discarded digits were below the width of the interval this row itself reports. || PRECISION CORRECTED 2026-09-09 under directive 1.13, second pass. An interval of 11.9-24.7 is +/-36% and supports two figures. The first pass rounded this row but stopped one figure short, because the gate then allowed two figures beyond what the interval supports - a tolerance I had given myself rather than the rule. The gate now enforces the rule exactly. Discarded digits were inside the row's own stated interval, so no claim changes.
"""
const K_EXCRETION_EXPONENT = 18.0

"""Fractional excretion of filtered potassium at the reference state [dimensionless]
Source (tier B, derived): DERIVED to close the potassium balance at the reference individual: FE_K = K.RENAL_FRACTION * K.INTAKE.NOMINAL / (RN.GFR.NOMINAL * K.PLASMA.REFERENCE). Sources on those rows.
Notes: IT LANDS AT ABOUT 10 PERCENT, WHICH IS WHERE THE TEXTBOOK PUTS IT - and that is a coincidence worth stating rather than a confirmation, because this row is DERIVED from the plasma potassium it would otherwise predict. It is the closure of the balance and not a measurement. || IT IS TEN TIMES THE FRACTIONAL EXCRETION OF SODIUM, which is the whole difference between the two ions in this nephron: sodium is reabsorbed and potassium is SECRETED distally, so its excretion can exceed what a purely reabsorptive nephron could deliver. The model does not represent the secretion, only its result. || WHAT IT MAKES IMPOSSIBLE: any statement about renal potassium handling as such. The model can say what happens to plasma potassium when intake or filtration changes, and cannot say anything about the distal nephron that was not put in here. || THE ROW ONCE SAID THIS LEVEL COULD NOT BE SOURCED BECAUSE THE CLEARANCE IS NOT MEASURED IN HEALTHY PEOPLE. THAT WAS FALSE and ADR 0021 amendment A7 retracts it: the Utrecht balance studies (Hene 1986 PMID 3523191, Hene 1988 PMID 3199680, Rabelink 1990 PMID 2266680) are six healthy volunteers each on controlled intake, and the search term was the bedside phrase rather than the physiology. What is true is narrower: no admissible study reports intake, plasma potassium AND glomerular filtration in the same healthy subjects, which is this model's own composite, so the row stays derived. || AND THE ROW BARELY MATTERS, WHICH IS MEASURED: sweeping it 0.04 to 0.16 moves steady-state plasma potassium only 4.18 to 3.87 mmol/L. The exponent pins the concentration, so falsifiable test 2 would be weak even with this row perfectly sourced. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13.
"""
const K_FRACTIONAL_EXCRETION = 0.1009

"""Dietary potassium intake [mmol/day] +/- 21.1-144.9 (range)
Source (tier A, reported): National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey 2007-2008, 2009-2010 and 2011-2012, public microdata, file DR1TOT, variable DR1TPOTA, restricted to recalls the survey itself flags reliable (DR1DRSTZ = 1). EXTRACTED HERE: validation/macula_densa_potassium_extract.py. n = 8893 adults 20+, not pregnant, weighted WTMEC2YR/3. Mean 69.06 mmol/day, median 64.56, 2.5th-97.5th percentile 21.1-144.9; men 78.82, women 59.84.
Notes: EXTENSIVE, AND SURFACE-LIKE: dietary intake tracks metabolic rate, which is what every other intake row in this model scales as since section 3.30. || A DIETARY RECALL UNDERSTATES INTAKE and the row says so rather than being quietly trusted. The same survey's sodium recall gives 153.6 mmol/day where 24-hour urine collections in US adults run higher, and the model's own BF.NA.INTAKE_NOMINAL is 205. Twenty-four-hour urinary potassium would be the better measurement and NHANES did not collect it in these cycles. || THE UNCERTAINTY IS THE POPULATION SPREAD, not an error on the mean: the mean of 8893 recalls is precise, and what the range says is that people differ sevenfold. || SEXED PAIR AVAILABLE AND NOT TAKEN - 78.8 against 59.8 mmol/day - because the potassium balance closes against a plasma concentration entered as `both`, and sexing one half against an unsexed other is the error metabolic_rate_prereg.md section 4 avoided for CO2 production.
