"""Exponent on intake in the urinary share of dietary potassium [dimensionless] +/- -0.08-0.87 (range)
Source (tier B, derived): Hene RJ, Koomans HA, Boer P, Dorhout Mees EJ. Adaptation to chronic potassium loading in normal man. Mineral and Electrolyte Metabolism 1986;12(3):165-72. PMID 3523191. SIX HEALTHY MALES, 18 days, controlled diet, 80 -> 300 mEq/day of potassium; urinary potassium 50 +/- 12 -> 233 +/- 45 mEq/day, so the non-renal loss went 30 -> 67 mmol/day. ABSTRACT-LEVEL ONLY - the paper is not open access and no full text was obtained, and the row says so rather than implying a full reading. It is the ONLY admissible study that measures the change WITHIN subjects, which is why it sets the shape and not the level. Extracted in validation/potassium_doseresponse_extract.py.
Notes: THE EXPONENT ON INTAKE IN THE URINARY SHARE, and the row that stops K.RENAL_FRACTION being a constant. Non-renal potassium loss goes as intake^(1 - p) = intake^0.61, so the share reaching the urine RISES with intake. || THE SHAPE WAS FIXED BEFORE THE DATA WERE SEEN, in section 5 of validation/potassium_doseresponse_prereg.md committed at 1698d2a, and the only thing the data chose was this number. || DERIVED FROM HENE'S TWO WITHIN-SUBJECT POINTS: the ratio of non-renal losses, 0.3750/0.2233 = 1.679, over an intake ratio of 3.75, gives p = ln(1.679)/ln(3.75) = 0.392, entered at two figures because that is what two means of six subjects support. || IT IS 1.6 STANDARD ERRORS FROM ZERO AND THE INTERVAL INCLUDES A CONSTANT FRACTION. Propagating the published SDs as standard errors of the mean (12/sqrt(6) and 45/sqrt(6)) gives p = 0.39 +/- 0.24, 95 percent interval -0.08 to 0.87. THE STATISTIC DOES NOT CARRY THIS ROW. What carries it is that colonic potassium secretion rises with intake, which is E1, so a constant share would require the gut to scale its losses exactly with the diet - and the owner's judgement, on 2026-09-08, that the one datum pointing the other way measured tablet absorption rather than renal handling. || ONE STUDY, SIX SUBJECTS, ABSTRACT ONLY. That is the weakest provenance of any structural row in this ledger and it is stated here rather than left to be discovered. A second within-subject dataset would either tighten it or refute it, and Holbrook 1984 (Am J Clin Nutr, PMID 6486085, metabolic balance on self-selected diets) is the candidate that was not obtainable. || RABELINK 1990's 0.80 AT 400 mmol/day IS NOT USED HERE and the pre-registration's own rule is why: that figure is the SECOND 24-hour period of the load, and section 2 requires the intake held five days. It was quoted as supporting evidence for two days and never qualified. || WHAT IT CANNOT DO: represent potassium DEPLETION. Below the reference intake this curve keeps falling, where a real kidney conserves hard and Brunner's depletion periods show urinary excretion EXCEEDING intake as a store empties. || HOLBROOK 1984 ARGUES AGAINST THIS ROW AND IS THE BETTER STUDY. Holbrook JT, Patterson KY, Bodner JE, Douglas LW, Veillon C, Kelsay JL, Mertz W, Smith JC. Am J Clin Nutr 1984;40(4):786-93. doi:10.1093/ajcn/40.4.786. PMID 6486085. ABSTRACT-LEVEL ONLY - not open access, and the publisher, the DOI redirect and the USDA repository were all tried without reaching the full text. 28 adults, one year, four 7-day balances, meals and beverages and urine AND FECES by atomic absorption, SELF-SELECTED FOOD DIETS - so the tablet-absorption objection that removed Cappuccio's marginal fraction does not touch it. Apparent absorption of potassium 85 percent, and it 'DID NOT CHANGE SIGNIFICANTLY OVER THE WIDE RANGE OF INTAKES'. Apparent absorption already nets out colonic secretion, so a flat value across intakes is a direct statement that p = 0. || WHAT DEFENDS THIS ROW IS RANGE AND ONLY RANGE. Holbrook's subjects ate what they chose, so the span is that of self-selected diets - roughly 38 to 115 mmol/day - and across exactly that span this curve moves f_renal 0.853 to 0.905, about five percentage points, which 28 people with balance-study noise would not resolve. Hene's 80 to 300 is the range the model must span and Holbrook's design cannot test it. THAT IS A DEFENCE, NOT A REFUTATION: Holbrook has 28 subjects to Hene's 6, measures the gut term directly rather than by subtraction, and explicitly tested constancy and found it. || RECORDED HERE RATHER THAN LEFT FOR A READER TO FIND, because a row whose best contrary evidence lives only in a commit message is a row that will be quoted without it. OPEN-QUESTIONS B12. || DECIDED 2026-09-08 BY THE OWNER, OPTION 1 OF OPEN-QUESTIONS B12: the row stands, on the range argument alone. THE DEFENCE IS NOW A TEST rather than a paragraph - the suite asserts that this curve moves f_renal less than six percentage points across Holbrook's plausible span (38 to 115 mmol/day; presently 0.052), which is the whole reason a 28-subject balance study could report constancy without contradicting it. If anything ever steepens this exponent that assertion fires and the defence is gone with it. || PRECISION CORRECTED 2026-09-09 under directive 1.13, second pass. The interval is -0.08 to 0.87 and includes zero; it supports one figure. The first pass rounded this row but stopped one figure short, because the gate then allowed two figures beyond what the interval supports - a tolerance I had given myself rather than the rule. The gate now enforces the rule exactly. Discarded digits were inside the row's own stated interval, so no claim changes.
"""
const K_NONRENAL_LOSS_EXPONENT = 0.4

"""Plasma potassium in healthy adults [mmol/L] +/- 0.320 (sd)
Source (tier A, reported): National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey 2007-2012, file BIOPRO, variable LBXSKSI. n = 8809 adults 20+, not pregnant, weighted. Mean 3.968 mmol/L, SD 0.320.
Notes: AN INPUT, NOT AN OUTPUT, AND ADR 0021's FALSIFIABLE TEST 2 IS THEREFORE VOID. The pre-registration named this row a target and forbade using it to set a parameter; that is exactly what K.FRACTIONAL_EXCRETION now does, and saying so is the only honest way to enter it. || WHY: renal potassium clearance in healthy adults could not be sourced. Searches for fractional potassium excretion, potassium clearance and renal potassium handling return diabetic ketoacidosis, chronic kidney disease, diuretic therapy, transplantation and Gitelman syndrome - directive 1.7 disqualifying a sixth literature, which macula_densa_potassium_prereg.md section 2 predicted in advance. Branch P4 said not to build a balance whose outflow is invented; the outflow's SHAPE is sourced from Brunner and only its LEVEL is not, which is a case the pre-registration did not anticipate and amendment 9 records. || FOURTH DEPENDENCY INVERSION IN THIS MODEL, after arterial PCO2 (ADR 0017), the thyroid operating point (section 3.26) and plasma bicarbonate (section 3.29). The pattern is now the rule rather than the exception: WHERE A CONCENTRATION IS MEASURED IN THOUSANDS OF PEOPLE AND ITS CLEARANCE IS MEASURED IN NOBODY HEALTHY, THE CONCENTRATION IS THE INPUT. || WHAT REMAINS A PREDICTION is the RESPONSE: how plasma potassium moves when intake or filtration changes depends on the sourced fractional excretion shape and not on this row.
