"""Half-saturation constant of the angiotensin II tubular effect, on the normalised pra axis [dimensionless]
Source (tier B, derived): Hall JE, Granger JP, Hester RL, Coleman TG, Smith MJ Jr, Cross RB. Mechanisms of escape from sodium retention during angiotensin II hypertension. Am J Physiol 1984;246(5 Pt 2):F627-F634.
Notes: HALF-SATURATION CONSTANT of the angiotensin II tubular effect, on this model's normalised pra axis. Derived 2026-09-19 with RAAS.ANGII.TUBULAR_VMAX from the same two measured points and the origin - see that row for the full derivation, the sources and every caveat. || Km = 1.0 means the effect is HALF-MAXIMAL at pra = 1.0, which is this model's rectified renin PLATEAU, and therefore already past half-maximal at the resting pra of 1.2956. THAT IS THE WHOLE CONTENT OF THE SATURATION FINDING: angiotensin II's tubular action is more than half spent at rest, so doubling it adds little. Hall's two studies say the same thing in their own units - a total of 0.0051 against a marginal 0.00144. || TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES. The value landing on exactly 1.0 is arithmetic from two-figure inputs and NOT a claim that the half-saturation point coincides with the plateau; a third figure on either source would move it. Directive 1.13. || MICHAELIS-MENTEN IS A CONVENTION. See RAAS.ANGII.TUBULAR_VMAX. SPECIES: dog. SCALING: none - Km is on a dimensionless normalised axis and its transfer rests on pra meaning the same physiological state in both species, which is the weakest step and is stated on the Vmax row.
