"""Maximal angiotensin II-dependent tubular sodium reabsorption, fraction of filtered load [dimensionless]
Source (tier B, derived): Hall JE, Granger JP, Hester RL, Coleman TG, Smith MJ Jr, Cross RB. Mechanisms of escape from sodium retention during angiotensin II hypertension. Am J Physiol 1984;246(5 Pt 2):F627-F634.
Notes: MAXIMAL ANGIOTENSIN II-DEPENDENT TUBULAR SODIUM REABSORPTION, as a fraction of the filtered load. Derived 2026-09-19 with RAAS.ANGII.TUBULAR_KM from TWO MEASURED POINTS AND THE ORIGIN, angii_saturating_prereg.md. NOT FITTED to anything this model is judged by. || THE TWO POINTS. f(pra = 1.2956) = 0.00510, from Hall 1977's five NORMAL dogs: intrarenal blockade of ENDOGENOUS angiotensin II took fractional sodium excretion from 1.23 +/- 0.19 to 1.74 +/- 0.28 percent with GLOMERULAR FILTRATION RATE AND ARTERIAL PRESSURE BOTH UNCHANGED, so the 0.51 percentage points is very nearly purely tubular. f(pra = 2.5795) = 0.00654, that total plus Hall 1984's marginal 24 mEq/day of 24/16724 = 0.00144 measured at FIXED renal perfusion pressure with plasma aldosterone already back at control. f(0) = 0 by definition: no angiotensin II, no angiotensin II-dependent reabsorption. || THE SOLUTION. Michaelis-Menten f(p) = Vmax*p/(Km+p) through those points gives Km = 1.0 and Vmax = 0.0091. TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES because both inputs carry two, directive 1.13. Check: 0.0091*1.2956/(1.0+1.2956) = 0.00514 against 0.00510, and 0.0091*2.5795/(1.0+2.5795) = 0.00656 against 0.00654. || MICHAELIS-MENTEN IS A CONVENTION AND NOT A MEASUREMENT. Two points and an origin determine a two-parameter saturating family and nothing more; the shape between and beyond them is assumed. It is the conventional choice for a receptor-mediated transport effect and the row says so rather than dressing it as sourced. ANY saturating curve through the same two points has a similar LOCAL SLOPE, which is the only thing the prediction this form was built to test turns on. || WHAT IT BUYS OVER THE LINE IT REPLACES. Local slope at the operating point is Vmax*Km/(Km+pra_ref)^2 = 0.00173 per unit pra against the superseded linear 0.0011 - 1.58 times steeper - because a straight line fitted across pra 1.296 to 2.58 averages over a range the curve flattens across. || THE MODEL'S TERM IS STILL A DEVIATION, fr_angii = f(pra) - f(pra_ref), because the baseline fractional reabsorption already contains angiotensin II's resting contribution. Identically zero at the operating point, so nothing pinned moves. || SPECIES: DOG, conscious, two preparations from the same laboratory - intrarenal blockade (n = 5 normal) and systemic infusion with servo-controlled renal perfusion pressure (n = 8). SCALING: none - both points are FRACTIONS OF THE FILTERED LOAD, which is intensive, so no body-size scaling applies; that is the same argument RAAS.RENIN.SYMPATHETIC_THRESHOLD_SHIFT makes for an intensive pressure, and it is still dog to human. || TWO POINTS DO NOT ESTABLISH A SATURATION CURVE. They are CONSISTENT with one. The preparations differ and the 1977 sodium-depleted arm is NOT usable for a tubular effect because glomerular filtration rate rose 22 percent in it. Only the normal-sodium arm is clean. || ================ THIS ROW IS THE SUPERSEDED LINEAR GAIN, 0.0011 per unit pra, AND ITS PROVENANCE IS CARRIED HERE VERBATIM. It was deleted from the ledger on 2026-09-19 by angii_saturating_prereg.md, not because anything in the derivation below was wrong but because the FORM it parameterised was. Hall 1977 and Hall 1984 together show the relation SATURATES - a total effect of 0.0051 of the filtered load at normal sodium against a marginal increment of only 0.00144 on raising angiotensin II to sodium-deprivation levels - and a term linear in pra cannot represent that. It is deleted rather than left beside its replacement because check_relations.py's unread-rows gate would fail an unused row, and because two copies of a fact is the failure this repository is organised against. Everything below is the record of how 0.0011 was obtained and why the deletion costs nothing. ================ SOURCED 2026-09-19 FROM HALL 1984, FULL TEXT READ, angii_tubular_prereg.md. PMID 6720967. Twelve conscious male mongrel dogs 21-27 kg (mean 22.2 +/- 0.8), chronically instrumented, sodium-deficient diet plus supplement to about 80 meq/day, ANG II [Asp1,Val5] at 5 ng/kg/min. EIGHT DOGS HAD RENAL PERFUSION PRESSURE SERVO-CONTROLLED AT THE CONTROL LEVEL by an inflatable aortic occluder driven by a proportional servo-control unit, 24 h a day. That preparation is the whole reason this row is possible: it excludes the pressure-natriuresis route by construction, and it cannot be performed in a human. Directive 1.15. || THE MAGNITUDE IS THE DAY-6 RATE AND NOT THE SIX-DAY AVERAGE. Cumulative sodium balance rose 210 +/- 37 meq over 6 days, which is 35 meq/day averaged - but that average is FRONT-LOADED over a transient the free arm shows directly (Table 1: UNaV falls to 20 meq/day on day 1 and recovers). At day 6 the servo arm excreted 56 +/- 5 meq/day against an intake of about 80, so the ONGOING retention is 24 +/- 5 meq/day. TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES, directive 1.13. || WHY THE DAY 6 POINT IS THE RIGHT ONE FOR A NON-ESCAPING TERM: at that point the renal perfusion pressure is STILL CLAMPED, so the pressure route is excluded by the preparation; plasma aldosterone has RETURNED TO CONTROL, 4.9 +/- 0.8 against a control of 4.6 +/- 1.0, so the aldosterone route is excluded BY MEASUREMENT rather than by assumption; and retention continues anyway. What is left is the direct tubular action of angiotensin II, which is exactly what ADR 0015 defines fr_angii to be. || AND HALL CONFIRMS ADR 0015'S STRUCTURAL CHOICE INDEPENDENTLY. His conclusion is that a rise in renal arterial pressure is ESSENTIAL for escape; releasing the occluder took urinary sodium from 56 to 322 +/- 35 meq/day in one day. So escape is PRESSURE-MEDIATED and not intrinsic to the tubular effect, which is why this term is added OUTSIDE the aldosterone escape state rather than inside it. The design was right before the source was found. || THE DERIVATION. Expressed as a fraction of the filtered load, which is intensive: 24 meq/day / (81.5 ml/min = 117.4 L/day x 142.5 meq/l = 16724 meq/day) = 0.00144. Anchored on Hall's own statement that the 5 ng/kg/min dose was calculated to reach plasma ANG II levels found during SODIUM DEPRIVATION, which on this model's pra axis is the low-salt arm: pra 2.5795 at 38 mEq/day against a resting 1.2956, a difference of 1.2839. So k_angii = 0.00144 / 1.2839 = 0.0011 per unit pra, TWO FIGURES. || SCALING: THE TRANSFER IS DONE ON THE FRACTION AND NOT ON THE RATE. A retention in meq/day is EXTENSIVE and would need a body-size scaling to cross species; a fraction of the filtered load is INTENSIVE and does not. That is the same argument RAAS.RENIN.SYMPATHETIC_THRESHOLD_SHIFT makes for an intensive pressure. It is the strongest transfer available and it is still dog to human, which this row states rather than hides. || THREE WEAK LINKS, NAMED IN THE PRE-REGISTRATION BEFORE ANY VALUE WAS ENTERED. (1) The cross-species fractional transfer above. (2) Mapping SODIUM DEPRIVATION onto this model's 38 mEq/day arm is THIS REPOSITORY'S JUDGEMENT AND NOT HALL'S - he names the physiological state, not a pra. (3) Hall's preparation has EXOGENOUS ANG II with SUPPRESSED ENDOGENOUS RENIN, plasma renin activity falling 0.27 to 0.04 ng/ml/h, and this model's pra conflates renin with angiotensin II and cannot represent that dissociation; the anchor therefore rests on total angiotensin II activity being comparable, not on the renin state matching. || NOT FITTED TO ANYTHING THIS MODEL IS JUDGED BY. ADR 0015 requires that the magnitude never be fitted to salt data, and it was not: it comes from a servo-controlled dog preparation at fixed pressure. Hall 1980's clamp contrast - control dogs taking under 7 mmHg across 5 to 500 meq/day against AngII-clamped dogs taking +42 percent - is ADR 0015's FALSIFIABLE TEST and was deliberately kept out of the estimation. || SPECIES: DOG, conscious, chronically instrumented, n = 8 in the servo arm. FULL TEXT READ - supplied by the owner on 2026-09-19, which is what made this row possible after the 2026-09-18 search recorded the mapping as the precise blocker. || THE ESTIMATION COHORT IS MALE - twelve conscious male mongrel dogs - and this row is entered as 'both' because it is a dimensionless fraction of the filtered load with no sexed input, not because the cohort was mixed. No sex difference in the direct tubular action of angiotensin II was sought or found here. Recorded the way RN.MD.RENIN_GAIN records its own all-male estimation set. || CORROBORATED AND QUALIFIED 2026-09-19 BY HALL 1977, FULL TEXT READ. Hall JE, Guyton AC, Trippodo NC, Lohmeier TE, McCaa RE, Cowley AW Jr. Intrarenal control of electrolyte excretion by angiotensin II. Am J Physiol 1977;232(6):F538-F544, PMID 879288. Supplied by the owner. || IT IS THE ENDOGENOUS VERSION OF THE SAME EXPERIMENT, AND IT NEEDS NO DOSE MAPPING AT ALL. Instead of infusing exogenous angiotensin II, Hall BLOCKED the endogenous hormone with intrarenal [Sar1,Ile8] angiotensin II at 0.25 ug/kg/min - so the contrast is a dog's own angiotensin II acting against the same dog with it blocked, in the same kidney. The anchor problem this row's derivation works around does not arise. || THE NORMAL-SODIUM ARM IS THE CLEAN ONE AND IT SITS AT THIS MODEL'S OWN OPERATING POINT. Five normal dogs, Table 1: fractional sodium excretion 1.23 +/- 0.19 percent rising to 1.74 +/- 0.28 percent after 90 min of blockade, with GLOMERULAR FILTRATION RATE UNCHANGED (0.83 +/- 0.08 to 0.86 +/- 0.11 ml/min per g, not significant) and mean arterial pressure unchanged (128.2 to 131.6 mmHg). So the 0.51 percentage point change is very nearly PURELY TUBULAR: endogenous angiotensin II accounts for 0.0051 of the filtered load being reabsorbed at normal sodium status. || AND ALDOSTERONE IS EXCLUDED AGAIN, BY A SECOND ROUTE. In four sodium-depleted dogs the blocker produced NO CONSISTENT CHANGES in plasma aldosterone concentration, so the natriuresis is not adrenal. That is the same conclusion Hall 1984 reaches by watching aldosterone return to control while retention continues - two different designs, same exclusion. || THE TWO STUDIES TOGETHER SAY THE RELATION IS SATURATING, AND THAT IS WHY THIS ROW'S TERM IS TOO WEAK. Hall 1977 gives the TOTAL angiotensin II-dependent tubular reabsorption at normal sodium as 0.0051 of the filtered load; Hall 1984 gives the MARGINAL increment on raising angiotensin II to sodium-deprivation levels as 24/16724 = 0.00144. The marginal is 0.28 of the total, so ROUGHLY A QUARTER OF THE RESTING EFFECT IS ADDED BY TAKING ANGIOTENSIN II TO SODIUM-DEPRIVATION LEVELS. The tubular action is largely SATURATED at normal angiotensin II. || THIS EXPLAINS THE REFUTATION RATHER THAN OVERTURNING IT. The marginal slope really is small - that is physiology, not a sourcing error - and the measured 1.2x amplification against ADR 0015's required 2x stands. What it indicts is the FORM: fr_angii linear in pra through a reference cannot represent a relation that is mostly saturated at rest with a small marginal slope. || A FALSIFIABLE PREDICTION FOR WHOEVER TAKES THIS NEXT, RECORDED BEFORE IT IS TRIED. A saturating form has a LARGER LOCAL SLOPE AT REST than a straight line fitted across pra 1.296 to 2.58, because the line averages over a range the curve flattens across. So replacing the linear term with a saturating one SHOULD RAISE the amplification above 1.2x. If it does not, the form is not what is wrong and ADR 0015's own suspicion - that the record is necessary and nowhere near sufficient - is the answer. || WHAT THESE NUMBERS DO NOT SUPPORT. Two points do not establish a saturation curve; they are CONSISTENT with one. The preparations differ - 1977 is intrarenal blockade in dogs whose kidneys averaged 59.5 +/- 4.1 g, 1984 is systemic infusion with servo-controlled pressure - and the sodium-depleted arm of 1977 is NOT usable for the tubular effect because glomerular filtration rate rose 22 percent in it, which the paper says accounts for an important part of the natriuresis. Only the normal-sodium arm is clean. NO VALUE ON THIS ROW WAS CHANGED on the strength of any of this.
"""
const RAAS_ANGII_TUBULAR_VMAX = 0.0091

"""Resting normalised plasma renin activity, the reference for the AngII tubular term [dimensionless]
Source (tier C, derived): Derived: this model's own resting pra with the AngII tubular term off. See notes.
Notes: DERIVED, and it exists so that RAAS.ANGII.TUBULAR_GAIN's term is IDENTICALLY ZERO AT THE OPERATING POINT. fr_angii = k_angii * (pra - pra_ref), so with pra_ref set to this model's own resting plasma renin activity the term vanishes at rest, the operating point does not move, and therefore the resting pra does not move either - the fixed point is self-consistent immediately and needs no iteration. || THE VALUE is the resting pra measured with the angiotensin II tubular term OFF, which is the state every other constant in this ledger was derived in. Measured by running: 1.2956. || IT IS NOT A MEASUREMENT AND MUST NEVER BE COMPARED WITH ONE. pra in this model is normalised so that 1.0 is the RECTIFIED PLATEAU, not resting renin - Raas.jl records that conflating the two is what broke RAAS.RENIN.PRESSURE_GAIN in the first place. This row is the model's own operating point on that normalised axis and carries no claim about human plasma renin activity. || CHANGING IT MOVES THE OPERATING POINT. If the resting pra ever changes - because a gain, a threshold or an arm upstream of renin changes - this row must be re-measured in the same pass, or fr_angii stops being zero at rest and every pinned resting value drifts with it.
"""
const RAAS_PRA_REFERENCE = 1.2956

"""Time constant of the renin response to a pressure step [day]  [!] ASSUMED
Source (tier C, assumed): 
Notes: ASSUMED, and deliberately not cited. The parked component attributed renin kinetics to Hofbauer KG et al. Pflugers Arch 1975; that paper was NOT opened, so per directive 1.5 it is not recorded here. 0.0035 day is about 5 minutes. WHY THE VALUE BARELY MATTERS: it is three orders of magnitude faster than the escape time constant and four faster than the renal-body fluid loop, so on this timebase the renin step is effectively instantaneous and any value in the minutes range gives the same trajectory. If it is ever made to matter, source it first. ADR 0002 would also justify making pra algebraic and dropping the state entirely.
"""
