"""Time of peak renal sodium excretion [day]  [!] ASSUMED
Source (tier B, assumed): Ueno M, Kawasaki T, Uezono K, Omae T, Matsuoka M. Relationship of urinary kallikrein excretion to renal water and sodium excretion. Metabolism 1983;32(5):433-437.
Notes: STILL ASSUMED AT 0.33 day, AND THE ACROPHASE IS CONTESTED TOO - the two best cosinor studies found are nearly ANTIPHASE. Ueno 1983, 20 healthy Japanese women on an ordinary diet, cosinor: acrophase of urinary sodium excretion at 16:55, i.e. late afternoon, closely tracking urine volume at 16:51 (r = 0.935). Under this component's convention with a 07:00 active-period start that is 0.41 day. Peng 2021, 85 healthy Chinese adults, cosinor: sodium excretion had a NOCTURNAL PEAK falling to its lowest around noon to afternoon - roughly the opposite. 0.33 day (about 15:00) is retained as a placeholder consistent with Ueno and inconsistent with Peng. It is NOT a compromise between them; there is no defensible compromise between antiphase estimates. Resolve with the Minors and Waterhouse constant-routine normative data before this parameter is used for anything.
"""
const CIRC_RENAL_NA_ACROPHASE = 0.33

"""Relative amplitude of circadian modulation of renal sodium handling [unitless]  [!] ASSUMED
Source (tier B, assumed): Kanabrocki EL, Scheving LE, Olwin JH, et al. Circadian variation in the urinary excretion of electrolytes and trace elements in men. Am J Anat 1983;166(2):121-148.
Notes: STILL ASSUMED AT 0.25, AND NOW WITH THE CONFLICT RECORDED RATHER THAN A SINGLE SOURCE. A wide sweep of the circadian sodium literature found the amplitude genuinely contested. UPPER END: Kanabrocki 1983, 11 healthy adult males, 3-hourly urine over 27 h with cosinor analysis, found a statistically significant population rhythm for sodium and reported that for almost every element the increase from lowest to highest 3-hour group mean exceeded 100%. A peak-to-trough ratio of 2 corresponds to a cosinor amplitude of 0.33, and >100% is a lower bound, so this supports 0.33 or more. LOWER END: Peng 2021, 85 healthy Chinese adults, every voided specimen over 24 h with cosinor analysis, found URINARY SODIUM SHOWED NON-SIGNIFICANT PARAMETERS IN THE COSINOR MODEL and that the sodium rhythm was less significant than potassium. That is close to an amplitude of zero. 0.25 sits between them and is NOT a pooled estimate - pooling a significant and a non-significant result is not defensible. THE RHYTHM'S EXISTENCE IS BETTER SUPPORTED THAN ITS SIZE: see CIRC.RENAL_NA.ENDOGENEITY_MARKER. THE PROPER SOURCE HAS BEEN IDENTIFIED AND NOT YET OBTAINED - Minors and Waterhouse 1990 provide normative ENDOGENOUS urinary sodium data from about 80 constant routines, which is exactly the unmasked quantity this parameter should carry. Get it before trusting any circadian sodium result.
"""
const CIRC_RENAL_NA_AMPLITUDE = 0.25

"""Lower limit of renal autoregulation [mmHg]
Source (tier A, reported): Finke R, Gross R, Hackenthal E, Huber J, Kirchheim HR. Threshold pressure for the pressure-dependent renin release in the autoregulating kidney of conscious dogs. Pflugers Arch 1983;399(2):102-110. PMID 6139786. Lower limit of renal blood flow autoregulation, measured directly by servo-controlled stepwise reduction of renal artery pressure in the conscious animal.
Notes: VALUE CHANGED 80.0 -> 63.9 mmHg ON 2026-08-27, AND SPECIES CORRECTED human -> dog. The previous value was NOT SOURCED: citation read 'Standard physiological reference. VERIFY.', which is not a reference, and species read 'human', which was not supported. This row was the last of the seven rows carrying that identical non-citation to be flagged as genuine debt rather than discharged, and HANDOVER 3.1 named it the one to look at first because CV.MAP.SETPOINT moved 93 -> 87 on the same day, carrying the operating point TOWARD this breakpoint. PRE-REGISTERED EXTRACTION: validation/autoreg_lower_prereg.md, committed at 3fbe260 BEFORE any paper was opened; executed by validation/autoreg_lower_extract.py. 26 PubMed queries in two sweeps, 1114 unique records screened. POOLING RULE, DECLARED BEFORE EXTRACTION per validation/pooling.md: single-source, n_studies k=1. No cross-species pooling was performed and no range midpoint was taken; both are prohibited for new entries. BRANCH 7 OF THE PRE-REGISTERED PROCEDURE WAS TAKEN. Branches 1-5 fail: NO human primary study reports a numeric lower breakpoint. The one that appears to - Textor SC et al, Ann Intern Med 1985;102(3):308-314, 'Critical perfusion pressure for renal function' - measures a STENOSIS threshold in bilateral atherosclerotic renovascular disease, not the intrinsic breakpoint of a normal kidney, and the sensitivity disappeared after revascularisation. Branch 6 fails too, and that is the finding of this pass: THE HUMAN EVIDENCE CONFLICTS AT THE SAME PRESSURE. At MAP 60 mmHg, Lessard MR and Trepanier CA, Anesthesiology 1991;74(5):860-865 (n=20, GFR by inulin, ERPF by PAH, MAP 59.8 +/- 0.4 for 237 min) found GFR not significantly further reduced and renal vascular resistance FALLING to maintain renal blood flow - the autoregulatory signature; while Hara T et al, J Clin Anesth 1998;10(7):539-545 (n=26, MAP 60 for 120 min) found creatinine clearance SIGNIFICANTLY DECREASED in both arms. Lessard is the methodologically stronger study, but choosing it after seeing both is precisely what pre-registration forbids, so branch 6's condition - that human studies ESTABLISH preservation - genuinely fails on its own terms. There is no lower pressure where the human evidence is unconflicted either: at MAP 50, Zayas VM et al, Am J Physiol 1993;265(5 Pt 2):F686-F692 and Toivonen J et al, Eur J Anaesthesiol 1991;8(1):13-20 both report renal function falling. ALL FOUR HUMAN STUDIES ARE ANAESTHESIA SAFETY STUDIES and directive 1.7 predicts what they returned: apply the test - had the physiology come out differently, the conclusion would have been 'this hypotensive technique is unsafe for the kidney'. The pressure-flow relationship is the INSTRUMENT there, not the subject, and every one of them carries a confound (volatile anaesthetic, sympathetic blockade, vasodilator, or all three), with anaesthesia depressing GFR before any pressure was lowered. THE ADOPTED SOURCE IS THE OPPOSITE KIND OF STUDY. Finke 1983 varied renal artery pressure from 160 to 40 mmHg in seven CONSCIOUS foxhounds under beta-blockade on a normal sodium diet (4.1 mmol/kg/day), reducing it in steps held constant by a servo-control system driving an inflatable renal artery cuff. Between 160 mmHg and resting pressure renal blood flow did not change; between resting pressure and the lower limit of autoregulation, average 63.9 mmHg, it rose only about 7%. The relationship IS the subject. No standard deviation is reported for the limit itself, so uncertainty_type stays none rather than inventing one - the paper does report 89.8 +/- 3.3 mmHg for its separate renin threshold, so the omission is the authors' and not a transcription loss. DECLARED CONFLICTS, LOGGED AND NOT RESOLVED. (1) Shipley RE, Study RS, Am J Physiol 1951;167(3):676-688, PMID 14903093 - the ANAESTHETISED dog study that is the origin of the textbook 80-180 and therefore of the value this row carried; its PubMed record carries the MeSH term 'Humans', a legacy-indexing artefact of the AJP back catalogue already flagged in autoreg_upper_prereg.md. Anaesthesia is the most likely single explanation of 80 vs 63.9. (2) Cupples WA, Braam B, Am J Physiol Renal Physiol 2007;292(4):F1105-F1123, PMID 17229679 - a tier B review putting the limit at ~75 mmHg in dog and ~85 in rat. pooling.md: the primary source wins and the divergence is logged. Averaging 75 and 85 would produce a number describing neither species. (3) The 2025 human review already cited in this row's previous note (doi 10.22514/sv.2025.001) holds that human evidence is insufficient for 80 as the lower limit; this extraction agrees and goes further - the human evidence that does exist puts the limit near 60. THE BRANCH CHOICE IS NOT LOAD-BEARING: branch 6 would have given 59.8 and branch 7 gives 63.9, a 4.1 mmHg difference, and no conclusion depends on which. Two independent lines - conscious dog with a measured breakpoint, anaesthetised humans bracketing 50 to 60 - different species, designs, decades and authors, both land near 60. NEITHER SUPPORTS 80. NO MODEL RESULT CHANGES. The three salt arms sit at MAP 81.900 / 84.450 / 87.001 mmHg, above the old 80 and above the new 63.9, so all remain on the autoregulation plateau and every steady state is bit-identical. The pre-registration declared this BEFORE extraction so that 'it does not change the answer' could not wave the change through, and so that a value above ~81.9 would have had to be escalated rather than quietly avoided. WHAT CHANGES IS MARGIN: the low-salt arm sat 1.9 mmHg above a piecewise kink and now sits 18.0 mmHg above it. STILL DEBT, AND THE REASON IS FIXED IN THE PRE-REGISTRATION. This is a dog number and the human experiment IS performable - humans have repeatedly been taken to 50-60 mmHg. The ethical-ceiling argument that earns RN.AUTOREG.UPPER its E2 standing under the ADR 0006 amendment does NOT transfer to this row, and was ruled out in the pre-registration before the search so it could not be reached for afterwards. What is missing is a study that lowers pressure in humans WITHOUT general anaesthesia and measures GFR by inulin at graded steps. Debt with a named primary source is a real improvement on a non-citation; it is not a closure. SEPARATELY, AND OUT OF SCOPE: Finke 1983 also measured the renin release threshold at 89.8 +/- 3.3 mmHg in conscious dog, against RAAS.RENIN.PRESSURE_THRESHOLD = 93.0 currently carried on van Ochten. NOT CHANGED HERE. Recorded because that row is the rectification point HANDOVER 3.1 found the model sitting exactly on, and a second independent primary source for it now exists in a paper this repo has read. THE PIECEWISE FORM REMAINS UNSOURCED: relations.csv row Renal.GFR keeps an empty form_citation and stays in GRANDFATHERED_UNSOURCED. A NUMBER was sourced, not an EQUATION. Finke in fact reports renal blood flow rising ~7% below resting pressure rather than staying flat, so the true plateau has a slight slope the model's ifelse does not represent.
"""
const RN_AUTOREG_LOWER = 63.9

"""Upper limit of renal autoregulation [mmHg]
Source (tier A, reported): Roman RJ, Cowley AW Jr. Characterization of a new model for the study of pressure-natriuresis in the rat. Am J Physiol 1985;248:F190-F198. PMID 3970209. Highest renal perfusion pressure at which GFR was measured and found unchanged.
Notes: EVIDENCE UNDER AN ETHICAL CEILING, NOT DEBT (ADR 0006 amended 2026-08-21). The rat provenance of this value is not a deficiency to be burned down. No human study raises arterial pressure to locate the failure of GFR autoregulation and none may; the human literature only ever lowers it. Where the human experiment cannot ethically exist, a well-conducted animal study is the evidence, not a substitute for it. This row is E2 with species, preparation and tested range recorded. WHAT REMAINS A REAL LIMITATION IS THE CENSORING, NOT THE SPECIES: 160 mmHg was the highest RPP tested in that preparation, so the true breakpoint is >= 160 and is not known to BE 160. UPPER BREAKPOINT MOVED 180 -> 160 mmHg ON 2026-08-21. The previous value was NOT SOURCED: citation read 'Standard physiological reference. VERIFY.' and species read 'human', neither of which was true. POOLING RULE, DECLARED BEFORE EXTRACTION per validation/pooling.md, pre-registration in validation/autoreg_upper_prereg.md: single-source, n_studies k=1. The pre-registration fixed, before any paper was read, that if zero HUMAN primary studies reported an upper breakpoint then the value could not be retained by cross-species pooling or by range-midpoint (both prohibited) and had to be brought inside the range where the model's own relations have support. SEARCH RESULT - THERE IS NO HUMAN PRIMARY EVIDENCE FOR AN UPPER BREAKPOINT. PubMed yields no human study that RAISES arterial pressure and measures the pressure at which GFR autoregulation fails; for obvious reasons the human literature only ever probes the LOWER limb by LOWERING pressure - Parving HH et al, Diabetologia 1984;27:547-552 (clonidine, MAP fall 16-18 mmHg, PMID 6442240), New JP et al, Diabetologia 1998;41:206-211 (trandolapril, MAP 103->93, PMID 9498655), Christensen PK et al, Kidney Int 2001 (PMID 11576357) and Diabetes Care 2003 (PMID 12502673). None of these reaches 160 mmHg, let alone 180. WHERE 180 ACTUALLY CAME FROM: Shipley RE, Study RS, Am J Physiol 1951;167:676-688, doi 10.1152/ajplegacy.1951.167.3.676, PMID 14903093 - DOG, acute alterations of renal artery pressure. That is the primary origin of the textbook '80-180 mmHg' and it is canine. (The PubMed MeSH record for PMID 14903093 carries the term 'Humans'; that is a known legacy-indexing artefact of the AJP back-catalogue, contradicted by the paper and by every secondary source that cites it as the canine range. Recorded here because the MeSH tag would otherwise look like human support.) 180 therefore entered this ledger as a dog number wearing a human label. WHAT IS ADOPTED: Roman RJ, Cowley AW Jr, Am J Physiol 1985;248:F190-F198, doi 10.1152/ajprenal.1985.248.2.F190, PMID 3970209. RAT, denervated kidney, vasopressin, aldosterone, corticosterone and noradrenaline clamped by infusion, RPP set by aortic clamp. Verbatim: 'Increasing RPP within the autoregulatory range from 90 to 160 mmHg produced 5- to 20-fold increases in urine flow and sodium excretion with no detectable changes in glomerular filtration rate, renal blood flow, or peritubular capillary pressure.' That is a direct primary measurement that GFR autoregulation is INTACT at 160 mmHg. THIS IS A CENSORED OBSERVATION, NOT A MEASURED BREAKPOINT. 160 mmHg is the highest RPP Roman and Cowley tested, so the true upper limit is >= 160; it is not known to BE 160. uncertainty_type is 'none' rather than a range because inventing a 160-180 interval would smuggle back the range-midpoint reasoning pooling.md prohibits. The model breakpoint is set at the edge of evidence, which is the conservative direction: it makes GFR fall with pressure slightly earlier than reality, rather than asserting a flat plateau in a region nothing has measured. WHY 160 AND NOT SOMETHING ELSE - THE TWO CONSTRAINTS COINCIDE. Roman and Cowley 1985 is ALSO the form source for the model's pressure natriuresis (see RN.PRESSURE_NATRIURESIS.SLOPE and ledger/relations.csv row Renal.FR_effective), where the LINEAR form is evidenced over roughly 55-160 mmHg. At 180 the autoregulation breakpoint sat OUTSIDE the range over which the equation it modifies has any support, so the model contained a kink in a region where its own natriuresis term was unevidenced. Setting the breakpoint to 160 makes the autoregulatory plateau and the natriuresis validity range terminate together, at the same pressure, from the SAME primary paper. That is not a coincidence chosen after the fact - the <=160 ceiling was written into the pre-registration before extraction. CROSS-SPECIES POOLING NOT PERFORMED, as prohibited. The dog value (Shipley 1951) and the rat value (Roman 1985) are NOT averaged. One rat study is adopted; the dog study is recorded as provenance archaeology for the discarded 180, not as a co-estimate. CORROBORATION, RECORDED AS CONTEXT AND NOT POOLED: Cupples WA, Braam B, Am J Physiol Renal Physiol 2007;292:F1105-F1123, doi 10.1152/ajprenal.00194.2006, describe the plateau as spanning from ~75 mmHg (dogs) or ~85 mmHg (rats) to >160 mmHg - note '>160', an open upper bound, not a fixed 180. Carlstrom M, Wilcox CS, Arendshorst WJ, Physiol Rev 2015;95:405-511, doi 10.1152/physrev.00042.2012, quote the conventional 80-180 mmHg. Both are reviews (tier B) and neither adds human primary data on the upper limit. SPECIES CHANGED human -> rat. TIER CHANGED B -> A: per SOURCES.md, tier A includes 'peer-reviewed primary research in animal models, flagged as such with species noted'. The tier RISES because the source is now a primary measurement rather than an unnamed reference, while the species flag FALLS to rat. Those are two separate axes and both moved honestly. KNOWN LIMITATION: the model is human. A rat autoregulatory breakpoint is being used in a human model because no human number exists. This is species extrapolation and is flagged as such. NO NUMERICAL EFFECT AT THE OPERATING POINT: IPE runs at MAP ~88-93 mmHg, inside [80, 160], where GFR ~ GFR0 either way. Verified: the ADR 0007 salt-step result is unchanged. The change buys honesty and removes an unevidenced kink, not different behaviour. WHAT IS STILL NOT SOURCED: the piecewise-flat-then-proportional FORM of the autoregulation equation, including its derivative discontinuity at both breakpoints. Only the breakpoint VALUE is sourced here. ledger/relations.csv row Renal.GFR therefore remains in the grandfathered unsourced list in tools/check_relations.py and its form_citation stays empty. LEDGER SCHEMA DEBT: validation/pooling.md specifies pooling_rule, n_studies and pooling_notes as columns of this CSV. Those columns do not exist yet, so the rule is recorded in prose here, following the precedent already set by RN.PRESSURE_NATRIURESIS.SLOPE. SCALING FROM RAT TO HUMAN: NO SCALING FACTOR IS APPLIED, AND THAT IS A DELIBERATE CLAIM, NOT AN OMISSION. This parameter is a PRESSURE (mmHg), an intensive variable, unlike the extensive quantities in this ledger (GFR, sodium excretion, blood volume) which would require allometric scaling by body or kidney mass. Mean arterial pressure in the conscious rat (~100-120 mmHg) and in the adult human (~93 mmHg here) are the same order of magnitude, and the autoregulatory plateau is generated by afferent-arteriolar myogenic tone and tubuloglomerular feedback, which are transmural-pressure-sensing mechanisms operating on the same pressure scale in both species rather than mass-dependent ones. Cupples and Braam 2007 report lower limits of ~75 mmHg (dog) and ~85 mmHg (rat) - a ~10 mmHg spread across a roughly 40-fold body-mass difference, which is the empirical evidence that this quantity does not scale with mass. The residual risk is therefore not a missing scale factor but genuine between-species difference in the breakpoint itself, of order 10 mmHg based on that spread, and it is unquantified in the upward direction because no species has been measured above 160 mmHg in this preparation. RE-FRAMED 2026-08-21 under the ADR 0006 amendment: an earlier version of this note treated 'not human' as a defect. It is not. What is genuinely unsourced here is the piecewise FORM of the autoregulation equation (see ledger/relations.csv row Renal.GFR) and the LOWER breakpoint - not this value's species. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13.
"""
const RN_AUTOREG_UPPER = 160.0

"""Glomerular filtration rate nominal [L/day] +/- 25.9 (sd)
Source (tier A, reported): Soares AA, Prates AB, Weinert LS, Veronese FV, de Azevedo MJ, Silveiro SP. Reference values for glomerular filtration rate in healthy Brazilian adults. BMC Nephrol 2013;14:54. doi:10.1186/1471-2369-14-54. PMID 23448550. GFR measured by 51Cr-EDTA single injection in 285 healthy adults aged 19-70, 106 +/- 18 mL/min/1.73 m2, reference intervals built to CLSI C28.
Notes: CHANGED 180.0 -> 152.6 L/day ON 2026-08-27. THE PREVIOUS VALUE WAS NOT SOURCED: the citation read 'Standard physiological reference. VERIFY.', which is not a reference, while extraction_method claimed `reported`. 180 L/day is 125 mL/min - the textbook figure - and MEASURED GFR in healthy adults is not 125. Pre-registered in validation/verify_rows_prereg.md, committed at e0195f4 before any source was opened. pooling_rule = single-source, k = 1. THE VALUE: 106 mL/min/1.73 m2 x 1440 / 1000 = 152.64 -> 152.6 L/day, 4 significant figures per directive 1.9. SD 18 mL/min/1.73 m2 -> 25.9 L/day. Measured by 51Cr-EDTA single injection, n = 285 healthy adults 19-70, reference intervals constructed to the CLSI C28 protocol. The relationship IS the subject of that paper - directive 1.7. SEX: NO DIFFERENCE, AND THAT IS A FINDING RATHER THAN AN OMISSION. Soares reports 108 +/- 18 male against 104 +/- 18 female, p = 0.134, and pooled the sexes for that reason. Under ADR 0014 this row therefore stays `both` with the cohort recorded, which is the rule's 'where only one value is supported, use it for both' branch and not a gap. CONVERGENT AND DELIBERATELY NOT POOLED: Denic A, Mathew J, Lerman LO, Lieske JC, Larson JJ, Alexander MP, Poggio E, Glassock RJ, Rule AD. Single-nephron glomerular filtration rate in healthy adults. N Engl J Med 2017;376(24):2349-2357. PMID 28614683. 1,388 healthy living kidney donors, iothalamate, mean GFR 115 +/- 24 mL/min ABSOLUTE and UNINDEXED. At a typical donor body surface area near 1.9 m2 that is about 105 mL/min/1.73 m2, agreeing closely with Soares - but the paper does not report the BSA needed to make that conversion, so it CANNOT be placed on this model's 1.73 m2 reference without an assumption. pooling.md prohibits pooling across incompatible measurement bases, and indexed against absolute is exactly that, so it is recorded as corroboration and NOT averaged in. Note also that Denic's cohort is screened kidney donors, i.e. selected for renal health above the population. WHY THE TEXTBOOK 125 IS HIGHER. The conventional figure predates routine clearance measurement in unselected healthy adults and is closer to a young-adult value; Soares finds 112 +/- 18 under 45 against 98 +/- 15 over 45, so an all-ages healthy mean sits well below 125. This is the same pattern as CV.MAP.SETPOINT and RN.AUTOREG.LOWER: a textbook convention entered as though it were a measurement. IT IS INDEXED TO 1.73 m2 AND THE MODEL SCALES BY MASS. src/scaling.jl already records that GFR properly scales with body surface area, sub-linearly in mass, and that linear scaling overstates the population spread. This row is the reference value at 1.73 m2; the mismatch between that normalisation and mass-linear scaling is the debt scaling.jl names, not a new one. RN.NA.FRACTIONAL_REABSORPTION MOVED WITH THIS ROW and had to: it is DERIVED as 1 - Na_intake/(GFR0*C_Na), so the two are not independent. Enforced by tools/check_closure.py, 'Na excretion == Na intake'.
"""
const RN_GFR_NOMINAL = 152.6

"""Fractional ECF volume half-span over which the GFR volume response is evidenced [dimensionless]
Source (tier B, derived): van den Bosch JJJON, Hessels NR, Visser FW, Krikken JA, Bakker SJL, Riphagen IJ, Navis GJ. Plasma sodium, extracellular fluid volume, and blood pressure in healthy men. Physiol Rep 2021;9(24):e15103. doi:10.14814/phy2.15103. PMID 34921521. PMC8683787. n = 70 healthy normotensive men, crossover, 7 days per level, intake verified by 24 h urinary sodium (230 +/- 67 against 38 +/- 26 mmol/24 h). Table 1: ECFV 17.4 +/- 1.66 against 16.5 +/- 1.54 L/1.73 m2 by 125I-iothalamate, BSA 2.04 +/- 0.15 against 2.03 +/- 0.15. THE HALF-SPAN OF THE TESTED EXTRACELLULAR VOLUME RANGE, derived from those four numbers; the paper does not report it as a range. The derivation is validation/renal_hemodynamics_extract.py.
Notes: THE CENSORING BOUND ON RN.GFR.VOLUME_SENSITIVITY, and it exists because a linear response has no support outside the volumes anyone measured. validation/renal_hemodynamics_prereg.md section 6 fixed this in advance: 'the functional form in this model is sourced only as a straight line between two measured intake levels, which is E2 inside the tested range and NOTHING AT ALL outside it, so the term is clamped to the pooled tested intake range, and that censoring is recorded on the row exactly as RN.AUTOREG.UPPER records its own.' This is that row. || THE DERIVATION. De-index each arm with its OWN body surface area, which is the correction HANDOVER section 3.12 records owing to ecf_salt_response_extract.py: high 17.4*2.04/1.73 = 20.518 L, low 16.5*2.03/1.73 = 19.361 L, mean 19.940, difference 1.157. The HALF-span about the mean is 0.5783/19.940 = 0.02900 -> 0.029. Two significant figures, per directive 1.9 and for the same reason RN.GFR.VOLUME_SENSITIVITY keeps two: the information is in a difference of two three-figure means and there is no third figure to keep. || IT IS A HALF-SPAN AND NOT A SPAN. The clamp is two-sided about the reference volume, so the bound is the distance from the mean to either arm, not the distance between the arms. Entering the span would double the evidenced range silently. || WHAT IT CENSORS, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSERTED. The model's chronic salt step of 103-205 mEq/day moves ECF volume about 2.4 percent either side of reference, so this bound does NOT bind on the chronic protocol - it is inert there, which is the correct outcome for a range that brackets the tested one. It DOES bind on the acute challenges: 2 L of saline expands ECF by roughly 9 percent, three times the evidenced half-span, and without the clamp the model would extrapolate a chronic 7-day relationship into a region no source reaches. || THE TIMESCALE EXTRAPOLATION IS SEPARATE AND IS DECLARED, NOT BOUNDED BY THIS ROW. The source is chronic, 7 days per level, and the model applies the response algebraically and therefore instantly. Conlin 1993 (PMID 7503952) shows the renal response to volume expansion per se within 3-7 hours, by saline or dextran alike, so an instantaneous term is fast rather than backwards - but nothing here sources a time constant, and a lag was deliberately NOT added because its tau would be identified by nothing, which is the defect RN.ANP.TAU already carries. || SPECIES human and the cohort is MEN, inheriting the sex conflict recorded in full on RN.GFR.VOLUME_SENSITIVITY: Pechere-Bertschi 2002 finds no change in renal haemodynamics on high salt in the follicular phase in 35 normotensive women. If that row becomes a sexed pair this bound must be re-derived with it.
"""
const RN_GFR_VOLUME_RANGE = 0.029

"""GFR sensitivity to extracellular volume expansion [dimensionless]
Source (tier B, derived): van den Bosch JJJON, Hessels NR, Visser FW, Krikken JA, Bakker SJL, Riphagen IJ, Navis GJ. Plasma sodium, extracellular fluid volume, and blood pressure in healthy men. Physiol Rep 2021;9(24):e15103. doi:10.14814/phy2.15103. PMID 34921521. PMC8683787. n = 70 healthy normotensive men, age 24 +/- 7, crossover, 7 days per level, intake verified by 24 h urinary sodium (230 +/- 67 against 38 +/- 26 mmol/24 h). GFR and extracellular fluid volume BOTH by 125I-iothalamate. Table 1: GFR 138 +/- 18 against 128 +/- 18 ml/min (p < 0.001); ECFV 17.4 +/- 1.66 against 16.5 +/- 1.54 L/1.73 m2 (p < 0.001); BSA 2.04 +/- 0.15 against 2.03 +/- 0.15 (p < 0.001). DERIVED FROM THOSE SIX NUMBERS - the ratio is not reported by the paper; the derivation is validation/renal_hemodynamics_extract.py.
Notes: THE FRACTIONAL GFR CHANGE PER FRACTIONAL EXTRACELLULAR VOLUME CHANGE, across chronic sodium intake in healthy humans. Pre-registered in validation/renal_hemodynamics_prereg.md, written BEFORE this extraction and sitting before it in history; verify with git log --diff-filter=A on that file. Reproduce with python validation/renal_hemodynamics_extract.py. || THE DERIVATION. ECFV is reported INDEXED to 1.73 m2 while GFR is reported ABSOLUTE, so ECFV is de-indexed using EACH ARM'S OWN BSA: (17.4*2.04 - 16.5*2.03)/1.73 = 1.157 L absolute, on a mean of 19.94 L, giving 0.05801 fractional. GFR gives 10/133 = 0.07519 fractional. S = 0.07519/0.05801 = 1.296 -> 1.30. Indexing the GFR instead of de-indexing the volume gives 1.324, 2 percent away, so the value does not turn on which side is converted. || TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES AND NOT THREE, per directive 1.9. The information is in the DIFFERENCES and dECFV is 0.9 L off two three-figure means; there is no third figure to keep. This is the opposite case to RN.NA.FRACTIONAL_REABSORPTION, where the difference of large numbers needed MORE digits on the inputs - here the inputs do not have them. || NO UNCERTAINTY IS ENTERED AND THAT IS DELIBERATE, not an omission. Numerator and denominator are measured in the SAME SUBJECTS BY THE SAME TRACER, so they are correlated, and the paper reports per-arm SDs rather than paired differences or their covariance. The pre-registration section 11 fixed in advance that an interval must not be fabricated by propagating the per-arm SDs as if independent. || pooling_rule = single-source, n_studies = 1 (columns pooling.md requires but this file does not have; recorded in prose by precedent, HANDOVER section 7). K IS 1 AND NOT 3, AND THAT IS A FINDING. Krikken 2007 (PMID 17091123, n = 95), Visser 2009 (PMID 19282825, n = 78) and this paper are THE SAME GRONINGEN COHORT - van den Bosch states in its Methods that it is a post-hoc analysis of the prior study, n = 70/93. Toering 2018 (PMID 28592435) is the same group. Pooling them would be the silent re-pooling validation/pooling.md prohibits and would have looked like k = 3. || CORROBORATED IN DIRECTION BY AN INDEPENDENT GROUP AND A DIFFERENT TRACER, AND NOT POOLED. Roos JC, Koomans HA, Dorhout Mees EJ, Delawi IM. Renal sodium handling in normal humans subjected to low, normal, and extremely high sodium supplies. Am J Physiol 1985;249(6 Pt 2):F941-7. PMID 3907374. Utrecht, n = 8, INULIN clearance rose 103 +/- 9 to 129 +/- 9 ml/min. NOT POOLED because its reported endpoints span 20 to 1128 meq/day, far outside this model's 103-205 and outside any diet; the 200 meq intermediate values are described but not printed and the full text is not open access. Direction only. || DECLARED CONFLICT, AND IT IS ABOUT SEX. The cohort is MEN. Under ADR 0014 the row is entered `both` because no sexed source with numbers was found, which is the rule's 'where only one value is supported, use it for both' branch - the same branch RN.GFR.NOMINAL took. BUT the pre-registered women sweep found that the only clean healthy-women study points the other way: Pechere-Bertschi A, Maillard M, Stalder H, Brunner HR, Burnier M. Renal segmental tubular response to salt during the normal menstrual cycle. Kidney Int 2002;61(2):425-31. PMID 11849382. 35 normotensive women, 40 against 250 mmol/day for 7 days: in the FOLLICULAR phase the rise in salt intake was associated with NO CHANGE in renal haemodynamics, with renal vasodilation in the LUTEAL phase instead. The companion study in women on oral contraceptives (PMID 12969156, n = 27) does find GFR and filtration fraction rising, p < 0.05, but that paper exists to show the oral-contraceptive renal salt response is altered. SO THIS ROW MAY BE A MALE NUMBER APPLIED TO WOMEN, exactly as CV.HEMATOCRIT.NOMINAL was before it was sourced as a pair. It is recorded, not hidden, and a sexed pair must NOT be entered on a direction alone. || WIRED 2026-09-03, HANDOVER section 4 item 1. It is read by Renal.jl