inst a measured 95, and 6 h urine 565.6 -> 576.6 mL against 563. The model therefore excretes MORE sodium and reports a SMALLER fractional rise. Whether that is right depends on glomerulotubular balance, which this model does not represent, and nothing here sources it. DO NOT refit anything to Jensen - HANDOVER section 4 item 2 says spending the only out-of-sample datum on a fit is how this line loses its one test. || THE FUNCTIONAL FORM IS CENSORED, NOT EXTRAPOLATED. See RN.GFR.VOLUME_RANGE. The clamp is inert on the chronic salt step and binds on the acute challenges, so every acute number above is at the bound rather than on the line. || THE TIMESCALE IS AN EXTRAPOLATION AND IS DECLARED. The source is chronic, 7 days per level; the term is algebraic and therefore instantaneous. Conlin 1993 (PMID 7503952) puts the renal response to volume expansion per se at 3-7 hours, so instantaneous is fast rather than backwards. No lag was added because its time constant would be identified by nothing, which is the debt RN.ANP.TAU already carries, and branch G3 forbids the structural addition anyway. || WHAT IT WOULD BE WORTH. 4.9578 -> 4.2019 mmHg per 100 mmol/day against a human 1.70-2.30. About a sixth of the gap. Closing it by this route alone would need a 26-32 percent GFR swing across the step; the sourced human response over the same 102 mmol/day is 4.0 percent. This is a contributor, not the explanation, and under the anti-double-count rule ADR 0013 and ADR 0015 must be re-estimated against it. || THE JENSEN FIGURES IN THIS NOTE ARE SUPERSEDED. CORRECTED 2026-09-17, HANDOVER section 3.45. This note records that wiring this term moved Jensen's peak fractional sodium excretion rise 82.5 -> 79.3 percent against a reported 123, 'so the model is now slightly further low on the only endpoint it was not fitted to'. THE ARITHMETIC IS CORRECT AND THE STANDING IT DESCRIBES IS NOT, for two reasons: the macula densa arm moved the endpoint three weeks later, and the comparison was a MODEL PEAK against a STUDY'S FINAL SAMPLE rather than like for like. On Jensen's own 210-240 minute window the model is at 110.1 percent against a measured 122. || THE MECHANISM PARAGRAPH IS WORTH KEEPING FOR THE REASON IT WAS WRITTEN. It explains that raising GFR dilutes both natriuretic terms in the FRACTIONAL measure while absolute excretion rises, so the model excretes more sodium and reports a smaller fractional rise. That is still exactly what this term does; it is simply no longer the dominant influence on the endpoint. || AND ITS CLOSING SENTENCE WAS RIGHT ABOUT SOMETHING ELSE. It says 'whether that is right depends on glomerulotubular balance, which this model does not represent, and nothing here sources it'. That remains true and remains unsourced. It is no longer needed to explain a magnitude deficit, because there is none; the open question it bears on is the LATE time course, which no published number currently bounds.
