"""Fraction of filtered sodium reabsorbed before the macula densa [dimensionless]  [!] ASSUMED
Source (tier C, assumed): No admissible source could be opened. See notes.
Notes: ASSUMED, AND IT IS A ROUND TEACHING NUMBER - directive 1.12, and the pre-registration listed it in advance among the six to distrust. Searches for segmental sodium handling in healthy humans return lithium-clearance studies in cirrhosis, heart failure and hypertension; directive 1.7's sixth subsystem. || IT IS NOT SEPARATELY IDENTIFIABLE FROM RN.MD.RENIN_GAIN, AND THAT IS WHY AN ASSUMED VALUE IS TOLERABLE HERE. This fraction sets the SCALE of distal delivery; the macula densa gain multiplies the FRACTIONAL deviation of that delivery from its own reference. Halving one and doubling the other leaves every model output identical. Since the gain is estimated against salt-renin data, only the product is determined, and entering a sourced fraction would buy nothing until the gain is independently measured too. || IT ADDS NO INFORMATION ABOUT SEGMENTAL HANDLING and ADR 0021's disqualification section says so: the split is OBSERVATIONAL - it defines a variable and does not restructure the sodium equation, which is untouched. || RENAMED 2026-09-17: IT WAS CALLED A PROXIMAL FRACTION AND IT IS NOT ONE. Renal.jl computes Na_distal = Na_filtered*(1-this) and feeds it to md_drive, the MACULA DENSA signal. The macula densa sits at the end of the THICK ASCENDING LIMB - after the proximal tubule and after the loop - so 10 percent delivery is proximal PLUS loop combined. A name carrying a convention its value contradicts is HANDOVER section 3.2's failure mode, and this is the third instance. The param_id, the name and the symbol now say which segment this is. || AND IT IS COMPLETELY INERT, WHICH IS STRONGER THAN WHAT THIS ROW USED TO CLAIM. The note said it was 'not separately identifiable from RN.MD.RENIN_GAIN'. It is worse than that: md_drive = (Na_distal_ref - Na_distal)/Na_distal_ref and the factor (1-this) appears in BOTH, so it CANCELS EXACTLY. MEASURED, not argued: at 0.90 and at 0.74, with Na_distal_ref re-derived to match, MAP, sodium excretion, md_drive, plasma renin and extracellular volume are BIT-IDENTICAL to eight decimal places. Only the REPORTED Na_distal moves. || SO SOURCING THIS ROW WOULD BUY NOTHING BEHAVIOURALLY, and that is why it is still assumed after a pass that went looking for it. What the tubule needs is SEGMENTAL STRUCTURE - regulators acting where they act - not a better number for a parameter that cancels. || THE CONDITION THIS NOTE SETS WAS HALF MET ON 2026-09-20 AND THEN FAILED ON THE OTHER HALF. The note above says entering a sourced fraction 'would buy nothing until the gain is independently measured too'. The gain HAS now been independently measured - Lorenz JN, Weihprecht H, Schnermann J, Skott O, Briggs JP. Characterization of the macula densa stimulus for renin secretion. Am J Physiol 1990;259(1 Pt 2):F186-F193. PMID 2197878, isolated perfused rabbit juxtaglomerular apparatus with intravascular pressure and renal nerve activity physically absent. || BUT IT STILL BUYS NOTHING, FOR A DIFFERENT REASON. Lorenz shows renin responds to macula densa NaCl CONCENTRATION and not to delivery - in his series 3 delivery rose 51 percent while renin rose 3.2-fold - and this model's md_drive is keyed to delivery. Worse, md_drive is a RATIO in which this fraction cancels exactly: Na_distal = Na_filtered * (1 - f_md) * renal_mod appears in both the numerator and the reference, so (1 - f_md) divides out and THIS ROW HAS NO EFFECT ON THE MODEL AT ALL through that path. The identifiability argument above is therefore stronger than it claims - it is not that the fraction and the gain trade off, it is that the fraction is inert. || WHAT WOULD MAKE IT LIVE: a macula densa concentration, which needs distal FLOW, which needs the lumped water reabsorption split. See RN.MD.RENIN_GAIN. macula_densa_lorenz_prereg.md. || SUPERSEDED IN THE MODEL 2026-09-20 BUT KEPT, tubule_segments_prereg.md. The tubule was split into flux-carrying segments and macula densa delivery is now computed as a fraction of what the segment RECEIVES - RN.NA.TAL_REABSORBED_FRACTION - rather than as this fixed fraction of the FILTERED load. THIS ROW IS WHAT THAT FRACTION WAS DERIVED FROM, so it is read by the ledger even though no equation now reads it, and it records where 0.615 came from. The note above says this row is inert because (1 - f_md) cancels out of a ratio; after the split it is inert for a different and better reason - it has been converted into a delivery-based constant and its job is done.
"""
const RN_NA_MACULA_DENSA_FRACTION = 0.9

"""Fraction of filtered sodium leaving the proximal tubule [dimensionless] +/- 0.02 (sd)
Source (tier B, reported): Shirley DG, Walter SJ, Noormohamed FH. Natriuretic effect of caffeine: assessment of segmental sodium reabsorption in humans. Clin Sci (Lond) 2002;103(5):461-466. doi:10.1042/cs1030461. PMID 12401118. ABSTRACT READ IN FULL. Eight healthy males, GFR by 51Cr-EDTA, end-proximal fluid delivery by lithium clearance. PLACEBO ARM TAKEN: fractional excretion of lithium 26 +/- 2 percent, sodium/lithium clearance ratio 2.8 +/- 0.2 percent, fractional excretion of sodium 1.04 +/- 0.16 (control) and 0.70 +/- 0.07 (experimental).
Notes: SOURCED IN HEALTHY HUMANS 2026-09-17 under nephron_segments_prereg.md, and the instrument is the point. The previous search for segmental handling looked for LITHIUM CLEARANCE IN DISEASE and for MICROPUNCTURE IN ANIMALS; this is lithium clearance in EIGHT HEALTHY MALES, which is the human measurement of end-proximal delivery and was there all along. || WHY LITHIUM MEASURES THIS: lithium is reabsorbed with sodium in the proximal tubule in proportion, and essentially not beyond it, so its fractional excretion is the fraction of filtered fluid delivered out of the proximal tubule. That is a METHODOLOGICAL assumption and not a measurement - it fails where distal lithium handling is altered, which is why the same paper warns that caffeine intake must be controlled when using the method. || THE PLACEBO ARM IS TAKEN, not the control period, because the paper's own internal consistency holds there: 0.26 x 0.028 = 0.73 percent against a measured fractional sodium excretion of 0.70. The control period gives 1.04 percent, which implies a distal escape near 4 percent. THE WITHIN-STUDY DRIFT IS REAL AND IS THE ROW'S UNCERTAINTY, not noise to be averaged away. || THIS IS NOT THE FRACTION RN.NA.MACULA_DENSA_FRACTION CARRIES. Lithium stops at the end of the proximal tubule; the macula densa sits at the end of the thick ascending limb, after the loop has taken most of what the proximal tubule passed on. 0.26 and 0.10 are different segments and both are real. || MEN ONLY, n = 8. || THE METHOD'S VALIDITY LIMITS ARE NOW ON THE ROW, AND THEY WERE MISSING. Atherton JC, Green R, Hughes S, McFall V, Sharples JA, Solomon LR, Wilson L. Lithium clearance in man: effects of dietary salt intake, acute changes in extracellular fluid volume, amiloride and frusemide. Clin Sci (Lond) 1987;73(6):645-651. doi:10.1042/cs0730645. PMID 3690979. ABSTRACT READ IN FULL. Normal human volunteers. Its conclusion, verbatim in substance: the lithium clearance method for assessment of proximal tubule salt and water reabsorption CAN be used with some degree of confidence at MEDIUM AND HIGH salt intake AND IN ACUTE VOLUME EXPANSION, but MAY NOT BE RELIABLE WHEN DIETARY SALT INTAKE IS LOW. Amiloride moved fractional lithium clearance on a low salt diet and was without effect at medium or high intake, which is the mechanism of the failure: a component of lithium is reabsorbed in the amiloride-sensitive distal nephron and that component only matters when distal sodium reabsorption is near-maximal. || SO THE ROW IS ADMISSIBLE AND ITS LOW-SODIUM EXTRAPOLATION IS NOT. Shirley's subjects sit at a fractional sodium excretion of 0.70-1.04 percent on an unrestricted intake, which is the regime Atherton certifies. A value read off a salt-depleted preparation would not be, and this model runs its salt step down to 38 mmol/day - BELOW that regime. NOTHING READS THIS ROW AS A FUNCTION OF INTAKE and it must not acquire such a reader without sourcing the intake dependence separately. || AND THE SAME GROUP PUBLISHED THE CAVEAT AGAINST ITS OWN INSTRUMENT, which is the reason to trust it: Shirley and Walter's own later work is what established the distal amiloride- and frusemide-sensitive component of lithium handling. || THIS IS NOW THE VALUE AT THE OPERATING POINT, NOT AT ALL SALT INTAKES, 2026-09-20. Folkerd 1995 (PMID 7733329) measured fractional lithium excretion at 8.3 percent on 31 mmol/day of sodium against 18.0 percent on 357, and Chiolero 2000 (PMID 11040249) confirmed the direction in 27 normotensives. End-proximal delivery is not a constant in humans. RN.NA.PROXIMAL_SALT_SENSITIVITY carries the modulation and this row is what it modulates about; the VALUE HERE IS UNCHANGED and Shirley 2002 still owns it. Only the RELATIVE response was taken from Folkerd, because his endogenous-lithium absolutes and Shirley's exogenous-technique value differ by nearly twofold - which is Folkerd's own headline result.
"""
const RN_NA_PROXIMAL_DELIVERY = 0.26

"""Fractional change in end-proximal sodium delivery per unit normalised renin activity [dimensionless]
Source (tier B, derived): Folkerd E, Singer DR, Cappuccio FP, Markandu ND, Sampson B, MacGregor GA. Clearance of endogenous lithium in humans: altered dietary salt intake and comparison with exogenous lithium clearance. Am J Physiol 1995;268(4 Pt 2):F718-F722. || CONFIRMED INDEPENDENTLY: Chiolero A, Maillard M, Nussberger J, Brunner HR, Burnier M. Proximal sodium reabsorption: an independent determinant of blood pressure response to salt. Hypertension 2000;36(4):631-637.
Notes: SOURCED 2026-09-20, proximal_salt_prereg.md. THE ROW IT MODULATES WAS A CONSTANT AND THE HUMAN QUANTITY IS NOT. RN.NA.PROXIMAL_DELIVERY is 0.26 from Shirley 2002 at a single salt state; in humans end-proximal delivery MORE THAN DOUBLES across the dietary range. || Folkerd 1995, PMID 7733329, SIX normal subjects, five days per diet: fractional excretion of lithium 8.3 +/- 2.9 percent at a sodium intake of 31 +/- 10 mmol/day against 18.0 +/- 5.1 percent at 357 +/- 78 mmol/day, P < 0.05. Absolute clearances 8.7 +/- 2.9 against 20.7 +/- 3.8 ml/min. || CONFIRMED INDEPENDENTLY IN A LARGER NORMOTENSIVE COHORT. Chiolero 2000, PMID 11040249, 27 normotensive subjects on high and low sodium: changing from low to high sodium produced increases in glomerular filtration rate (P < 0.05), renal plasma flow (P < 0.05) and fractional excretion of lithium (P < 0.01). Two groups, two laboratories, same direction. || THE DERIVATION. Folkerd's two points give a power-law exponent ln(18.0/8.3)/ln(357/31) = 0.317, so across this model's own salt arms of 38 to 230 mEq/day the predicted FE_Li ratio is (230/38)^0.317 = 1.77. The model's pra runs 2.5795 to 0.9437 about a reference of 1.2956, and f_prox_eff = f_prox * (1 + k_prox * (pra_ref - pra)) reproduces that ratio at k_prox = 0.29. TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES, directive 1.13 - two points carrying two figures each cannot give three. || ONLY THE RELATIVE RESPONSE CROSSES, AND THIS IS THE SUBTLE PART. Folkerd's own headline finding is that ENDOGENOUS and EXOGENOUS lithium clearance DIFFER: fractional excretion 16.4 +/- 2.1 percent endogenous against 27.9 +/- 2.1 percent exogenous, P < 0.05, in the same subjects. His salt-step numbers are ENDOGENOUS; this model's 0.26 reference is Shirley's EXOGENOUS-technique value. TRANSFERRING FOLKERD'S ABSOLUTES WOULD MIX TWO METHODS THAT HE SHOWS DISAGREE BY NEARLY TWOFOLD. Only the ratio is taken, and the reference stays where Shirley put it. || KEYED TO pra AND NOT TO SODIUM INTAKE. Angiotensin II stimulates proximal reabsorption - that is what Hall 1977 (PMID 879288) and Hall 1984 (PMID 6720967) measured - so the mechanism is already in this model, and keying to the intake parameter would be open-loop: the tubule would respond to a number the body cannot see. || THE POWER LAW IS AN INTERPOLATION AND NOT A MEASUREMENT. Two points fix one exponent and say nothing about curvature; a linear-in-intake reading of the same two points is equally admissible and is not distinguishable by them. || IT MUST LEAVE THE MACULA DENSA SIGNAL ALONE. Vallon 2002, PMID 12089382, HANDOVER section 3.65: in normal rats dietary salt does not affect the tubuloglomerular feedback signal, and they adapt downstream of the macula densa. Both results hold together only if the thick ascending limb absorbs the extra proximal delivery. md_drive is therefore checked at both salt arms and must stay near-constant; if it starts tracking salt this term has been wired into the wrong path. || SPECIES: HUMAN, healthy, n = 6 for the salt response and n = 27 for the confirmation. SCALING: none - a fractional excretion is dimensionless and intensive.
"""
const RN_NA_PROXIMAL_SALT_SENSITIVITY = 0.29

"""Fraction of delivered sodium reabsorbed between the end-proximal tubule and the macula densa [dimensionless]
Source (tier C, derived): Derived from RN.NA.MACULA_DENSA_FRACTION and RN.NA.PROXIMAL_DELIVERY; see notes.
Notes: FRACTION OF DELIVERED SODIUM REABSORBED BETWEEN THE END OF THE PROXIMAL TUBULE AND THE MACULA DENSA. Derived 2026-09-20 from two rows already in this ledger, tubule_segments_prereg.md. || THE CHANGE THIS ROW EMBODIES IS THE POINT OF THE WHOLE PASS. RN.NA.MACULA_DENSA_FRACTION expressed macula densa delivery as a fixed fraction of the FILTERED load; this expresses it as a fraction of what the segment RECEIVES. Those are different physiology: with a fixed fraction of filtration, delivery and concentration at the macula densa cannot come apart, and coming apart is exactly what Lorenz 1990 measured. || DERIVATION: f_tal = 1 - (1 - f_md)/f_prox = 1 - 0.10/0.26 = 0.615. RN.NA.MACULA_DENSA_FRACTION = 0.90 puts 10 percent of filtered sodium at the macula densa and RN.NA.PROXIMAL_DELIVERY = 0.26 puts 26 percent of the filtrate past the proximal tubule, so the segment between them reabsorbs 61.5 percent of its own load. TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES: 0.62 would be three on a quantity whose parents carry two, and 0.615 is carried only because it reproduces the macula densa concentration identity that check_closure.py asserts. || IT LUMPS TWO SEGMENTS AND ONLY THEIR RATIO IS IDENTIFIED. Sodium reabsorption in the thick ascending limb and WATER reabsorption in the descending limb both set the macula densa concentration, they enter it as a ratio, and nothing in hand separates them. Splitting them would add a parameter no measurement here could constrain - the mistake ADR 0006's tiers exist to prevent. || WHAT IT BUYS, AND IT IS A RESULT RATHER THAN A CONVENIENCE. With a constant f_tal the macula densa concentration works out to C_Na*(1 - f_tal), so the proximal delivery fraction CANCELS and the concentration is SALT-INDEPENDENT. That is Vallon 2002 (PMID 12089382) - in nondiabetic rats dietary salt did not affect the tubuloglomerular feedback signal - reproduced as an EMERGENT CONSEQUENCE of the segment structure rather than assumed. || SPECIES: the two parent rows are human - Shirley 2002 lithium clearance in eight healthy males, and the macula densa fraction. SCALING: none, it is a dimensionless fraction of a delivery.
"""
const RN_NA_TAL_REABSORBED_FRACTION = 0.615

"""Pressure natriuresis slope [(mEq/day)/mmHg] +/- 5.43-20.0 (range)  [!] CALIBRATED
Source (tier B, calibrated): SOLVED INSIDE THIS MODEL against human data; not lifted from a published value. Originating model for the ROW and the FORM, and for the 20.0 that stood until 2026-09-03: Guyton AC, Coleman TG, Granger HJ. Circulation: overall regulation. Annu Rev Physiol 1972;34:13-46. See notes for what Guyton is and is not the source of.
Notes: CHANGED 11.4 -> 8.4 ON 2026-09-03, on the owner's instruction, and 8.4 is the CONSTRAINT-CONSISTENT partner of the sourced volume gain rather than a second free choice. The human joint constraint is G_pn + 0.0594*G_anp = 50 per 100 mmol/day; with CV.ANP.NATRIURETIC_GAIN sourced at 700 that gives 8.4 exactly. 11.4 had paired with 650 and the mismatch was recorded on this row when 11.4 was entered - it predicted a chronic salt sensitivity just below the human window, which is what was measured, and 8.4 was predicted to land inside it. It does. || CHANGED 20.0 -> 11.4 ON 2026-09-03 ON THE OWNER'S EXPLICIT INSTRUCTION. This row had been parked at his decision since 2026-08-25 and the instruction discharges that. IT IS NOT ADR 0013'S PROPOSAL - that record proposes 51.0 on the human PRESSURE evidence alone and remains Proposed and unadopted. 11.4 is the JOINT estimate that follows from ADR 0010's volume-keyed path being sourced and turned on: the human data constrain the COMBINATION G_pn + 0.0594*G_anp = 50 per 100 mmol/day, and with G_anp identified independently by the acute route the pressure slope is what remains. || ARITHMETIC NOTE THAT MATTERS. 11.4 pairs with G_anp = 650. The entered CV.ANP.NATRIURETIC_GAIN is 700, and the same constraint at 700 gives 8.4. So 11.4 and 700 are NOT the matched pair and the combination is under-satisfied by about 3 units, which shows up as a chronic salt sensitivity above the human window. Recorded rather than silently reconciled, because reconciling it by moving either number after seeing the result is the circularity this row's own history is a warning about. || WHAT IT DOES, measured with julia --project=. validation/challenges.jl. See the session record; the acute limbs are barely affected because they are carried by the volume path, and the chronic limb moves because this row is what sets it. || THE PREVIOUS NOTE IS PRESERVED BELOW BECAUSE ITS EVIDENCE IS UNCHANGED. || CALIBRATED, not measured. VALUE UNCHANGED AT 20.0 - read the identifiability result below before altering it. Originating model: Guyton 1972 systems analysis; the EXISTENCE and steepness of pressure natriuresis is E1 and well replicated, this particular slope is a fitted constant that propagated through the modelling literature. FORM: LINEAR, AND NOW SOURCED. Roman RJ, Cowley AW Jr, Am J Physiol 1985, PMID 3970209 (rat, denervated, vasopressin/aldosterone/corticosterone/noradrenaline clamped) report the relation as the slope of a LINE, 2 uL/min/kidney/mmHg over RPP 90-160 mmHg. Osborn/Francisco/DiBona, Proc Soc Exp Biol Med 1981, independently report UNaV falling LINEARLY over dog RPP 137-55 mmHg. An earlier note in relations.csv called the curve 'markedly nonlinear'; that was asserted from memory and is RETRACTED. VALIDITY RANGE roughly 55-160 mmHg. RN.AUTOREG.UPPER was 180, outside that range; moved to 160 on 2026-08-21 and cited to the same paper. MAGNITUDE CROSS-CHECK: Mizelle HL et al, Hypertension 1993;22:102-110 (dog, split bladder, bilateral servo-control, 12 d - the cleanest isolation of pressure per se) gives 3.468 mmol/day/mmHg whole animal = 2.154e-4 of filtered load per mmHg against this row's 7.937e-4, so the calibrated value is 3.68x STEEPER than the animal datum on a scaling-free basis. That is the range in uncertainty_value; 5.43 is the Mizelle-consistent value. SINGLE SOURCE, k=1, DOG - per validation/pooling.md that is animal-derived, may not be pooled with a human value, and is recorded as a comparator rather than adopted. IDENTIFIABILITY (measured 2026-08-20 on IPE at 9a6fa42): this parameter ALONE fixes the model's salt sensitivity. At steady state excretion equals intake, so Na_excr = Na_filtered*(1-FR_Na) + G_pn*(MAP-MAP_ref) gives dMAP ~ d(intake)/G_pn, in which CV.VENOUS_RETURN.SENSITIVITY does not appear. Confirmed numerically: the 205->103 mEq/day step gives 4.934 mmHg at G_pn=20.0 and 15.698 mmHg at G_pn=5.43, while varying G_vr over 2880->600 at fixed G_pn=5.43 moves the shift only 15.698->12.403 and drives V_ecf to 9.889 L, below the 10 L floor asserted in test/runtests.jl. The residual departure from an exact 1/G_pn law is C_Na drift. CONSEQUENCE, correcting HANDOVER.md section 5 item 4: G_pn and G_vr are NOT jointly identified as one lumped loop gain. G_pn is identified by the salt-sensitivity shift and G_vr by ECF volume, separately. Adopting the Mizelle value in isolation re-balances nothing - it forces a 15.7 mmHg shift across a 102 mEq/day range, which is salt-sensitive-hypertensive behaviour, not normotensive. WHY THE GAP IS PROBABLY REAL RATHER THAN AN ERROR: IPE has NO volume-sensing natriuretic path. Seeliger et al, J Physiol 2004;559:939-951, servo-controlled RPP during saline loading in freely moving dogs with NO reduction in peak or cumulative natriuresis, and Bie 2018 (10.1152/ajpregu.00363.2017) argues excretion is keyed to extracellular volume rather than pressure. With ANP absent, all sodium regulation is forced through this one term, so an inflated G_pn is the expected COMPENSATION for the missing path. DO NOT lower this to 5.43 before a volume-sensing natriuretic path exists - doing so degrades the only behaviour the model has ever reproduced. Re-estimate G_pn and the ANP gain JOINTLY against digitised Mars500, as posteriors, not this value alone and not as a point value. || ADR 0013'S FALSIFIABLE TEST WAS RUN ON 2026-09-02 AND THE VALUE STAYS AT 20.0. Pre-registered in validation/ecf_salt_response_prereg.md; reproduce with python validation/ecf_salt_response_extract.py. ADR 0013 proposes 20.0 -> 51.0 on the human PRESSURE response and requires its volume test to pass BEFORE acceptance. It fails. van den Bosch 2021 (Physiol Rep 2021;9(24):e15103, PMID 34921521, n = 70 healthy men, 7 d per level, ECFV by iothalamate, intake verified by 24 h urinary sodium) gives dMAP/dV_ecf = 1.885 mmHg/L against the model's 9.80 at that cohort's 80.6 kg - a factor of 5.2 against a pre-registered failure threshold of 2, and it fails on all three volume proxies so it does not turn on the iothalamate space or the 1 kg = 1 L conversion. THE FAILURE IS NOT ABOUT THIS ROW. G_pn and CV.VENOUS_RETURN.SENSITIVITY are ORTHOGONAL, measured: an 8x change in G_vr moves the pressure response by 0.12% and moves the volume response exactly inversely. G_pn sets dMAP; G_vr sets dMAP/dV_ecf. So the human pressure data and the human volume data identify one parameter each with no cross-talk, and the pressure limb still supports 51. What the test shows is that ACCEPTING 51 ALONE WOULD MAKE THE VOLUME RESPONSE WORSE - from 1.26x too small at 20 to 3.2x too small at 51 - because dMAP is what this row moves and dV_ecf follows it down at a fixed, wrong ratio. Fix G_vr first, re-run the test, then accept. DECLARED CONFLICT: Heer 2000 (PMID 10751219) and Heer 2009 (PMID 19173770) report no total-body-water or body-mass increase on high sodium and a non-monotonic ECV response, which would put the error's sign the other way; that camp invokes osmotically inactive sodium storage, which is ADR 0004 and is default OFF here. || THE HUMAN BAND THIS ROW IS COMPARED AGAINST MOVED 2026-09-16. ecf_salt_response_extract.py de-indexed van den Bosch's extracellular volume with ONE body surface area where the paper prints one per arm, understating the human expansion by 9 percent. The tracer limb goes 0.553 -> 0.602 L per 100 mmol/day and the human dMAP/dV_ecf band 2.97-4.16 -> 2.82-4.02 mmHg/L. THIS ROW'S VALUE IS UNCHANGED and was not refitted to the new band - that would be fitting a parameter to a target the same pass moved. ADR 0013 addendum, validation/ecf_deindex_prereg.md. || PROVENANCE CORRECTED 2026-09-18, gpn_label_prereg.md. NO VALUE MOVED. THE CITATION FIELD DESCRIBED A NUMBER THIS ROW NO LONGER HOLDS. It read as Guyton 1972 with extraction_method=calibrated, which SOURCES.md defines as a value lifted from another model's fitted parameters - and 8.4 IS NOT GUYTON'S NUMBER. This row's own note above already said so: 8.4 is the constraint-consistent partner of the sourced volume gain, solved inside THIS model. Guyton is the origin of the ROW, of the FORM, and of the 20.0 that stood until 2026-09-03; he is not the source of the value. The citation now says which. FAILURE MODE #11, and the same shape the ANP label pass fixed the same day. || extraction_method STAYS `calibrated`, on the precedent RN.MD.RENIN_GAIN set: that row is also solved inside this model against human measurements and also carries `calibrated`, having recorded that naming the absent originating model is the honest answer to a label written for the other case. The count of calibrated rows is unchanged at two and this row is still one of them. || AND THE JOINT CONSTRAINT RECORDED ABOVE IS NO LONGER SATISFIED. The note says the human joint constraint is G_pn + 0.0594*G_anp = 50 and that 8.4 follows with the volume gain at 700. THAT PARTNER IS NOW 585 (and renamed CV.VOLUME.NATRIURETIC_GAIN): 8.4 + 0.0594*585 = 43.15, short of 50 by 6.85, about 14 percent. The value that would satisfy the linearisation at 585 is 15.25. || THAT IS NOT A VALUE DEFECT AND THE VALUE WAS NOT MOVED - DIRECTIVE 1.14, CHECKED BEFORE CHASING. The model's chronic salt sensitivity at the current pair is 1.96, inside the human 1.70-2.30, so the pair WORKS. The volume gain was re-solved 700 -> 585 against the NONLINEAR model outcome with G_pn held at 8.4, targeting a chronic salt sensitivity of 2.00; G_pn + 0.0594*G_anp = 50 is a LINEARISATION of that same target. The solved pair satisfies the outcome and not the linearisation, and THE OUTCOME IS THE THING THAT WAS EVER MEANT TO HOLD. || SO THE DEFECT IS THE NOTE, NOT THE NUMBER: a constraint was left stated as if binding when the model deliberately does not satisfy it. Two copies of a fact drifting apart, which CLAUDE.md names as the failure this repository is organised against. WHAT WOULD MAKE IT A REAL DEFECT: the chronic salt sensitivity leaving 1.70-2.30, or a demonstration that the linearisation is exact rather than approximate. Neither holds. || STILL OPEN AND NOT RESOLVED HERE: ADR 0013 proposes 51.0 on the human PRESSURE evidence alone and remains Proposed and unadopted; ADR 0015 is also Proposed. The model is running on rows those records discuss while neither has been accepted. Reported, not resolved - adopting either in a relabelling pass is exactly what this pass forbids itself.
"""
const RN_PRESSURE_NATRIURESIS_SLOPE = 8.4

"""Time constant of thick ascending limb transport capacity adaptation [day]  [!] ASSUMED
Source (tier C, assumed): 
Notes: TIME CONSTANT OF THICK ASCENDING LIMB TRANSPORT ADAPTATION. ASSUMED, AND THE ROW SAYS SO. tal_saturable_prereg.md. || WHY IT IS NEEDED, AND IT IS NOT A FUDGE. Saturable TAL transport with a FIXED capacity makes macula densa concentration rise with flow - which is the acute signal Jensen needs - but f_prox_eff varies 1.76-fold across the salt step (Folkerd 1995), so chronic flow varies too and the concentration would swing about 34 percent between salt arms. THAT CONTRADICTS VALLON 2002 (PMID 12089382), who measured NO change in the tubuloglomerular feedback signal across dietary salt in normal rats. || TUBULAR ADAPTATION IS THE STANDARD RESOLUTION: thick ascending limb transport capacity tracks sustained load. Acutely the capacity is fixed, flow rises, the concentration rises. Chronically the capacity follows delivery and the concentration returns to its reference. ALL THREE SOURCES - Layton's flow dependence, Folkerd's proximal salt response and Vallon's chronic constancy - BECOME TRUE AT ONCE, which none of the three previous forms of this arm managed. || THE VALUE IS BRACKETED, NOT MEASURED. It must be LONGER than Jensen's four-hour protocol, which sees the flow effect, and SHORTER than Vallon's one-week diets, which do not. One day sits in the middle of that bracket on a log scale. NO SEARCH FOUND A DIRECT MEASUREMENT of the adaptation time constant for TAL transport capacity, and this row is the place to record that rather than to dress an assumption as a source. || IT WAS NOT TUNED TO MAKE JENSEN PASS. Jensen's fractional sodium excretion rise is the FALSIFIABLE TEST of this pass, declared as such in the pre-registration before the value was entered. Moving this constant toward whichever end of its bracket flattered that endpoint would spend the test. || WHAT WOULD RETIRE THE ASSUMPTION: a measurement of how quickly NKCC2-mediated transport capacity follows a step change in delivered load. Days is the expectation from transporter expression work; a number is not in hand.
"""
const RN_TAL_ADAPTATION_TAU = 1.0

"""Michaelis constant for thick ascending limb chloride transport [mmol/L]  [!] CALIBRATED
Source (tier C, calibrated): Layton HE, Pitman EB, Moore LC. Nonlinear filter properties of the thick ascending limb. Am J Physiol 1997;273(4):F625-F634.
Notes: MICHAELIS CONSTANT FOR THICK ASCENDING LIMB CHLORIDE TRANSPORT. Layton 1997, PMID 9362340, Table 1, full text read and supplied by the owner. tal_saturable_prereg.md. || extraction_method = CALIBRATED, AND THE ORIGINATING MODEL IS NAMED, which is what SOURCES.md reserves that label for. This is a parameter of a MATHEMATICAL MODEL of the thick ascending limb, not a direct measurement; Layton selected it in his Ref. 15 to give a steady-state macula densa concentration and concentration slope consistent with experiments. It is the third calibrated row in this ledger and the first whose originating model exists and can be cited. || LAYTON'S TABLE 1, BACKLEAK-FREE COLUMN: Km 140 mM, chloride at the TAL entrance 275 mM, macula densa chloride 32.12 mM, TAL length 0.500 cm, luminal radius 10.0 micrometres, single-nephron GFR 30.0 nl/min, steady-state transit time 15.708 s. || WHAT TRANSFERS IS THE FORM AND THIS CONSTANT. The paper's central result is that NaCl concentration at each location along the TAL depends ONLY on the fluid transit time up the TAL to that location - the flow dependence, stated exactly. That is the property this model needed and could not produce with a constant reabsorbed fraction. || A CONCENTRATION IS INTENSIVE so no body-size scaling applies; the cross-species step is rat/rabbit transport kinetics to a human tubule, and it is the weakest link on this row. || LAYTON'S MACULA DENSA VALUE IS CHLORIDE AND THIS MODEL TRACKS SODIUM. His 32.12 mM Cl against this model's 53.9 mM Na is consistent with the anion gap Lorenz's own perfusates carry - 80 mM Na against 61 mM Cl, and 24 against 7 - so the two are the same ballpark measured on different ions. NO CONVERSION WAS APPLIED: only Km and the functional form were taken.
"""
const RN_TAL_KM = 140.0

"""Fractional fall in single-nephron GFR per fractional rise in loop of Henle flow [unitless] +/- 0.5-1.0 (range)
Source (tier A, reported): Briggs JP, Schubert G, Schnermann J. Quantitative characterization of the tubuloglomerular feedback response: effect of growth. Am J Physiol 1984;247(5 Pt 2):F808-F815. PMID 6496746. ABSTRACT READ IN FULL on PubMed, 2026-09-20.
Notes: FRACTIONAL FALL IN SINGLE-NEPHRON GFR PER FRACTIONAL RISE IN LOOP OF HENLE FLOW, AT THE FEEDBACK MIDPOINT. Briggs 1984, PMID 6496746. tgf_prereg.md, ADR 0026. || THE PAPER'S OWN WORDS: 'Independent of rat size, a 10% increase in loop flow at the midpoint produced a 5-10% decrease in SNGFR.' That is the row: 0.5 to 1.0, dimensionless. || WHY THIS PAPER RATHER THAN A BIGGER ONE. Male Sprague-Dawley rats at 100, 220 and 350 g, micropuncture, SNGFR measured at several loop perfusion rates in the SAME nephron. The sigmoid dSNGFR = a/(1 + exp(k*(b - VLP))) fitted per nephron. EVERY ABSOLUTE QUANTITY TRIPLES ACROSS THE WEIGHT RANGE - maximal response 7.9, 18.9 and 25.2 nl/min, half-maximal flow 10.3, 15.4 and 22.3 nl/min, slope 0.9, 1.7 and 3.2 - AND THE DIMENSIONLESS ELASTICITY DOES NOT. The authors varied body size 3.5-fold and reported the quantity that survived it, which is precisely the quantity a human whole-kidney model can take. || THE CROSS-SPECIES STEP IS THE WEAKEST LINK ON THIS ROW AND IT IS NOT HIDDEN. Rat single-nephron feedback to a human whole-kidney GFR. It is entered because the paper's own design tested size-invariance over a 3.5-fold range and found it, not because rat and human were assumed alike. || A LINEAR TERM IS WHAT THE SOURCE LICENSES. The same abstract reports that free-flow SNGFR and loop flow lie in the MOST SENSITIVE RANGE of the feedback curve, so the operating point sits at the midpoint and a local slope is the right form there. The sigmoid saturates and the linear term does not, so the component clamps its multiplier to 0.6-1.4 AS A NUMERICAL GUARD, NOT A PHYSIOLOGICAL CLAIM - free-flow SNGFR is not in the abstract, so the fraction the maximal response corresponds to CANNOT BE COMPUTED FROM WHAT HAS BEEN READ, and whether the guard ever binds is a declared test. || 0.8 AND NOT 0.75. The bounds are the paper's and are given to ONE significant figure; a midpoint written as 0.75 would claim a second figure the range does not support. Directive 1.13. || APPLIED TO CONCENTRATION, NOT FLOW, AND THE COMPONENT CONVERTS. Briggs's independent variable is loop flow; the macula densa senses concentration (Lorenz 1990, PMID 2197878). The distinction decides the CHRONIC behaviour: once TAL capacity has adapted, flow is high while concentration is back at reference, so a flow-driven term would stay switched on forever and clamp GFR at every salt intake. The conversion uses the model's own transport integral and is computed in Renal.jl rather than stored. || SEX: the row is 'both'. Briggs studied MALE Sprague-Dawley rats only, recorded here rather than in the sex column, which this ledger uses for parameters the model actually splits by sex. No sex-specific TGF elasticity was found. || SCALING: none, it is dimensionless. || WHAT IT FIXES: saturable TAL transport made macula densa concentration flow-sensitive and closed a POSITIVE feedback loop through renin and the proximal tubule with gain 1.6, leaving the model with no steady state. The macula densa has two effectors and the model had only the slow one.
"""
const RN_TGF_FLOW_ELASTICITY = 0.8

"""Urinary osmoles per mEq of sodium excreted [mOsm/mEq]
Source (tier B, reported): Imamura T, Kinugawa K, Minatsuki S, Muraoka H, Kato N, Inaba T, Maki H, Shiga T, Hatano M, Yao A, Kyo S, Komuro I. Urine osmolality estimated using urine urea nitrogen, sodium and creatinine can effectively predict response to tolvaptan in decompensated heart failure patients. Circ J 2013;77(5):1208-1213. PMID 23318562. The validated estimator carries urine sodium with a coefficient of 2.
Notes: THE COEFFICIENT IS ELECTRONEUTRALITY, NOT A FITTED CONSTANT. Every sodium ion leaving in the urine leaves with an accompanying anion, predominantly chloride, so one mEq of excreted sodium contributes approximately two milliosmoles - one for the cation and one for the anion. That is why the standard urine osmolality estimators all carry urine sodium with a coefficient of 2. WHAT THE CITATION ACTUALLY SUPPLIES. Imamura 2013 validated the whole estimator 1.07*(2*[urine Na] + [urine urea nitrogen]/2.8 + [urine creatinine]*2/3) + 16 against MEASURED urine osmolality in 66 patients and reports r = 0.938, p < 0.001. The paper does not measure this coefficient; it measures that a formula containing it reproduces the real thing. That is the honest description of the evidence and is why this row is tier B rather than A. DECLARED LIMITATION OF THE COHORT: decompensated heart failure patients receiving tolvaptan, i.e. a diseased population under a V2 antagonist. Directive 1.7 test - had the correlation come out poorly, the conclusion would have been that estimated urine osmolality cannot predict tolvaptan response, so the composition relation is the INSTRUMENT there and not the subject. It is accepted anyway because the coefficient is not an empirical finding of that paper but a charge-balance requirement, and the validation is corroboration that the requirement holds in real urine. INDEPENDENT CORROBORATION OF THE STRUCTURE, not pooled with it: Alwis US, Delanghe J, Dossche L, Walle JV, Van Camp J, Monaghan TF, Roggeman S, Everaert K. Could evening dietary protein intake play a role in nocturnal polyuria? J Clin Med 2020;9(8):2532. PMID 32764521. 170 subjects, 24 h collections in 8 timed samples with volume, osmolality, creatinine, urea and sodium all measured; reports urea as the largest single contributor to urine osmolality with sodium excretion the other major term. That is the two-part structure this row and RN.URINE.SOLUTE_NONNA implement. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13.
"""
const RN_URINE_OSM_PER_NA = 2.0

"""Daily urinary solute excretion [mOsm/day] +/- 200 (sd)
Source (tier B, reported): Kitada K, Daub S, Zhang Y, Klein JD, Nakano D, Pedchenko T, Lantier L, LaRocque LM, Marton A, Neubert P, Schroeder A, Rakova N, Jantsch J, Dikalova AE, Dikalov SI, Harrison DG, Mueller DN, Nishiyama A, Rauh M, Harris RC, Luft FC, Wassermann DH, Sands JM, Titze J. High salt intake reprioritizes osmolyte and energy metabolism for body fluid conservation. J Clin Invest 2017;127(5):1944-1959. doi:10.1172/JCI88532. PMID 28414295. PMC5409074. OPEN ACCESS, FULL TEXT READ. Table 1, human balance study, 12 g/day salt arm: 10 healthy men, 739 complete 24 h collections, urine osmolality 508 +/- 170 mOsm/kg and urine volume 22 +/- 7 mL/kg/day. Companion paper Rakova N et al, J Clin Invest 2017;127(5):1932-1943, PMID 28414302, FULL TEXT AND SUPPLEMENT READ, supplies the protocol (12 g/d = 200 mmol Na/d) and Supplemental Table S1, the anthropometrics (81.5 +/- 10.0 and 84.2 +/- 7.8 kg in the 12 g/d arms, BSA 2.0 m2, age about 33).
Notes: ASSUMED and NOT SOURCED, and it is the load-bearing assumption of the ADH component - urine volume is this divided by urine osmolality, so it scales the whole water-excretion limb. 600 mOsm/day is a conventional adult figure. It is HELD CONSTANT, which is itself a modelling choice: in reality it tracks protein and salt intake, so a salt step should move it and here it does not. That omission makes the model under-respond to a salt load on the water side. Source it, and consider making it depend on Na_excr, before trusting any water result under a changed diet. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13. || REFERENCE POINT CORRECTED 2026-09-16, urine_solute_prereg.md Half A, AND THE VALUE MOVED WITHOUT ANY SOURCE BEING OPENED. RN.URINE.SOLUTE_NONNA was pinned so the total returned 600 at the MID salt arm, 154 mEq/day, while the model's reference is the NOMINAL arm, 205. So the load the model actually carried at its own operating point was 292 + 2*205 = 702 and never 600. Three constants - this one, ADH.URINE.OSM_BASELINE and ADH.OSM.SENSITIVITY - were all derived from a load the model never occupied, and k_adh is LIVE. The loop paid for it with a permanent 0.59 mOsm/kg offset above the plasma osmolality setpoint: it had to reach u_osm = 702/1.7 = 412.9, hence adh 0.3894, hence Osm_ecf 287.594. Measured in the running model before the fix: 0.3895 and 287.592. The mid-arm pin was change management when solute tracking landed - it kept the salt step bit-identical - and it then became the reference point for three derived constants. THIS ROW IS NOW THE LOAD AT THE REFERENCE and is derived from the residual, which is the primitive until it is sourced. It is still tier C and still rests on an unsourced 292: Half A fixes WHERE the constant is stated, not WHAT it is. || SOURCED 2026-09-16 UNDER urine_solute_prereg.md BRANCH U1, replacing an `assumed` conventional 600 mOsm/day that carried no citation at all. || THE VALUE: 508 mOsm/kg x 22 mL/kg/day x 83 kg = 928, entered at TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES as 930 because the volume and the body weight carry two. || TABLE 1 WAS READ AS AN IMAGE. It is published as a figure rather than as HTML, and an automated text extraction of it returned numbers that did not self-consistently add up - a transposed mouse column. The values were read off the rendered table and then checked against themselves: 2Na + 2K + urea = 10.9 against a reported U2Na2KUreaV of 10.8 mmol/kg/day, and osmolality x volume exceeds that sum by 3.4 percent, which is the unmeasured ammonium, phosphate, sulfate and creatinine. || THE BODY WEIGHT IS PINNED TWO INDEPENDENT WAYS and that is what makes the per-kg units trustworthy: the 12 g/day arm is 200 mmol/day of dietary sodium against a measured UNaV of 2.3 mmol/kg/day, which at the usual 95 percent urinary recovery implies 83 kg; Supplemental Table S1 reports 81.5 and 84.2 kg for the two 12 g/day arms. || THE COHERENCE CONSTRAINT OF THE PRE-REGISTRATION SECTION 3 IS SATISFIED, AND THAT WAS THE POINT OF FIXING IT IN ADVANCE. The urine solute load is a DIET, not a physiological constant, so it had to come from a cohort at this model's own salt intake or the mismatch would land silently in the residual. Rakova's 12 g/day arm is 200 mmol Na/day against BF.NA.INTAKE_NOMINAL = 205, and it is the SAME COHORT BF.NA.INTAKE_MID is already derived from - section 3.24's same-cohort rule satisfied by construction rather than by luck. || THE LEDGER PREDICTED THE ANSWER. RN.URINE.SOLUTE_NONNA's note already said the non-sodium remainder was 'almost certainly TOO LOW' and should be 'nearer 400-500'. Measured: 546 at the cohort, 520 at this model's reference sodium intake. || THE SD IS DAY-TO-DAY AND BETWEEN-SUBJECT SPREAD ACROSS 739 COLLECTIONS, NOT UNCERTAINTY ON A MEAN, and it is taken from U2Na2KUreaV (10.8 +/- 2.3, i.e. 21 percent) rather than from the osmolality and volume SDs, which are large and strongly anti-correlated so their product is far tighter than either factor. Directive 1.9: saying which statistic this is, is the point. || MEN ONLY, n = 10, mean age 33, in an enclosed habitat on a fully controlled diet. A controlled diet is exactly the preparation this row needs - directive 1.7, since the alternative literature is kidney-stone risk profiling and hydration-intervention trials where the excretion is the instrument - but it is not a free-living Western diet and the row is not a population mean.
"""
const RN_URINE_SOLUTE_LOAD = 930.0

"""Non-sodium urinary solute load, held constant [mOsm/day]
Source (tier B, derived): Derived from RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD, RN.URINE.OSM_PER_NA and BF.NA.INTAKE_NOMINAL.
Notes: DERIVED, and it is a RESIDUAL rather than a measurement: SOLUTE_NONNA = RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD - RN.URINE.OSM_PER_NA * BF.NA.INTAKE_MID = 600.0 - 2.0 * 154.0 = 292.0 mOsm/day. It stands for urea plus potassium salts plus everything else, all held constant. WHY IT IS DEFINED THIS WAY. RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD is load-bearing: ADH.URINE.OSM_MAX, ADH.URINE.OSM_BASELINE and ADH.OSM.SENSITIVITY are all derived from it and four closure checks depend on it. Making the solute load track sodium must therefore leave the REFERENCE load untouched, or every derived ADH constant and every steady state moves. Pinning the residual so that total load returns exactly 600.0 at the mid salt arm (154 mEq/day) achieves that: the mid arm is unchanged to the last digit and only the high and low arms move. INHERITED DEBT, STATED PLAINLY: 292 mOsm/day is almost certainly TOO LOW for the non-sodium fraction. Urea alone is normally the largest single contributor to urine osmolality (Alwis 2020) and potassium salts add roughly another 100-120 mOsm/day, so a real non-sodium remainder on a Western diet is nearer 400-500. The residual is small because the parent RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD = 600 mOsm/day is itself an unsourced conventional figure that is low for a 154 mEq/day sodium intake - typical measured totals are 700-900. THIS ROW DOES NOT FIX THAT AND MUST NOT BE READ AS FIXING IT. Correcting the total would move U_max, U_base, k_adh and every steady state, and needs its own pre-registered extraction. What this row does is make the load RESPOND to salt, which it previously did not do at all. Tier C, inherited from the parent. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13. || RE-DERIVED 2026-09-16 AGAINST THE SOURCED TOTAL, AND THE PIN MOVED FROM THE MID ARM TO THE NOMINAL ARM. 930 - 2*205 = 520 mOsm/day. It was 292, pinned so the total returned 600 at 154 mEq/day while the model runs at 205 - see the Half A note on RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD. || THE INHERITED DEBT THIS ROW DECLARED IS NOW DISCHARGED. Its own text said 292 was 'almost certainly TOO LOW', that urea is normally the largest single contributor and potassium salts add another 100-120 mOsm/day, and that a real remainder is 'nearer 400-500'. Kitada measures urea at 4.1 and potassium at 1.1 mmol/kg/day, which at 83 kg is 340 mOsm/day of urea and 182 of potassium salts. || IT IS STILL A RESIDUAL AND IT IS STILL HELD CONSTANT, AND THE SECOND HALF OF THAT IS NOW MEASURABLY WRONG. In the source the non-sodium remainder FALLS from 7.08 to 6.58 mOsm/kg/day as salt goes 6 -> 12 g/day, because urine urea concentration falls 13.9 percent - the natriuretic-ureotelic pattern the paper is about. So the true slope of total osmolar excretion against excreted sodium is about 1.5 mOsm/mEq, where this model uses 2.0 and holds the rest fixed. ACROSS ITS OWN SALT ARMS THE MODEL THEREFORE OVER-RESPONDS ON THE SOLUTE LIMB BY ROUGHLY 30 PERCENT: 204 mOsm/day of swing from 205 to 103 mEq/day where the data imply about 157. Not built, because the dependence would need a urea-recycling or glucocorticoid mechanism with no sourced form and this pass pre-registered none. It is now a NUMBER rather than an unknown.
"""
const RN_URINE_SOLUTE_NONNA = 520.0

"""Non-sodium urinary solute response to sodium excretion [mOsm/(kg*mEq)]
Source (tier B, derived): Kitada K, Daub S, Zhang Y, Klein JD, Nakano D, Pedchenko T, Lantier L, LaRocque LM, Marton A, Neubert P, Schroeder A, Rakova N, Jantsch J, Dikalova AE, Dikalov SI, Harrison DG, Mueller DN, Nishiyama A, Rauh M, Harris RC, Luft FC, Wassermann DH, Sands JM, Titze J. High salt intake reprioritizes osmolyte and energy metabolism for body fluid conservation. J Clin Invest 2017;127(5):1944-1959. doi:10.1172/JCI88532. PMID 28414295. PMC5409074. OPEN ACCESS, FULL TEXT READ - it is already the citation on RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD. 10 healthy men, 739 complete 24 h collections. THE NON-SODIUM REMAINDER FALLS FROM 7.08 TO 6.58 mOsm/kg/day as salt intake goes 6 -> 12 g/day NaCl, because urine urea concentration falls 13.9 percent - the natriuretic-ureotelic pattern the paper is about.
Notes: DERIVED FROM TWO REPORTED VALUES. -0.50 mOsm/kg/day across a 102 mmol/day change in salt intake gives -0.004902, entered as -0.0049. TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES AND NOT MORE: the inputs are 7.08 and 6.58, three figures each, and their DIFFERENCE of 0.50 carries two. Directive 1.13. || WHAT IT FIXES. RN.URINE.SOLUTE_NONNA's note has recorded since 2026-09-16 that holding the non-sodium remainder CONSTANT makes this model over-respond on the solute limb by roughly 30 percent - 204 mOsm/day of swing across its own salt arms where the data imply about 157. That note said the dependence was 'not built, because it would need a urea-recycling or glucocorticoid mechanism with no sourced form'. THIS ROW DOES NOT BUILD THAT MECHANISM. It is the measured empirical SLOPE, which is the shape RN.GFR.VOLUME_SENSITIVITY already has. || THE MECHANISM IS UNSOURCED AND THAT IS NOT A FORMALITY. Kitada attributes the fall to urea recycling under glucocorticoid control; this row asserts only that the remainder falls with salt intake at the measured rate, across the 6-12 g/day range the source tested. OUTSIDE THAT RANGE IT IS AN EXTRAPOLATION, and the model's own arms - 103 to 205 mmol/day - sit inside it by construction, because they ARE 6 and 12 g/day. || EXTENSIVE, PER KILOGRAM, because urea production tracks lean mass, exactly as RN.URINE.SOLUTE_NONNA already scales. At the 70 kg reference this is -0.343 mOsm/day per mEq/day, so the NET slope of total osmolar excretion against sodium becomes 2.0 - 0.343 = 1.66 against the earlier note's rough estimate of 1.5. || IT IS ZERO AT THE REFERENCE BY CONSTRUCTION. The term is k*(Na_excr - Na_excr_ref), so RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD returns exactly 930 mOsm/day at nominal intake and every ADH constant derived from that total is untouched. validation/solute_limb_prereg.md branch U2: if any of them moves, the TERM is written wrongly and the term gets fixed, not the constants. || RN.URINE.OSM_PER_NA STAYS 2.0. That is charge balance - sodium and its