"""
const RN_URINE_SOLUTE_NONNA_SLOPE = -0.0049

"""Volume-keyed natriuresis time constant [day] +/- 0.10-0.25 (range)
Source (tier C, derived): Drummer C, Heer M, Baisch F, Blomqvist CG, Lang RE, Maass H, Gerzer R. Diuresis and natriuresis following isotonic saline infusion in healthy young volunteers before, during, and after HDT. Acta Physiol Scand Suppl 1992;604:101-11. PMID 1324562. 6 healthy men, 0.9% saline 22 mL/kg over 20 min: sodium excretion rose 3-fold, LESS THAN 15% of the infused sodium was excreted acutely, and urine flow and sodium excretion remained significantly elevated for MORE THAN 48 HOURS. The paper's own conclusion is that excretion of an acute isotonic load REQUIRES SEVERAL DAYS. That is the qualitative evidence that the volume-natriuresis relation is lagged rather than instantaneous.
Notes: RE-ESTIMATED 2026-09-03, 0.50 -> 0.15 d, with CV.ANP.NATRIURETIC_GAIN, against the corrected G_pn = 8.4 and the sourced G_vr = 1400. Identified from LOBO's two 6 h endpoints, which agree independently: matching the 563 mL urine gives 0.171 d and matching the 95 mmol sodium gives 0.166 d. Entered as 0.15, which reproduces both to within 0.5 percent - 566 mL and 95.1 mmol. || IT MOVED BY A FACTOR OF 3.3 AND THE REASON IS NOT ANP. The pressure slope fell 20 -> 8.4 over the same period, so the pressure path now contributes far less to the acute natriuresis and the volume path has to respond faster to reproduce the same 6 h excretion. THIS PARAMETER IS NOT IDENTIFIED BY ANP PHYSIOLOGY; it is identified by requiring the model to match one acute human dataset given everything else, and it will move again if anything upstream of it does. Tier C, and the tier is doing work. || THE DRUMMER CORROBORATION IS WEAKER THAN THE PREVIOUS NOTE CLAIMED, and that is corrected here rather than left standing. Drummer 1992 (PMID 1324562) says excretion of an acute isotonic load takes SEVERAL DAYS and that sodium excretion is elevated beyond 48 h. At 0.15 d this lag reaches 95 percent of its steady state in about 11 h, which is hours rather than days. The reconciliation is that Drummer describes the time course of EXCRETING THE LOAD, which in this model is set by how slowly the volume itself decays, not by this lag. Drummer still supports the EXISTENCE of a lag - the algebraic form was refuted without one - but it does not constrain its VALUE, and the previous note overstated that. || WHAT WOULD FALSIFY IT: a human isotonic-loading study reporting the full cumulative sodium excretion curve out to 72 h. Drummer measured one; the abstract does not give the series and the full text would settle this row. || THIS ROW'S FALSIFICATION CLAUSE IS DISCHARGED, AND THE ANSWER IS THAT THE ROW IS NOT THE PROBLEM. 2026-09-17, HANDOVER section 3.46, validation/late_time_course_prereg.md branch L2. The clause asked for 'a human isotonic-loading study reporting the full cumulative sodium excretion curve out to 72 h'. FOUND: Drummer C, Gerzer R, Heer M, Molz B, Bie P, Schlossberger M, Stadaeger C, Roecker L, Strollo F, Heyduck B, et al. Effects of an acute saline infusion on fluid and electrolyte metabolism in humans. Am J Physiol 1992;262(5 Pt 2):F744-54. doi:10.1152/ajprenal.1992.262.5.F744. PMID 1590419. ABSTRACT READ IN FULL; the full text was not obtained, so the interval SERIES is still not in hand and nothing depends on one. Six healthy volunteers, SUPINE, strictly controlled, nine days, 2 L of isotonic saline in 25 min, 48 h of collections AND a 48 h control experiment. || IT IS A DIFFERENT PAPER FROM THE DRUMMER 1992 IN THIS ROW'S CITATION COLUMN. That one is PMID 1324562, the same group's head-down-tilt study, and HDT is its experimental variable. This one has no tilt, which makes it cleanly admissible where the other is admissible only in its control arm. || WHAT IT MEASURES: elevated body weight returned to baseline with an APPROXIMATE HALF-LIFE OF 7 HOURS, and the largest increase in fluid and electrolyte excretion between 3 and 22 h postinfusion. THE MODEL GIVES 13.10 h for the volume excursion and 5.9 h for the peak - so it passes the timing of the peak and FAILS the decay by a factor of 1.9. || AND THE FAILURE IS NOT THIS ROW'S. Swept in bench/late_time_course.jl from 1.0 d down to 0.001 d - effectively instantaneous - the half-life bottoms out at 11.97 h. TWELVE HOURS IS A FLOOR NO VALUE OF THIS LAG CAN PASS, and the measurement is 7. That is not evidence that 0.15 d is the wrong value; it is evidence that THE LAG IS NOT WHAT SETS THIS QUANTITY, which is what this row's own note already said about Drummer's timescale. The tail is set by the natriuretic GAINS, and no single one of them reaches 7 h without taking the chronic salt sensitivity below the human 1.70-2.30. || SO THE ROW IS UNCHANGED AND STILL IDENTIFIED BY LOBO ALONE. What the search bought is not a value but a DISQUALIFICATION: the late time course cannot constrain this lag, so the dimension in which it is unidentified is narrower than it looked. What would still constrain it is the SHAPE inside the first 12 hours, which needs Drummer's interval series and therefore its full text. || THE FULL TEXT WAS PURSUED ON 2026-09-17 AND IT IS CLOSED. EVERY ROUTE TRIED, RECORDED SO NOBODY REPEATS THE SEARCH. Drummer 1992, Am J Physiol 262(5 Pt 2):F744-54, PMID 1590419, doi 10.1152/ajprenal.1992.262.5.F744. (a) Europe PMC: isOpenAccess=N, inEPMC=N, inPMC=N, hasPDF=N, single full-text link marked 'Subscription required'. (b) OpenAlex: oa_status CLOSED, best_oa_location null, FOUR locations and none open - the publisher, PubMed, the DLR electronic library and a University of Copenhagen research profile. (c) journals.physiology.org returns HTTP 403. (d) THE AUTHORS' OWN INSTITUTIONAL REPOSITORY, elib.dlr.de/27251, holds a metadata record that says in terms 'Dieses Archiv kann nicht den Volltext zur Verfuegung stellen' - this archive cannot provide the full text. || SO THE INTERVAL SERIES THIS ROW ASKED FOR IS NOT OBTAINABLE without institutional access, and the row stays where HANDOVER section 3.46 left it: identified by Lobo's two 6 h cumulative endpoints, with the late time course constraining the GAINS and not this lag. The falsification clause is NOT discharged - it is BLOCKED, which is a different thing and is recorded as such. Same standing as RESP.CO2.ARTERIAL_RESTING's Crapo 1999. || AND THE COMPANION PAPER IS CLOSED TOO: PMID 1324562, the head-down-tilt study, isOpenAccess=N. So is Luft FC et al, Am J Kidney Dis 1983;2(4):464-70, PMID 6823962, oa_status CLOSED - which would otherwise have been the sharpest instrument available, because it infused 2 L of saline over 2 h into normal men at FOUR prior sodium intakes (10, 300, 600 and 800 mEq/day) and reports that the natriuresis DEPENDS ON PRIOR INTAKE, with fractional sodium excretion of 6-7 percent maximal at 600 mEq/day. That is the acute response measured at four chronic operating points and it would bear directly on the conflict in section 3.47. ABSTRACT ONLY. || THE FULL TEXT ARRIVED 2026-09-17 AND IT CHANGES WHAT THIS ROW'S FAILURE MEANS. HANDOVER section 3.49, bench/drummer_fulltext.jl. The owner supplied Drummer 1992 (PMID 1590419) from institutional access after the search recorded above came back closed. THE ABSTRACT CARRIED ONE HALF-LIFE AND THE PAPER CARRIES TWO: 'a half-life of ~7 h for returning to baseline body weights was found. The respective half-life for reachieving SODIUM BALANCE WAS 10 h.' || MEASURED: the model's VOLUME excursion half-life is 13.33 h against 7.0, which is 1.90x too slow, but its SODIUM excursion half-life is 12.52 h against 10.0, only 1.25x. The interval series agrees far better than the half-lives suggest - over the bulk 3-22 h window the model excretes 1429 mL against 1322 and 220.6 mmol against 261.0. THE SODIUM LIMB IS ROUGHLY RIGHT AND THE WATER LIMB IS NOT. || SO SECTION 3.46's THREE-FOLD GAIN REQUIREMENT WAS A WATER PROBLEM PUSHED THROUGH A SODIUM LEVER, and section 3.47's conclusion that Drummer and Jensen disagree by twofold IS WITHDRAWN. Jensen measures FRACTIONAL SODIUM EXCRETION and the model is 1.25x off on sodium; the two agree. || THIS ROW IS STILL NOT THE PROBLEM AND THAT PART STANDS. The lag floors at 11.97 h when made instantaneous. What the full text adds is that the quantity to fix is not the natriuretic gain either: Drummer's weight returns FASTER than his sodium, a half-life ratio of 0.70 against the model's 1.065, and in this model extracellular volume is tied to extracellular sodium so water cannot leave ahead of salt. That is ADR 0004's compartment, which is PROVISIONAL and switched off. || PROTOCOL CORRECTIONS: the dose is 30 mL/kg and not the '2 litres' the abstract rounds to, 2.1 L at 70 kg; the subjects were SUPINE throughout and ate and drank nothing from 06:30 to noon, so the first three post-infusion hours are a fasting measurement. || RENAMED 2026-09-18, RN.ANP.TAU -> RN.VOLUME_NATRIURESIS.TAU, anp_label_prereg.md. NO NUMBER MOVED AND THE MODEL OUTPUT IS BIT-IDENTICAL - that was the pass's only test. FAILURE MODE #11: a name carrying a convention its value contradicts. The `name` field already read 'Volume-keyed natriuretic gain' and ADR 0010's own Decision already called it 'a lumped volume-keyed natriuretic term, algebraic in V_blood, with ANP as its named evidence base rather than as a state'. THE param_id DID NOT SAY THAT, and the param_id is what appears in code, in LedgerParams.jl and in the GUI. || AND 'ANP AS ITS NAMED EVIDENCE BASE' WAS ITSELF WRONG. This gain was estimated from WHOLE-BODY ISOTONIC EXPANSION - Lobo 2001 and Jensen 2013 - which measures the TOTAL natriuretic response to a volume load. ANP was the mechanism assumed to carry that total, not the thing measured. HANDOVER section 3.57 supplied the counter-evidence: Lohmeier's split-bladder dogs give a denervated/innervated sodium ratio near 0.56 at sustained elevated pressure, so RENAL SYMPATHETIC WITHDRAWAL carries a substantial share of exactly the response this gain was fitted to. The gain is not wrong. The attribution was. || WHAT THIS LUMP CONTAINS, NAMED AND NOT QUANTIFIED: atrial natriuretic peptide; renal sympathetic withdrawal via cardiopulmonary volume receptors; and whatever else volume expansion does to tubular sodium handling that neither of those covers. THE SHARE IS DELIBERATELY NOT SPLIT. Lohmeier's ratio implies a nerve share near 47 percent, but the authors report a latent impairment in sodium excretion from the denervated kidneys, making that an UPPER BOUND rather than an estimate; the protocol is ANG II hypertension and not a salt step; n = 5 and abstract only. The supportable statement is 'between zero and about a half, biased high', and a parameter with that interval buys nothing. Directive 1.14. || THE TRAP THIS RENAME EXISTS TO PREVENT: a future ANP infusion dose-response MUST NOT be added on top of this gain. This gain ALREADY CONTAINS ANP's contribution, along with the neural one. Sourcing an ANP-specific gain would require SPLITTING this row, not supplementing it. || OLD IDENTIFIER RN.ANP.TAU IS RETAINED IN THE NOTE TEXT ABOVE AND THROUGHOUT THIS LEDGER'S HISTORY, which is append-only and was not rewritten - searching the old name still finds the record.
