about 2%. So the slope is NOT where the model's 2.4x thyrotropin discrepancy comes from, and that is now arithmetic rather than assertion. || WHY THIS ROW MOVED, AND IT IS A UNIT ERROR RATHER THAN AN IMPRECISE NUMBER. The ledger previously composed a pituitary line measured on one free-thyroxine assay (Benhadi 2010) with a free-thyroxine concentration measured by equilibrium dialysis (Braverman 1973), and predicted a euthyroid thyrotropin 2.2x the measured population value. THE TWO SCALES ARE NOT COMMENSURATE AND THAT IS NOW MEASURED: at essentially identical TOTAL thyroxine - 7.75 ug/dL in NHANES against Braverman's 7.30, agreeing to 6% - the free fraction is 0.0104% by immunoassay against 0.0180% by dialysis, a ratio of 1.73. Two methods agreeing on the total hormone to a few per cent and disagreeing by nearly two-fold on the free fraction are not two measurements of one quantity. thyroid_prereg.md section 2 prohibited POOLING across free-thyroxine assays and should have prohibited COMPOSING across them too. || THE PREVIOUS VALUE, 0.1352, WAS THE MEAN OF TWO MEASURED SLOPES ON TWO OTHER ASSAYS, and entering it against a third assay's concentration is what produced the 2.2x error. The dimensionless product b*FT4* is scale-invariant and is what actually transfers; it is now its own row. || The range is THY.LOOP_GAIN's range divided by the same concentration.
