Source (tier A, derived): DERIVED. a = ln(THY.TSH.EUTHYROID) + THY.LOOP_GAIN = ln(1.512) + 2.277. Sources are those two rows; nothing new is sourced here.
Notes: THE LOAD-BEARING WEAKNESS OF THIS AXIS, AND IT IS SAID HERE RATHER THAN DISCOVERED LATER. The slope has two independent estimates agreeing to 1% and the euthyroid FT4 has two agreeing to within a standard deviation; THIS NUMBER HAS ONE SOURCE AND IT CARRIES THE WHOLE OF THE MODEL'S DISCREPANCY IN THYROTROPIN. Reconstructing it from independent euthyroid pairs at the agreed slope gives 2.72 (Maushart euthyroid visit), 2.82 (Maushart 3-month) and 2.59 (NHANES III geometric mean TSH at the sourced FT4) - all 0.6 to 0.9 log units below 3.454, a factor of 1.9-2.4 in TSH. || WHY IT WOULD BE HIGH, AND THIS IS THE THIRD INSTANCE OF THE SAME PATTERN IN THIS REPOSITORY: an intercept is a line extrapolated to FT4 = 0 from data that never approached zero. The same extrapolation forced the ADR 0017 amendment - a chemoreflex line extrapolated below its measured range put ventilation at 19.3 L/min against a real 6.2 - and produced HANDOVER section 3.22's censoring bound. Benhadi's cohort is also mean age 60 and NHANES III reports TSH rising with age. || IT IS NOT REPLACED BY ANY OF THE RECONSTRUCTIONS. Every one of them is built from a measured euthyroid TSH, which is the quantity ADR 0019's falsifiable test 2 judges the loop against; fitting to it would be HANDOVER section 3.15's error committed deliberately. The model reports a euthyroid TSH 2.4x the NHANES III geometric mean and nothing downstream consumes TSH. || CUT FROM 3.453878 TO 3.454 ON 2026-09-05. Seven significant figures on a number whose source reports three ('1.50') is exactly the false precision directive 1.9 exists to stop. || AND THE ARITHMETIC THAT CONVICTS THIS ROW RATHER THAN THE SLOPE: sweeping the slope across its full two-source spread moves the predicted euthyroid thyrotropin by 2%, against a discrepancy of 2.4x. Nothing about the slope's uncertainty reaches that. The intercept does, because slope and intercept in a regression over a narrow range are strongly anti-correlated and the intercept is the