od before this pass - 20 percent of that from Half A's reference-point correction and the rest from the sourcing. This is the gain of the whole osmoregulatory limb and it was low because its parent was both unsourced and stated at the wrong salt arm. Antidiuretic activity at the reference is now 0.533, inside the 0.2-0.6 the pre-registration required and with room to move both ways. || SOURCED 2026-09-18 FROM BAYLIS 1986, adh_osmotic_map_prereg.md. THIS ROW WAS EXPLICITLY UNSOURCED and its own note said so: 'no sourced map from plasma vasopressin to urine osmolality was found'. Baylis IS that map. Eight healthy male adults, sustained water load, PAIRED plasma and urine osmolality: plasma 286.5 +/- 2.0 -> 279.2 +/- 2.4 mmol/kg (p < 0.001) with urine 867 +/- 54 -> 69 +/- 3 mmol/kg. Mapped onto this model's normalised activity with U_min = 50 and U_max = 982: adh 0.8766 -> 0.0204, so k = 0.8562 / 7.3 = 0.1173. TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES AND NOT MORE - the numerator is a difference of three-figure urine values and the denominator a difference of four-figure plasma values carrying two, directive 1.13. || IT FELL 32 PERCENT, 0.17777 -> 0.12. The old value was DERIVED to pin ONE point, the 24 h baseline water balance, and pinning one point fixes no slope at all; a measured slope now replaces it and the BASELINE PIN MOVES TO THE THRESHOLD, which is the row that was carrying the wrong quantity. || THE INTERVAL IS HONEST AND WIDE. p < 0.001 at df = 7 bounds t > 5.41, so the paired SE of the 7.3 mmol/kg fall is under 1.35 and the 95 percent interval on it is at worst 4.1-10.5, giving k in 0.082-0.208. THE OLD 0.17777 IS INSIDE THAT, so Baylis does NOT refute it on the slope alone - directive 1.14, and the pre-registration says so in section 0.1. What Baylis settles is the THRESHOLD's kind, not the slope's value. || NOT ZERBE'S SENSITIVITY, which is 0.12-1.66 pg/ml per mOsm/kg of PLASMA VASOPRESSIN. The numerical coincidence with 0.12 is exactly that and means nothing: different quantity, different units."),
    :ADH_OSM_THRESHOLD => Provenance("ADH.OSM.THRESHOLD", "mOsm/kg", 282.56, "C", "derived", "Derived to position the Baylis 1986 slope at this model's 24 h operating point; contained by the measured individual range of Zerbe RL, Miller JZ, Robertson GL. The reproducibility and heritability of individual differences in osmoregulatory function in normal human subjects. J Lab Clin Med 1991;117(1):51-59.", "Linear regression of plasma vasopressin on plasma osmolality during hypertonic saline infusion. Individual thresholds ranged 280 to 288 mOsm/kg and the frequency distribution across 80 healthy adults was essentially normal, so the MIDPOINT 284.0 is adopted and the range is carried as the uncertainty rather than an SD, which the paper does not give in a poolable form. Thresholds correlated within monozygotic but not dizygotic twins. NOTE the companion SENSITIVITY spans 0.12 to 1.66 pg/ml per mOsm/kg - fourteen-fold, and highly reproducible within a subject at r = 0.94 - which is NOT used here because this model carries no plasma vasopressin concentration. That spread is the obvious population covariate for src/ensemble.jl. Verified against PMID 1987308 on 2026-08-25. || PRECISION CORRECTED 2026-09-09 under directive 1.13, second pass. Trailing zero read as a fourth figure against an interval of 280-288. The first pass rounded this row but stopped one figure short, because the gate then allowed two figures beyond what the interval supports - a tolerance I had given myself rather than the rule. The gate now enforces the rule exactly. Discarded digits were inside the row's own stated interval, so no claim changes. || RE-KINDED AND INVERTED 2026-09-18, adh_osmotic_map_prereg.md. FAILURE MODE #11 - A NAME CARRYING A CONVENTION ITS VALUE CONTRADICTS. This row was tier A, extraction_method=reported, named 'Osmotic threshold for vasopressin RELEASE', and sourced from Zerbe's regressions of PLASMA VASOPRESSIN on plasma osmolality. THE MODEL NEVER USED IT AS THAT: in Adh.jl it