    :ADH_URINE_OSM_BASELINE => Provenance("ADH.URINE.OSM_BASELINE", "mOsm/kg", 547.06, "C", "derived", "Derived from RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD, BF.H2O.INTAKE_NOMINAL and BF.H2O.INSENSIBLE_LOSS.", "PRECISION: rounded to 3 significant figures on 2026-08-27. The sources do not support more, and carrying digits that do not exist forced the closure gate and the test suite to chase arithmetic noise. Derived rows inherit the significant figures of their inputs and are rounded to match; the closure tolerance was moved to 1e-3 in the same change, which is still an order of magnitude tighter than the 6% error that motivated that gate. || DERIVED: solute load / (intake - insensible loss) = 600 / 1.7 = 352.941176. Used ONLY by the disabled branch, where it holds urine osmolality at the value that reproduces the old placeholder water excretion exactly, so enabled=false recovers pre-ADH behaviour. Closure constraint enforced by tools/check_closure.py. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13. || REFERENCE POINT CORRECTED 2026-09-16, urine_solute_prereg.md Half A, AND THE VALUE MOVED WITHOUT ANY SOURCE BEING OPENED. RN.URINE.SOLUTE_NONNA was pinned so the total returned 600 at the MID salt arm, 154 mEq/day, while the model's reference is the NOMINAL arm, 205. So the load the model actually carried at its own operating point was 292 + 2*205 = 702 and never 600. Three constants - this one, ADH.URINE.OSM_BASELINE and ADH.OSM.SENSITIVITY - were all derived from a load the model never occupied, and k_adh is LIVE. The loop paid for it with a permanent 0.59 mOsm/kg offset above the plasma osmolality setpoint: it had to reach u_osm = 702/1.7 = 412.9, hence adh 0.3894, hence Osm_ecf 287.594. Measured in the running model before the fix: 0.3895 and 287.592. The mid-arm pin was change management when solute tracking landed - it kept the salt step bit-identical - and it then became the reference point for three derived constants. Recomputed as 702/1.7 = 412.94. It is the urine osmolality that reproduces the reference water excretion, and it is now computed at the reference load rather than at 600. || RE-DERIVED 2026-09-16 FROM THE SOURCED SOLUTE LOAD. The figures beyond the two the parent supports are NOT a precision claim: this row exists to close an identity check_closure.py asserts, which is the one exemption CLAUDE.md states, and rounding it to the parent's precision breaks that identity by more than the 1e-3 closure tolerance. 930/1.7 = 547.06 mOsm/kg. Against Kitada's MEASURED urine osmolality of 508 +/- 170 mOsm/kg in the same arm - well inside one SD, and it is NOT a fit: the model's urine volume is fixed by its own water balance at 1.7 L/day and the load is sourced independently, so this agreement was free."),
    :ADH_URINE_OSM_MAX => Provenance("ADH.URINE.OSM_MAX", "mOsm/kg", 982.0, "A", "reported", "Tryding N, Berg B, Ekman S, Nilsson JE, Sterner G, Harris A. DDAVP test for renal concentration capacity. Age-related reference intervals. Scand J Urol Nephrol 1988;22(2):141-145.", "SOURCED 2026-09-01, AND THE TEXTBOOK 1200 WAS HIGH. Was 1200.0 mOsm/kg, DERIVED as RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD / RN.H2O.OBLIGATORY_LOSS = 600 / 0.5 - maximal concentrating ability computed from an obligatory volume that was itself `assumed` with an empty citation. The dependency was backwards: MAXIMAL CONCENTRATING ABILITY IS WHAT IS MEASURED, by water deprivation or DDAVP, and the obligatory volume is what follows from it given a solute load. RN.H2O.OBLIGATORY_LOSS is now derived from this row. Pre-re-registered in validation/dependency_inversion_prereg.md, which was committed BEFORE any paper was opened and sits immediately before this change in main's history. NO COMMIT SHA IS CITED, DELIBERATELY - rebase-merge rewrites it and every such citation already in this ledger points at a commit main does not contain. Verify the ordering instead: git log --diff-filter=A -- validation/dependency_inversion_prereg.md. NOW REPORTED: Tryding 1988. Maximal urinary concentration after a single 4 microgram subcutaneous injection of DDAVP in 212 healthy adults aged 20 to 80. Mean values decline significantly with age, 982 mOsm/kg at 20 years to 823 at 80. 982 IS THE VALUE AT THE YOUNG ADULT END and is adopted because U_max is a CEILING - the saturation limit of the ADH curve in Adh.jl, reached at adh = 1 - rather than a population mean. The age decline is real, is roughly 16% across six decades, and this model has no age dimension: recorded as debt, not approximated. The same paper reports that maximal osmolality did not differ significantly between strict fluid restriction and liberal intake before the DDAVP, so the number is not an artefact of the deprivation protocol. CORROBORATED, NOT POOLED. Bech AP, Wetzels JFM, Nijenhuis T. Reference values of renal tubular function tests are dependent on age and kidney function. Physiol Rep 2017;5(23):e13542. Median maximal urine osmolality after desmopressin 1002 mOsm/kg in healthy individuals aged 18-50, 820 in healthy individuals over 50, 624 with compromised renal function. Two independent studies thirty years apart, both near 1000 in young healthy adults and both near 820 in the old. NOT POOLED: Bech reports a MEDIAN of 10 subjects and Tryding a fitted mean by age, and pooling.md forbids inventing a rule that fits them. SINGLE SOURCE, k = 1, rule 6. WHAT WAS SCREENED AND NOT USED. Fourteen further queries over two sweeps returned paediatric series, lithium and polycystic-kidney cohorts, desmopressin bioequivalence studies and hydration-marker papers. Nadvornikova 1980 (Clin Nephrol 1980;14(3):142-147, 45 healthy volunteers, standardised DDAVP protocol) is the right design but reports no normative osmolality in the record read. Curtis 1979 (Br Med J 1979;1(6159):304-305) reports that most normal subjects reach maximal concentration spontaneously over 24 hours, which supports the protocol but gives no value. NO DISPERSION IS ENTERED. Tryding reports age-related reference INTERVALS; the record read gives the age-related means, not an SD at a given age. Directive 1.12 wants a centre with spread and this row has only the centre - recorded rather than invented. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :ADH_URINE_OSM_MIN => Provenance("ADH.URINE.OSM_MIN", "mOsm/kg", 50.0, "C", "assumed", "", "ASSUMED and NOT SOURCED. A relationship-shaped search for maximal and minimal urinary concentrating ability in healthy humans returned lithium cohorts, hydration indices and clinical case series - none characterising the limit in a healthy population in a form this model can consume. 50 mOsm/kg is the conventional figure and it is recorded as an assumption rather than dressed in a textbook citation nobody opened. IT SETS THE MAXIMAL DIURESIS: with the assumed solute load it gives 12.0 L/day, which is plausible but unverified. Source this before using the component near the dilute end. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :BF_BODY_MASS_P05_FEMALE => Provenance("BF.BODY_MASS.P05", "kg", 50.7, "A", "reported", "Fryar CD, Gu Q, Afful J, Carroll MD, Ogden CL. Anthropometric Reference Data for Children and Adults: United States, August 2021-August 2023. Vital and Health Statistics Series 3, No. 50. Atlanta (GA): National Center for Health Statistics; 2025 Jun. doi:10.15620/cdc/174595. Table 3, weight in kilograms for adults age 20 and older, by sex. Read from the NCBI Bookshelf reproduction of Table 3.", "NHANES Table 3 percentile, adults 20 and older, same source and extraction as BF.BODY_MASS.TYPICAL. Entered as a PERCENTILE, NOT converted to a standard deviation, and that is deliberate. The source reports a standard error of the MEAN and a percentile distribution; it does not report a population SD. Two standard estimators disagree materially because body weight is right-skewed - (P95-P05)/3.29 gives 21.6 kg for males while IQR/1.349 gives 18.7 kg, and the male mean 90.3 sits above the median 86.6 - so picking one AFTER seeing the numbers is exactly the unfalsifiable choice validation/pooling.md exists to prevent, and the pre-registration did not declare an SD estimator. NO SD IS ENTERED. The ensemble samples Sobol between these bounds, which is what sample_population actually does with its argument anyway - it only ever took the 1st and 99th percentiles of the distribution it was handed and sampled uniformly between them. KNOWN LIMITATION, now explicit rather than disguised by a Normal: the sampled population is UNIFORM over this range, not weight-distributed, so it over-represents the tails. Fixing that needs a declared distribution family and an SD estimator chosen in advance, which is its own pre-registration."),
