    :BF_BODY_MASS_P95_MALE => Provenance("BF.BODY_MASS.P95", "kg", 131.8, "A", "reported", "Fryar CD, Gu Q, Afful J, Carroll MD, Ogden CL. Anthropometric Reference Data for Children and Adults: United States, August 2021-August 2023. Vital and Health Statistics Series 3, No. 50. Atlanta (GA): National Center for Health Statistics; 2025 Jun. doi:10.15620/cdc/174595. Table 3, weight in kilograms for adults age 20 and older, by sex. Read from the NCBI Bookshelf reproduction of Table 3.", "NHANES Table 3 percentile, adults 20 and older, same source and extraction as BF.BODY_MASS.TYPICAL. Entered as a PERCENTILE, NOT converted to a standard deviation, and that is deliberate. The source reports a standard error of the MEAN and a percentile distribution; it does not report a population SD. Two standard estimators disagree materially because body weight is right-skewed - (P95-P05)/3.29 gives 21.6 kg for males while IQR/1.349 gives 18.7 kg, and the male mean 90.3 sits above the median 86.6 - so picking one AFTER seeing the numbers is exactly the unfalsifiable choice validation/pooling.md exists to prevent, and the pre-registration did not declare an SD estimator. NO SD IS ENTERED. The ensemble samples Sobol between these bounds, which is what sample_population actually does with its argument anyway - it only ever took the 1st and 99th percentiles of the distribution it was handed and sampled uniformly between them. KNOWN LIMITATION, now explicit rather than disguised by a Normal: the sampled population is UNIFORM over this range, not weight-distributed, so it over-represents the tails. Fixing that needs a declared distribution family and an SD estimator chosen in advance, which is its own pre-registration."),
    :BF_BODY_MASS_REFERENCE => Provenance("BF.BODY_MASS.REFERENCE", "kg", 70.0, "C", "assumed", "", "THE ROW HANDOVER SECTION 9 SAYS IS MISSING: 'body_mass is not a ledger row and is the largest un-modelled dimorphism'. It was a hard-coded 70.0 default on three function signatures. It is now one number in one place and the model scales to it. ASSUMED, NOT SOURCED, AND DELIBERATELY SO. 70 kg is the conventional reference adult and it is entered here as the value the code already used, so that promoting it to the ledger changes NOTHING - size_factor returns exactly 1.0 and every existing result is bit-identical. Dressing it in a citation at the same time as changing the model structure would have made two changes at once and left neither testable. THE SEXED PAIR IS THE NEXT STEP AND THE SOURCE IS ALREADY WHITELISTED. SOURCES.md admits ICRP Publication 89 (reference anatomical and physiological values) as tier A, and it carries reference adult masses by sex. That extraction is NOT done here and the numbers are NOT guessed at: per directive 1.5 a citation nobody opened is worse than an honest assumption. When it lands this row becomes a male/female pair under ADR 0014 and, because every extensive quantity in the model now scales through it, it will move the whole model for both sexes at once - which is exactly what the handover means by calling body mass the largest un-modelled dimorphism. WHAT SCALES THROUGH IT: see src/scaling.jl. Extensive quantities scale linearly, intensive ones do not. Linear-in-mass is exact for the volumes, which are already entered as mass fractions, and is an APPROXIMATION for GFR and cardiac output, which are conventionally normalised to body surface area and therefore grow sub-linearly with mass. Linear scaling OVERSTATES their population spread. Fixing that needs height, which this model does not carry. Recorded as debt rather than approximated with an unsourced allometric exponent. CLARIFIED 2026-08-27 WHEN THE NHANES EXTRACTION LANDED. This row is the mass at which this ledger's EXTENSIVE CONSTANTS ARE STATED - GFR 180 L/day, CO 7200 mL/min, blood volume 5.0 L are textbook values for a roughly 70 kg, 1.73 m2 adult - so it is a normalisation convention belonging to those sources, NOT a claim about how much people weigh. It stays `both` and stays 70.0. The claim about people is BF.BODY_MASS.TYPICAL, which IS a male/female pair (90.3 / 77.9 kg, NHANES 2021-2023). Moving THIS row to a population mean would rescale every extensive constant against a denominator its own sources never used, turning GFR into 232 L/day by arithmetic rather than by measurement. The two rows are different quantities and ADR 0014 applies to each separately. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :BF_BODY_MASS_TYPICAL_FEMALE => Provenance("BF.BODY_MASS.TYPICAL", "kg", 77.9, "A", "reported", "Fryar CD, Gu Q, Afful J, Carroll MD, Ogden CL. Anthropometric Reference Data for Children and Adults: United States, August 2021-August 2023. Vital and Health Statistics Series 3, No. 50. Atlanta (GA): National Center for Health Statistics; 2025 Jun. doi:10.15620/cdc/174595. Table 3, weight in kilograms for adults age 20 and older, by sex. Read from the NCBI Bookshelf reproduction of Table 3.", "NHANES TABLE 3, ADULTS AGE 20 AND OLDER. Male mean 90.3 kg (SEM 0.9, n = 2,688); female mean 77.9 kg (SEM 0.8, n = 3,254). Sample sizes unweighted, estimates weighted to the US population. Pregnant females excluded. Pre-registered in validation/body_mass_prereg.md, committed at 3fd859b before the source was opened. pooling_rule = single-source, k = 1. THIS IS NOT THE SAME QUANTITY AS BF.BODY_MASS.REFERENCE AND MUST NOT BE MERGED WITH IT. The reference row is the mass at which this ledger's EXTENSIVE CONSTANTS are stated - GFR 180 L/day, cardiac output 7200 mL/min, blood volume 5.0 L are all textbook values for a roughly 70 kg, 1.73 m2 adult. It is a normalisation convention belonging to those sources, not a claim about how much people weigh. THIS row is a claim about how much people weigh. Setting the reference to 90.3 would scale GFR to 232 L/day by construction, which is not a measurement of anything - it would restate the same physiology against a different denominator and silently rescale every constant whose source assumed 70 kg. The prereg said the sexed pair 'must move the model'. It moves the POPULATION, which is where body mass actually lives; the single-individual default stays at the reference so that no existing result moves for a reason unrelated to this extraction. WHAT THIS EXPOSES, AND IT IS NOT SMALL: a typical US adult male at 90.3 kg is 1.29x the reference mass, and scaling.jl already records that linear-in-mass scaling OVERSTATES the spread of GFR and cardiac output, which are properly normalised to body surface area. Applying linear scaling out to 1.29x is using it at the edge of where it is defensible. The US adult population this describes has a mean BMI in the obese range, so the model's typical individual is now heavier than the lean reference adults in whom GFR0 and CO0 were characterised. That is a real limitation of using a population survey as a physiological reference and it is recorded rather than hidden."),
    :BF_BODY_MASS_TYPICAL_MALE => Provenance("BF.BODY_MASS.TYPICAL", "kg", 90.3, "A", "reported", "Fryar CD, Gu Q, Afful J, Carroll MD, Ogden CL. Anthropometric Reference Data for Children and Adults: United States, August 2021-August 2023. Vital and Health Statistics Series 3, No. 50. Atlanta (GA): National Center for Health Statistics; 2025 Jun. doi:10.15620/cdc/174595. Table 3, weight in kilograms for adults age 20 and older, by sex. Read from the NCBI Bookshelf reproduction of Table 3.", "NHANES TABLE 3, ADULTS AGE 20 AND OLDER. Male mean 90.3 kg (SEM 0.9, n = 2,688); female mean 77.9 kg (SEM 0.8, n = 3,254). Sample sizes unweighted, estimates weighted to the US population. Pregnant females excluded. Pre-registered in validation/body_mass_prereg.md, committed at 3fd859b before the source was opened. pooling_rule = single-source, k = 1. THIS IS NOT THE SAME QUANTITY AS BF.BODY_MASS.REFERENCE AND MUST NOT BE MERGED WITH IT. The reference row is the mass at which this ledger's EXTENSIVE CONSTANTS are stated - GFR 180 L/day, cardiac output 7200 mL/min, blood volume 5.0 L are all textbook values for a roughly 70 kg, 1.73 m2 adult. It is a normalisation convention belonging to those sources, not a claim about how much people weigh. THIS row is a claim about how much people weigh. Setting the reference to 90.3 would scale GFR to 232 L/day by construction, which is not a measurement of anything - it would restate the same physiology against a different denominator and silently rescale every constant whose source assumed 70 kg. The prereg said the sexed pair 'must move the model'. It moves the POPULATION, which is where body mass actually lives; the single-individual default stays at the reference so that no existing result moves for a reason unrelated to this extraction. WHAT THIS EXPOSES, AND IT IS NOT SMALL: a typical US adult male at 90.3 kg is 1.29x the reference mass, and scaling.jl already records that linear-in-mass scaling OVERSTATES the spread of GFR and cardiac output, which are properly normalised to body surface area. Applying linear scaling out to 1.29x is using it at the edge of where it is defensible. The US adult population this describes has a mean BMI in the obese range, so the model's typical individual is now heavier than the lean reference adults in whom GFR0 and CO0 were characterised. That is a real limitation of using a population survey as a physiological reference and it is recorded rather than hidden."),
    :BF_BSA_REFERENCE_FEMALE => Provenance("BF.BSA.REFERENCE", "m2", 1.751, "A", "derived", "DERIVED at BF.BODY_MASS.REFERENCE and BF.HEIGHT.REFERENCE by the Du Bois formula. Du Bois D, Du Bois EF. A formula to estimate the approximate surface area if height and weight be known. Arch Intern Med 1916;17:863-71 - NOT OPENED. The formula is taken from a source that prints it: Kobayashi T, et al. Achieving clinically optimal balance between accuracy and simplicity of a formula for manual use. PLoS One 2023;18(1):e0280569. PMC9858735. OPEN ACCESS, READ IN FULL: 'BSA[m2] = BW[kg]^0.425 x BH[cm]^0.725 x 0.007184'. Directive 1.5: the citation is what was read, with the origin named.", "0.007184 * 70^0.425 * 162.11^0.725 = 1.7484 m2, at the SAME 70 kg reference mass as the male row - the sex difference here is height alone, because BF.BODY_MASS.REFERENCE is a single `both` row. Real women are lighter as well as shorter, so this pair understates the difference in surface area. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13. || RE-DERIVED 2026-09-16 AT THE RE-PAIRED HEIGHT, deindexing_prereg.md Stage 1. The formula and its citation are unchanged; only the height it is evaluated at moved. || AND THIS ROW STOPPED BEING UNCONNECTED ON THE SAME DAY. Nothing in this model read it - verified across every Julia and Python file outside the generated LedgerParams.jl - which is the failure ADR 0006 records for Circadian and what directive 1.11 guards. It was sourced on 2026-09-05 and did no work for eleven days."),
    :BF_BSA_REFERENCE_MALE => Provenance("BF.BSA.REFERENCE", "m2", 1.824, "A", "derived", "DERIVED at BF.BODY_MASS.REFERENCE and BF.HEIGHT.REFERENCE by the Du Bois formula. Du Bois D, Du Bois EF. A formula to estimate the approximate surface area if height and weight be known. Arch Intern Med 1916;17:863-71 - NOT OPENED. The formula is taken from a source that prints it: Kobayashi T, et al. Achieving clinically optimal balance between accuracy and simplicity of a formula for manual use. PLoS One 2023;18(1):e0280569. PMC9858735. OPEN ACCESS, READ IN FULL: 'BSA[m2] = BW[kg]^0.425 x BH[cm]^0.725 x 0.007184'. Directive 1.5: the citation is what was read, with the origin named. || HOW GOOD THE CLASSIC FORMULAS ARE, against DIRECTLY MEASURED surface area: Rybarczyk-Kapuscik A, et al. An improvement of body surface area formulas using the 3D scanning technique. Int J Occup Med Environ Health 2024;37(2):205-19. PMC11142402. OPEN ACCESS, READ IN FULL. 179 adults 3D-scanned. The best-performing established formula carries a mean absolute error of 0.0249 m2, about 1.3 percent, and the established formulas are 'well-suited for estimating BSA values among individuals with a normal physique' while their accuracy 'diminishes when applied to BMI ranges' outside it. That paper's conclusion is that no single universal formula is possible, which is why this row records the alternatives rather than claiming one is correct.", "0.007184 * 70^0.425 * 175.83^0.725 = 1.8545 m2. || IT IS NOT 1.73, AND THAT IS WORTH SAYING BECAUSE 1.73 IS EVERYWHERE. The renal indexing convention of 1.73 m2 is a 1928 figure for an average adult of that era, not a measurement of anyone here; directive 1.12 lists it among the round numbers to distrust. The model's reference adult has 7 percent more surface than the convention, so any quantity de-indexed from a per-1.73-m2 figure must be multiplied by 1.8545/1.73 and not taken as-is. || WHY THE DU BOIS FORMULA AND NOT A BETTER ONE. body_size_scaling_prereg.md section 3 fixed the rule before the search: where formulas are comparable, take the one the INDEXED LITERATURE used, because the point of this row is to make that literature usable. The 1.73 m2 renal convention and most cardiac indexing descend from Du Bois. Newer formulas fit direct measurements slightly better and are recorded on BF.SIZE.EXPONENT. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13. || RE-DERIVED 2026-09-16 AT THE RE-PAIRED HEIGHT, deindexing_prereg.md Stage 1. The formula and its citation are unchanged; only the height it is evaluated at moved. || AND THIS ROW STOPPED BEING UNCONNECTED ON THE SAME DAY. Nothing in this model read it - verified across every Julia and Python file outside the generated LedgerParams.jl - which is the failure ADR 0006 records for Circadian and what directive 1.11 guards. It was sourced on 2026-09-05 and did no work for eleven days."),
