    :BF_HEIGHT_REFERENCE_FEMALE => Provenance("BF.HEIGHT.REFERENCE", "cm", 162.4, "A", "reported", "National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey 2007-2008, 2009-2010 and 2011-2012, public microdata, file BMX (body measures), variables BMXHT and BMXWT - MEASURED, not self-reported. EXTRACTED HERE: validation/body_size_scaling_extract.py, pre-registered in validation/body_size_scaling_prereg.md before any number was computed. n = 9300 adults aged 20+, not pregnant, weighted with WTMEC2YR/3 for three pooled cycles: men n = 4605, height 175.83 cm (SD 7.60), weight 88.40 kg; women n = 4695, height 162.11 cm (SD 7.08), weight 75.24 kg.", "See the male row. Women n = 4695, mean weight 75.24 kg. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13. || RE-PAIRED TO THE REFERENCE MASS 2026-09-16, deindexing_prereg.md Stage 1, AND IT NEEDED NO NEW SOURCE. This row was the mean height of ALL adults, who average 88.4 kg in men, while the model's reference individual weighs 70. Its own note already called that a known inconsistency that 'matters by a few per cent for de-indexing' - and the correction the de-indexing pass exists to make is 7 percent, in the same direction. Using a mispaired body surface area to fix an indexing error is failure mode 9. || THE QUANTITY IS NOW THE SURVEY-WEIGHTED MEAN HEIGHT OF ADULTS AT THE REFERENCE MASS - the same NHANES 2007-2012 BMX microdata already in validation/data/nhanes and already read by body_size_scaling_extract.py, restricted to 70 +/- 2 kg. n = 366 men and 445 women unweighted, weighted mean weight 70.1 and 70.0 kg. || THE BAND IS A TIE-BREAK AND NOT A DECISION: across +/-1, +/-2, +/-3 and +/-5 kg the resulting BSA spans 0.17 percent in men and 0.22 in women. || THE ASYMMETRY IS THE POINT. NHANES women average 75.2 kg, so the all-adult mean was already nearly right for a 70 kg woman - 162.1 against 162.4, branch B2. NHANES men average 88.4 kg, so it described a man 4 cm taller than a 70 kg man really is: 175.8 against 171.8, BMI 22.6 against a measured 23.7. Branch B1. || BF.BODY_MASS.REFERENCE IS STILL 70 kg AND STILL `assumed`, and the model's reference individual is still lighter than the population it is scaled against. That debt is untouched here and deindexing_prereg.md section 4 forbids moving it in this pass."),
    :BF_HEIGHT_REFERENCE_MALE => Provenance("BF.HEIGHT.REFERENCE", "cm", 171.8, "A", "reported", "National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey 2007-2008, 2009-2010 and 2011-2012, public microdata, file BMX (body measures), variables BMXHT and BMXWT - MEASURED, not self-reported. EXTRACTED HERE: validation/body_size_scaling_extract.py, pre-registered in validation/body_size_scaling_prereg.md before any number was computed. n = 9300 adults aged 20+, not pregnant, weighted with WTMEC2YR/3 for three pooled cycles: men n = 4605, height 175.83 cm (SD 7.60), weight 88.40 kg; women n = 4695, height 162.11 cm (SD 7.08), weight 75.24 kg.", "THE FIRST HEIGHT IN THIS MODEL, and it exists so that body surface area does. HANDOVER section 4 item 10 recorded the absence as the blocker for sub-linear scaling and for using the large body of INDEXED cardiovascular and renal literature - Luu 2022 (n = 3,206) and Zhan 2024 (n = 12,812) were both rejected for reporting indexed volumes only. || IT IS NOT PAIRED WITH THE MODEL'S REFERENCE MASS AND THAT IS A KNOWN INCONSISTENCY. This is the mean height of adults averaging 88.4 kg (men); the model's reference individual is 70 kg, and shorter people weigh less, so the mean height of 70 kg adults is lower than this. It does not matter for model BEHAVIOUR - size_factor is 1 at the reference mass for any exponent - and it matters by a few per cent for de-indexing. Recorded rather than adjusted, because adjusting it would mean inventing a height-for-weight relation this row does not source. || BF.BODY_MASS.REFERENCE IS 70 kg AND IS `assumed`. NHANES says the US adult mean is 88.4 kg for men and 75.2 for women. The model's reference individual is lighter than the population it is scaled against, which is a separate debt. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13. || RE-PAIRED TO THE REFERENCE MASS 2026-09-16, deindexing_prereg.md Stage 1, AND IT NEEDED NO NEW SOURCE. This row was the mean height of ALL adults, who average 88.4 kg in men, while the model's reference individual weighs 70. Its own note already called that a known inconsistency that 'matters by a few per cent for de-indexing' - and the correction the de-indexing pass exists to make is 7 percent, in the same direction. Using a mispaired body surface area to fix an indexing error is failure mode 9. || THE QUANTITY IS NOW THE SURVEY-WEIGHTED MEAN HEIGHT OF ADULTS AT THE REFERENCE MASS - the same NHANES 2007-2012 BMX microdata already in validation/data/nhanes and already read by body_size_scaling_extract.py, restricted to 70 +/- 2 kg. n = 366 men and 445 women unweighted, weighted mean weight 70.1 and 70.0 kg. || THE BAND IS A TIE-BREAK AND NOT A DECISION: across +/-1, +/-2, +/-3 and +/-5 kg the resulting BSA spans 0.17 percent in men and 0.22 in women. || THE ASYMMETRY IS THE POINT. NHANES women average 75.2 kg, so the all-adult mean was already nearly right for a 70 kg woman - 162.1 against 162.4, branch B2. NHANES men average 88.4 kg, so it described a man 4 cm taller than a 70 kg man really is: 175.8 against 171.8, BMI 22.6 against a measured 23.7. Branch B1. || BF.BODY_MASS.REFERENCE IS STILL 70 kg AND STILL `assumed`, and the model's reference individual is still lighter than the population it is scaled against. That debt is untouched here and deindexing_prereg.md section 4 forbids moving it in this pass."),
    :BF_ICF_MASS_FRACTION => Provenance("BF.ICF.MASS_FRACTION", "unitless", 0.344, "A", "reported", "Zhang N et al. PMC6751809.", "34.4 +/- 4.0 percent of body weight, same cohort and method as BF.TBW.MASS_FRACTION. Consistency check: ICF + ECF = 55.2 which matches TBW as reported."),
    :BF_ICF_ECF_OSMOTIC_TAU => Provenance("BF.ICF_ECF.OSMOTIC_TAU", "min", 30.0, "B", "assumed", "Convention pending primary source.", "ASSUMED. Fast relative to every integrative timescale of interest. Per ADR 0002 this sits well SLOWER than baroreflex so it is not a candidate for the fast block; per ADR 0003 it is a Conservation-class coupling and must not be partitioned across. || THE CHECK THIS NOTE DEMANDED HAS NOW BEEN RUN, AND IT CAME BACK HALF WRONG. The note used to end: 'Sensitivity to this value should be near zero on multi-day runs - verify that in testing, and if it is not, the compartment structure is wrong.' Nobody verified it for the life of the project. Run on 2026-09-02 with validation/challenges.jl section 5. ON MULTI-DAY RUNS IT IS INDEED NEAR ZERO, so the compartment structure is not indicted. ON ACUTE PROTOCOLS IT IS THE DOMINANT TERM: a 1.4 L oral water load over 30 min moves the PEAK plasma osmolality excursion from 8.8 mOsm/kg at tau = 1 min to 12.9 at the assumed 30 and 17.6 at 120, and the intracellular share of the load falls 0.74 -> 0.46 -> 0.19 L across the same range. A FACTOR OF TWO IN AN UNSOURCED CONSTANT, on exactly the protocol class the ADH component exists to reproduce. || AT tau = 1 min THE MODEL REPRODUCES THE PURE-DILUTION BOUND (8.81 against 8.8 computed from water retained over total body water), which is the physically correct limit. At 30 min it EXCEEDS that bound by 47%, because the intracellular compartment has not taken its share by the end of a 30-minute load. That is a bound, not a source. || A SOURCING PASS WAS RUN ON 2026-09-02 AND FOUND NOTHING USABLE. 10 queries over two sweeps: osmotic equilibration time course in humans, hypertonic infusion kinetics, transcellular water shift kinetics, cell volume regulation half-times, aquaporin water permeability, and the volume-kinetics literature. The volume-kinetics work (Hahn and colleagues) models plasma and interstitial spaces rather than the ICF-ECF osmotic exchange this parameter governs, so it is not the same quantity and may not be converted. Erythrocyte water permeability is measured but a single cell type does not give a whole-body constant. THE ROW STAYS `assumed` AND STAYS HONEST - what changed is that it is now a MEASURED, BOUNDED, SEARCHED debt instead of an unexamined one. || CONSEQUENCE, AND IT BINDS: no acute osmotic result from this model may be reported as a magnitude until this is sourced. Directions and multi-day steady states are unaffected. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :BF_NA_INTAKE_LOW => Provenance("BF.NA.INTAKE_LOW", "mEq/day", 103.0, "A", "derived", "Rakova N et al. Cell Metab 2013;17(1):125-131.", "DERIVED from 6 g/day NaCl protocol level. 6 / 58.44 = 103 mmol. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :BF_NA_INTAKE_MID => Provenance("BF.NA.INTAKE_MID", "mEq/day", 154.0, "A", "derived", "Rakova N et al. Cell Metab 2013;17(1):125-131.", "DERIVED from 9 g/day NaCl protocol level. 9 / 58.44 = 154 mmol. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :BF_NA_INTAKE_NOMINAL => Provenance("BF.NA.INTAKE_NOMINAL", "mEq/day", 205.0, "A", "derived", "Rakova N et al. Cell Metab 2013;17(1):125-131.", "DERIVED from the Mars500 protocol salt level of 12 g/day NaCl. 12 g NaCl / 58.44 g/mol = 205 mmol Na. Protocol levels were 12, 9 and 6 g/day held for 30-60 days each. Use these three as the validation step inputs, not a free parameter. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :BF_NA_OSMOTICALLY_INACTIVE_FRACTION => Provenance("BF.NA.OSMOTICALLY_INACTIVE_FRACTION", "unitless", 0.15, "B", "assumed", "Rakova N et al. Long-term space flight simulation reveals infradian rhythmicity in human Na+ balance. Cell Metab 2013;17(1):125-131.", "ASSUMED PLACEHOLDER. Rakova et al establish that total-body Na+ is stored and is NOT a simple function of salt intake, and that total-body Na+ and extracellular water are not tightly coupled. They do not report a single storage fraction. This value is a placeholder to make the third compartment functional and MUST be replaced by estimation against the Mars500 balance series. See ADR 0004. || OPENED THE SOURCES AND ENTERED NOTHING, AND THE REASON IS THAT NONE OF THEM MEASURES THIS QUANTITY. 2026-09-17, HANDOVER section 3.51. THREE DIFFERENT THINGS: this row is a STEADY-STATE RATIO, Na_store over Na_ecf. Olde Engberink 2017 (PMID 28132715, read in full) gives the fraction of cations ACUTELY CLEARED FROM BODY WATER that is not retrieved in urine over 4 h - 47 to 55 percent, a buffering fraction of a LOAD. Rakova 2013 (Cell Metab 17(1):125-131, Open Archive, read) gives the SWING AMPLITUDE of total-body sodium at fixed intake, plus or minus 200-400 mmol, without parallel changes in body weight or extracellular water. A buffering fraction, a swing amplitude and a steady-state ratio are not interchangeable, and entering any of them here is what section 3.1 of the pre-registration was written to prevent - it anticipated the 23Na MRI version of that trap and this is a different one. || AND SECTION 3.2's CAVEAT APPLIES AT FULL STRENGTH. Olde Engberink's quantity is INFERRED from a mismatch between plasma sodium change and urinary excretion through the Adrogue-Madias and Nguyen-Kurtz formulas; their own stated limitation is that they 'did not directly measure the amount of nonosmotic Na+ stored in the tissues'. Their subjects were SALT-DEPLETED, baseline fractional sodium excretion 0.08 percent, and the paper itself says the capacity may be large BECAUSE of that. || STAGE 1's DIAGNOSTIC OF ABOUT 0.40 IS NOT ENTERED AND MUST NOT BE. It is what the model needs to reproduce Drummer's half-life ratio, which is precisely what makes it inadmissible as a value. THE ROW STAYS ASSUMED."),
    :BF_NA_PLASMA_SETPOINT => Provenance("BF.NA.PLASMA_SETPOINT", "mEq/L", 140.0, "B", "reported", "Standard clinical reference interval.", "VERIFY - clinical reference range, needs a citable primary source. Widely reproduced but traced here only to convention. || PRECISION CORRECTED 2026-09-09 under directive 1.13, second pass. Trailing zero read as a fourth figure against a reference interval of 135-145. The first pass rounded this row but stopped one figure short, because the gate then allowed two figures beyond what the interval supports - a tolerance I had given myself rather than the rule. The gate now enforces the rule exactly. Discarded digits were inside the row's own stated interval, so no claim changes."),
    :BF_NA_STORAGE_TAU => Provenance("BF.NA.STORAGE_TAU", "day", 0.1, "B", "assumed", "Olde Engberink RHG, Rorije NMG, van den Born BJH, Vogt L. Quantification of nonosmotic sodium storage capacity following acute hypertonic saline infusion in healthy individuals. Kidney Int 2017;91(3):738-745. doi:10.1016/j.kint.2016.12.004. PMID 28132715. READ IN FULL. 12 healthy normotensive non-smoking men on a low-sodium diet, 2.4 percent NaCl over 30 min dosed to 5 mmol Na per L total body water, 4 h follow-up. Osmotic inactivation is measurable at 2 h and larger at 4 h: 92 +/- 26 mmol of sodium or potassium should have been excreted and 51 mmol was, so only 47 and 55 percent of expected sodium and potassium excretion were retrieved in urine.", "ORDER OF MAGNITUDE, NOT A MEASUREMENT, AND ONE SIGNIFICANT FIGURE FOR THAT REASON. 2026-09-17, HANDOVER section 3.52, validation/sodium_store_structure_prereg.md branch X1. A first-order store with tau = 0.1 d is 57 percent loaded at 2 h and 81 percent at 4 h, which is what Olde Engberink observes; the 0.05-0.2 d range is the span consistent with that observation window and nothing narrower is supportable. Directive 1.9. || WHAT IT REPLACES AND WHY THE OLD VALUE WAS WRONG IN KIND. The previous 7 d was taken from Rakova's circaseptan rhythm - and that paper reports 'peaks at about 6 DAYS period length', not 7. But the rounding was the small error: A RHYTHM PERIOD IS NOT A FIRST-ORDER RELAXATION TIME CONSTANT. An oscillation against a relaxation; no arithmetic converts one into the other. The row's own previous note admitted the value was 'chosen to match the reported weekly infradian rhythm period rather than derived from it'. Failure mode 11. || THE SCOPE DECISION THIS VALUE BELONGS TO. ADR 0004's single compartment is now explicitly scoped to the ACUTE process. Three timescales are in evidence - 2-4 h, about 6 days, monthly and longer - and one first-order compartment carries one. THE CIRCASEPTAN AND MONTHLY RHYTHMS ARE OUT OF SCOPE FOR THIS MODEL, declared rather than forgotten: it has no machinery to generate an infradian rhythm and no protocol longer than the 30-day salt step in which one would show. || IT OVERLAPS THE MODEL'S OWN REQUIREMENT AND THAT IS DECLARED. Stage 1's diagnostic wanted 0.05-0.25 d to reproduce Drummer's half-life ratio. This row is sourced from a DIFFERENT study, a different manoeuvre and a different tonicity, and 0.1 d is NOT the best-performing value in that sweep - 0.25 d was. Read the overlap as two independent routes agreeing that the process takes HOURS, which is the claim, and not as confirmation of either. || CAVEATS THAT STAY ON THE ROW. The source's quantity is INFERRED from a mismatch between plasma sodium change and urinary excretion through the Adrogue-Madias and Nguyen-Kurtz formulas, and its own limitation is that it 'did not directly measure the amount of nonosmotic Na+ stored in the tissues'. Subjects were SALT-DEPLETED - baseline fractional sodium excretion 0.08 percent - and the paper says the capacity may be large BECAUSE of that. HYPERTONIC, not isotonic. n = 12 men. Tier B and it does not improve. || NOTHING READS THIS ROW IN THE DEFAULT BUILD. storage defaults to false and ADR 0004 stays PROVISIONAL."),
    :BF_OSM_PLASMA_SETPOINT => Provenance("BF.OSM.PLASMA_SETPOINT", "mOsm/kg", 287.0, "B", "reported", "Standard clinical reference interval.", "VERIFY - as above. Needed to close the osmotic equilibration between ICF and ECF. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :BF_SIZE_EXPONENT => Provenance("BF.SIZE.EXPONENT", "dimensionless", 0.5083, "A", "derived", "DERIVED by weighted regression of ln(BSA) on ln(mass) over the NHANES adults below, with BSA from the Du Bois formula. National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey 2007-2008, 2009-2010 and 2011-2012, public microdata, file BMX (body measures), variables BMXHT and BMXWT - MEASURED, not self-reported. EXTRACTED HERE: validation/body_size_scaling_extract.py, pre-registered in validation/body_size_scaling_prereg.md before any number was computed. n = 9300 adults aged 20+, not pregnant, weighted with WTMEC2YR/3 for three pooled cycles: men n = 4605, height 175.83 cm (SD 7.60), weight 88.40 kg; women n = 4695, height 162.11 cm (SD 7.08), weight 75.24 kg. || Du Bois D, Du Bois EF. A formula to estimate the approximate surface area if height and weight be known. Arch Intern Med 1916;17:863-71 - NOT OPENED. The formula is taken from a source that prints it: Kobayashi T, et al. Achieving clinically optimal balance between accuracy and simplicity of a formula for manual use. PLoS One 2023;18(1):e0280569. PMC9858735. OPEN ACCESS, READ IN FULL: 'BSA[m2] = BW[kg]^0.425 x BH[cm]^0.725 x 0.007184'. Directive 1.5: the citation is what was read, with the origin named. || HOW GOOD THE CLASSIC FORMULAS ARE, against DIRECTLY MEASURED surface area: Rybarczyk-Kapuscik A, et al. An improvement of body surface area formulas using the 3D scanning technique. Int J Occup Med Environ Health 2024;37(2):205-19. PMC11142402. OPEN ACCESS, READ IN FULL. 179 adults 3D-scanned. The best-performing established formula carries a mean absolute error of 0.0249 m2, about 1.3 percent, and the established formulas are 'well-suited for estimating BSA values among individuals with a normal physique' while their accuracy 'diminishes when applied to BMI ranges' outside it. That paper's conclusion is that no single universal formula is possible, which is why this row records the alternatives rather than claiming one is correct.", "THE NUMBER THAT CHANGES BEHAVIOUR. src/scaling.jl scaled every extensive quantity LINEARLY in mass and said so against itself: 'GFR and cardiac output are conventionally normalised to BODY SURFACE AREA, which grows sub-linearly with mass, so linear scaling OVERSTATES their spread across a population.' This is that exponent. At 95 kg the size factor falls from 1.357 to 1.168. || IT IS A POPULATION REGRESSION, NOT A GEOMETRIC LAW, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE POINT. The geometric surface law gives 2/3 and Kleiber's metabolic law 3/4; this is 0.51, and it is lower because in a real adult population mass varies more from ADIPOSITY than from frame size, and fat adds mass with little height and little surface. That is the right exponent for the question this model asks - how does a heavier PERSON differ from a lighter one - and it is the wrong one if body_mass is ever reinterpreted as frame size, where 2/3 would apply. Stated here because the two readings differ by 25 percent at 95 kg. || THE RANGE IS THE FORMULA CHOICE, NOT A CONFIDENCE INTERVAL: Du Bois 0.5083, Mosteller 0.5574, Gehan-George 0.5631, Haycock 0.5836 over the same 9300 adults. The regression's own standard error is far smaller. || ONE EXPONENT FOR EVERY SURFACE-LIKE QUANTITY IS A LUMPING, fixed in the pre-registration before the number was known: glomerular filtration tracks surface area, metabolic rate tracks nearer mass^0.75, and dietary intake tracks metabolic rate. Giving them different exponents would break the sodium and water balances, which require intake and clearance to scale together. THE CONSEQUENCE IS THAT THIS MODEL CANNOT REPRESENT THE REAL SMALL RISE IN ARTERIAL PRESSURE WITH BODY SIZE, and the body-size testset asserts pressure invariance as a property of the closure rather than as physiology. || VOLUMES ARE STILL LINEAR IN MASS and that is now the weaker half: extracellular volume is entered as a mass FRACTION, but fat carries less water than lean tissue, so the model also overstates the fluid volumes of heavy people. Not fixed here - one change at a time - and it needs a body-composition row this model does not have."),
    :BF_TBW_MASS_FRACTION => Provenance("BF.TBW.MASS_FRACTION", "unitless", 0.552, "A", "reported", "Zhang N et al. Association between the content of intracellular and extracellular fluid and the amount of water intake among Chinese college students. PMC6751809.", "Reported as 55.2 +/- 6.2 percent of body weight by bioelectrical impedance, n=159 young adults. NOTE this is below the conventional textbook 60 percent; BIA and isotope dilution disagree systematically and the cohort is young Chinese adults. VERIFY against a second population before relying on it. Candidate cross-check: ICRP 89."),
    :BF_THIRST_OSM_THRESHOLD => Provenance("BF.THIRST.OSM_THRESHOLD", "mOsm/kg", 285.23, "A", "reported", "Hughes F, Mythen M, Montgomery H. The sensitivity of the human thirst response to changes in plasma osmolality: a systematic review. Perioper Med (Lond) 2018;7:1. doi:10.1186/s13741-017-0081-4. OPEN ACCESS, READ 2026-09-21.", "OSMOTIC THRESHOLD FOR THIRST ONSET, POOLED. Hughes 2018, a SYSTEMATIC REVIEW of 12 trials and 167 participants that had already pooled with a stated method - which is pooling.md's rule 1 and its PREFERRED outcome, so its estimate is taken and NOT re-pooled. pooling_rule = meta-analysis, k = 12. thirst_prereg.md sections 8 and 9, ADR 0030. || 285.23 +/- 1.29 mOsm/kg (95% CI). Thirst intensity on a visual analogue scale against plasma osmolality, linear regression to define the threshold, in every constituent trial. || THIS ROW REPLACED A SINGLE-SOURCE ONE AND THE REPLACEMENT CHANGED THE MODEL. It was entered on 2026-09-21 as Thompson 1986's 281 mOsm/kg (PMID 3791867, n = 10, the same hypertonic-infusion paradigm and the same group as ADH.OSM.THRESHOLD). Thompson is one of the twelve trials pooled here. Pooling moved the threshold 281 -> 285.23 and, because the derived thirst gain divides by (287 - threshold), SHRANK THAT DENOMINATOR FROM 6.00 TO 1.77 mOsm/kg. || DIRECTIVE 1.12 SCORED ON A CLAIM, AND THE REVIEW SETTLES IT WITH 12 TRIALS RATHER THAN ONE. thirst_prereg.md section 2 recorded the teaching claim that the thirst threshold sits ABOVE the vasopressin threshold so that urine concentrates before thirst begins. Hughes finds thirst 285.23 +/- 1.29 against vasopressin 284.3 +/- 0.71, overlapping confidence intervals, and concludes both 'lie in the middle of the normal range of plasma osmolality'. THE TEXTBOOK ORDERING IS NOT SUPPORTED. || IT VIOLATES TWO OF THIS PASS'S OWN DECLARED ADMISSIBILITY CRITERIA AND THE DIRECTION OF BIAS IS STATED RATHER THAN THE REVIEW DISCARDED. Section 3 excluded patient cohorts and the elderly; Hughes includes 'both healthy controls and those with a range of pathologies' (its inclusion rule being no condition directly affecting thirst) at a mean age of 41, range 20-78. Osmotic thirst is BLUNTED with age, so including older participants biases the pooled threshold UPWARD. The healthy-young threshold is therefore probably AT OR BELOW 285.23, which makes the signal larger and the derived gain smaller - the deviation is CONSERVATIVE for this model rather than flattering. || POPULATION SPREAD IS NOT PUBLISHED AND IS NOT INVENTED HERE. uncertainty_type is `ci` because 1.29 is a 95% interval ON THE MEAN, which is what the review reports. Treating it as a standard error over 167 participants would imply a between-subject SD of about 8.5 mOsm/kg, but between-study heterogeneity is folded into that interval - the review says so - so 8.5 is an UPPER BOUND on population spread and not a measurement. A population model needs the real SD; OPEN-QUESTIONS records what it would take. || SCALING: none, it is an osmolality. || IT INHERITS B19. The 1.77 mOsm/kg gap to BF.OSM.PLASMA_SETPOINT is a difference between two numbers from different cohorts that already do not compose, and thirst_prereg.md section 4 forbade fixing that here."),
    :BLOOD_HB_CONCENTRATION_FEMALE => Provenance("BLOOD.HB.CONCENTRATION", "g/dL", 13.2, "A", "reported", "Morales-Mendoza E, Suarez-Ramos MDP, Godoy-Corredor M, Gomez-Lopera N, Combariza-Vallejo JF, Murcia J, Isaza-Ruget MA. Reference intervals for hemoglobin and hematocrit adjusted for altitude, sex, and age: a big data-based study in the Colombian population. Med Sci (Basel) 2026;14(1):136. doi:10.3390/medsci14010136. PMID 41892851. PMC13027793. OPEN ACCESS, tables read. Hb results from 667,857 apparently healthy adults 2022-2024, stratified by sex, age and residential altitude; LOW-ALTITUDE stratum (<1100 m a.s.l.) taken, Table 2, median with IQR.", "Women 18-50, low-altitude stratum, n = 102,894, median 13.2 (IQR 12.6-13.7) g/dL. The male row carries the full note. || THE AGE BANDS DIFFER BETWEEN THE SEXES IN THE SOURCE - women 18-50, men 18-64 - because the paper stratifies around the menopause. The model has no age dimension so it cannot represent that, and the difference is recorded rather than smoothed over."),
    :BLOOD_HB_CONCENTRATION_MALE => Provenance("BLOOD.HB.CONCENTRATION", "g/dL", 15.3, "A", "reported", "Morales-Mendoza E, Suarez-Ramos MDP, Godoy-Corredor M, Gomez-Lopera N, Combariza-Vallejo JF, Murcia J, Isaza-Ruget MA. Reference intervals for hemoglobin and hematocrit adjusted for altitude, sex, and age: a big data-based study in the Colombian population. Med Sci (Basel) 2026;14(1):136. doi:10.3390/medsci14010136. PMID 41892851. PMC13027793. OPEN ACCESS, tables read. Hb results from 667,857 apparently healthy adults 2022-2024, stratified by sex, age and residential altitude; LOW-ALTITUDE stratum (<1100 m a.s.l.) taken, Table 2, median with IQR.", "Men 18-64, low-altitude stratum, n = 71,624, median 15.3 (IQR 14.7-15.9) g/dL. || SEXED FROM THE START, per ADR 0014 and because haemoglobin is one of the largest sexed differences in human physiology - 16 percent here. Entering it `both` would have repeated the CV.HEMATOCRIT.NOMINAL failure, where the unisex 45 percent turned out to be the male value applied to women. || SAME PAPER, SAME COHORT AND SAME STRATUM AS CV.HEMATOCRIT.NOMINAL, and that is what makes ADR 0018's fourth falsifiable test worth running: haemoglobin and haematocrit are entered from independent MEASUREMENTS rather than derived from one another, so their ratio is a check that can fail. It gives a mean corpuscular haemoglobin concentration of 33.8 g/dL of red cells in men and 33.4 in women, both inside the human 32-36. check_closure.py asserts it. || ALTITUDE MATTERS AND THE STRATUM IS DELIBERATE. The same table gives 16.7 g/dL for men above 2000 m. This model is sea level only - ADR 0017 omits the hypoxic drive and ADR 0018 takes barometric pressure as sea level - so the low-altitude stratum is the matching one. || EXTENSIVE? NO. A CONCENTRATION IS INTENSIVE. Oxygen CONTENT per dL does not change because a person is larger; the DELIVERY does, through cardiac output, which already scales."),
    :BLOOD_O2_AA_GRADIENT => Provenance("BLOOD.O2.AA_GRADIENT", "mmHg", 10.0, "C", "assumed", "NOT SOURCED. The correct source was identified and could not be opened: Crapo RO, Jensen RL, Hegewald M, Tashkin DP. Arterial blood gas reference values for sea level and an altitude of 1,400 meters. Am J Respir Crit Care Med 1999;160(5 Pt 1):1525-31. doi:10.1164/ajrccm.160.5.9806006. PMID 10556115. Healthy LIFETIME NONSMOKERS, n = 96 at sea level, ages 18-81, reporting PaO2, PaCO2 and the CALCULATED alveolar-arterial PO2 difference as reference EQUATIONS. Europe PMC reports isOpenAccess=N and no PMC record.", "ASSUMED, AND THIS IS THE ROW validation/blood_gas_prereg.md SECTION 6 FORBIDS TUNING. It is the least precisely known stage of the chain and it sits directly upstream of arterial saturation, which is the output ADR 0018's first falsifiable test judges. || IT WAS NOT TUNED, AND THAT IS DEMONSTRATED RATHER THAN ASSERTED. Swept from 5 to 25 mmHg, arterial saturation moves 97.3 to 94.4 percent and stays inside the human 95-99 across the plausible range. The prediction does not turn on this row, so it cannot be accused of having been chosen - see validation/blood_gas_extract.py section 5. || THE REASON IS THE SHAPE OF THE CURVE, and it cuts both ways: above about 80 mmHg the sigmoid is flat, so large changes in tension buy almost no change in saturation. That is what makes the prediction robust AND what makes it a weak test of the curve. A real test needs the steep part, which means hypoxia, and ADR 0017 omits the hypoxic drive so this model must not be run there. || AGE-BLIND. The difference widens substantially across adult life and this model has no age dimension, so this is a young-adult figure - the same debt ADH.URINE.OSM_MAX carries. || ONE ARTICLE WOULD DISCHARGE THREE ASSUMED ROWS across two subsystems, this one plus RESP.CO2.ARTERIAL_RESTING and the arterial PO2 it implies. It is the highest-value single acquisition in this repository. || INTENSIVE. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :BLOOD_O2_BINDING_CAPACITY => Provenance("BLOOD.O2.BINDING_CAPACITY", "mL/g", 1.391, "B", "derived", "Derived. See notes.", "Haemoglobin binds four O2 per tetramer, so the capacity is four molar volumes per molecular weight: 4 x 22414 mL/mol / 64458 g/mol = 1.3909 -> 1.391 mL/g. || THE 1.34 AGAINST 1.39 QUESTION, SETTLED IN THE OPEN BECAUSE validation/blood_gas_prereg.md SECTION 3 REQUIRED IT. Empirical whole-blood figures cluster nearer 1.34 and the gap is conventionally attributed to inactive haemoglobin species - carboxyhaemoglobin and methaemoglobin - which are non-zero even in healthy non-smokers. || THE PHYSIOLOGICAL ARGUMENT FAVOURS 1.34 AND IT IS NOT TAKEN. ADR 0018 omits inactive species, so a capacity that implicitly includes them would match the TOTAL haemoglobin a clinical analyser reports, which is exactly what BLOOD.HB.CONCENTRATION is. Using 1.391 therefore OVERSTATES arterial oxygen content by roughly 4 percent. || IT IS TAKEN ANYWAY BECAUSE 1.34 COULD NOT BE SOURCED IN AN ADMISSIBLE PREPARATION and 1.391 follows from two physical constants. A traceable number that is 4 percent high beats an untraceable one that is right: the error is declared, one-directional and known, which is a form of wrongness this ledger can work with. || IT AFFECTS CONTENT AND DELIVERY ONLY. Saturation and tension are untouched, so ADR 0018's first falsifiable test does not depend on this choice. || INTENSIVE."),
    :BLOOD_O2_CURVE_A => Provenance("BLOOD.O2.CURVE_A", "mmHg^3", 23400.0, "A", "reported", "Severinghaus JW. Simple, accurate equations for human blood O2 dissociation computations. J Appl Physiol Respir Environ Exerc Physiol 1979;46(3):599-602. doi:10.1152/jappl.1979.46.3.599. PMID 35496. ABSTRACT READ - the equation and its stated accuracy are both in the abstract, which is the part used. The paper fits the STANDARD HUMAN BLOOD O2 dissociation curve to within +/- 0.0055 fractional saturation over 0 < S < 1.", "ONE OF TWO COEFFICIENTS OF A SINGLE PUBLISHED FIT. S = 1/(A/(PO2^3 + B*PO2) + 1). THEY ARE NOT SEPARATELY MEASURABLE AND MUST MOVE TOGETHER OR NOT AT ALL - neither has a physical meaning on its own, and changing one without the other produces a curve nobody published. || BRANCH B3 OF validation/blood_gas_prereg.md, AND THE PRE-REGISTRATION PREFERRED OTHERWISE. Section 4 preferred the Hill form because its two parameters - P50 and the exponent - are separately measurable and would get separate rows with separate provenance. THE DECOMPOSITION FAILED: searching for a measured P50 and Hill exponent in healthy humans returns chronic obstructive lung disease, sickle cell disease, chronic hypoxaemia, sleep apnoea and congenital heart disease, every one excluded by section 2, several of them existing to characterise a pulse oximeter rather than the physiology (directive 1.7). So a published fit is taken whole, with its citation and its stated error, and the loss of decomposability is the recorded cost. || A PUBLISHED EQUATION FITTED TO MEASURED DATA IS NOT ANOTHER MODEL'S OUTPUT, which is the distinction validation/targets.md draws and the reason this is admissible at all. || THE IMPLIED P50 IS 26.86 Torr, SOLVED FROM THE EQUATION AND DELIBERATELY NOT A LEDGER ROW, so it cannot silently disagree with the curve it comes from. check_closure.py asserts it lands in the human range, which makes it a TEST of the adopted equation rather than a second definition of the same quantity. Severinghaus's own Bohr equation uses PO2/26.6 as its reference, so 26.86 is consistent with the paper to within 1 percent. || FIXED AT NORMAL pH AND TEMPERATURE. ADR 0018 records what that disqualifies: the Bohr effect, fever and hypothermia, 2,3-DPG, and the increased offloading in exercising muscle. pH needs bicarbonate, which is renal and absent. || INTENSIVE."),
    :BLOOD_O2_CURVE_B => Provenance("BLOOD.O2.CURVE_B", "mmHg^2", 150.0, "A", "reported", "Severinghaus JW. Simple, accurate equations for human blood O2 dissociation computations. J Appl Physiol Respir Environ Exerc Physiol 1979;46(3):599-602. doi:10.1152/jappl.1979.46.3.599. PMID 35496. ABSTRACT READ - the equation and its stated accuracy are both in the abstract, which is the part used. The paper fits the STANDARD HUMAN BLOOD O2 dissociation curve to within +/- 0.0055 fractional saturation over 0 < S < 1.", "ONE OF TWO COEFFICIENTS OF A SINGLE PUBLISHED FIT. S = 1/(A/(PO2^3 + B*PO2) + 1). THEY ARE NOT SEPARATELY MEASURABLE AND MUST MOVE TOGETHER OR NOT AT ALL - neither has a physical meaning on its own, and changing one without the other produces a curve nobody published. || BRANCH B3 OF validation/blood_gas_prereg.md, AND THE PRE-REGISTRATION PREFERRED OTHERWISE. Section 4 preferred the Hill form because its two parameters - P50 and the exponent - are separately measurable and would get separate rows with separate provenance. THE DECOMPOSITION FAILED: searching for a measured P50 and Hill exponent in healthy humans returns chronic obstructive lung disease, sickle cell disease, chronic hypoxaemia, sleep apnoea and congenital heart disease, every one excluded by section 2, several of them existing to characterise a pulse oximeter rather than the physiology (directive 1.7). So a published fit is taken whole, with its citation and its stated error, and the loss of decomposability is the recorded cost. || A PUBLISHED EQUATION FITTED TO MEASURED DATA IS NOT ANOTHER MODEL'S OUTPUT, which is the distinction validation/targets.md draws and the reason this is admissible at all. || THE IMPLIED P50 IS 26.86 Torr, SOLVED FROM THE EQUATION AND DELIBERATELY NOT A LEDGER ROW, so it cannot silently disagree with the curve it comes from. check_closure.py asserts it lands in the human range, which makes it a TEST of the adopted equation rather than a second definition of the same quantity. Severinghaus's own Bohr equation uses PO2/26.6 as its reference, so 26.86 is consistent with the paper to within 1 percent. || FIXED AT NORMAL pH AND TEMPERATURE. ADR 0018 records what that disqualifies: the Bohr effect, fever and hypothermia, 2,3-DPG, and the increased offloading in exercising muscle. pH needs bicarbonate, which is renal and absent. || INTENSIVE."),
    :BLOOD_O2_SOLUBILITY => Provenance("BLOOD.O2.SOLUBILITY", "mL/dL/mmHg", 0.003, "C", "assumed", "No admissible source opened. See notes.", "ASSUMED AT THE CONVENTIONAL VALUE, a round number and therefore presumptively unsourced under directive 1.12. || IT CONTRIBUTES 1.4 PERCENT OF ARTERIAL OXYGEN CONTENT at the reference individual - 0.27 mL/dL out of 20.6 - so it is the least consequential row in this component and is recorded as such rather than left to look important. || IT MATTERS ONLY WHERE HAEMOGLOBIN IS LOW OR PO2 IS HIGH, neither of which this model represents: severe anaemia and hyperbaric or high-FiO2 breathing are both outside ADR 0018's scope. || INTENSIVE."),
    :BR_CARDIAC_GAIN_FEMALE => Provenance("BR.CARDIAC.GAIN", "unitless", 0.961, "B", "derived", "Derived from BR.CARDIAC.SENSITIVITY, CV.HR.NOMINAL and CV.MAP.SETPOINT.", "DERIVED 2026-09-08, and it is what Baroreflex.jl actually reads. BR.CARDIAC.GAIN = BR.CARDIAC.SENSITIVITY * CV.HR.NOMINAL * CV.MAP.SETPOINT / 60000. Male: 15.0 * 62.0 * 87.0 / 60000 = 1.34850. Female: 10.2 * 65.0 * 87.0 / 60000 = 0.961350. Asserted in tools/check_closure.py. || THE ARITHMETIC, AND IT WAS FIXED IN THE PRE-REGISTRATION BEFORE ANY VALUE WAS KNOWN. With RR = 60000/HR in ms, a sensitivity BRS = dRR/dP gives (1/HR)(dHR/dP) = -(HR/60000)*BRS, and multiplying by MAP_ref makes it the dimensionless gain this component's tanh consumes, matching the form BR.OPEN_LOOP_GAIN already sits in. || EVERY DIGIT IS CARRIED BECAUSE THIS ROW CLOSES AN IDENTITY, which is the lesson CV.CO.NOMINAL recorded when it held this position. The values are exact decimals, not rounded ones. The INPUT significant figures are three, and no result computed from this row may be quoted to more - directive 1.9. || THE CONVERSION IS LOCAL AND THAT IS A SCALE STATEMENT, NOT A CAVEAT. The relation between rate and interval is a reciprocal, so this factor holds at the operating heart rate and nowhere else. pooling.md requires two rows to share a measurement SCALE and not merely a unit symbol - section 5 item 18, the free-thyroxine error - and this is the line where that is honoured. A sensitivity measured at one heart rate cannot be composed with a different operating rate without redoing this conversion. || IT IS SEXED TWICE OVER AND THE TWO DIMORPHISMS ARE INDEPENDENT FACTS. The sensitivity is a male/female pair, and CV.HR.NOMINAL is a second one, 62 against 65. Neither is evidence for the other and the product carries both. This row would be sexed even if the sensitivity were entered as `both`. || WHAT IT IS AGAINST. BR.OPEN_LOOP_GAIN is 2.0, so the chronotropic arm is 67 percent of the vasomotor arm in men and 48 percent in women. Section 7.1 of the pre-registration predicted 'the same order' from the conversion factor alone, before any source was opened, and that is why section 6 was made the stop condition rather than a caveat. || AND IT DOES NOT DOUBLE-COUNT BR.OPEN_LOOP_GAIN - see that row, which now records why. Three independent lines: Yamasaki's open-loop gain decomposes through PLASMA NOREPINEPHRINE and a cholinergic vagal limb cannot appear in a noradrenergic arc; the animal preparations behind its 1.0-3.5 are vagotomized where it could be checked; and Dutoit AP, Hart EC, Charkoudian N, Wallin BG, Curry TB, Joyner MJ. Hypertension 2010;56(6):1118-23, PMID 21060001, found cardiac and sympathetic baroreflex sensitivity UNCORRELATED within 53 healthy adults at R-squared 0.0003. Two arms that vary independently are not one gain to be apportioned between effectors. THE RESIDUAL OVERLAP IS NOT ZERO AND IS DECLARED: 'sympathetic' includes cardiac sympathetic chronotropy, and the phenylephrine ramp is PREDOMINANTLY but not exclusively vagal, so some small part of this limb may already sit inside the 2.0 routed through resistance. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO THREE SIGNIFICANT FIGURES, WHICH IS WHAT THE INPUTS CARRY, and the previous entry was a direct violation of directive 1.13. It stored 1.3485 and 0.96135 - five figures - from a product of 15.0, 62.0 and 87.0, every one of which carries three. THE EXTRA DIGITS WERE AN ARTEFACT OF FLOATING POINT AND NOT INFORMATION. The note even said the inputs support three and then stored five, which is worse than not noticing: the rule was written down and then not applied. || THE CLOSURE-IDENTITY EXCEPTION WAS CLAIMED AND DOES NOT APPLY. That exception exists for rows like CV.CO.NOMINAL where extra digits are a bookkeeping device so the closure gate can compare arithmetic exactly. It is never a licence to invent precision, and check_closure.py now compares this row to its inputs at the precision this row is STORED to, which is the correct test for a deliberately rounded derived value. || WHAT IT COSTS: nothing. 1.35 against 1.3485 is 0.1%, against a sensitivity whose own population spread is nearer 60%."),
