 women. Section 7.1 of the pre-registration predicted 'the same order' from the conversion factor alone, before any source was opened, and that is why section 6 was made the stop condition rather than a caveat. || AND IT DOES NOT DOUBLE-COUNT BR.OPEN_LOOP_GAIN - see that row, which now records why. Three independent lines: Yamasaki's open-loop gain decomposes through PLASMA NOREPINEPHRINE and a cholinergic vagal limb cannot appear in a noradrenergic arc; the animal preparations behind its 1.0-3.5 are vagotomized where it could be checked; and Dutoit AP, Hart EC, Charkoudian N, Wallin BG, Curry TB, Joyner MJ. Hypertension 2010;56(6):1118-23, PMID 21060001, found cardiac and sympathetic baroreflex sensitivity UNCORRELATED within 53 healthy adults at R-squared 0.0003. Two arms that vary independently are not one gain to be apportioned between effectors. THE RESIDUAL OVERLAP IS NOT ZERO AND IS DECLARED: 'sympathetic' includes cardiac sympathetic chronotropy, and the phenylephrine ramp is PREDOMINANTLY but not exclusively vagal, so some small part of this limb may already sit inside the 2.0 routed through resistance. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO THREE SIGNIFICANT FIGURES, WHICH IS WHAT THE INPUTS CARRY, and the previous entry was a direct violation of directive 1.13. It stored 1.3485 and 0.96135 - five figures - from a product of 15.0, 62.0 and 87.0, every one of which carries three. THE EXTRA DIGITS WERE AN ARTEFACT OF FLOATING POINT AND NOT INFORMATION. The note even said the inputs support three and then stored five, which is worse than not noticing: the rule was written down and then not applied. || THE CLOSURE-IDENTITY EXCEPTION WAS CLAIMED AND DOES NOT APPLY. That exception exists for rows like CV.CO.NOMINAL where extra digits are a bookkeeping device so the closure gate can compare arithmetic exactly. It is never a licence to invent precision, and check_closure.py now compares this row to its inputs at the precision this row is STORED to, which is the correct test for a deliberately rounded derived value. || WHAT IT COSTS: nothing. 1.35 against 1.3485 is 0.1%, against a sensitivity whose own population spread is nearer 60%."),
    :BR_CARDIAC_SENSITIVITY_FEMALE => Provenance("BR.CARDIAC.SENSITIVITY", "ms/mmHg", 10.2, "B", "reported", "Laitinen T, Hartikainen J, Vanninen E, Niskanen L, Geelen G, Lansimies E. Age and gender dependency of baroreflex sensitivity in healthy subjects. J Appl Physiol 1998;84(2):576-583.", "SOURCED 2026-09-08 under validation/chronotropic_baroreflex_prereg.md, which was committed BEFORE any source was opened. Reproduce with python validation/chronotropic_baroreflex_extract.py. NO COMMIT SHA IS CITED, DELIBERATELY - rebase-merge rewrites it and every such citation already in this ledger points at a commit main does not contain. Verify the ordering instead: git log --diff-filter=A -- validation/chronotropic_baroreflex_prereg.md. || THE MEASURED QUANTITY, AND THE MODEL CONSUMES A DERIVED ONE. Baroreflex sensitivity is reported in ms/mmHg because what is measured is the lengthening of the cardiac interval against a pressure ramp. BR.CARDIAC.GAIN is derived from this row and is what Baroreflex.jl reads. Sourcing the milliseconds and deriving the dimensionless gain is the dependency direction section 3.6 established and sections 3.26, 3.29 and 3.31 each repeated. || THE METHOD IS PART OF THE QUANTITY. Phenylephrine bolus injection - a pharmacological ramp, which prereg section 2.1 admits because it perturbs pressure by tens of mmHg over tens of seconds, a timescale this cycle-averaged model can represent at the 1 s sampling ADR 0002 fixes inside a challenge window. SPONTANEOUS-SEQUENCE AND SPECTRAL SENSITIVITIES ARE EXCLUDED AND ARE NOT A SUBSTITUTE: they are computed from beat-to-beat fluctuations ADR 0002 states in terms this model cannot represent. || THAT EXCLUSION IS MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED. Bonyhay I, Risk M, Freeman R. PLoS One 2013;8(11):e79513, PMC3828383, OPEN ACCESS READ IN FULL: the same 18 healthy adults measured both ways give modified Oxford 15.7 +/- 9.2 against a transfer-function modulus 19.4 +/- 10.5 ms/mmHg, P < 0.05, mean relative difference 20.7 percent, typical error 3.9 and limit of agreement 10.8 ms/mmHg. The methods correlate across subjects, r = 0.85, and do NOT agree within them. Prereg branch C6 would have widened admissibility had they agreed; it does not fire. || READING LEVEL: ABSTRACT ONLY. journals.physiology.org returns 403 and there is no PubMed Central record. Directive 1.5 - what was opened is the PubMed abstract and nothing else. || NO DISPERSION IS ENTERED AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. The abstract prints 15.0 +/- 1.2 and 10.2 +/- 1.1 without stating whether that is SD or SEM. At n = 117 split by sex, and against SDs near 9 reported by every other study in this literature, these are almost certainly SEM. Directive 1.9 and the BF.BODY_MASS.TYPICAL precedent: where the estimator is not stated, none is chosen after the fact. || THE SEX PAIR IS THE FIRST IN THE NEURAL SUBSYSTEM, and one good study disagrees. Laitinen reports men 15.0 against women 10.2, P < 0.01, and finds age and gender the two most important correlates, together over half the variance. The direction is corroborated by the sex-differences literature, which reports women having blunted cardiovagal sensitivity during a rapid hypertensive stimulus. IT IS CONTRADICTED BY Wada N, Singer W, Gehrking TL, Sletten DM, Schmelzer JD, Kihara M, Low PA. Muscle Nerve 2014;50(4):535-540, PMID 24477673, PMC4115054, READ IN FULL: 166 normal subjects aged 18-81, 73 men and 93 women, VALSALVA manoeuvre, BRS 7.3 +/- 3.6 ms/mmHg, and 'we did not find a gender effect in BRS indices'. TWO METHODS, TWO ANSWERS, AND THEY ARE NOT POOLED - pooling.md bars pooling across measurement methods and prereg section 2.1 prefers the ramp, so Laitinen governs and Wada is recorded as a method-discordant comparison rather than averaged away. Wada is also a lower LEVEL by half, which is itself a method difference and not a disagreement about people. || AGE IS NOT REPRESENTED AND THIS IS A MIXED-AGE COHORT VALUE. Laitinen reports BRS correlating with age at r = -0.65 across 23-77 years, and Wada gives ln BRS = 2.72 - 0.02 x age. This model has no age dimension, so the entered value is a cohort mean over that range - the same debt ADH.URINE.OSM_MAX carries and records rather than approximates. || DIRECTIVE 1.7 WAS PREDICTED TO BITE FOR THE EIGHTH SUBSYSTEM AND IT DID. Most of this literature measures baroreflex sensitivity as a PROGNOSTIC MARKER after myocardial infarction, where depressed sensitivity predicts mortality and the relationship is the instrument rather than the subject. The 6 ms/mmHg figure that recurs in it is a risk-stratification cutoff and is not a measurement of anything in health; it is listed in prereg section 4 as a number not to enter. Laitinen is admissible precisely because its subject IS the relationship - age and gender dependency in healthy people."),
    :BR_CARDIAC_SENSITIVITY_MALE => Provenance("BR.CARDIAC.SENSITIVITY", "ms/mmHg", 15.0, "B", "reported", "Laitinen T, Hartikainen J, Vanninen E, Niskanen L, Geelen G, Lansimies E. Age and gender dependency of baroreflex sensitivity in healthy subjects. J Appl Physiol 1998;84(2):576-583.", "SOURCED 2026-09-08 under validation/chronotropic_baroreflex_prereg.md, which was committed BEFORE any source was opened. Reproduce with python validation/chronotropic_baroreflex_extract.py. NO COMMIT SHA IS CITED, DELIBERATELY - rebase-merge rewrites it and every such citation already in this ledger points at a commit main does not contain. Verify the ordering instead: git log --diff-filter=A -- validation/chronotropic_baroreflex_prereg.md. || THE MEASURED QUANTITY, AND THE MODEL CONSUMES A DERIVED ONE. Baroreflex sensitivity is reported in ms/mmHg because what is measured is the lengthening of the cardiac interval against a pressure ramp. BR.CARDIAC.GAIN is derived from this row and is what Baroreflex.jl reads. Sourcing the milliseconds and deriving the dimensionless gain is the dependency direction section 3.6 established and sections 3.26, 3.29 and 3.31 each repeated. || THE METHOD IS PART OF THE QUANTITY. Phenylephrine bolus injection - a pharmacological ramp, which prereg section 2.1 admits because it perturbs pressure by tens of mmHg over tens of seconds, a timescale this cycle-averaged model can represent at the 1 s sampling ADR 0002 fixes inside a challenge window. SPONTANEOUS-SEQUENCE AND SPECTRAL SENSITIVITIES ARE EXCLUDED AND ARE NOT A SUBSTITUTE: they are computed from beat-to-beat fluctuations ADR 0002 states in terms this model cannot represent. || THAT EXCLUSION IS MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED. Bonyhay I, Risk M, Freeman R. PLoS One 2013;8(11):e79513, PMC3828383, OPEN ACCESS READ IN FULL: the same 18 healthy adults measured both ways give modified Oxford 15.7 +/- 9.2 against a transfer-function modulus 19.4 +/- 10.5 ms/mmHg, P < 0.05, mean relative difference 20.7 percent, typical error 3.9 and limit of agreement 10.8 ms/mmHg. The methods correlate across subjects, r = 0.85, and do NOT agree within them. Prereg branch C6 would have widened admissibility had they agreed; it does not fire. || READING LEVEL: ABSTRACT ONLY. journals.physiology.org returns 403 and there is no PubMed Central record. Directive 1.5 - what was opened is the PubMed abstract and nothing else. || NO DISPERSION IS ENTERED AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. The abstract prints 15.0 +/- 1.2 and 10.2 +/- 1.1 without stating whether that is SD or SEM. At n = 117 split by sex, and against SDs near 9 reported by every other study in this literature, these are almost certainly SEM. Directive 1.9 and the BF.BODY_MASS.TYPICAL precedent: where the estimator is not stated, none is chosen after the fact. || THE SEX PAIR IS THE FIRST IN THE NEURAL SUBSYSTEM, and one good study disagrees. Laitinen reports men 15.0 against women 10.2, P < 0.01, and finds age and gender the two most important correlates, together over half the variance. The direction is corroborated by the sex-differences literature, which reports women having blunted cardiovagal sensitivity during a rapid hypertensive stimulus. IT IS CONTRADICTED BY Wada N, Singer W, Gehrking TL, Sletten DM, Schmelzer JD, Kihara M, Low PA. Muscle Nerve 2014;50(4):535-540, PMID 24477673, PMC4115054, READ IN FULL: 166 normal subjects aged 18-81, 73 men and 93 women, VALSALVA manoeuvre, BRS 7.3 +/- 3.6 ms/mmHg, and 'we did not find a gender effect in BRS indices'. TWO METHODS, TWO ANSWERS, AND THEY ARE NOT POOLED - pooling.md bars pooling across measurement methods and prereg section 2.1 prefers the ramp, so Laitinen governs and Wada is recorded as a method-discordant comparison rather than averaged away. Wada is also a lower LEVEL by half, which is itself a method difference and not a disagreement about people. || AGE IS NOT REPRESENTED AND THIS IS A MIXED-AGE COHORT VALUE. Laitinen reports BRS correlating with age at r = -0.65 across 23-77 years, and Wada gives ln BRS = 2.72 - 0.02 x age. This model has no age dimension, so the entered value is a cohort mean over that range - the same debt ADH.URINE.OSM_MAX carries and records rather than approximates. || DIRECTIVE 1.7 WAS PREDICTED TO BITE FOR THE EIGHTH SUBSYSTEM AND IT DID. Most of this literature measures baroreflex sensitivity as a PROGNOSTIC MARKER after myocardial infarction, where depressed sensitivity predicts mortality and the relationship is the instrument rather than the subject. The 6 ms/mmHg figure that recurs in it is a risk-stratification cutoff and is not a measurement of anything in health; it is listed in prereg section 4 as a number not to enter. Laitinen is admissible precisely because its subject IS the relationship - age and gender dependency in healthy people. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :BR_CARDIAC_TAU => Provenance("BR.CARDIAC.TAU", "s", 0.4, "A", "reported", "La Rovere MT, Pinna GD, Raczak G. Baroreflex sensitivity: measurement and clinical implications. Ann Noninvasive Electrocardiol 2008;13(2):191-207.", "ADDED 2026-09-08 BECAUSE THE MODEL DEMANDED IT, NOT BECAUSE THE PRE-REGISTRATION WANTED IT. validation/chronotropic_baroreflex_prereg.md section 5 made the chronotropic arm ALGEBRAIC by default and put the burden on ADDING a lag, on the directive 1.10 grounds that a 200-600 ms effector is quasi-static against a six-hour protocol. THAT WAS REFUTED BY BUILDING IT. || WHY. An algebraic hr_mod closes an INSTANTANEOUS ALGEBRAIC LOOP through arterial pressure: cardiac output sets MAP, MAP sets the baroreflex error, the error sets heart rate, and heart rate sets cardiac output. structural_simplify resolved it by promoting Blood.CO to a state - so the 'stateless' arm cost a state anyway, in another component, where it meant nothing. The vasomotor arm never had this problem because BR.EFFECTOR.TAU makes tpr_mod a STATE, and a lag is exactly what breaks an algebraic loop. || SO THE STATE IS PAID FOR EITHER WAY AND THIS IS THE HONEST PLACE TO PUT IT: on a variable with a physical meaning and a sourced time constant, rather than on an implicit nonlinear unknown inside the blood component. Ten states before, eleven after, and the eleventh is br.hr_mod. || THE VALUE. La Rovere reports the parasympathetic limb of the baroreflex acting on heart rate in 200-600 ms, against 2-3 s for the sympathetic vasomotor limb - the same sentence BR.EFFECTOR.TAU is entered from, and the reason ADR 0009 gave for lumping the arms in the first place. 0.4 s is the midpoint of that reported range, entered the same way BR.EFFECTOR.TAU takes 3.0 from 2.0-3.0. THE RANGE IS THE UNCERTAINTY AND IT IS THREEFOLD. || IT IS SEVEN TIMES FASTER THAN THE VASOMOTOR EFFECTOR, which is the physiology: vagal transmission is fast and cholinergic, sympathetic vasomotor transmission is slow and noradrenergic. That ratio is now the smallest declared time constant in the model and it widens the stiffness range - 0.4 s against a 10.3 day thyroxine turnover is about 2.2 million. ADR 0003 stays deferred on state count, but this is the fastest thing in the model and a multirate partition may not cut across it. || IT IS ALSO WHAT THE COUPLING DECLARATION READS. baroreflex_couplings() carried a hardcoded 0.5 s for one commit, which is directive 1.4's violation - a number in a component rather than in the ledger. It now reads this row."),
    :BR_EFFECTOR_TAU => Provenance("BR.EFFECTOR.TAU", "s", 3.0, "A", "reported", "La Rovere MT, Pinna GD, Raczak G. Baroreflex sensitivity: measurement and clinical implications. Ann Noninvasive Electrocardiol 2008;13(2):191-207.", "Cardiac and vasomotor sympathetic activation occurs with a 2-3 second delay and reaches maximal effect more slowly. Parasympathetic activation is far faster (200-600 ms) but acts on heart rate, which is not a state in this cycle-averaged model (ADR 0002) - hence the lumped single-arm treatment. 3.0 s is the upper end of the stated range. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :BR_HR_MAX_FRACTION => Provenance("BR.HR.MAX_FRACTION", "unitless", 0.5, "C", "assumed", "Saturation bound; see notes.", "ASSUMED, and it is the same convention BR.TPR.MAX_FRACTION carries for the vasomotor arm - 0.5, entered for the same reason and with the same standing, which is none. It bounds the fractional heart-rate excursion the tanh characteristic can produce, so heart rate cannot be driven negative or to an absurd tachycardia under a large pressure excursion. At 0.5 the bound is a heart rate between 31 and 93 in men. || DIRECTIVE 1.12: THIS IS A ROUND NUMBER AND IT IS ENTERED AS `assumed` RATHER THAN DRESSED UP. No source was sought for it in this pass and none is claimed. It is inert at the operating point and at every steady state, because the reflex resets and the drive returns to zero, so it can only matter in a transient large enough to reach the bound - which no protocol this model currently runs comes close to. || WHAT WOULD MAKE IT MATTER: a haemorrhage, lower-body negative pressure or orthostatic protocol, none of which this model can run, because the cardiopulmonary receptors ADR 0009 names as a separate component do not exist. If one is ever built, this row must be sourced before any result that reaches the bound is reported."),
    :BR_OPEN_LOOP_GAIN => Provenance("BR.OPEN_LOOP_GAIN", "unitless", 5.62, "B", "reported", "Yamasaki F, Sato T, Sato K, Diedrich A. Analytic and integrative framework for understanding human sympathetic arterial baroreflex function. Front Neurosci 2021;15:707345.", "2.0 -> 5.62 ON 2026-09-09, AT THE OWNER'S EXPLICIT INSTRUCTION, AND THE CITATION DID NOT CHANGE. That is the whole point of this row's history: it cited Yamasaki 2021 the entire time and carried a number from his INTRODUCTION instead of his RESULT. || WHAT IT WAS. 2.0, the mid-point of an ANIMAL range of 1.0-3.5 that Yamasaki cites in his introduction from Kent 1972, Shoukas and Sagawa 1973, McRitchie 1976, Burattini 1994, Sato 1999 and Sunagawa 2001 - isolated carotid sinus or aortic arch perfusion in dog and rabbit. The note also repeated his sentence that the invasive approach is not applicable to humans and that human open-loop gain HAS NOT BEEN CLARIFIED. || THAT SENTENCE IS THE PAPER'S PROBLEM STATEMENT AND THE PAPER IS THE ANSWER TO IT. Yamasaki F, Sato T, Sato K, Diedrich A. Front Neurosci 2021;15:707345, PMC8322947, OPEN ACCESS, READ IN FULL. He measured it in humans: GL(0) = 5.62 +/- 0.98 SUPINE and GL(15) = 3.75 +/- 0.62 at 15 degrees head-up tilt. Table 2, and both Methods and the table legend state 'Values are means +/- SD'. THE SUPINE VALUE IS TAKEN because supine is the posture this model means by nominal - the same reason CV.HR.NOMINAL takes the supine Fenland figure. || IT IS THE SAME QUANTITY IN THE SAME UNITS AS THE MODEL'S SLOT. GL is defined as the product of the mechanoneural and neuromechanical arcs, with ARTERIAL PRESSURE as the output variable - the ratio of an arterial pressure change to a baroreceptor pressure change, dimensionless. The addendum to ADR 0009 computes that this model's G_br is exactly that: the tanh slope at the operating point is G_br/MAP_ref and dMAP/dtpr_mod is about MAP_ref, so the product is G_br. Commensurable by definition rather than by assumption. || AND IT IS THE VAGAL-EXCLUDED GAIN, WHICH IS EXACTLY WHAT THIS ROW SHOULD NOW HOLD. Yamasaki blocked vagal effects with atropine 0.4 mg/kg in every protocol, so 5.62 is sympathetic arterial pressure control with the cardiac vagal limb removed. Since 2026-09-08 that limb is a SEPARATE effector, BR.CARDIAC.GAIN under ADR 0022, so the two rows now partition the reflex the way the measurements do. Before ADR 0022 this change would have LOST the vagal contribution entirely; after it, it is the coherent pairing. THE RESIDUAL OVERLAP IS UNCHANGED AND DECLARED: atropine removes vagal chronotropy but not cardiac sympathetic chronotropy, so a small part of the cardiac limb sits inside 5.62 as well as inside BR.CARDIAC.GAIN. || THE WEAKNESSES, AND THEY ARE REAL. n = 7. Healthy male volunteers aged 19-37. THE AUTHORS THEMSELVES DISCLAIM REPRESENTATIVENESS in their limitations: 'we could not conclude that the parameters are representative of a normal population', and they call for larger studies across ages and health statuses. It is entered `both` under ADR 0014's rule that a single supported value serves both sexes, so THIS IS A MALE NUMBER APPLIED TO WOMEN - the CV.HEMATOCRIT.NOMINAL failure exactly, declared rather than hidden. It is also a YOUNG cohort against a model whose stroke volume comes from 45-74 year olds and whose heart rate comes from 29-65, and baroreflex gain falls steeply with age. || WHY IT IS STILL BETTER THAN WHAT IT REPLACED. Seven young men measured directly beat six animal studies read through an introduction, in the right species, the right posture and the right units. The uncertainty is now a reported SD rather than the spread of somebody else's citations. || THE ETHICAL-CEILING ARGUMENT IN ADR 0009'S ADDENDUM IS FALSIFIED BY THIS SAME PAPER. That addendum tiered this row E2 on the grounds that opening the baroreflex loop is 'definitionally terminal' and so not performable in humans. Yamasaki performed it - not by isolating a carotid sinus, but by constructing an equilibrium diagram from graded tilt and ganglionic blockade, which reaches the open-loop gain without opening the loop surgically. THE QUANTITY WAS OBTAINABLE ALL ALONG BY A DIFFERENT METHOD, which is HANDOVER section 3.19's lesson repeated: that pass missed the venous return relation by searching for the wrong OBJECT. Ask what is measurable, not whether the obvious preparation is permitted. || IT CHANGES NO STEADY STATE AND EVERY TRANSIENT. The reflex resets, so tpr_mod returns to 1 whatever the gain and no resting value, no chronic salt sensitivity and no pressure-volume ratio moves. What moves is buffering: total loop gain goes from about 3.35 to about 6.97 with ADR 0022's arm included, so a pressure disturbance is attenuated roughly twice as hard. THE CONSEQUENCES FOR THE ACUTE LIMB ARE MEASURED AND REPORTED IN HANDOVER section 3.39 RATHER THAN COMPENSATED FOR: nothing else was re-estimated, and in particular RN.MD.RENIN_GAIN and CV.ANP.NATRIURETIC_GAIN were NOT re-solved."),
    :BR_RESET_TAU => Provenance("BR.RESET.TAU", "day", 1.0, "B", "assumed", "Dampney RAL. Resetting of the baroreflex control of sympathetic vasomotor activity during natural behaviors. Front Physiol 2017.", "ASSUMED. Baroreflex resetting is well established qualitatively - the reflex re-centres on prevailing pressure over hours to days, which is WHY it cannot set long-run arterial pressure. A specific human time constant is not reported in the sources consulted. 1 day is an order-of-magnitude placeholder. CRITICAL: this parameter is what makes the baroreflex a fast buffer rather than a long-term regulator. If it were infinite the reflex would set long-run pressure and the Guyton claim in ADR 0007 would be false. Sensitivity to it must be tested. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :BR_TPR_MAX_FRACTION => Provenance("BR.TPR.MAX_FRACTION", "unitless", 0.5, "B", "assumed", "Saturation bound; see notes.", "ASSUMED. The baroreflex characteristic is sigmoidal and saturates; an unbounded linear gain would let TPR go negative under large pressure excursions. 0.5 means TPR can move at most +/-50 percent from baseline by reflex action alone. Placeholder chosen to keep the model well-posed under hemorrhage-scale perturbations, not extracted from a reported response range."),
    :CIRC_CV_ACROPHASE => Provenance("CIRC.CV.ACROPHASE", "day", 0.583, "A", "derived", "Shea SA, Hilton MF, Hu K, Scheer FAJL. Existence of an endogenous circadian blood pressure rhythm in humans that peaks in the evening. Circ Res 2011;108(8):980-984.", "PRECISION: rounded to 3 significant figures on 2026-08-27. The sources do not support more, and carrying digits that do not exist forced the closure gate and the test suite to chase arithmetic noise. Derived rows inherit the significant figures of their inputs and are rounded to match; the closure tolerance was moved to 1e-3 in the same change, which is still an order of magnitude tighter than the 6% error that motivated that gate. || WAS 0.25 day (13:00) AND ASSUMED. Shea 2011 reports that all six peaks - systolic and diastolic across three protocols - occurred at a circadian phase corresponding to approximately 21:00, the biological evening. Under this component's convention that phase 0 is the start of the active period, taken as 07:00 for humans, 21:00 is 14 h later = 0.583333 day. DERIVED only in that clock time is converted to the component's phase convention. NOTE WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE MODEL: the ENDOGENOUS BP peak is in the EVENING, whereas the familiar ambulatory dip is NOCTURNAL. They are not the same rhythm and are not even in phase, which is the second reason the old dip-fraction parameterisation was wrong. Shea also reports the BP rhythm was unrelated in phase to those of cortisol, catecholamines, cardiac vagal modulation, heart rate or urine flow."),
    :CIRC_CV_MAP_AMPLITUDE => Provenance("CIRC.CV_MAP.AMPLITUDE", "unitless", 0.017, "A", "derived", "Shea SA, Hilton MF, Hu K, Scheer FAJL. Existence of an endogenous circadian blood pressure rhythm in humans that peaks in the evening. Circ Res 2011;108(8):980-984.", "PRECISION: rounded to 2 significant figures on 2026-08-27. The sources do not support more, and carrying digits that do not exist forced the closure gate and the test suite to chase arithmetic noise. Derived rows inherit the significant figures of their inputs and are rounded to match; the closure tolerance was moved to 1e-3 in the same change, which is still an order of magnitude tighter than the 6% error that motivated that gate. || REPLACES CIRC.CV_MAP.DIP_FRACTION = 0.15, WHICH WAS THE WRONG QUANTITY BY ABOUT NINE TIMES AND ALSO THE WRONG PHASE. That parameter encoded the 10-20% NOCTURNAL AMBULATORY DIP as though it were the endogenous circadian driver. It is not. Degaute 1991 (31 healthy young men, ambulatory BP with simultaneous polysomnography) found RECUMBENCY AND SLEEP ACCOUNT FOR 65-75% OF THE NOCTURNAL DECLINE, concluding that posture and the wake-sleep transition are the major factors in the 24-h BP rhythm. THE ENDOGENOUS COMPONENT IS REAL BUT SMALL. Shea 2011 measured it under three complementary protocols in 28 normotensive adults - a 38-h constant routine and forced desynchrony at 28-h and 20-h sleep/wake cycles - and found significant circadian rhythms in all three with almost identical profiles: peak-to-trough 3-6 mmHg systolic and 2-3 diastolic. Converting through MAP = DBP + (SBP-DBP)/3 gives 2.33-4.00 mmHg peak-to-trough, so a cosinor amplitude of 0.0125 to 0.0215 of MAP. Midpoint adopted, range carried. DERIVED because the MAP conversion is arithmetic on reported SBP and DBP, not a reported MAP amplitude. LIVE CONTROVERSY, RECORDED NOT RESOLVED: Kerkhof 1998 (25 normotensives, 26-h constant routine, stated power >0.95) found NO significant endogenous BP rhythm, and Van Dongen 2001 replicated that absence five times in six subjects. Shea's forced-desynchrony design separates circadian from behavioural influence more strongly than a constant routine alone and is followed here, but the disagreement is genuine and this parameter should be treated as the weakest in the cardiovascular arm."),
    :CIRC_EFFECTOR_TAU => Provenance("CIRC.EFFECTOR.TAU", "s", 3600.0, "C", "assumed", "Recent advances in understanding the circadian clock in renal physiology. PMC6350809.", "ASSUMED. The source describes Per1 as an early aldosterone target gene regulating ENaC, SGLT1, NHE3 and ET-1, and notes Per genes have short half-lives, but does not report an effector delay. 1 h is an order-of-magnitude placeholder. This is a Neurohumoral coupling tau per ADR 0003 and is what makes the clock safe to partition across. || CITATION FLAGGED 2026-08-25 BY AUDIT. This row cites a title and a PMC identifier with NO AUTHOR LIST and no DOI, and the title does not resolve on a PubMed title search. It must be replaced with a full citation - authors, journal, year, volume, pages - or removed. Until then the parameter should be treated as uncited, not as tier-supported. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :CIRC_PERIOD => Provenance("CIRC.PERIOD", "day", 1.0, "A", "reported", "Circadian rhythms and the kidney. Nat Rev Nephrol 2018;14:626-635.", "Free-running human period is slightly over 24 h but entrained period is 24 h. This model has no entrainment mechanism (see Circadian.jl) so the entrained value is the correct one to use. If constant-routine or shift-work protocols are ever added this must become a free-running period with an entrainment path. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :CIRC_RENAL_NA_ACROPHASE => Provenance("CIRC.RENAL_NA.ACROPHASE", "day", 0.33, "B", "assumed", "Ueno M, Kawasaki T, Uezono K, Omae T, Matsuoka M. Relationship of urinary kallikrein excretion to renal water and sodium excretion. Metabolism 1983;32(5):433-437.", "STILL ASSUMED AT 0.33 day, AND THE ACROPHASE IS CONTESTED TOO - the two best cosinor studies found are nearly ANTIPHASE. Ueno 1983, 20 healthy Japanese women on an ordinary diet, cosinor: acrophase of urinary sodium excretion at 16:55, i.e. late afternoon, closely tracking urine volume at 16:51 (r = 0.935). Under this component's convention with a 07:00 active-period start that is 0.41 day. Peng 2021, 85 healthy Chinese adults, cosinor: sodium excretion had a NOCTURNAL PEAK falling to its lowest around noon to afternoon - roughly the opposite. 0.33 day (about 15:00) is retained as a placeholder consistent with Ueno and inconsistent with Peng. It is NOT a compromise between them; there is no defensible compromise between antiphase estimates. Resolve with the Minors and Waterhouse constant-routine normative data before this parameter is used for anything."),
    :CIRC_RENAL_NA_AMPLITUDE => Provenance("CIRC.RENAL_NA.AMPLITUDE", "unitless", 0.25, "B", "assumed", "Kanabrocki EL, Scheving LE, Olwin JH, et al. Circadian variation in the urinary excretion of electrolytes and trace elements in men. Am J Anat 1983;166(2):121-148.", "STILL ASSUMED AT 0.25, AND NOW WITH THE CONFLICT RECORDED RATHER THAN A SINGLE SOURCE. A wide sweep of the circadian sodium literature found the amplitude genuinely contested. UPPER END: Kanabrocki 1983, 11 healthy adult males, 3-hourly urine over 27 h with cosinor analysis, found a statistically significant population rhythm for sodium and reported that for almost every element the increase from lowest to highest 3-hour group mean exceeded 100%. A peak-to-trough ratio of 2 corresponds to a cosinor amplitude of 0.33, and >100% is a lower bound, so this supports 0.33 or more. LOWER END: Peng 2021, 85 healthy Chinese adults, every voided specimen over 24 h with cosinor analysis, found URINARY SODIUM SHOWED NON-SIGNIFICANT PARAMETERS IN THE COSINOR MODEL and that the sodium rhythm was less significant than potassium. That is close to an amplitude of zero. 0.25 sits between them and is NOT a pooled estimate - pooling a significant and a non-significant result is not defensible. THE RHYTHM'S EXISTENCE IS BETTER SUPPORTED THAN ITS SIZE: see CIRC.RENAL_NA.ENDOGENEITY_MARKER. THE PROPER SOURCE HAS BEEN IDENTIFIED AND NOT YET OBTAINED - Minors and Waterhouse 1990 provide normative ENDOGENOUS urinary sodium data from about 80 constant routines, which is exactly the unmasked quantity this parameter should carry. Get it before trusting any circadian sodium result."),
    :CV_ARTERIAL_COMPLIANCE_FEMALE => Provenance("CV.ARTERIAL.COMPLIANCE", "mL/mmHg", 2.394, "B", "derived", "Derived from CV.SV.NOMINAL and CV.PP.CENTRAL_NOMINAL; form validated by Chemla 1998.", "RECOMPUTED 2026-09-01 when CV.SV.NOMINAL was sourced. C_art = SV0 / PP0 = 75 / 33.0 = 2.273 mL/mmHg. Was 2.333. DERIVED, and this is why agreement with the sourced 109/76 is CONSISTENCY AND NOT VALIDATION: the compliance is defined so that the reconstruction returns the sourced pulse pressure at the nominal stroke volume. It cannot disagree at the operating point. What it can do is get the RESPONSE wrong, and that is what moves now - a larger stroke volume with a proportionally larger compliance leaves nominal PP identical while changing how PP responds to a volume excursion. The Chemla 1998 SV/PP estimator is defined on AORTIC pressure, which is why PP here is the central value. See CV.MAP.SETPOINT for what happened the last time a brachial number was mixed with a central one. || RE-DERIVED 2026-09-16 FROM THE DE-INDEXED CV.SV.NOMINAL, deindexing_prereg.md Stage 2. The derivation and its citation are unchanged; only the stroke volume it is evaluated at moved. 79/33 = 2.3939 mL/mmHg."),
    :CV_ARTERIAL_COMPLIANCE_MALE => Provenance("CV.ARTERIAL.COMPLIANCE", "mL/mmHg", 2.697, "B", "derived", "Derived from CV.SV.NOMINAL and CV.PP.CENTRAL_NOMINAL; form validated by Chemla 1998.", "RECOMPUTED 2026-09-01 when CV.SV.NOMINAL was sourced. C_art = SV0 / PP0 = 96 / 33.0 = 2.909 mL/mmHg. Was 2.445. DERIVED, and this is why agreement with the sourced 109/76 is CONSISTENCY AND NOT VALIDATION: the compliance is defined so that the reconstruction returns the sourced pulse pressure at the nominal stroke volume. It cannot disagree at the operating point. What it can do is get the RESPONSE wrong, and that is what moves now - a larger stroke volume with a proportionally larger compliance leaves nominal PP identical while changing how PP responds to a volume excursion. The Chemla 1998 SV/PP estimator is defined on AORTIC pressure, which is why PP here is the central value. See CV.MAP.SETPOINT for what happened the last time a brachial number was mixed with a central one. || RE-DERIVED 2026-09-16 FROM THE DE-INDEXED CV.SV.NOMINAL, deindexing_prereg.md Stage 2. The derivation and its citation are unchanged; only the stroke volume it is evaluated at moved. 89/33 = 2.6970 mL/mmHg, the Chemla estimator SV/PP."),
    :CV_BLOOD_VOLUME_NOMINAL_FEMALE => Provenance("CV.BLOOD_VOLUME.NOMINAL", "L", 4.92, "A", "reported", "Oberholzer L, Montero D, Robach P, Siebenmann C, Ryrsoe CK, Bonne TC, Breenfeldt Andersen A, Bejder J, Karlsen T, Edvardsen E, Ronnestad BR, Hamarsland H, Cepeda-Lopez AC, Rittweger J, Treff G, Ahlgrim C, Almquist NW, Hallen J, Lundby C. Determinants and reference values for blood volume and total hemoglobin mass in women and men. Am J Hematol 2024;99(1):88-98. doi:10.1002/ajh.27162. PMID 38032792. Carbon monoxide rebreathing in 263 women and 319 men, age 10-93: blood volume 80.3 +/- 10.8 mL/kg body mass in men and 70.3 +/- 11.3 in women.", "CHANGED 5.0 L `both` -> 5.62 male / 4.92 female ON 2026-08-27, AND IT IS NOW A REAL DIMORPHISM. The previous value was NOT SOURCED: citation read 'Standard physiological reference. VERIFY.' while extraction_method claimed `reported`. 5.0 L is the round teaching figure, like 120/80 and 37 degrees - a pedagogical convention, not a measurement. Pre-registered in validation/verify_rows_prereg.md at e0195f4 before any source was opened. pooling_rule = single-source, k = 1. THE VALUE: measured per kg of body mass, so it is entered at the reference mass. 80.3 mL/kg * 70 kg = 5.621 -> 5.62 L male; 70.3 * 70 = 4.921 -> 4.92 L female, 3 significant figures per directive 1.9. SD 10.8 and 11.3 mL/kg -> 0.76 and 0.79 L. n = 319 men and 263 women, ages 10-93, BMI 14-41, plus 107 athletes analysed separately. Because blood volume is reported PER KG, it composes correctly with src/scaling.jl, which already scales this row by body mass. THE SEX DIFFERENCE IS BODY COMPOSITION, NOT A SEPARATE MECHANISM, AND THE PAPER SAYS SO. Blood volume per kg BODY MASS is 25% lower in women, but per kg LEAN MASS it is 6% HIGHER (110.9 +/- 12.5 women against 105.3 +/- 11.2 men). Lean body mass was the strongest single predictor, R2 = 0.72. This is the same shape as the Katori 1979 finding recorded under CV.SV.NOMINAL - cardiac and stroke INDEX show no sex difference once normalised - and it says the model would dissolve this pair into a lean-mass row if it carried body composition. It does not, so the pair stands. DECLARED METHOD LIMITATION, IN THE AUTHORS' OWN WORDS: these values may only be valid for blood volume assessed by CO rebreathing. Cross-method comparison is prohibited by pooling.md and none was attempted. WHAT FOLLOWS FROM IT: CV.PLASMA.ECF_FRACTION and CV.CENTRAL.VOLUME_NOMINAL are DERIVED from this row and become sexed with it. The operating point does NOT move: f_pv is derived so that f_pv*V_ecf/(1-Hct) = BV0, and VC0 = f_c*BV0, so V_central - VC0 is unchanged at nominal, stroke volume is unchanged, and MAP is unchanged. What changes is that the blood volume is now a measurement instead of a round number. STILL RESTING ON UNSOURCED HAEMATOCRIT: f_pv is computed with CV.HEMATOCRIT.NOMINAL = 0.45, which is itself still one of the VERIFY rows and is expected to be dimorphic. When it is sourced, f_pv must be re-derived per sex."),
    :CV_BLOOD_VOLUME_NOMINAL_MALE => Provenance("CV.BLOOD_VOLUME.NOMINAL", "L", 5.62, "A", "reported", "Oberholzer L, Montero D, Robach P, Siebenmann C, Ryrsoe CK, Bonne TC, Breenfeldt Andersen A, Bejder J, Karlsen T, Edvardsen E, Ronnestad BR, Hamarsland H, Cepeda-Lopez AC, Rittweger J, Treff G, Ahlgrim C, Almquist NW, Hallen J, Lundby C. Determinants and reference values for blood volume and total hemoglobin mass in women and men. Am J Hematol 2024;99(1):88-98. doi:10.1002/ajh.27162. PMID 38032792. Carbon monoxide rebreathing in 263 women and 319 men, age 10-93: blood volume 80.3 +/- 10.8 mL/kg body mass in men and 70.3 +/- 11.3 in women.", "CHANGED 5.0 L `both` -> 5.62 male / 4.92 female ON 2026-08-27, AND IT IS NOW A REAL DIMORPHISM. The previous value was NOT SOURCED: citation read 'Standard physiological reference. VERIFY.' while extraction_method claimed `reported`. 5.0 L is the round teaching figure, like 120/80 and 37 degrees - a pedagogical convention, not a measurement. Pre-registered in validation/verify_rows_prereg.md at e0195f4 before any source was opened. pooling_rule = single-source, k = 1. THE VALUE: measured per kg of body mass, so it is entered at the reference mass. 80.3 mL/kg * 70 kg = 5.621 -> 5.62 L male; 70.3 * 70 = 4.921 -> 4.92 L female, 3 significant figures per directive 1.9. SD 10.8 and 11.3 mL/kg -> 0.76 and 0.79 L. n = 319 men and 263 women, ages 10-93, BMI 14-41, plus 107 athletes analysed separately. Because blood volume is reported PER KG, it composes correctly with src/scaling.jl, which already scales this row by body mass. THE SEX DIFFERENCE IS BODY COMPOSITION, NOT A SEPARATE MECHANISM, AND THE PAPER SAYS SO. Blood volume per kg BODY MASS is 25% lower in women, but per kg LEAN MASS it is 6% HIGHER (110.9 +/- 12.5 women against 105.3 +/- 11.2 men). Lean body mass was the strongest single predictor, R2 = 0.72. This is the same shape as the Katori 1979 finding recorded under CV.SV.NOMINAL - cardiac and stroke INDEX show no sex difference once normalised - and it says the model would dissolve this pair into a lean-mass row if it carried body composition. It does not, so the pair stands. DECLARED METHOD LIMITATION, IN THE AUTHORS' OWN WORDS: these values may only be valid for blood volume assessed by CO rebreathing. Cross-method comparison is prohibited by pooling.md and none was attempted. WHAT FOLLOWS FROM IT: CV.PLASMA.ECF_FRACTION and CV.CENTRAL.VOLUME_NOMINAL are DERIVED from this row and become sexed with it. The operating point does NOT move: f_pv is derived so that f_pv*V_ecf/(1-Hct) = BV0, and VC0 = f_c*BV0, so V_central - VC0 is unchanged at nominal, stroke volume is unchanged, and MAP is unchanged. What changes is that the blood volume is now a measurement instead of a round number. STILL RESTING ON UNSOURCED HAEMATOCRIT: f_pv is computed with CV.HEMATOCRIT.NOMINAL = 0.45, which is itself still one of the VERIFY rows and is expected to be dimorphic. When it is sourced, f_pv must be re-derived per sex."),
    :CV_CENTRAL_CO_SENSITIVITY => Provenance("CV.CENTRAL.CO_SENSITIVITY", "(L/day)/L", 5600.0, "C", "derived", "Derived from CV.VENOUS_RETURN.SENSITIVITY and CV.CENTRAL.FRACTION.", "MOVED WITH CV.VENOUS_RETURN.SENSITIVITY ON 2026-09-03, 11520 -> 5600. Derived as G_vr / f_c = 1400 / 0.25. They are NOT independent and must not be entered as if they were. || DERIVED: G_vc = G_vr / f_central = 2880 / 0.25. This is what makes ADR 0012 stage 1 a change of variables rather than a change of behaviour - G_vc x (V_central - VC0) equals G_vr x (V_blood - BV0) identically. VERIFIED 2026-08-24 that breaking it is caught twice: a 10% error fails tools/check_closure.py outright and moves the salt-step MAP levels by 8e-5 relative, which the 1e-9 assertion in test/runtests.jl also catches. INHERITS EVERY WEAKNESS OF G_vr, which is CALIBRATED, not measured, and which ADR 0011 is in the process of replacing. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :CV_CENTRAL_FRACTION => Provenance("CV.CENTRAL.FRACTION", "unitless", 0.25, "C", "assumed", "", "PLACEHOLDER, NOT SOURCED, AND INERT AT ADR 0012 STAGE 1. Do not quote this number anywhere. Stage 1 introduces the central/peripheral partition as a change of variables only: CV.CENTRAL.CO_SENSITIVITY and CV.CENTRAL.VOLUME_NOMINAL are both derived from this value so that it cancels out of the CO relation algebraically. VERIFIED 2026-08-24 by perturbation: setting f_central to 0.30 with both derived values recomputed moves the salt-step shift by 3.7e-10 relative, i.e. not at all physiologically. 0.25 IS NOT AN ARBITRARY READABLE VALUE - it is a power of two and therefore exact in binary, which is why it deviates from the pre-partition result by only 3.5e-13 where 0.30 deviates by 3.7e-10. A non-power-of-two placeholder is equally correct algebraically but noisier. IT MUST BE SOURCED UNDER A PRE-REGISTRATION BEFORE STAGE 2, when f_central becomes posture-dependent and stops cancelling. At that point this row is load-bearing and this note must be replaced, not amended."),
    :CV_CENTRAL_VOLUME_NOMINAL_FEMALE => Provenance("CV.CENTRAL.VOLUME_NOMINAL", "L", 1.23, "C", "derived", "Derived from CV.CENTRAL.FRACTION and CV.BLOOD_VOLUME.NOMINAL.", "BECAME SEX-SPECIFIC 2026-08-27 because CV.BLOOD_VOLUME.NOMINAL did. This row is DERIVED and is not independent of it. Enforced by tools/check_closure.py, which runs per sex. DERIVED: VC0 = f_central x BV0 = 0.25 x 5. Closure constraint - if either factor changes this must be recomputed. Enforced by tools/check_closure.py."),
    :CV_CENTRAL_VOLUME_NOMINAL_MALE => Provenance("CV.CENTRAL.VOLUME_NOMINAL", "L", 1.405, "C", "derived", "Derived from CV.CENTRAL.FRACTION and CV.BLOOD_VOLUME.NOMINAL.", "BECAME SEX-SPECIFIC 2026-08-27 because CV.BLOOD_VOLUME.NOMINAL did. This row is DERIVED and is not independent of it. Enforced by tools/check_closure.py, which runs per sex. DERIVED: VC0 = f_central x BV0 = 0.25 x 5. Closure constraint - if either factor changes this must be recomputed. Enforced by tools/check_closure.py."),
    :CV_CO_NOMINAL_FEMALE => Provenance("CV.CO.NOMINAL", "L/day", 7394.0, "B", "derived", "Derived from CV.HR.NOMINAL and CV.SV.NOMINAL.", "DERIVED 2026-09-01, AND THE DEPENDENCY IS NOW THE RIGHT WAY ROUND. CO0 = HR0 * SV0 * 1440 / 1000 = 65.0 * 75 * 1.44 = 7020.0 L/day (4.88 L/min). Exact - no rounding, because a derived row that closes an identity must carry every digit, the lesson CV.SV.NOMINAL recorded when it held that position. WHAT THIS ROW WAS. 7200 L/day, a `both` row, `assumed` with an EMPTY citation since branch 6 of validation/verify_rows_prereg.md, carrying the conventional 5 L/min x 1440. Its own note said the dependency was backwards and named this as the fix: stroke volume is what echocardiography and CMR MEASURE, cardiac output is what they compute. That is now done. Pre-re-registered in validation/dependency_inversion_prereg.md, which was committed BEFORE any paper was opened and sits immediately before this change in main's history. NO COMMIT SHA IS CITED, DELIBERATELY - rebase-merge rewrites it and every such citation already in this ledger points at a commit main does not contain. Verify the ordering instead: git log --diff-filter=A -- validation/dependency_inversion_prereg.md. THE TEXTBOOK 5 L/min WAS LOW, WHICH IS DIRECTIVE 1.12 AGAIN. Measured stroke volume with measured supine resting heart rate gives 5.95 L/min in men and 4.88 in women, against a unisex conventional 5.00. Five of these rows have now been opened and the round number was materially wrong in five of them. TWO COHORTS, AND THAT IS INHERENT TO DERIVING A PRODUCT. SV0 is Petersen 2017 (UK Biobank CMR, 45-74) and HR0 is Gonzales 2023 (Fenland, supine chest sensor, 29-65). Both are supine resting measurements in health-screened adults, which is the condition this model means by nominal, but they are not the same people: Petersen's own cohort heart rate during scanning is 67-70 bpm, above Fenland's supine 63.5. Using Petersen's HR instead would give 6.4 L/min in men. The mismatch is real and is why this is `derived` rather than `reported`. OVERDETERMINATION, AND THE RULE THAT PREVENTS IT. CO = HR x SV is an identity, so at most two of the three may be sourced. HR0 and SV0 are; this one is derived. If a cardiac output is ever sourced directly it REPLACES one of them, it does not join them. NO uncertainty is carried here. The dispersion lives on SV0 (sd 20 / 14 mL) and HR0 (sd 8.9), and combining them would need their covariance, which neither source reports. The sd 1150 this row used to carry belonged to the 5 L/min convention and went with it. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13. || RE-DERIVED 2026-09-16 FROM THE DE-INDEXED CV.SV.NOMINAL, deindexing_prereg.md Stage 2. The derivation and its citation are unchanged; only the stroke volume it is evaluated at moved. 65 x 79 x 1440/1000 = 7394.4 L/day."),
    :CV_CO_NOMINAL_MALE => Provenance("CV.CO.NOMINAL", "L/day", 7947.0, "B", "derived", "Derived from CV.HR.NOMINAL and CV.SV.NOMINAL.", "DERIVED 2026-09-01, AND THE DEPENDENCY IS NOW THE RIGHT WAY ROUND. CO0 = HR0 * SV0 * 1440 / 1000 = 62.0 * 96 * 1.44 = 8570.88 L/day (5.95 L/min). Exact - no rounding, because a derived row that closes an identity must carry every digit, the lesson CV.SV.NOMINAL recorded when it held that position. WHAT THIS ROW WAS. 7200 L/day, a `both` row, `assumed` with an EMPTY citation since branch 6 of validation/verify_rows_prereg.md, carrying the conventional 5 L/min x 1440. Its own note said the dependency was backwards and named this as the fix: stroke volume is what echocardiography and CMR MEASURE, cardiac output is what they compute. That is now done. Pre-re-registered in validation/dependency_inversion_prereg.md, which was committed BEFORE any paper was opened and sits immediately before this change in main's history. NO COMMIT SHA IS CITED, DELIBERATELY - rebase-merge rewrites it and every such citation already in this ledger points at a commit main does not contain. Verify the ordering instead: git log --diff-filter=A -- validation/dependency_inversion_prereg.md. THE TEXTBOOK 5 L/min WAS LOW, WHICH IS DIRECTIVE 1.12 AGAIN. Measured stroke volume with measured supine resting heart rate gives 5.95 L/min in men and 4.88 in women, against a unisex conventional 5.00. Five of these rows have now been opened and the round number was materially wrong in five of them. TWO COHORTS, AND THAT IS INHERENT TO DERIVING A PRODUCT. SV0 is Petersen 2017 (UK Biobank CMR, 45-74) and HR0 is Gonzales 2023 (Fenland, supine chest sensor, 29-65). Both are supine resting measurements in health-screened adults, which is the condition this model means by nominal, but they are not the same people: Petersen's own cohort heart rate during scanning is 67-70 bpm, above Fenland's supine 63.5. Using Petersen's HR instead would give 6.4 L/min in men. The mismatch is real and is why this is `derived` rather than `reported`. OVERDETERMINATION, AND THE RULE THAT PREVENTS IT. CO = HR x SV is an identity, so at most two of the three may be sourced. HR0 and SV0 are; this one is derived. If a cardiac output is ever sourced directly it REPLACES one of them, it does not join them. NO uncertainty is carried here. The dispersion lives on SV0 (sd 20 / 14 mL) and HR0 (sd 8.9), and combining them would need their covariance, which neither source reports. The sd 1150 this row used to carry belonged to the 5 L/min convention and went with it. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13. || RE-DERIVED 2026-09-16 FROM THE DE-INDEXED CV.SV.NOMINAL, deindexing_prereg.md Stage 2. The derivation and its citation are unchanged; only the stroke volume it is evaluated at moved. 62 x 89 x 1440/1000 = 7946.9 L/day, which is 5.52 L/min against 5.95 before. The conventional 5 L/min is a teaching figure this ledger already distrusts, and the new value sits closer to it without having been aimed at it."),
    :CV_DBP_CENTRAL_NOMINAL => Provenance("CV.DBP.CENTRAL_NOMINAL", "mmHg", 76.0, "A", "reported", "Gomez-Sanchez M, Gomez-Sanchez L, Patino-Alonso C, Recio-Rodriguez JI, Alonso-Dominguez R, Sanchez-Aguadero N, Sanchez CL, Sanchez ER, Garcia-Ortiz L, Gomez-Marcos MA. Reference values of central blood pressure and central haemodynamic parameters and their relationship with cardiovascular risk factors in a Spanish population: early vascular ageing study. J Hypertens 2021;39(11):2147-2156.", "Central diastolic, median. 501 adults WITHOUT cardiovascular disease, random sample stratified by age and sex, mean age 56, 50.3% women, SphygmoCor pulse wave analysis. CENTRAL (aortic) pressure, which is the pressure Chemla's SV/PP relation is defined on - not brachial. The paper reports all parameters greater in men except heart rate and ejection duration, so a male/female pair is available and is NOT entered here: the abstract gives pooled medians only, and a sexed pair must come from sex-stratified values rather than by splitting a median. MEDIAN, not mean - the paper reports medians. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :CV_HEMATOCRIT_NOMINAL_FEMALE => Provenance("CV.HEMATOCRIT.NOMINAL", "unitless", 0.395, "A", "reported", "Morales-Mendoza E, Suarez-Ramos MDP, Godoy-Corredor M, Gomez-Lopera N, Combariza-Vallejo JF, Murcia J, Isaza-Ruget MA. Reference intervals for hemoglobin and hematocrit adjusted for altitude, sex, and age: a big data-based study in the Colombian population. Med Sci (Basel) 2026;14(1):136. doi:10.3390/medsci14010136. PMID 41892851. 662,024 hematocrit results from apparently healthy adults, 2022-2024, stratified by sex, age and residential altitude; LOW-ALTITUDE stratum (<1100 m a.s.l.) taken.", "CHANGED 0.45 `both` -> 0.453 male / 0.395 female ON 2026-08-28. The previous value was NOT SOURCED: citation read 'Standard physiological reference. VERIFY.' while extraction_method claimed `reported`. Pre-registered in validation/verify_rows_prereg.md at e0195f4. pooling_rule = single-source, k = 1. THE INCUMBENT 0.45 WAS ESSENTIALLY THE MALE VALUE APPLIED TO EVERYONE. That is the specific shape of the error here, and it is why the prereg fixed in advance that this row was expected to be dimorphic: androgen-driven erythropoiesis is not a body-size effect and does not dissolve into body_mass. THE VALUE: median hematocrit in the LOW-ALTITUDE stratum, men 18-64 45.3% and women 18-50 39.5%. The low-altitude stratum is taken deliberately - hematocrit rises with residential altitude, and this model carries no altitude. In the same cohort the men's median climbs 45.3 -> 48.9% from <1100 m to 2000-3000 m, so choosing the stratum matters more than the choice between plausible sources. Medians rather than means because the paper reports medians. INDEPENDENT CORROBORATION, DELIBERATELY NOT POOLED: Fulgoni VL 3rd, Agarwal S, Kellogg MD, Lieberman HR. Establishing pediatric and adult RBC reference intervals with NHANES data using piecewise regression. Am J Clin Pathol 2019;151(2):128-142. PMID 30285066. NHANES 1999-2012, n = 44,328, nationally representative US healthy population. It reports REFERENCE INTERVALS, not central values: male 20-35 y 40.2-51.0%, female 20-45 y 32.5-45.2%. Both adopted medians sit comfortably inside those intervals. It is NOT pooled with the Colombian medians - an interval and a median are not the same statistic, the populations differ, and pooling.md prohibits range-midpoint for new entries, which is the only way an interval could have been turned into a point estimate here. FULGONI INDEPENDENTLY MAKES THE TEXTBOOK POINT: it reports that suggested hematocrit and haemoglobin reference values for older adult males are SUBSTANTIALLY LOWER than current standards - a male lower limit of 35.0% at 71-79 years against a non-age-based conventional 38.8%. THE MODEL IS CURRENTLY INSENSITIVE TO THIS ROW, AND THAT IS A STRUCTURAL FACT WORTH KNOWING RATHER THAN A REASON NOT TO SOURCE IT. Haematocrit enters only through V_blood = f_pv*V_ecf/(1-Hct), and CV.PLASMA.ECF_FRACTION is DERIVED as BV0*(1-Hct)/V_ecf, so f_pv/(1-Hct) = BV0/V_ecf identically and the haematocrit cancels. Verified empirically: entering this pair left every simulated result bit-identical. It will bite the moment f_pv is sourced independently - plasma volume as a fraction of extracellular volume is directly measurable - or when anything depending on viscosity or oxygen carriage exists. Until then this row is honest provenance for a quantity the model reports but does not yet use. || THIS ROW BECAME IDENTIFIABLE ON 2026-09-02, AND HANDOVER SECTION 3.5 SAID IT WOULD NOT. That section recorded haematocrit as non-identifiable: CV.PLASMA.ECF_FRACTION is DERIVED as BV0*(1-Hct)/V_ecf, so f_pv/(1-Hct) = BV0/V_ecf and this row cancelled out of every result - verified empirically at the time, a 15% sex difference leaving every output identical to seven figures. THAT CANCELLATION IS IN THE LEVEL ONLY. Cardiovascular.jl computed V_blood as V_plasma/(1-Hct) with Hct constant, which makes red cell volume expand with plasma; over a 30 day salt step red cell mass is fixed, so that was wrong. The equation is now V_blood ~ V_plasma + Hct*BV0, and the DERIVATIVE dV_blood/dV_ecf is f_pv = BV0*(1-Hct)/V_ecf0, which depends on this row. The sourced 0.453/0.395 pair now moves a result: the male/female ECF excursion ratio goes 1.069 -> 1.182, and the part that is new is entirely haematocrit because it is the only term that stopped cancelling. ADR 0014's falsifiable test is satisfied a second time. THE GENERAL LESSON, RECORDED BECAUSE IT COST TWO WRONG CONCLUSIONS: a parameter can be non-identifiable in a model's steady-state LEVELS and identifiable in its RESPONSES, so testing whether a pair moves the answer at one operating point is not a test of identifiability at all."),
