that was wrong. The equation is now V_blood ~ V_plasma + Hct*BV0, and the DERIVATIVE dV_blood/dV_ecf is f_pv = BV0*(1-Hct)/V_ecf0, which depends on this row. The sourced 0.453/0.395 pair now moves a result: the male/female ECF excursion ratio goes 1.069 -> 1.182, and the part that is new is entirely haematocrit because it is the only term that stopped cancelling. ADR 0014's falsifiable test is satisfied a second time. THE GENERAL LESSON, RECORDED BECAUSE IT COST TWO WRONG CONCLUSIONS: a parameter can be non-identifiable in a model's steady-state LEVELS and identifiable in its RESPONSES, so testing whether a pair moves the answer at one operating point is not a test of identifiability at all."),
    :CV_HR_NOMINAL_FEMALE => Provenance("CV.HR.NOMINAL", "1/min", 65.0, "B", "derived", "Gonzales TI, Jeon JY, Lindsay T, Westgate K, Perez-Pozuelo I, Hollidge S, Wijndaele K, Rennie K, Forouhi N, Griffin S, Wareham N, Brage S. Resting heart rate is a population-level biomarker of cardiorespiratory fitness: the Fenland Study. PLoS One 2023;18(5):e0285272.", "PRECISION: rounded to 2 significant figures on 2026-08-27. The sources do not support more, and carrying digits that do not exist forced the closure gate and the test suite to chase arithmetic noise. Derived rows inherit the significant figures of their inputs and are rounded to match; the closure tolerance was moved to 1e-3 in the same change, which is still an order of magnitude tighter than the 6% error that motivated that gate. || SUPINE resting heart rate. The Fenland Study reports a POOLED supine mean of 63.5 +/- 8.9 bpm across 10865 adults aged 29-65 (5722 women, 5143 men), and separately that 'on average, RHR was 3 bpm higher in women compared to men'. Solving those two statements with the exact group sizes gives male 61.9201 and female 64.9201. DERIVED, not read off: the paper does not tabulate sex-stratified supine means in the text, so this is arithmetic on two reported quantities. CAVEAT, RECORDED: the 3 bpm sentence says 'RHR' without naming a posture, and the paper reports three (seated 67.6, supine 63.5, sleeping 56.9). It is applied to supine here because supine is the posture this model uses, but if the difference is in fact a seated or pooled-across-posture figure the split is misattributed. The POOLED value is solid; the SPLIT carries this caveat. SUPINE IS THE POSTURE THE MODEL NEEDS: CV.CO.NOMINAL is itself derived from the conventional resting 5 L/min, which is a supine figure. Measured by chest sensor after at least 1 h rest, value taken from the final 3 min of a 6 min recording - a better-defined resting condition than a clinic pulse. COHORT: general population recruited from GP lists in Cambridgeshire, NOT health-screened. Uncertainty carries the POOLED SD; the paper gives no sex-stratified SD. CORROBORATION SEARCHED FOR AND NOT FOUND, recorded so it is not re-searched: three query families over resting heart rate by sex - reference/normal values, sex-differences, and population cohorts - returned risk-association studies (RHR as a predictor of stroke, cancer mortality, atrial fibrillation) rather than reference-value studies reporting sex-stratified resting means. This is a SINGLE-SOURCE pair and pooling.md rule 6 applies: say so, do not dress it as consensus. It is still better than the shared male-nominal value it replaces, because the pairing rule makes the female case explicit rather than implicit. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :CV_HR_NOMINAL_MALE => Provenance("CV.HR.NOMINAL", "1/min", 62.0, "B", "derived", "Gonzales TI, Jeon JY, Lindsay T, Westgate K, Perez-Pozuelo I, Hollidge S, Wijndaele K, Rennie K, Forouhi N, Griffin S, Wareham N, Brage S. Resting heart rate is a population-level biomarker of cardiorespiratory fitness: the Fenland Study. PLoS One 2023;18(5):e0285272.", "PRECISION: rounded to 2 significant figures on 2026-08-27. The sources do not support more, and carrying digits that do not exist forced the closure gate and the test suite to chase arithmetic noise. Derived rows inherit the significant figures of their inputs and are rounded to match; the closure tolerance was moved to 1e-3 in the same change, which is still an order of magnitude tighter than the 6% error that motivated that gate. || SUPINE resting heart rate. The Fenland Study reports a POOLED supine mean of 63.5 +/- 8.9 bpm across 10865 adults aged 29-65 (5722 women, 5143 men), and separately that 'on average, RHR was 3 bpm higher in women compared to men'. Solving those two statements with the exact group sizes gives male 61.9201 and female 64.9201. DERIVED, not read off: the paper does not tabulate sex-stratified supine means in the text, so this is arithmetic on two reported quantities. CAVEAT, RECORDED: the 3 bpm sentence says 'RHR' without naming a posture, and the paper reports three (seated 67.6, supine 63.5, sleeping 56.9). It is applied to supine here because supine is the posture this model uses, but if the difference is in fact a seated or pooled-across-posture figure the split is misattributed. The POOLED value is solid; the SPLIT carries this caveat. SUPINE IS THE POSTURE THE MODEL NEEDS: CV.CO.NOMINAL is itself derived from the conventional resting 5 L/min, which is a supine figure. Measured by chest sensor after at least 1 h rest, value taken from the final 3 min of a 6 min recording - a better-defined resting condition than a clinic pulse. COHORT: general population recruited from GP lists in Cambridgeshire, NOT health-screened. Uncertainty carries the POOLED SD; the paper gives no sex-stratified SD. CORROBORATION SEARCHED FOR AND NOT FOUND, recorded so it is not re-searched: three query families over resting heart rate by sex - reference/normal values, sex-differences, and population cohorts - returned risk-association studies (RHR as a predictor of stroke, cancer mortality, atrial fibrillation) rather than reference-value studies reporting sex-stratified resting means. This is a SINGLE-SOURCE pair and pooling.md rule 6 applies: say so, do not dress it as consensus. It is still better than the shared male-nominal value it replaces, because the pairing rule makes the female case explicit rather than implicit. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :CV_MAP_SETPOINT => Provenance("CV.MAP.SETPOINT", "mmHg", 87.0, "A", "derived", "Gomez-Sanchez M, Gomez-Sanchez L, Patino-Alonso C, Recio-Rodriguez JI, Alonso-Dominguez R, Sanchez-Aguadero N, Sanchez CL, Sanchez ER, Garcia-Ortiz L, Gomez-Marcos MA. Reference values of central blood pressure and central haemodynamic parameters and their relationship with cardiovascular risk factors in a Spanish population: early vascular ageing study. J Hypertens 2021;39(11):2147-2156.", "CHANGED 93.0 -> 87.0 ON 2026-08-27, AND THE OLD VALUE WAS A CATEGORY ERROR. 93 was the textbook BRACHIAL 120/80 convention - 80 + 40/3 = 93.33, matching to 0.3 mmHg - carried under the citation 'Standard physiological reference. VERIFY.' It was never verified, and this is that verification failing. NOW DERIVED from the sourced CENTRAL pressure: MAP = DBP + PP/3 = 76 + 33/3 = 87.0, on 501 adults without cardiovascular disease, age- and sex-stratified random sample. WHY CENTRAL RATHER THAN BRACHIAL. Chemla's SV/PP compliance relation is defined on aortic pressure, so the reconstruction needs a central reference. Mixing a brachial MAP with a central pulse pressure produced a form factor of 0.515 - IMPOSSIBLE, since the fraction of pulse pressure above mean must be below 0.5 when diastole is longer than systole. That contradiction is what exposed the mixing. TRUE MEAN PRESSURE IS NEARLY AMPLIFICATION-INVARIANT: pulse pressure amplifies distally, mean pressure barely does. So there is one MAP, not a central and a brachial one, and the fixed 1/3 form factor giving 87 centrally and 89.8 brachially is an artefact of the formula rather than physiology - which is exactly what reconstruct.jl says about treating the form factor as constant. STILL AN INITIALISATION VALUE AND A VALIDATION TARGET, NOT A SETPOINT THE MODEL ENFORCES. Arterial pressure remains an output of the loop. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :CV_PLASMA_ECF_FRACTION_FEMALE => Provenance("CV.PLASMA.ECF_FRACTION", "unitless", 0.2044, "B", "derived", "Derived to close the loop at nominal.", "RE-DERIVED 2026-08-28 when CV.HEMATOCRIT.NOMINAL became a sourced male/female pair. f_pv = BV0*(1-Hct)/V_ecf, so it moves with either parent. NOTE the identity that makes haematocrit currently non-identifiable in this model: f_pv/(1-Hct) = BV0/V_ecf, so the Hct in the numerator cancels the one in the denominator of the blood-volume equation. BECAME SEX-SPECIFIC 2026-08-27 because CV.BLOOD_VOLUME.NOMINAL did. This row is DERIVED and is not independent of it. Enforced by tools/check_closure.py, which runs per sex. PRECISION: rounded to 4 significant figures on 2026-08-27. The sources do not support more, and carrying digits that do not exist forced the closure gate and the test suite to chase arithmetic noise. Derived rows inherit the significant figures of their inputs and are rounded to match; the closure tolerance was moved to 1e-3 in the same change, which is still an order of magnitude tighter than the 6% error that motivated that gate. || DERIVED and NOT rounded: f_pv = BV0 x (1-Hct) / V_ecf = 5.0 x 0.55 / 14.56 = 0.188874. Rounding to 0.20 put blood volume 0.295 L above nominal, which through the venous return gain produced MAP = 104 rather than 93 mmHg. The conventional textbook figure is 0.25; the discrepancy comes from using the measured ECF fraction (20.8% of body mass) rather than the textbook 20%. This is a CLOSURE constraint - if Hct BF.ECF.MASS_FRACTION or CV.BLOOD_VOLUME.NOMINAL change this must be recomputed. Enforced by tools/check_closure.py. || THE DERIVATION IS UNCHANGED AND ITS MEANING IS NOT, 2026-09-02. This row is still DERIVED as BV0*(1-Hct)/V_ecf and its value has not moved. What changed is that it is now the WHOLE of dV_blood/dV_ecf rather than one factor in f_pv/(1-Hct). Cardiovascular.jl stopped letting red cell volume expand with plasma - V_blood is V_plasma + Hct*BV0, not V_plasma/(1-Hct) - so the blood volume response to an ECF change is exactly this fraction. It fell 0.386 -> 0.211 as a sensitivity, which is a factor of 1/(1-Hct) = 1.83, and it closed 1.83x of the 2.7-5.2x discrepancy ADR 0013's falsifiable test measured against seven human primaries. SOURCING THIS ROW INDEPENDENTLY IS NOW WORTH MORE THAN IT WAS: plasma volume as a fraction of extracellular volume is measurable, and it is no longer only the level that depends on it. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :CV_PLASMA_ECF_FRACTION_MALE => Provenance("CV.PLASMA.ECF_FRACTION", "unitless", 0.2111, "B", "derived", "Derived to close the loop at nominal.", "RE-DERIVED 2026-08-28 when CV.HEMATOCRIT.NOMINAL became a sourced male/female pair. f_pv = BV0*(1-Hct)/V_ecf, so it moves with either parent. NOTE the identity that makes haematocrit currently non-identifiable in this model: f_pv/(1-Hct) = BV0/V_ecf, so the Hct in the numerator cancels the one in the denominator of the blood-volume equation. BECAME SEX-SPECIFIC 2026-08-27 because CV.BLOOD_VOLUME.NOMINAL did. This row is DERIVED and is not independent of it. Enforced by tools/check_closure.py, which runs per sex. PRECISION: rounded to 4 significant figures on 2026-08-27. The sources do not support more, and carrying digits that do not exist forced the closure gate and the test suite to chase arithmetic noise. Derived rows inherit the significant figures of their inputs and are rounded to match; the closure tolerance was moved to 1e-3 in the same change, which is still an order of magnitude tighter than the 6% error that motivated that gate. || DERIVED and NOT rounded: f_pv = BV0 x (1-Hct) / V_ecf = 5.0 x 0.55 / 14.56 = 0.188874. Rounding to 0.20 put blood volume 0.295 L above nominal, which through the venous return gain produced MAP = 104 rather than 93 mmHg. The conventional textbook figure is 0.25; the discrepancy comes from using the measured ECF fraction (20.8% of body mass) rather than the textbook 20%. This is a CLOSURE constraint - if Hct BF.ECF.MASS_FRACTION or CV.BLOOD_VOLUME.NOMINAL change this must be recomputed. Enforced by tools/check_closure.py. || THE DERIVATION IS UNCHANGED AND ITS MEANING IS NOT, 2026-09-02. This row is still DERIVED as BV0*(1-Hct)/V_ecf and its value has not moved. What changed is that it is now the WHOLE of dV_blood/dV_ecf rather than one factor in f_pv/(1-Hct). Cardiovascular.jl stopped letting red cell volume expand with plasma - V_blood is V_plasma + Hct*BV0, not V_plasma/(1-Hct) - so the blood volume response to an ECF change is exactly this fraction. It fell 0.386 -> 0.211 as a sensitivity, which is a factor of 1/(1-Hct) = 1.83, and it closed 1.83x of the 2.7-5.2x discrepancy ADR 0013's falsifiable test measured against seven human primaries. SOURCING THIS ROW INDEPENDENTLY IS NOW WORTH MORE THAN IT WAS: plasma volume as a fraction of extracellular volume is measurable, and it is no longer only the level that depends on it. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :CV_PP_CENTRAL_NOMINAL => Provenance("CV.PP.CENTRAL_NOMINAL", "mmHg", 33.0, "A", "derived", "Derived from CV.SBP.CENTRAL_NOMINAL and CV.DBP.CENTRAL_NOMINAL.", "DERIVED: PP = SBP - DBP = 109 - 76 = 33 mmHg, which reproduces the central pulse pressure of 33 mmHg the paper reports independently. That agreement is a check on the extraction, not a coincidence. Closure constraint. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :CV_PULSE_FORM_FACTOR => Provenance("CV.PULSE.FORM_FACTOR", "unitless", 0.3333, "C", "assumed", "", "ASSUMED at the conventional one third, which is the familiar MAP = DBP + PP/3. Discharges the TODO in reconstruct.jl, which carried this as a HARDCODED default of 0.4 and demanded a ledger row before the function was used for anything - directive 1.4 is now satisfied. NAME AND JUSTIFICATION CORRECTED 2026-08-27; THE VALUE IS UNCHANGED AND WAS ALWAYS RIGHT. This row was entered as 'Fraction of pulse pressure lying ABOVE mean pressure', and 0.3333 is the fraction lying BELOW it. Against the tier A central pressures in this same ledger - SBP 109, DBP 76, PP 33, MAP 87 - the fraction above the mean is (109-87)/33 = 0.667 and the fraction below is (87-76)/33 = 0.333. The old note's reasoning was inverted in the same way: it said 'the fraction of pulse pressure above mean must be below 0.5 because diastole is longer than systole', and diastole being longer is exactly why MAP sits NEARER DIASTOLIC, which makes the fraction BELOW the mean the one that must be under 0.5. The 0.515 that exposed the brachial/central mixing was (93-76)/33, a below-mean fraction, so the impossibility argument itself was correct and only its label was wrong. WHY IT MATTERED. check_closure.py uses this row as MAP = DBP + k*PP, i.e. with the CORRECT below-mean meaning, and passes. reconstruct.jl defined its form_factor as the fraction ABOVE the mean and computed SBP = MAP + form_factor*PP. Wiring the ledger row into that function under the shared name 'form factor' would have produced SBP 98.0 and DBP 65.0 against the sourced 109 and 76 - both wrong by PP/3 = 11 mmHg, in opposite directions - and NO GATE WOULD HAVE CAUGHT IT, because the closure check never reaches reconstruct.jl. Found while connecting that file, not by any check. reconstruct.jl now takes k_below explicitly and names the convention in its signature. NOT CONSTANT IN REALITY, and reconstruct.jl calls treating it as constant the single largest error source in the reconstruction: it varies with heart rate, arterial stiffness, disease state, and - as the 87 versus 89.8 discrepancy shows - with measurement site. Source it before any claim about pulse pressure as an endpoint."),
    :CV_SBP_CENTRAL_NOMINAL => Provenance("CV.SBP.CENTRAL_NOMINAL", "mmHg", 109.0, "A", "reported", "Gomez-Sanchez M, Gomez-Sanchez L, Patino-Alonso C, Recio-Rodriguez JI, Alonso-Dominguez R, Sanchez-Aguadero N, Sanchez CL, Sanchez ER, Garcia-Ortiz L, Gomez-Marcos MA. Reference values of central blood pressure and central haemodynamic parameters and their relationship with cardiovascular risk factors in a Spanish population: early vascular ageing study. J Hypertens 2021;39(11):2147-2156.", "Central systolic, median. 501 adults WITHOUT cardiovascular disease, random sample stratified by age and sex, mean age 56, 50.3% women, SphygmoCor pulse wave analysis. CENTRAL (aortic) pressure, which is the pressure Chemla's SV/PP relation is defined on - not brachial. The paper reports all parameters greater in men except heart rate and ejection duration, so a male/female pair is available and is NOT entered here: the abstract gives pooled medians only, and a sexed pair must come from sex-stratified values rather than by splitting a median. MEDIAN, not mean - the paper reports medians. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :CV_SV_NOMINAL_FEMALE => Provenance("CV.SV.NOMINAL", "mL", 79.0, "A", "reported", "Petersen SE, Aung N, Sanghvi MM, Zemrak F, Fung K, Paiva JM, Francis JM, Khanji MY, Lukaschuk E, Lee AM, Carapella V, Kim YJ, Leeson P, Piechnik SK, Neubauer S. Reference ranges for cardiac structure and function using cardiovascular magnetic resonance (CMR) in Caucasians from the UK Biobank population cohort. J Cardiovasc Magn Reson 2017;19(1):18.", "SOURCED 2026-09-01, AND THE DEPENDENCY NOW RUNS THE WAY THE MEASUREMENT DOES. Was 80.7 / 77.0 mL, DERIVED from CV.CO.NOMINAL and CV.HR.NOMINAL - a measured quantity computed from an `assumed` one whose citation was empty. Pre-re-registered in validation/dependency_inversion_prereg.md, which was committed BEFORE any paper was opened and sits immediately before this change in main's history. NO COMMIT SHA IS CITED, DELIBERATELY - rebase-merge rewrites it and every such citation already in this ledger points at a commit main does not contain. Verify the ordering instead: git log --diff-filter=A -- validation/dependency_inversion_prereg.md. HANDOVER section 4 item 1. NOW REPORTED: Petersen 2017, UK Biobank CMR. Left ventricular stroke volume 75 +/- 14 mL in 432 women. Cohort: 804 participants remaining after exclusion for non-Caucasian ethnicity, known cardiovascular disease and any condition affecting chamber size or function, of whom 800 (368 men, 432 women) have ventricular measurements. Ages 45-74. SINGLE SOURCE, k = 1, pooling.md rule 6 - stated plainly and not dressed as consensus. TECHNIQUE, RECORDED BECAUSE IT IS NOT INTERCHANGEABLE. Cardiovascular magnetic resonance, steady-state free precession at 1.5 T, manual contouring, PAPILLARY MUSCLES AND TRABECULATIONS INCLUDED IN THE LV VOLUME. Zhan 2024 (Circ Cardiovasc Imaging 2024;17(2):e016090, 254 studies screened, 12,812 healthy adults) exists because that convention moves LV volumes, and it pools the two conventions SEPARATELY rather than across them. pooling.md prohibits pooling across incompatible measurement methods, so this row is single-source BY RULE and not by neglect. WHAT WAS SCREENED AND WHY IT WAS NOT USED, so the next attempt does not repeat it. (1) Zhan 2024, the meta-analysis pooling.md rule 1 would have preferred: reports lower and upper reference LIMITS, indexed to body surface area. range-midpoint is prohibited for new entries and the model carries no BSA, so it fails twice. (2) Luu 2022, Canadian Alliance for Healthy Hearts and Minds (J Cardiovasc Magn Reson 2022;24(1):2), n = 3206, multi-ethnic, anatomically correct contouring - LARGER AND BETTER DESIGNED THAN THE ROW ADOPTED, and reports LVSV INDEXED ONLY (46 +/- 8 male, 42 +/- 7 female mL/m2). Converting it needs a BSA formula, which this model does not carry and which the pre-registration explicitly refused to introduce as a side effect. It also contours papillary muscles into LV MASS rather than volume, so it could not be pooled with Petersen in any case. (3) Patel 2021 WASE (PMID 34044105) paywalled, no numbers in the abstract - recorded against CV.CO.NOMINAL since 2026-08-31 and still unopened. (4) Addetia 2022 WASE 3D echo (J Am Soc Echocardiogr 2022;35(5):449-459), n = 1589 - indexed only, and 3D echo is a different technique. (5) Salton 2002 Framingham Offspring (n = 142) and Le Ven 2016 (n = 434, mean age 26) - both report the direction but not absolute stroke volume in the record read. NOT NORMALISED TO THE REFERENCE MASS, AND THAT IS RECORDED DEBT RATHER THAN A CHOICE. This value is EXTENSIVE and is multiplied by size_factor = body_mass / BF.BODY_MASS.REFERENCE, so it should be stated at 70 kg. Petersen reports body weight by AGE GROUP (71, 71, 69 kg) but NOT by sex, so the cohort mean is essentially the reference mass while the male and female subgroups plainly are not. The pre-registration fixed the rule in advance: normalise where cohort mass is reported by sex, otherwise enter as reported and record the mismatch. So this pair still carries a body-size component, and in the ensemble - where mass is sampled by sex - that component is counted twice. Fixing it needs sex-stratified cohort mass or a BSA row. AND THE PREDICTION FIXED IN ADVANCE WAS HALF WRONG, WHICH IS THE USEFUL PART. The previous note on this row cited Katori 1979 for NO significant sex difference in stroke INDEX and concluded the dimorphism is body size, not the heart. Petersen indexes to BSA and the difference SURVIVES: 49 +/- 10 male against 45 +/- 8 female mL/m2, a 9% male excess where the absolute difference is 28%. Luu 2022 finds the same on 3206 people (46 vs 42), Salton 2002 states volumetric measures remain greater in men after BSA adjustment, and Le Ven 2016 states all gender differences remained after normalisation. Four independent cohorts. So body size carries roughly two thirds of the difference and about a third is not size - the pair was expected to collapse the way ECF per kg did when blood volume and haematocrit were sourced, and it does not. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13. || DE-INDEXED 2026-09-16, deindexing_prereg.md Stage 2, AND IT NEEDED NO NEW SOURCE. 96 -> 89 mL (male), 75 -> 79 (female). Petersen reports the SAME cohort both ways - absolute 96 and 75 mL, indexed 49 +/- 10 and 45 +/- 8 mL/m2 - so the implied cohort body surface area is their quotient: 1.959 m2 in the men and 1.667 in the women. The model's reference individual has 1.824 and 1.751. The entered value is now the indexed figure at the model's own reference body, 49 x 1.824 and 45 x 1.751, at the two significant figures the indexed pair carries. || THIS ROW DESCRIBED ITS OWN DEFECT AND THE FIX IS THE ONE IT ASKED FOR. Its previous note: 'this pair still carries a body-size component, and in the ensemble - where mass is sampled by sex - that component is counted twice. Fixing it needs sex-stratified cohort mass or a BSA row.' The BSA row landed on 2026-09-05 and did nothing for eleven days. || THE PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTION HELD, AND IT COULD HAVE FAILED. deindexing_prereg.md section 6 fixed in advance that the male value must move DOWN and the female UP, because the reference mass is 70 kg for both sexes - light for a man, heavy for a woman. Measured: -6.9 and +5.0 percent. Had both moved the same way the reference BSA pair would have been wrong and branch B5 would have stopped the stage. || AND IT HALVES THE APPARENT SEX DIFFERENCE. The absolute pair carried 28 percent, of which Petersen's own indexing says 9 percent survives body size. This pair carries 13 percent - the indexed 9 plus the 4 percent difference in reference surface area, which is height alone at a shared 70 kg. The rest was Petersen's men being larger than Petersen's women, which the ensemble was already counting through sexed mass sampling. || WHAT IS UNCHANGED: the source, the cohort, the technique and the screening record. Luu 2022 is still unusable - it contours papillary muscles into LV MASS rather than volume, so it cannot be pooled with Petersen whatever the indexing - and Zhan 2024 still reports reference LIMITS, which pooling.md prohibits for new entries. HANDOVER section 4 item 10 claimed BSA would unlock both. It unlocks neither."),
