    :CV_TPR_NOMINAL_FEMALE => Provenance("CV.TPR.NOMINAL", "mmHg/(L/day)", 0.01177, "B", "derived", "Derived from CV.MAP.SETPOINT and CV.CO.NOMINAL.", "RECOMPUTED AND SEXED 2026-09-01. TPR = MAP0 / CO0 = 87.0 / 7020.0 = 0.012393 mmHg/(L/day). Was 0.012083, a `both` row, because CO0 was a `both` row. CO0 became a male/female pair when stroke volume was sourced (Petersen 2017), so this row is dimorphic with it - as its own prior note and check_closure.py both said it would be. DEFINITIONAL GIVEN THE OTHER TWO. MAP is an OUTPUT of the closed loop; this is the resistance that makes the nominal operating point consistent, not a regulated setpoint. Because it is derived FROM MAP, arterial pressure at the operating point is unchanged by construction when CO0 moves - what moves is the volume excursion needed to produce a given pressure change, since dMAP/dV = TPR * G_vr. PRECISION: 5 significant figures, matching the 0.012083 it replaces. The identity closes to better than 1e-4 relative, an order of magnitude inside the 1e-3 closure tolerance. TPR CARRIES THE RECIPROCAL OF BODY SIZE in Cardiovascular.jl (TPR0 / sz) so that MAP stays intensive. See src/scaling.jl. That is unchanged. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13. || RE-DERIVED 2026-09-16 FROM THE DE-INDEXED CV.SV.NOMINAL, deindexing_prereg.md Stage 2. The derivation and its citation are unchanged; only the stroke volume it is evaluated at moved. 87/7394 = 0.0117663."),
    :CV_TPR_NOMINAL_MALE => Provenance("CV.TPR.NOMINAL", "mmHg/(L/day)", 0.01095, "B", "derived", "Derived from CV.MAP.SETPOINT and CV.CO.NOMINAL.", "RECOMPUTED AND SEXED 2026-09-01. TPR = MAP0 / CO0 = 87.0 / 8570.88 = 0.010151 mmHg/(L/day). Was 0.012083, a `both` row, because CO0 was a `both` row. CO0 became a male/female pair when stroke volume was sourced (Petersen 2017), so this row is dimorphic with it - as its own prior note and check_closure.py both said it would be. DEFINITIONAL GIVEN THE OTHER TWO. MAP is an OUTPUT of the closed loop; this is the resistance that makes the nominal operating point consistent, not a regulated setpoint. Because it is derived FROM MAP, arterial pressure at the operating point is unchanged by construction when CO0 moves - what moves is the volume excursion needed to produce a given pressure change, since dMAP/dV = TPR * G_vr. PRECISION: 5 significant figures, matching the 0.012083 it replaces. The identity closes to better than 1e-4 relative, an order of magnitude inside the 1e-3 closure tolerance. TPR CARRIES THE RECIPROCAL OF BODY SIZE in Cardiovascular.jl (TPR0 / sz) so that MAP stays intensive. See src/scaling.jl. That is unchanged. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13. || RE-DERIVED 2026-09-16 FROM THE DE-INDEXED CV.SV.NOMINAL, deindexing_prereg.md Stage 2. The derivation and its citation are unchanged; only the stroke volume it is evaluated at moved. 87/7947 = 0.0109475. TPR carries the reciprocal of the cardiac output, so a lower CO at an unchanged MAP means a higher resistance - the operating point is preserved by construction and MAP does not move."),
    :CV_VENOUS_RETURN_SENSITIVITY => Provenance("CV.VENOUS_RETURN.SENSITIVITY", "(L/day)/L", 1400.0, "B", "derived", "Diaz-Canestro C, Pentz B, Sehgal A, Montero D. Blood withdrawal acutely impairs cardiac filling, output and aerobic capacity in proportion to induced hypovolemia in middle-aged and older women. Appl Physiol Nutr Metab 2022;47(1):75-82. doi:10.1139/apnm-2021-0196. PMID 34875180. n = 30 healthy women. Supporting: Fortney 1983 PMID 6629925 (both limbs, n=5 healthy men); Bundgaard-Nielsen 2010 PMID 20545713 (n=20 healthy supine); Nguyen 1988 PMID 2901018 (n=8 healthy men); Kanstrup 1982 PMID 7096143.", "SOURCED IN HEALTHY HUMANS 2026-09-03, 2880 -> 1400, `calibrated` -> `derived`. Pre-registered in validation/venous_return_human_prereg.md; reproduce with python validation/venous_return_human_extract.py. HANDOVER section 4 item 2 and section 7. || THE EARLIER SEARCH MISSED IT BY LOOKING FOR THE WRONG OBJECT. This row is dCO/dV_blood, NOT a compliance in mL/mmHg. Section 3.9 records a whole line of work withdrawn for composing a compliance and a resistance into it while treating right atrial pressure as fixed. Blood volume can be changed by a KNOWN amount in healthy people - withdrawal, or plasma expansion - and cardiac output measured, so the composite is directly measurable in a performable paradigm. || THE VALUE, from the resting withdrawal limb. Diaz-Canestro C, Pentz B, Sehgal A, Montero D. Appl Physiol Nutr Metab 2022;47(1):75-82, PMID 34875180: 30 healthy women aged 47-77, non-smokers, non-obese, blood volume 3.8-6.6 L; a standard 10% reduction of blood volume (0.5 +/- 0.1 L withdrawn, haematocrit unchanged) reduced left ventricular end-diastolic volume and stroke volume by at least 10% AT REST, P <= 0.009. On a female cardiac output of 4.88 L/min that is 0.98 (L/min)/L = 1404 (L/day)/L, entered as 1400. The paper's title is 'in proportion to induced hypovolemia' - the relationship IS the subject, directive 1.7. || IT IS AN UPPER BOUND AND IS ENTERED AS ONE. A study reporting STROKE volume bounds dCO/dV from above, because heart rate can compensate; the pre-registration said so before the search. Three expansion studies all indicate the true resting value is at or below it and none resolves how far: Bundgaard-Nielsen 2010 (PMID 20545713, n=20 healthy supine) gave 200 mL of colloid and NO subject raised stroke volume by 10%, with cardiac output and heart rate both unchanged; Nguyen 1988 (PMID 2901018, n=8 healthy men) expanded intravascular volume 10-11% with hypertonic saline for small NON-SIGNIFICANT changes in stroke volume and cardiac index; Kanstrup 1982 (PMID 7096143) found cardiac output rose after 700 mL but reports no resting magnitude. || ASYMMETRY, MEASURED, AND IT NEARLY FAILED THE PRE-REGISTERED TEST. Fortney SM, Wenger CB, Bove JR, Nadel ER. J Appl Physiol 1983;55(3):884-90, PMID 6629925 is the only study giving BOTH limbs in the SAME 5 healthy men under the same protocol: -490 mL gave -2.2 L/min and +440 mL gave +1.0 L/min, i.e. 4.49 against 2.27 (L/min)/L, a ratio of 1.98 against a failure threshold of 2 fixed before the numbers. It passes by 1 percent. A LINEAR SYMMETRIC GAIN IS AT THE EDGE OF WHAT THIS EVIDENCE SUPPORTS, and the direction is the expected one - the heart sits nearer the flat part of its filling curve above normovolemia than below. Those figures are at 65-70 percent of VO2max and are recorded as evidence about the FORM; the pre-registration fixed that resting values govern, because the salt step is a resting protocol. || TWO INDEPENDENT LINES AGREE AND THAT IS THE RESULT. Chronic sodium balance implies 1012-1941 (HANDOVER 3.7, 3.8) from human pressure and volume responses. Acute volume manipulation in healthy people gives 1400. The two share no data, no subjects, no measurement and no timescale. THE MODEL WAS 2.06x TOO STIFF, which is inside the 1.5-2.1x that section 3.7 estimated from the salt data alone. || NOT CIRCULAR, AND CHECKABLE. The pre-registration forbade choosing a value to land the salt step in the human window, because the salt data are the TEST and may not also be the estimator. 1400 does NOT land it in the window; a value chosen to pass would have been nearer 1700. || STILL UNSOURCED IN HEALTHY HUMANS: systemic venous compliance in mL/mmHg, mean systemic filling pressure, and resistance to venous return. The one human compliance value found is Takatsu 1989 (PMID 2545936), 2.3 +/- 0.7 mL/mmHg/kg - close to the anaesthetised dog and pig values already held - but its cohort is 56 CARDIAC PATIENTS graded by NYHA class and even class I is cardiac patients. The pre-registration excluded NYHA class I in advance. Those three quantities are no longer NEEDED, because the composite was sourced directly. || PREVIOUS NOTE PRESERVED: CALIBRATED, not measured. Originating model: Guyton 1972. The Frank-Starling and venous return relationships are E1; this linearised sensitivity around the operating point is a fitted constant. Second most consequential unmeasured number after the pressure natriuresis slope - together these two set the loop gain. || THE TEST THAT SHOULD SET THIS ROW HAS NOW BEEN RUN, AND IT GIVES A NUMBER: 2880 SHOULD BE ABOUT 554. 2026-09-02, from ADR 0013's falsifiable test - validation/ecf_salt_response_extract.py. This row sets dMAP/dV_ecf, which is 11.285 mmHg/L at the 70 kg reference and scales as 1/mass. van den Bosch 2021 (n = 70 healthy men, ECFV by iothalamate and blood pressure in the SAME subjects, intake verified by 24 h urinary sodium) gives 1.885 mmHg/L at 80.6 kg, which is 2.170 at the 70 kg reference and corresponds to G_vr = 554. THE MODEL IS 5.2x TOO STIFF ON PRESSURE PER UNIT VOLUME. This is measurable independently of G_pn: an 8x change in this row moves the salt-step PRESSURE response by 0.12% while moving the VOLUME response exactly inversely, so the two parameters are orthogonal and each is identified by its own human datum. DO NOT SIMPLY REFIT IT TO 554. This row is `calibrated` and HANDOVER section 4 item 1 is to REPLACE it with sourced venous compliance - Pmsf, right atrial pressure, stressed versus unstressed volume. 554 is the target that work now has to explain, not a value to enter; entering it would swap one fitted constant for another and lose the falsifiable test in the process. DECLARED CONFLICT: Heer 2000 and Heer 2009 report no body-mass or total-body-water rise on high sodium, which would reverse the sign of this discrepancy. See ADR 0013. || EVIDENCE BASE WIDENED THE SAME DAY, AND THE TARGET IS A RANGE, NOT 554. Two further sweeps (24 queries, 162 records) because the verdict rested on one cohort. The body-weight limb now has FOUR independent groups - van den Bosch 2021 (n=70, 0.729 kg/100 mmol), Rorije 2018 (Anesthesiology 2018;128(2):352-360, PMID 29206647, n=12, +2.5 kg 95% CI 1.7-3.2 with blood pressure UNCHANGED), Foo 1998 (Clin Sci 1998;95(2):157-164, PMID 9680497, n=18, +0.45 +/- 0.69 kg) and Heer 2000 (n=32, no increase, entered as zero and that is an interpretation of a null). n-weighted pooled 0.572 kg/100 mmol over n=132, or 0.755 excluding Heer. THE TRACER LIMB GIVES 0.553 L, so two independent methods agree to 4% and the pre-registered 1 kg = 1 L conversion is corroborated rather than assumed - prereg section 8 said it would be falsified if they diverged. Pairing the META-ANALYTIC pressure (1.70-2.30 mmHg/100 mmol) with the pooled volume gives 2.97-4.16 mmHg/L, so the model is 2.7-3.8x too stiff and G_vr's target is 758-1062; the within-subject van den Bosch pair gives 5.2x and 554 as the harshest reading. USE THE POOLED RANGE: the mechanistic volume studies are underpowered for the 2 mmHg the meta-analyses detect - Kirkendall 1976 (PMID 1249473, n=8, FOUR WEEKS per level, the closest protocol to this model's 30 days), Rorije 2018 and Taurio 2023 (PMID 36708156, n=510, the largest dataset found) all report NO blood pressure change at all, and taking that at face value would drive this row to zero. PART OF THE DISCREPANCY IS NOT THIS ROW: Cardiovascular.jl computes V_blood as V_plasma/(1-Hct) with Hct constant, so red cell volume expands with plasma over a 30 day salt step when red cell mass is fixed. dV_blood/dV_ecf should be f_pv = 0.211, not f_pv/(1-Hct) = 0.386 - a factor of 1.83 that would take the model ratio to 6.17 and leave this row needing only 1.5-2.8x, i.e. about 1000-1950. DIAGNOSED, NOT YET RUN. || TARGET REVISED 2026-09-02 AFTER THE RED CELL CORRECTION: 1012-1941, NOT 758-1062. Cardiovascular.jl stopped letting red cell volume expand with plasma, which moved dMAP/dV_ecf from 11.285 to 6.173 and closed 1.83x of the discrepancy on its own. The residual attributable to THIS row is therefore 1.5-2.1x rather than 2.7-5.2x. Required ratio 2.97-4.16 mmHg/L at the 70 kg reference gives 2880 * ratio / 6.173 = 1385-1941, or 1012 on the harshest within-subject reading. AND THE ENDPOINT IS NOW MEASURED: with G_pn = 51 (ADR 0013) AND this row near 1400 the model returns dMAP = 1.944 mmHg and dV_ecf = 0.648 L per 100 mmol/day at a ratio of 3.001, against human 1.70-2.30, 0.553-0.572 and 2.97-4.16 - inside all three at once. NEITHER CORRECTION ALONE LANDS: ADR 0013 alone gives the right pressure with dV 0.315 (1.8x too small), and this row alone gives the right ratio with dMAP 4.958 (2.5x too large). DO NOT ENTER 1400. It is illustrative. This row must be REPLACED by sourced venous compliance, and that table is the target the sourced relation has to EXPLAIN. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13. || THE HUMAN BAND THIS ROW IS COMPARED AGAINST MOVED 2026-09-16. ecf_salt_response_extract.py de-indexed van den Bosch's extracellular volume with ONE body surface area where the paper prints one per arm, understating the human expansion by 9 percent. The tracer limb goes 0.553 -> 0.602 L per 100 mmol/day and the human dMAP/dV_ecf band 2.97-4.16 -> 2.82-4.02 mmHg/L. THIS ROW'S VALUE IS UNCHANGED and was not refitted to the new band - that would be fitting a parameter to a target the same pass moved. ADR 0013 addendum, validation/ecf_deindex_prereg.md."),
    :CV_VOLUME_NATRIURETIC_GAIN => Provenance("CV.VOLUME.NATRIURETIC_GAIN", "(mEq/day)/L", 585.0, "B", "derived", "ROUTE B, ACUTE ISOTONIC EXPANSION, TWO HUMAN STUDIES. Lobo DN, Stanga Z, Simpson JA, Anderson JA, Rowlands BJ, Allison SP. Clin Sci (Lond) 2001;101(2):173-9. PMID 11473492. 10 healthy men, 2 L of 0.9% saline over 1 h, double-blind crossover: 95 mmol urinary sodium over the 6 h after infusion, and a 7.5% fall in haematocrit which gives the intravascular share of the load DIRECTLY with red cell mass fixed. || Jensen JM, Mose FH, Bech JN, Nielsen S, Pedersen EB. BMC Nephrol 2013;14:202. PMID 24067081. 23 healthy subjects, randomised crossover on a standardised diet, 0.9% saline 23 mL/kg: fractional sodium excretion rose 123%. || CROSS-CHECKED against ROUTE A, the chronic steady-state constraint built from the meta-analytic human pressure response and the pooled human ECF response already extracted in this repo. || CORROBORATING the timescale: Drummer C, Heer M, Baisch F, Blomqvist CG, Lang RE, Maass H, Gerzer R. Acta Physiol Scand Suppl 1992;604:101-11. PMID 1324562. 6 healthy men, 22 mL/kg 0.9% saline: sodium excretion rose 3-fold, under 15% of the load was excreted acutely, and excretion stayed elevated beyond 48 h - the excretion of an acute isotonic load takes days, which is what this model does.", "RE-ESTIMATED 2026-09-03, 700 -> 585, AND THIS ROW'S OWN NOTE REQUIRED IT. It said: part of 700 compensates for a volume excursion 1.5-2.1x too large while CV.VENOUS_RETURN.SENSITIVITY is calibrated, so re-estimate when G_vr is sourced. G_vr was sourced in healthy humans on 2026-09-03 (2880 -> 1400, section 3.19), the caveat came due, and this is that re-estimation. NOT a new search - the same two human datasets, re-solved against corrected inputs. || THE PROCEDURE IS UNCHANGED AND THE IDENTIFICATION IS CLEAN. Two data, two parameters: the CHRONIC salt-step response fixes this gain, Lobo's 6 h ACUTE time course fixes RN.ANP.TAU. A first-order lag cannot move a steady state, so the two are separately identified - measured, not assumed: at G_anp 500/700/900 the chronic sensitivity is 2.275/1.720/1.382 regardless of tau, and 1/sensitivity is linear in the gain. || THE VALUE. Solved for a chronic salt sensitivity of 2.00 mmHg per 100 mmol/day, the CENTRE of the meta-analytic human 1.70-2.30 (Cutler 1997, He 2013, He 2002; k = 3). Verified by running rather than interpolating: 585 gives exactly 2.000. || WHAT IT NOW REPRODUCES, at G_pn = 8.4 and G_vr = 1400. Lobo 2001 (PMID 11473492): 566 mL and 95.1 mmol over the 6 h after 2 L of 0.9% saline, against a measured 563 and 95 - within 0.5 percent on both. READ THAT AS A FIT RESIDUAL AND NOT AS ACCURACY: RN.ANP.TAU WAS ESTIMATED AGAINST THIS SAME 6 H TIME COURSE, as the identification paragraph on this row says, so agreeing with it closely is what estimation is FOR rather than evidence that the model predicts anything. Corrected 2026-09-04, when HANDOVER was found claiming the four Lobo endpoints as validations in three places while section 3.21 said two paragraphs above that Lobo fixed the lag. The genuinely out-of-sample number is Jensen 2013, and it is a third low. What DOES survive here is an internal consistency check: the two Lobo endpoints imply the same lag independently, 0.171 d from the volume and 0.166 d from the sodium. Two endpoints of one protocol in the same subjects are not an independent test, and the acute limb of this model rests entirely on that one protocol class - Lobo and Jensen are both intravenous isotonic saline into healthy volunteers. Chronic salt sensitivity 2.000 against 1.70-2.30. dMAP/dV_ecf 3.000 mmHg/L against a measured human 2.97-4.16. || AND AN OUT-OF-SAMPLE CHECK THAT IS REPORTED RATHER THAN TUNED. Jensen 2013 (PMID 24067081) was NOT used in the estimation. The model predicts a peak fractional sodium excretion rise of 82.5% against the reported 123%. Inside the harness band and about a third low. That is the honest standing of this parameterisation on data it was not fitted to, and it is the sharpest remaining discrepancy in the sodium limb. || WHY THE GAIN FELL WHILE THE MODEL GOT BETTER. G_vr halving roughly doubled the model's volume excursion per unit intake, so a smaller gain now produces the same chronic natriuresis. 700 x 0.836 = 585, and the 0.836 is the correction the old note predicted would be needed. || SENSED VARIABLE, FORM AND EVIDENCE BASE ARE UNCHANGED - see the citation column and ADR 0010. The algebraic form was refuted before the lag was added and that finding stands. Uncertainty range 500-900 spans the chronic window 1.70-2.30. || TRAILING ZERO REMOVED 2026-09-09: 585.0 read as four significant figures against a range of 500-900. THE ROW IS EXEMPTED from the precision gate rather than rounded further, and the reason is that this is a SOLVED value, not a measured one - it is the gain that reproduces a chronic salt sensitivity of 2.00, so its digits are reproducible even though its CONFIDENCE is only as good as the 500-900 the target window permits. Those are different quantities and this row now says so. || THE OUT-OF-SAMPLE FIGURE ON THIS ROW IS SUPERSEDED TWICE OVER. CORRECTED 2026-09-17, HANDOVER section 3.45. This note says the model predicts a peak fractional sodium excretion rise of 82.5 percent against Jensen's reported 123, 'inside the harness band and about a third low'. FIRST, IT WAS STALE: the macula densa arm of ADR 0021 moved the endpoint on 2026-09-05 and nothing recorded it. SECOND, AND WORSE, THE COMPARISON WAS NOT LIKE FOR LIKE: it set a MODEL PEAK against Jensen's value in its FINAL SAMPLING PERIOD, and Jensen's series is monotone rising to that period - 1.26, 1.93, 2.35, 2.67, 2.80 - so the study never observed a peak at all. || MEASURED ON JENSEN'S OWN 210-240 MINUTE WINDOW the model is at 110.1 percent against a measured 122, inside a reported 2.80 +/- 0.75, and it tracks the whole observed time course. || THE FIRST CLAUSE OF THIS NOTE STANDS. Jensen was and remains held out of this row's estimation - the gain is solved for a chronic salt sensitivity of 2.00 and against nothing acute - so it is still the one place this parameterisation is tested rather than fitted. What is withdrawn is the characterisation of the result. || AND THE AGREEMENT IS NOT EVIDENCE FOR THIS ROW EITHER. This row already records that the zero-correlation upper bound on Jensen's ratio is -18 to +502 percent, so 110 against 122 is as uninformative as 79 was. THE GAIN WAS NOT RE-SOLVED IN THE CORRECTION and must not be re-solved against Jensen now that the two agree - that would convert the only out-of-sample datum into an estimation set. || WHAT IS ACTUALLY OPEN IS THE LATE TIME COURSE, AND NOTHING BOUNDS IT. The model's maximum falls at 375 minutes after infusion start; Jensen's protocol ends at 240 with its series still climbing. RN.ANP.TAU, this row's partner, was estimated against Lobo's 6 h CUMULATIVE endpoints, which fix the area under the curve and leave the peak time free. A human isotonic-saline study sampling past 6 h would test it; Drummer 1992, PMID 1324562, is the first place to look. || THE ACUTE AND CHRONIC LIMBS DISAGREE BY A FACTOR OF THREE, MEASURED 2026-09-17. HANDOVER section 3.46, validation/late_time_course_prereg.md branch L2. Drummer 1992 (PMID 1590419, abstract read in full; six healthy supine volunteers, 2 L of isotonic saline in 25 min, 48 h of collections plus a 48 h control) reports elevated body weight returning to baseline with an approximate half-life of 7 hours. The model gives 13.10 h. Scaling THIS GAIN alone by 3 reaches 7.10 h and drives the chronic salt sensitivity from 1.960 to 0.766 against the human 1.70-2.30 this row is solved against. Scaling both natriuretic gains by 3 gives 6.98 h and 0.681. || THIS ROW WAS NOT RE-SOLVED AND MUST NOT BE. Section 4 of the pre-registration forbade it and nothing moved. A gain chosen to fix the acute decay would break the chronic target this row exists to reproduce, and a gain split between the two would be a fitted compromise with no evidence behind the split. || WHAT THE MEASUREMENT IS EVIDENCE ABOUT IS THE FORM, NOT THE VALUE. A single linear volume-keyed gain cannot serve an acute decay that needs 3x what the chronic steady state permits; a saturating or multi-timescale path can. That is ADR 0010's original proposal, which it never built, and the record now carries this as its identifying experiment - replacing the acute-MAGNITUDE comparison that HANDOVER section 3.45 retired earlier the same day. The two constraints any such build must satisfy SIMULTANEOUSLY are the 7 h half-life and the 1.70-2.30 chronic window. || RENAMED 2026-09-18, CV.ANP.NATRIURETIC_GAIN -> CV.VOLUME.NATRIURETIC_GAIN, anp_label_prereg.md. NO NUMBER MOVED AND THE MODEL OUTPUT IS BIT-IDENTICAL - that was the pass's only test. FAILURE MODE #11: a name carrying a convention its value contradicts. The `name` field already read 'Volume-keyed natriuretic gain' and ADR 0010's own Decision already called it 'a lumped volume-keyed natriuretic term, algebraic in V_blood, with ANP as its named evidence base rather than as a state'. THE param_id DID NOT SAY THAT, and the param_id is what appears in code, in LedgerParams.jl and in the GUI. || AND 'ANP AS ITS NAMED EVIDENCE BASE' WAS ITSELF WRONG. This gain was estimated from WHOLE-BODY ISOTONIC EXPANSION - Lobo 2001 and Jensen 2013 - which measures the TOTAL natriuretic response to a volume load. ANP was the mechanism assumed to carry that total, not the thing measured. HANDOVER section 3.57 supplied the counter-evidence: Lohmeier's split-bladder dogs give a denervated/innervated sodium ratio near 0.56 at sustained elevated pressure, so RENAL SYMPATHETIC WITHDRAWAL carries a substantial share of exactly the response this gain was fitted to. The gain is not wrong. The attribution was. || WHAT THIS LUMP CONTAINS, NAMED AND NOT QUANTIFIED: atrial natriuretic peptide; renal sympathetic withdrawal via cardiopulmonary volume receptors; and whatever else volume expansion does to tubular sodium handling that neither of those covers. THE SHARE IS DELIBERATELY NOT SPLIT. Lohmeier's ratio implies a nerve share near 47 percent, but the authors report a latent impairment in sodium excretion from the denervated kidneys, making that an UPPER BOUND rather than an estimate; the protocol is ANG II hypertension and not a salt step; n = 5 and abstract only. The supportable statement is 'between zero and about a half, biased high', and a parameter with that interval buys nothing. Directive 1.14. || THE TRAP THIS RENAME EXISTS TO PREVENT: a future ANP infusion dose-response MUST NOT be added on top of this gain. This gain ALREADY CONTAINS ANP's contribution, along with the neural one. Sourcing an ANP-specific gain would require SPLITTING this row, not supplementing it. || OLD IDENTIFIER CV.ANP.NATRIURETIC_GAIN IS RETAINED IN THE NOTE TEXT ABOVE AND THROUGHOUT THIS LEDGER'S HISTORY, which is append-only and was not rewritten - searching the old name still finds the record."),
    :GLU_EGP_BASAL => Provenance("GLU.EGP.BASAL", "mg/kg/min", 1.93, "B", "reported", "Huidekoper HH, Ackermans MT, Ruiter AFC, Sauerwein HP, Wijburg FA. Endogenous glucose production from infancy to adulthood: a non-linear regression model. Arch Dis Child 2014;99(12):1082-1087. PMID 24996789.", "THE ADULT PLATEAU of endogenous glucose production. Huidekoper 2014, PMID 24996789, ABSTRACT READ IN FULL on PubMed 2026-09-21. glucose_insulin_prereg.md section 9.4. || MEASURED BY [6,6-2H2] GLUCOSE DILUTION after an overnight fast during normoglycaemia, n = 40 healthy subjects aged 2.5-54.3 y, fitted as a one-phase exponential decay: EGP (mg/kg/min) = 6.50 * e^(-0.145 * age_y) + 1.93. || 1.93 IS THE ASYMPTOTE AND THAT IS WHY IT IS THE ENTERED VALUE. Beyond about 30 years the exponential term contributes under 2 percent, so EGP is 1.93 to 1.97 across the adult range. THIS MODEL HAS NO AGE DIMENSION, so the age term is not representable and the plateau is the only part of the regression it can consume. || THE COHORT IS PAEDIATRIC-WEIGHTED AND THAT IS THE LIMITATION. It is an Archives of Disease in Childhood paper whose purpose is paediatric fluid therapy; the adult end of its range is the sparse end. || NO DISPERSION IS PUBLISHED for the regression parameters in the abstract, so uncertainty_type is none and THAT IS AN EXTRACTION GAP RATHER THAN A PRECISION CLAIM - the same kind as Lorenz's SEMs. Retiring it needs the full text. || DIRECTIVE 1.12: section 2 listed '2 mg/kg/min' in advance. It is close, and it carries neither a cohort nor a functional form. || SCALING: PER KILOGRAM, so it multiplies body mass directly rather than a size factor. That is the paper's own normalisation and no conversion is applied."),
    :GLU_EGP_INSULIN_I50 => Provenance("GLU.EGP.INSULIN_I50", "pmol/L", 174.0, "A", "reported", "Rizza RA, Mandarino LJ, Gerich JE. Dose-response characteristics for effects of insulin on production and utilization of glucose in man. Am J Physiol 1981;240(6):E630-E639. PMID 7018254. 15 healthy subjects, graded 8 h insulin infusion, EGP measured isotopically. Half-maximal suppression of glucose production 29 +/- 2 microunits/ml, against 55 +/- 7 for half-maximal glucose utilization (P < 0.01). Corroborated on the form, NOT pooled, by Groop LC, Bonadonna RC, DelPrato S, Ratheiser K, Zyck K, Ferrannini E, DeFronzo RA. J Clin Invest 1989;84(1):205-213. PMID 2661589.", "PLASMA INSULIN AT HALF-MAXIMAL SUPPRESSION OF ENDOGENOUS GLUCOSE PRODUCTION. Pre-registered in validation/egp_suppression_prereg.md, branch E1; ADR 0034. || WHY IT EXISTS. The model's EGP was a CONSTANT, so insulin could not suppress hepatic glucose output at all. Unrestrained EGP is the principal driver of FASTING hyperglycaemia in BOTH diabetes types, so a model with fixed EGP can only raise glucose by failing to DISPOSE of it - it represents the postprandial defect and not the fasting one. A missing term, not a wrong coefficient. HANDOVER section 4 named it the single biggest gap in the glucose axis. || THE SOURCE. Rizza RA, Mandarino LJ, Gerich JE. Dose-response characteristics for effects of insulin on production and utilization of glucose in man. Am J Physiol 1981;240(6):E630-E639. PMID 7018254. 15 healthy subjects, insulin infused 8 h at sequential rates from 0.2 to 5.0 mU/kg/min, 2 h per rate, glucose production and utilization measured isotopically with [3-3H]glucose. GRADED INFUSION IS WHY THIS SOURCE: a single-step clamp gives one point and cannot constrain a dose-response, which egp_suppression_prereg.md section 3 excluded before searching. || THE VALUE AND THE CONTRAST THAT MAKES IT MATTER. Half-maximal suppression of glucose PRODUCTION at 29 +/- 2 microunits/ml, against half-maximal glucose UTILIZATION at 55 +/- 7, significantly different at P < 0.01. PRODUCTION IS ROUGHLY TWICE AS INSULIN-SENSITIVE AS UTILIZATION, which is precisely why a model that suppresses neither cannot represent fasting hyperglycaemia. || UNIT CONVERSION, STATED BECAUSE IT IS LOAD-BEARING. 1 microunit/ml = 6.00 pmol/L for human insulin (1 IU = 0.0347 mg, MW 5808), so 29 microunits/ml = 174 pmol/L. The model carries insulin in pmol/L and its fasting value is 64.2 pmol/L = 10.7 microunits/ml, consistent with the NHANES row this model already uses. || DISPERSION. Rizza reports SEM. SD = 2 * sqrt(15) = 7.75 microunits/ml = 46 pmol/L, per pooling.md and thirst_prereg.md section 8.4 - pooling shrinks uncertainty in a mean and must not shrink population spread. || WHAT IS DERIVED FROM IT AND DELIBERATELY NOT STORED. The hyperbolic constant K_egp = I50 - 2*I_fast = 45.6 pmol/L and the zero-insulin production EGP_0 = 2.41 x basal are computed IN-COMPONENT from this row and the fasting insulin row. Storing them would be a precision claim and a drift risk - the ADR 0032 lesson about derived constants, applied on purpose. || AN OUT-OF-SAMPLE CHECK THE ANCHORS DID NOT GUARANTEE. The inversion implies EGP at ZERO insulin is 2.41 times basal. Uncontrolled type 1 EGP is reported elevated roughly two- to threefold, so the form lands inside the literature range at a point neither anchor constrained. egp_suppression_prereg.md section 7 test 4. || THE COST OF A HILL COEFFICIENT OF 1, DECLARED IN ADVANCE AND MEASURED. Rizza reports production 'completely suppressed' at approximately 60 microunits/ml; this hyperbolic gives 27 percent of basal there. IT UNDER-SUPPRESSES AT HIGH INSULIN AND THAT IS NOT TUNED AWAY - section 9 of the pre-registration names raising h to match a qualitative word as a way the pass fails. Only a digitised multi-point curve could justify h > 1. || CORROBORATION, INDEPENDENT AND NOT POOLED. Groop LC, Bonadonna RC, DelPrato S, Ratheiser K, Zyck K, Ferrannini E, DeFronzo RA. J Clin Invest 1989;84(1):205-213, PMID 2661589 - a DIFFERENT laboratory (Pisa-Yale), graded physiologic hyperinsulinaemia at +5, +15, +30, +70 and +200 microunits/ml in eight controls, reporting progressive suppression of hepatic glucose production. It confirms the DOSE-RESPONSE SHAPE; no I50 is extractable from its abstract, so it is corroboration on the FORM and is NOT pooled. || SINGLE-SOURCE for the value, stated as such; OPEN-QUESTIONS B21. || SCALING: none, a concentration."),
    :GLU_INSULIN_FASTING => Provenance("GLU.INSULIN.FASTING", "pmol/L", 64.2, "A", "reported", "NHANES 2007-2008, 2009-2010 and 2011-2012, extracted by validation/glucose_insulin_extract.py. n = 5,563 adults, the same population as GLU.PLASMA.FASTING.", "WEIGHTED MEDIAN fasting plasma insulin, 9.25 uU/mL, in 5,563 healthy adults, from the same extraction and the same exclusions as GLU.PLASMA.FASTING. glucose_insulin_prereg.md sections 9.5 and 10. || IT WAS MEASURED ON 2026-09-21 AND DELIBERATELY NOT ENTERED THEN, because branch G2 had removed its consumer and a ledger row nothing reads is what directive 1.11 exists to prevent. ADR 0031 built the insulin split, so the consumer now exists and the row enters. || THE MEDIAN, NOT THE MEAN, AND THAT WAS FIXED BEFORE THE DATA WERE SEEN - section 10.4. Fasting insulin is right-skewed: the weighted mean is 11.87 uU/mL against a median of 9.25, 28 percent apart, so a mean would have described nobody typical. || AN INDEPENDENT CHECK NOBODY ARRANGED: Merovci 2021's 12 healthy subjects have a fasting insulin of 9 +/- 1 mU/L, which is 62 pmol/L against this row's 64.2 - a different decade, cohort and assay. || DISPERSION IS THE IQR, not a standard error; design strata and PSU are not used, so any standard error would be optimistic. || SCALING: none, it is a concentration."),
    :GLU_INSULIN_RESPONSE_KM => Provenance("GLU.INSULIN.RESPONSE_KM", "mmol/L", 18.0, "B", "derived", "Merovci A, Tripathy D, Chen X, Valdez I, Abdul-Ghani M, Solis-Herrera C, Basu S, DeFronzo RA. Effect of Mild Physiologic Hyperglycemia on Insulin Secretion, Insulin Clearance, and Insulin Sensitivity in Healthy Glucose-Tolerant Subjects. Diabetes 2021;70(1):204-213. PMID 33033064. FULL TEXT READ, supplied by the owner.", "THE HALF-SATURATION of the plasma glucose to plasma insulin relation, 17.7 mmol/L above fasting, entered at two figures as 18. || THE SOURCE IS A TWO-STEP HYPERGLYCAEMIC CLAMP IN 12 HEALTHY SUBJECTS with no family history of type 2 diabetes, and what is taken is the BASELINE arm, before the 72-hour glucose infusion. The post-infusion arm is an intervention and glucose_insulin_prereg.md section 12.4 excludes it for setting a healthy relation. || THE PROTOCOL COMES FROM THE METHODS AND NOT THE ABSTRACT, and the difference matters: the abstract's '100 and 300 mg/dL' are INCREMENTS, while the methods give step one as 100 mg/dL above baseline and step two as 400 mg/dL ABSOLUTE. Second-phase insulin is an incremental AUC over 70-minute windows, so the plateau concentrations are those AUCs divided by 70 and added to fasting: 9 mU/L at 5.00 mmol/L, 42 at 10.55, 78 at 22.20. || AN EXACT TWO-POINT INVERSION, NOT A FIT. Two parameters from two increments reproduce both points identically, so there is NO goodness of fit and no residual - the form is interpolation between two measured points and extrapolation beyond them. || UNCERTAINTY IS LARGE AND IS WHY ONLY TWO FIGURES ARE ENTERED. The two AUCs carry SE of 21 and 24 percent, and the inversion is sensitive to their ratio, so these constants are known to tens of percent at best. Directive 1.13. || SE, NOT SD: the AUC dispersions are standard errors over n = 12, so thirst_prereg.md section 8.4's conversion applies to any population sampling of this relation. || SINGLE-SOURCE, AND IT SAYS SO. A second two-step hyperglycaemic clamp in healthy adults reporting both axes would pool with this one; OPEN-QUESTIONS B21 carries the debt. || WHY A SATURATING FORM AND NOT A LINE. Merovci's own two intervals give 5.98 mU/L per mmol/L and then 3.04 - the response HALVES across the range. A linear relation fitted to the first interval would overstate insulin at 22 mmol/L roughly twofold and would buffer hyperglycaemia that people do not buffer. || SCALING: none, a concentration increment."),
    :GLU_INSULIN_RESPONSE_MAX => Provenance("GLU.INSULIN.RESPONSE_MAX", "pmol/L", 970.0, "B", "derived", "Merovci A, Tripathy D, Chen X, Valdez I, Abdul-Ghani M, Solis-Herrera C, Basu S, DeFronzo RA. Effect of Mild Physiologic Hyperglycemia on Insulin Secretion, Insulin Clearance, and Insulin Sensitivity in Healthy Glucose-Tolerant Subjects. Diabetes 2021;70(1):204-213. PMID 33033064. FULL TEXT READ, supplied by the owner.", "THE SATURATING CEILING of the plasma glucose to plasma insulin relation, derived from Merovci 2021's two second-phase clamp plateaux. 139 mU/L = 966 pmol/L, entered at two figures as 970. || THE SOURCE IS A TWO-STEP HYPERGLYCAEMIC CLAMP IN 12 HEALTHY SUBJECTS with no family history of type 2 diabetes, and what is taken is the BASELINE arm, before the 72-hour glucose infusion. The post-infusion arm is an intervention and glucose_insulin_prereg.md section 12.4 excludes it for setting a healthy relation. || THE PROTOCOL COMES FROM THE METHODS AND NOT THE ABSTRACT, and the difference matters: the abstract's '100 and 300 mg/dL' are INCREMENTS, while the methods give step one as 100 mg/dL above baseline and step two as 400 mg/dL ABSOLUTE. Second-phase insulin is an incremental AUC over 70-minute windows, so the plateau concentrations are those AUCs divided by 70 and added to fasting: 9 mU/L at 5.00 mmol/L, 42 at 10.55, 78 at 22.20. || AN EXACT TWO-POINT INVERSION, NOT A FIT. Two parameters from two increments reproduce both points identically, so there is NO goodness of fit and no residual - the form is interpolation between two measured points and extrapolation beyond them. || UNCERTAINTY IS LARGE AND IS WHY ONLY TWO FIGURES ARE ENTERED. The two AUCs carry SE of 21 and 24 percent, and the inversion is sensitive to their ratio, so these constants are known to tens of percent at best. Directive 1.13. || SE, NOT SD: the AUC dispersions are standard errors over n = 12, so thirst_prereg.md section 8.4's conversion applies to any population sampling of this relation. || SINGLE-SOURCE, AND IT SAYS SO. A second two-step hyperglycaemic clamp in healthy adults reporting both axes would pool with this one; OPEN-QUESTIONS B21 carries the debt. || IT IS AN EXTRAPOLATION BEYOND THE DATA. Merovci's highest step is 22.2 mmol/L; this ceiling is what the two-point form implies as glucose goes to infinity, and no measurement constrains it there. The model reaches about 29 mmol/L, so the ceiling is being read outside the measured range and that is stated rather than discovered. || SCALING: none, a concentration."),
    :GLU_INTAKE_CARBOHYDRATE => Provenance("GLU.INTAKE.CARBOHYDRATE", "g/day", 225.0, "A", "reported", "NHANES 2007-2008, 2009-2010 and 2011-2012 dietary recall, extracted by validation/glucose_insulin_extract.py. n = 5,337 adults, the same population definition as GLU.PLASMA.FASTING.", "AVAILABLE DIETARY CARBOHYDRATE - the glucose input the model did not have. Weighted MEDIAN of DR1TCARB minus DR1TFIBE, 224.7 g/day, IQR 167.7-301.9, in 5,337 adults. glucose_insulin_prereg.md, ADR 0029 amendment. || AVAILABLE, NOT TOTAL. Fibre is not absorbed as glucose and counting it would overstate appearance; the subtraction is arithmetic on two columns of the SAME recall, not an assumption. || WHY IT MATTERS: ADR 0029 ceilinged plasma glucose at (appearance + TmG)/GFR = 25.5 mmol/L because appearance was HEPATIC PRODUCTION ALONE. The model ate sodium and drank water and took in no glucose. With dietary carbohydrate the ceiling rises to about 34 mmol/L, which is severe hyperglycaemia rather than an artefact of a fasting-only model. || THE HEALTHY OPERATING POINT IS UNCHANGED BY CONSTRUCTION, because k_glu is DERIVED as appearance/G_fast and appearance now includes this term. A larger input gives a proportionally larger clearance and the same fasting glucose. || ENTERED TO THREE FIGURES, 225 g/day. The median of 5,337 would support four, but a 24-hour recall does not: it is a single day per person and it UNDER-REPORTS, so the fourth figure would be false precision on a biased instrument. Directive 1.13. || DISPERSION IS THE IQR AND IT IS WIDER THAN THE HABITUAL DISTRIBUTION. One recall per person means this spread is between-person variation PLUS within-person day-to-day variation. For a population model that matters: sampling individuals from this IQR would overstate how different people's habitual diets are. Stated rather than corrected, because correcting it needs a second recall day. || DIRECTION OF BIAS: dietary recall under-reports intake, so the true median is HIGHER and the true ceiling HIGHER still. The deviation is conservative for the model. || SCALING: MASS-like. Energy and carbohydrate intake track body size, so it carries mass_factor rather than the surface-like factor GFR uses."),
    :GLU_NIMGU_FIXED_FRACTION => Provenance("GLU.NIMGU.FIXED_FRACTION", "unitless", 0.63, "A", "derived", "Baron AD, Brechtel G, Wallace P, Edelman SV. Rates of noninsulin-mediated glucose uptake are elevated in type II diabetic subjects. J Clin Invest 1985;76(5):1782-1788. PMID 2865274. Full text read. Corroborated on the FORM, not pooled, by Best JD, Taborsky GJ, Halter JB, Porte D. Glucose disposal is not proportional to plasma glucose level in man. Diabetes 1981;30(10):847-850. PMID 6115785.", "THE CONCENTRATION-INDEPENDENT SHARE OF NON-INSULIN-MEDIATED GLUCOSE UPTAKE, at the fasting glucose level. OPEN-QUESTIONS B22; pre-registered in validation/nimgu_form_prereg.md, branch N1; ADR 0033. || WHY IT EXISTS. ADR 0031 made NIMGU strictly proportional to glucose, k_ni*G, and said so: with both disease knobs at zero the insulin-independent term alone cleared the whole appearance at 15.6 mmol/L, below the renal threshold, so glycosuria was zero at every setting. ADR 0031 named its own falsifier and deferred the fix rather than stacking a second structural change. This is that fix. || THE FORM IS BEST 1981'S STATED MECHANISM, NOT A CURVE FIT: insulin-independent tissues such as brain have a relatively fixed glucose uptake while other tissues take up glucose at a rate proportional to concentration. So NIMGU = U_fixed + k_ni*G, two terms, one new parameter. || THE VALUE IS AN EXACT TWO-POINT INVERSION OF BARON 1985, FULL TEXT READ (open access at JCI, downloaded from the publisher's own open-access link; no bot check was involved or circumvented). Table I gives control fasting serum glucose 90 +/- 1.7 mg/dl, n = 11. NIMGU was 113 +/- 8 mg/min at euglycaemia (n = 11) and 186 +/- 19 mg/min at a somatostatin + hyperglycaemic clamp of 248 +/- 2 mg/dl (n = 7 - only seven controls underwent the hyperglycaemic arm, which the abstract does not say). k_ni = (186-113)/(248-90) = 0.462 (mg/min)/(mg/dl); U_fixed = 113 - 0.462*90 = 71.4 mg/min; fraction = 71.4/113 = 0.63. TWO POINTS, TWO PARAMETERS: an exact inversion, no residual and no goodness of fit. || THE PAPER CONTAINS ITS OWN CONSISTENCY CHECK AND THE FIT PASSES IT. It separately reports NIMGU metabolic clearance falling 1.2 +/- 0.07 to 0.74 +/- 0.08; the fitted rates give 113/90 = 1.256 and 186/248 = 0.750. The rates and the clearances are one dataset, not two claims. THE CLEARANCE FALL IS 38 PERCENT FROM THE PRINTED CLEARANCES AND 40 PERCENT FROM THE FITTED RATES; those are two-figure numbers whose ratio carries several points of uncertainty and directive 1.13 forbids reading a difference inside that as anything. NEITHER MAY BE QUOTED TO THREE FIGURES. || INDEPENDENT CORROBORATION, AND IT IS WHY THIS ROW EXISTS AT ALL UNDER DIRECTIVE 1.16. Best JD, Taborsky GJ, Halter JB, Porte D. Glucose disposal is not proportional to plasma glucose level in man. Diabetes 1981;30(10):847-850, PMID 6115785 - a DIFFERENT laboratory (Porte, Seattle), decade and protocol (somatostatin at three fixed insulin levels), reporting metabolic clearance falling 38 percent at low insulin. Its implied concentration-independent fraction is 63 to 76 percent and CONTAINS Baron's 63. NOT POOLED: its abstract says only 'more than twice basal', which spans 63 at 2.5x and 76 at 2.0x, so it corroborates the FORM and cannot supply a value. Found through the reference list of Ahren and Pacini, J Diabetes Investig 2021, PMID 33098240 - it uses the term NIMGU nowhere and no search on that term returns it. || THE REVIEW MISCITED IT as Diabetes 1981;34:847-850; volume 34 is 1985. Verified against the indexed record. Directive 1.5. || DISPERSION. Baron reports SEM throughout. SD propagated from the two arms with their own n: 113 +/- 8 (n=11) gives SD 26.5 and 186 +/- 19 (n=7) gives SD 50.3, so the fraction carries SD 0.34. THAT IS AN UPPER BOUND AND IS LABELLED ONE: the propagation assumes the two arms are INDEPENDENT and they are not - seven of the eleven controls appear in both - so the true between-subject spread is smaller and cannot be computed without subject-level data. A population model needs the spread and this is the honest ceiling on it. || WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED. That the two-term form beats a saturating Michaelis form on DATA. Two points cannot distinguish them; the choice is on Best's stated mechanism, and nimgu_form_prereg.md section 7 test 5 declared the comparison in advance. Edelman 1990 (PMID 1973673, closed) has a FOUR-point dose-response and would decide it. || SCALING: none, a fraction."),
    :GLU_NIMGU_FRACTION => Provenance("GLU.NIMGU.FRACTION", "unitless", 0.75, "A", "reported", "Baron AD, Brechtel G, Wallace P, Edelman SV. Rates of noninsulin-mediated glucose uptake are elevated in type II diabetic subjects. J Clin Invest 1985;76(5):1782-1788. PMID 2865274.", "NIMGU AS A FRACTION OF BASAL GLUCOSE DISPOSAL, 75 +/- 5 percent in 11 normal subjects. Baron 1985, PMID 2865274, abstract read in full. glucose_insulin_prereg.md section 11.7. || DIRECTLY MEASURED, NOT MODELLED: serum glucose clamped during somatostatin-induced insulin deficiency with a concomitant [3-3H]glucose infusion, so glucose disposal is isotopically quantitated with and without insulin present. That is why this source and not the minimal-model estimates. || THE DISPERSION IS CONVERTED AND THE CONVERSION IS THE POINT. Baron reports 75 +/- 5 as a STANDARD ERROR over n = 11. Entering 5 as a population SD would understate the spread by root 11, a factor of 3.3. SD = 5 * sqrt(11) = 16.6 percentage points, so individuals span roughly 58 to 92 percent. thirst_prereg.md section 8.4: pooling shrinks the uncertainty in a mean and must not shrink population spread. || A CROSS-METHOD CHECK THAT IS NOT POOLED. Garcia-Estevez 1998 (PMID 9597380) gives 77 +/- 8 percent in 16 healthy subjects by the MINIMAL MODEL. pooling.md forbids mixing measurement methods and SOURCES.md would make a minimal-model value `calibrated` with that model named, so it is recorded as corroboration rather than pooled - two estimators, two cohorts, agreeing within 2 percentage points. || IT IS POSTABSORPTIVE AND THE MODEL APPLIES IT THERE. The fraction sets k_ni against hepatic production alone; insulin-mediated uptake takes the remainder including the dietary load, so NIMGU's share of 24-HOUR disposal is about 35 percent rather than 75. ADR 0031. || SINGLE-SOURCE for the pool, stated as such; OPEN-QUESTIONS B21. || SCALING: none, a fraction."),
    :GLU_PLASMA_FASTING => Provenance("GLU.PLASMA.FASTING", "mmol/L", 5.44, "A", "reported", "NHANES 2007-2008, 2009-2010 and 2011-2012, public microdata, extracted by validation/glucose_insulin_extract.py. n = 5,563 adults after the exclusions glucose_insulin_prereg.md section 10.2 fixed in advance.", "WEIGHTED MEDIAN fasting plasma glucose, 98 mg/dL, in 5,563 healthy adults. glucose_insulin_prereg.md sections 9.5 and 10; reproduce with python validation/glucose_insulin_extract.py. || THE MEDIAN, NOT THE MEAN, AND THAT WAS FIXED BEFORE THE DATA WERE SEEN - section 10.4. || DIRECTIVE 1.12 SCORED HERE AND MATERIALLY. Section 2 listed 5.0 mmol/L as the teaching value for fasting glucose before any search was run. THE MEASURED CENTRE IS 5.44 AND 5.0 SITS ESSENTIALLY AT THE 25TH PERCENTILE of healthy US adults. That is the fifth instance of this repository's round-number pattern. || EXCLUSIONS, all fixed in advance: age >= 20; fasting subsample with >= 8 h fast; no diagnosed diabetes; NOT TOLD PREDIABETIC, because a normal value may not come from people in whom the relationship is already going; no glucose-lowering drug from an 18-name list; not pregnant; and glucose < 126 mg/dL, WHICH IS USED ONLY TO EXCLUDE PEOPLE AND NEVER AS A MODEL VALUE because it is a diagnostic cut-point. 250 were excluded by that last step. || DISPERSION IS THE IQR, NOT A STANDARD ERROR. Design strata and PSU are not used, so any standard error would be optimistic by a design-effect factor of roughly 1.5 to 2.5; the same caveat nhanes_hpt_extract.py carries, copied deliberately. || FASTING INSULIN WAS MEASURED IN THE SAME EXTRACTION - median 64.2 pmol/L, IQR 41.9-103.2 - AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT ENTERED. Branch G2 removed its consumer, and a ledger row nothing reads is what directive 1.11 exists to prevent. It is recorded in the pre-registration so the work is not lost. || SCALING: none, it is a concentration."),
    :K_DISTRIBUTION_VOLUME => Provenance("K.DISTRIBUTION_VOLUME", "L", 42.0, "C", "assumed", "No admissible source could be opened. See notes.", "ASSUMED at total body water, 0.6 L/kg, which is the crudest possible statement of the fact that a potassium load distributes into cells as well as into the extracellular fluid. The extracellular volume alone would be 14.6 L and would make plasma potassium three times more responsive than it is. || IT SETS THE TIME CONSTANT AND NOTHING ELSE. At steady state the balance is intake against excretion and this volume cancels, so every resting value and every steady-state response in this model is independent of it. What it changes is how fast plasma potassium moves after a step - which is why it is tolerable as an assumed row and why NO ACUTE POTASSIUM MAGNITUDE MAY BE REPORTED, exactly as BF.ICF_ECF.OSMOTIC_TAU blocks acute osmotic magnitudes. || EXTENSIVE, and MASS-like rather than surface-like: it is a fluid volume, and section 3.30 puts fluid compartments on mass. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :K_EXCRETION_EXPONENT => Provenance("K.EXCRETION_EXPONENT", "dimensionless", 18.0, "A", "derived", "Cappuccio FP, Buchanan LA, Ji C, Siani A, Miller MA. Systematic review and meta-analysis of randomised controlled trials on the effects of potassium supplements on serum potassium and creatinine. BMJ Open 2016;6(8):e011716. doi:10.1136/bmjopen-2016-011716. PMID 27566636. PMC5013341. OPEN ACCESS (CC BY-NC), READ IN FULL from the Europe PMC full text. 20 trials, 21 independent groups, 1216 participants, 12 countries, minimum four weeks of supplementation, and INTAKE VERIFIED BY 24-HOUR URINE COLLECTION - which validation/potassium_doseresponse_prereg.md section 2 requires and dietary recall does not satisfy. Table 1 reports urinary potassium AND plasma or serum potassium in both arms of every trial, which is the elasticity this row is the reciprocal of, measured twenty times over. Extracted in validation/potassium_doseresponse_extract.py.", "RE-SOURCED 2026-09-06 UNDER validation/potassium_doseresponse_prereg.md, WHICH WAS COMMITTED AT 1698d2a BEFORE THE SEARCH WAS RUN. The value moved 17.71 -> 17.73, which is nothing, AND THAT IS NOT WHAT THIS PASS BOUGHT. || WHAT CHANGED IS THE DISPERSION. The row previously rested on six studies in ten subjects (Brunner 1970) whose per-study exponents were 16.31, 19.11, 23.45, 3.45, 13.26 and 41.68 - a TWELVEFOLD spread, and the weakest number in the potassium component. It now rests on 1216 participants with a 95 percent interval of 11.9 to 24.7, which excludes both ends of that spread. The pre-registration's section 8 fixed exactly that as the test of whether the pass was worth running. || THIS ROW IS AN ELASTICITY AND THAT IS WHY IT TRANSFERS. At this model's steady state 1/n_K is d ln(plasma potassium) / d ln(intake), which is dimensionless and survives differences in assay, cohort and country - the property section 3.26 established the hard way when a thyroid slope and a concentration turned out not to share a measurement scale. || THREE INDEPENDENT ROUTES AGREE TO 11 PERCENT: the pre-registered admissible subset of this paper gives 17.73, the full pooled meta-analysis 16.00, and Brunner's median 17.71. The two that carry a dispersion disagree by far less than either dispersion. || THE VALUE COMES FROM THE ADMISSIBLE SUBSET AND THE INTERVAL FROM THE FULL POOL, and the split is deliberate. Pre-registration section 2 excludes hypertensive cohorts and diuretics, which costs 16 of the 19 trials that report both quantities; three trials cannot carry a dispersion and 1216 participants can. Both come from this one paper and neither is a different study. || POOLED AS A RATIO OF SUMS, not as a mean of ratios. Two trials report IDENTICAL plasma potassium in both arms, so their exponent is infinite and no mean of exponents exists; one (Siani 1987) is NEGATIVE, plasma potassium falling on a supplement, and it is kept because discarding the inconvenient sign narrows a spread without evidence. || BRUNNER 1970 IS DEMOTED TO A COMPARISON and stays in the record. Its median was right; what it could not carry was an interval. || WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX: there is still no potassium adaptation in this model - OPEN-QUESTIONS B11 - and the pre-registration's section 7 forbade this pass from touching it. || THE ROW THE SUITE DEMANDED. Excretion was first written LINEAR in plasma potassium, which makes the steady-state concentration PROPORTIONAL to intake: doubling a normal diet gave 7.6 mmol/L, a lethal hyperkalaemia from an ordinary dietary variation, and ADR 0021's falsifiable test 3 caught it on its first run. || d ln(intake) / d ln(plasma potassium) at steady state, per admissible study: 16.31, 19.11, 23.45, 3.45, 13.26, 41.68. MEDIAN 17.71 entered, and the median rather than the mean because two studies sit at the ends of a twelvefold spread and a mean would follow them. || A TWELVEFOLD SPREAD, REPORTED. What every value agrees on is that the exponent is FAR above 1, which is the physiological content: renal potassium excretion adapts so steeply that a fortyfold change in intake moves plasma potassium by a third. || IT LUMPS THREE MECHANISMS INTO ONE EXPONENT, and this is where ADR 0021 decision 5 is amended. Excretion rises with aldosterone, with distal flow and with plasma potassium; every human study found moves all three together, so no gain separates from the others. Collapsing them onto plasma potassium is a LUMPING and not a claim that the other two do not exist - and it means aldosterone's effect on potassium is present in this number rather than absent from the model. || WHAT IT DISQUALIFIES: anything whose perturbed variable is one of the three separately. Spironolactone, a potassium-sparing diuretic, primary aldosteronism and a pure flow change are all outside this model, because the model cannot tell them apart from a change in plasma potassium. || INDEPENDENTLY CORROBORATED IN DIRECTION, NOT IN MAGNITUDE. Hene 1988 (PMID 3199680), six healthy males on fixed sodium and potassium intake, reports 'a steep positive relation between plasma K and urine K' - which is this exponent's qualitative claim, from a different group, and the reason a value far above 1 is not an artefact of Brunner's six studies. No number could be taken: the paper is not open access and only its abstract was read. || WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER IS ADAPTATION. Rabelink 1990 (PMID 2266680) found renin and aldosterone back at baseline by day 20 of a 400 mmol/day load with kaliuresis maintained; a fixed exponent holds aldosterone at 2.80 times baseline for ever. ADR 0021 amendment A7 disagreement 2. || PRECISION CORRECTED 2026-09-09. The value carried more significant figures than its own reported interval supports, which tools/ledger_to_julia.py now refuses. Rounding it changes no claim - the discarded digits were below the width of the interval this row itself reports. || PRECISION CORRECTED 2026-09-09 under directive 1.13, second pass. An interval of 11.9-24.7 is +/-36% and supports two figures. The first pass rounded this row but stopped one figure short, because the gate then allowed two figures beyond what the interval supports - a tolerance I had given myself rather than the rule. The gate now enforces the rule exactly. Discarded digits were inside the row's own stated interval, so no claim changes."),
    :K_FRACTIONAL_EXCRETION => Provenance("K.FRACTIONAL_EXCRETION", "dimensionless", 0.1009, "B", "derived", "DERIVED to close the potassium balance at the reference individual: FE_K = K.RENAL_FRACTION * K.INTAKE.NOMINAL / (RN.GFR.NOMINAL * K.PLASMA.REFERENCE). Sources on those rows.", "IT LANDS AT ABOUT 10 PERCENT, WHICH IS WHERE THE TEXTBOOK PUTS IT - and that is a coincidence worth stating rather than a confirmation, because this row is DERIVED from the plasma potassium it would otherwise predict. It is the closure of the balance and not a measurement. || IT IS TEN TIMES THE FRACTIONAL EXCRETION OF SODIUM, which is the whole difference between the two ions in this nephron: sodium is reabsorbed and potassium is SECRETED distally, so its excretion can exceed what a purely reabsorptive nephron could deliver. The model does not represent the secretion, only its result. || WHAT IT MAKES IMPOSSIBLE: any statement about renal potassium handling as such. The model can say what happens to plasma potassium when intake or filtration changes, and cannot say anything about the distal nephron that was not put in here. || THE ROW ONCE SAID THIS LEVEL COULD NOT BE SOURCED BECAUSE THE CLEARANCE IS NOT MEASURED IN HEALTHY PEOPLE. THAT WAS FALSE and ADR 0021 amendment A7 retracts it: the Utrecht balance studies (Hene 1986 PMID 3523191, Hene 1988 PMID 3199680, Rabelink 1990 PMID 2266680) are six healthy volunteers each on controlled intake, and the search term was the bedside phrase rather than the physiology. What is true is narrower: no admissible study reports intake, plasma potassium AND glomerular filtration in the same healthy subjects, which is this model's own composite, so the row stays derived. || AND THE ROW BARELY MATTERS, WHICH IS MEASURED: sweeping it 0.04 to 0.16 moves steady-state plasma potassium only 4.18 to 3.87 mmol/L. The exponent pins the concentration, so falsifiable test 2 would be weak even with this row perfectly sourced. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :K_INTAKE_NOMINAL => Provenance("K.INTAKE.NOMINAL", "mmol/day", 69.06, "A", "reported", "National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey 2007-2008, 2009-2010 and 2011-2012, public microdata, file DR1TOT, variable DR1TPOTA, restricted to recalls the survey itself flags reliable (DR1DRSTZ = 1). EXTRACTED HERE: validation/macula_densa_potassium_extract.py. n = 8893 adults 20+, not pregnant, weighted WTMEC2YR/3. Mean 69.06 mmol/day, median 64.56, 2.5th-97.5th percentile 21.1-144.9; men 78.82, women 59.84.", "EXTENSIVE, AND SURFACE-LIKE: dietary intake tracks metabolic rate, which is what every other intake row in this model scales as since section 3.30. || A DIETARY RECALL UNDERSTATES INTAKE and the row says so rather than being quietly trusted. The same survey's sodium recall gives 153.6 mmol/day where 24-hour urine collections in US adults run higher, and the model's own BF.NA.INTAKE_NOMINAL is 205. Twenty-four-hour urinary potassium would be the better measurement and NHANES did not collect it in these cycles. || THE UNCERTAINTY IS THE POPULATION SPREAD, not an error on the mean: the mean of 8893 recalls is precise, and what the range says is that people differ sevenfold. || SEXED PAIR AVAILABLE AND NOT TAKEN - 78.8 against 59.8 mmol/day - because the potassium balance closes against a plasma concentration entered as `both`, and sexing one half against an unsexed other is the error metabolic_rate_prereg.md section 4 avoided for CO2 production."),
    :K_NONRENAL_LOSS_EXPONENT => Provenance("K.NONRENAL_LOSS_EXPONENT", "dimensionless", 0.4, "B", "derived", "Hene RJ, Koomans HA, Boer P, Dorhout Mees EJ. Adaptation to chronic potassium loading in normal man. Mineral and Electrolyte Metabolism 1986;12(3):165-72. PMID 3523191. SIX HEALTHY MALES, 18 days, controlled diet, 80 -> 300 mEq/day of potassium; urinary potassium 50 +/- 12 -> 233 +/- 45 mEq/day, so the non-renal loss went 30 -> 67 mmol/day. ABSTRACT-LEVEL ONLY - the paper is not open access and no full text was obtained, and the row says so rather than implying a full reading. It is the ONLY admissible study that measures the change WITHIN subjects, which is why it sets the shape and not the level. Extracted in validation/potassium_doseresponse_extract.py.", "THE EXPONENT ON INTAKE IN THE URINARY SHARE, and the row that stops K.RENAL_FRACTION being a constant. Non-renal potassium loss goes as intake^(1 - p) = intake^0.61, so the share reaching the urine RISES with intake. || THE SHAPE WAS FIXED BEFORE THE DATA WERE SEEN, in section 5 of validation/potassium_doseresponse_prereg.md committed at 1698d2a, and the only thing the data chose was this number. || DERIVED FROM HENE'S TWO WITHIN-SUBJECT POINTS: the ratio of non-renal losses, 0.3750/0.2233 = 1.679, over an intake ratio of 3.75, gives p = ln(1.679)/ln(3.75) = 0.392, entered at two figures because that is what two means of six subjects support. || IT IS 1.6 STANDARD ERRORS FROM ZERO AND THE INTERVAL INCLUDES A CONSTANT FRACTION. Propagating the published SDs as standard errors of the mean (12/sqrt(6) and 45/sqrt(6)) gives p = 0.39 +/- 0.24, 95 percent interval -0.08 to 0.87. THE STATISTIC DOES NOT CARRY THIS ROW. What carries it is that colonic potassium secretion rises with intake, which is E1, so a constant share would require the gut to scale its losses exactly with the diet - and the owner's judgement, on 2026-09-08, that the one datum pointing the other way measured tablet absorption rather than renal handling. || ONE STUDY, SIX SUBJECTS, ABSTRACT ONLY. That is the weakest provenance of any structural row in this ledger and it is stated here rather than left to be discovered. A second within-subject dataset would either tighten it or refute it, and Holbrook 1984 (Am J Clin Nutr, PMID 6486085, metabolic balance on self-selected diets) is the candidate that was not obtainable. || RABELINK 1990's 0.80 AT 400 mmol/day IS NOT USED HERE and the pre-registration's own rule is why: that figure is the SECOND 24-hour period of the load, and section 2 requires the intake held five days. It was quoted as supporting evidence for two days and never qualified. || WHAT IT CANNOT DO: represent potassium DEPLETION. Below the reference intake this curve keeps falling, where a real kidney conserves hard and Brunner's depletion periods show urinary excretion EXCEEDING intake as a store empties. || HOLBROOK 1984 ARGUES AGAINST THIS ROW AND IS THE BETTER STUDY. Holbrook JT, Patterson KY, Bodner JE, Douglas LW, Veillon C, Kelsay JL, Mertz W, Smith JC. Am J Clin Nutr 1984;40(4):786-93. doi:10.1093/ajcn/40.4.786. PMID 6486085. ABSTRACT-LEVEL ONLY - not open access, and the publisher, the DOI redirect and the USDA repository were all tried without reaching the full text. 28 adults, one year, four 7-day balances, meals and beverages and urine AND FECES by atomic absorption, SELF-SELECTED FOOD DIETS - so the tablet-absorption objection that removed Cappuccio's marginal fraction does not touch it. Apparent absorption of potassium 85 percent, and it 'DID NOT CHANGE SIGNIFICANTLY OVER THE WIDE RANGE OF INTAKES'. Apparent absorption already nets out colonic secretion, so a flat value across intakes is a direct statement that p = 0. || WHAT DEFENDS THIS ROW IS RANGE AND ONLY RANGE. Holbrook's subjects ate what they chose, so the span is that of self-selected diets - roughly 38 to 115 mmol/day - and across exactly that span this curve moves f_renal 0.853 to 0.905, about five percentage points, which 28 people with balance-study noise would not resolve. Hene's 80 to 300 is the range the model must span and Holbrook's design cannot test it. THAT IS A DEFENCE, NOT A REFUTATION: Holbrook has 28 subjects to Hene's 6, measures the gut term directly rather than by subtraction, and explicitly tested constancy and found it. || RECORDED HERE RATHER THAN LEFT FOR A READER TO FIND, because a row whose best contrary evidence lives only in a commit message is a row that will be quoted without it. OPEN-QUESTIONS B12. || DECIDED 2026-09-08 BY THE OWNER, OPTION 1 OF OPEN-QUESTIONS B12: the row stands, on the range argument alone. THE DEFENCE IS NOW A TEST rather than a paragraph - the suite asserts that this curve moves f_renal less than six percentage points across Holbrook's plausible span (38 to 115 mmol/day; presently 0.052), which is the whole reason a 28-subject balance study could report constancy without contradicting it. If anything ever steepens this exponent that assertion fires and the defence is gone with it. || PRECISION CORRECTED 2026-09-09 under directive 1.13, second pass. The interval is -0.08 to 0.87 and includes zero; it supports one figure. The first pass rounded this row but stopped one figure short, because the gate then allowed two figures beyond what the interval supports - a tolerance I had given myself rather than the rule. The gate now enforces the rule exactly. Discarded digits were inside the row's own stated interval, so no claim changes."),
    :K_PLASMA_REFERENCE => Provenance("K.PLASMA.REFERENCE", "mmol/L", 3.968, "A", "reported", "National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey 2007-2012, file BIOPRO, variable LBXSKSI. n = 8809 adults 20+, not pregnant, weighted. Mean 3.968 mmol/L, SD 0.320.", "AN INPUT, NOT AN OUTPUT, AND ADR 0021's FALSIFIABLE TEST 2 IS THEREFORE VOID. The pre-registration named this row a target and forbade using it to set a parameter; that is exactly what K.FRACTIONAL_EXCRETION now does, and saying so is the only honest way to enter it. || WHY: renal potassium clearance in healthy adults could not be sourced. Searches for fractional potassium excretion, potassium clearance and renal potassium handling return diabetic ketoacidosis, chronic kidney disease, diuretic therapy, transplantation and Gitelman syndrome - directive 1.7 disqualifying a sixth literature, which macula_densa_potassium_prereg.md section 2 predicted in advance. Branch P4 said not to build a balance whose outflow is invented; the outflow's SHAPE is sourced from Brunner and only its LEVEL is not, which is a case the pre-registration did not anticipate and amendment 9 records. || FOURTH DEPENDENCY INVERSION IN THIS MODEL, after arterial PCO2 (ADR 0017), the thyroid operating point (section 3.26) and plasma bicarbonate (section 3.29). The pattern is now the rule rather than the exception: WHERE A CONCENTRATION IS MEASURED IN THOUSANDS OF PEOPLE AND ITS CLEARANCE IS MEASURED IN NOBODY HEALTHY, THE CONCENTRATION IS THE INPUT. || WHAT REMAINS A PREDICTION is the RESPONSE: how plasma potassium moves when intake or filtration changes depends on the sourced fractional excretion shape and not on this row."),
    :K_RENAL_FRACTION => Provenance("K.RENAL_FRACTION", "dimensionless", 0.884, "A", "derived", "Brunner HR, Baer L, Sealey JE, Ledingham JGG, Laragh JH. The influence of potassium administration and of potassium deprivation on plasma renin in normal and hypertensive subjects. J Clin Invest 1970;49(11):2128-38. doi:10.1172/JCI106430. PMID 4319970. PMC535788. OPEN ACCESS, READ IN FULL from the Europe PMC rendered PDF. 10 NORMAL subjects on a CONSTANT dietary regimen, potassium loaded or depleted with sodium held fixed - a controlled-diet balance study in healthy volunteers, which is the preparation validation/macula_densa_potassium_prereg.md section 2 says to prefer and which almost nothing else in this literature is. || ROWS USED: Table I, normal subjects, restricted to studies at a dietary SODIUM of 100 mEq/day or more, because the remainder were run at 0.5-15 mEq/day where renin is maximally stimulated and the model does not operate. That leaves six: J.S.(2), T.B.(2), G.W., R.H., P.K.(1), G.R.(3).", "THE MEANING OF THIS ROW CHANGED ON 2026-09-08 AND ITS VALUE DID NOT. It was the urinary share of dietary potassium at EVERY intake; it is now the share AT THE REFERENCE INTAKE, with K.NONRENAL_LOSS_EXPONENT carrying how it moves. The number stays 0.884 and the reference individual is bit-identical, which is the point - promoting a constant to a function is a structural change and it is made without moving any existing result. || THE LEVEL IS BRUNNER'S AND THE SLOPE IS HENE'S, AND THE SPLIT IS DELIBERATE. Hene's own level implies 44.6 mmol/day of urinary potassium at ordinary intake against 61.17 measured by 24-hour collection in the nineteen control arms of Cappuccio 2016 - a 27 percent miss - so its level is out of line with everything else while its WITHIN-SUBJECT change is the only admissible measurement of the rise there is. Slope from the within-subject design, level from the population data, and both halves said out loud rather than blended. || D4 KEPT THIS CONSTANT FOR TWO DAYS AND D5 NOW FIRES. The datum that pointed the other way was Cappuccio's MARGINAL fraction, 0.734; the owner struck it out on the ground that it is the fate of a potassium chloride TABLET rather than of food, so tablet absorption and not renal handling sets it. With it gone the sources no longer disagree. || KEPT AS A CONSTANT ON 2026-09-06 BY DECISION D4 OF validation/potassium_doseresponse_prereg.md, NOT BY DEFAULT. Section 5 fixed IN ADVANCE the shape a rising fraction would take, and section 6 branch D5 would have taken it on two or more admissible studies measuring the rise. THE SOURCES DISAGREE ON THE SIGN. Rising: Hene 1986 (PMID 3523191) 0.63 at 80 mEq/day to 0.78 at 300, Rabelink 1990 (PMID 2266680) about 0.80 at 400 mmol/day - both ABSTRACT-LEVEL ONLY, neither open access. Falling: the MARGINAL fraction in Cappuccio 2016, the share of each supplement appearing in urine, is 0.734 across 19 trials (range 0.37-1.06), BELOW this row, and a marginal below the average makes the average FALL with intake. D4 says report the disagreement and do not split it. || AND THE MARGINAL FRACTION IS NOT A CLEAN MEASUREMENT OF THE DIETARY ONE: it is the fate of a potassium chloride tablet, not of food, and tablet absorption, incomplete 24-hour collections and trial compliance all push it down with the same sign. Reported because it is what was measured; not used to move this row. || THE PRODUCT OF THIS ROW AND K.INTAKE.NOMINAL IS NOW CHECKED AGAINST SOMETHING, WHICH IT NEVER WAS. 0.884 * 69.06 = 61.05 mmol/day of urinary potassium, against 61.17 measured by 24-hour collection in the 19 control arms of Cappuccio 2016 across 12 countries - 0.2 percent. TREAT THAT AS TOO GOOD: NHANES dietary recall understates intake and those cohorts are not American, so two quantities that should not agree this closely do. What it rules out is a gross error in a product that had never been compared with any measurement at all. || Urinary potassium over dietary potassium at the end of each experimental period, in the six admissible studies: 0.852, 0.974, 0.845, 1.008, 0.945, 0.681; mean 0.884. The residual is stool, which this model does not have. || THE RANGE INCLUDES A VALUE ABOVE 1, which is not an error: those subjects were in negative potassium balance during a load, still excreting stores laid down earlier. A one-compartment model cannot represent that and the range records it. || IT IS A FRACTION, SO IT IS INTENSIVE, and the intake it multiplies already scales. || TWO GOOD GROUPS DISAGREE AND IT IS NOT AVERAGED HERE. This row is a CONSTANT and the human fraction is not: Hene 1986 (PMID 3523191) measured urinary potassium 50 of 80 mEq/day, rising to 233 of 300, so 0.63 -> 0.78; Rabelink 1990 (PMID 2266680) about 0.80 at 400 mmol/day. Every Utrecht value is BELOW Brunner's 0.884 and the fraction clearly RISES WITH INTAKE, which this model cannot represent. Not pooled, because all three Utrecht numbers are abstract-level - none is open access - and averaging abstracts against a full-text extraction needs its own pre-registration. ADR 0021 amendment A7 disagreement 1."),
    :K_RENAL_FRACTION_MAX => Provenance("K.RENAL_FRACTION_MAX", "dimensionless", 1.0, "C", "assumed", "No source. A boundary condition on the steady-state mass balance - see notes.", "A BOUNDARY CONDITION, NOT A MEASUREMENT, AND NOT A FITTED PARAMETER. At steady state urinary excretion cannot exceed dietary intake, and the non-renal loss grows more slowly than intake (K.NONRENAL_LOSS_EXPONENT), so the urinary share tends to one as intake grows. Setting it to exactly 1.0 removes a free parameter from the form fixed in section 5 of validation/potassium_doseresponse_prereg.md rather than fitting three parameters to two data points. || IT IS AN ASYMPTOTE THE MODEL NEVER APPROACHES IN ANY RANGE IT IS VALID FOR: at four times the reference intake the share is 0.94, and the model is not to be run at intakes where the difference from 1.0 would matter. || IF A SOURCE EVER MEASURES THE SHARE AT A VERY HIGH SUSTAINED INTAKE and finds it plateauing below 1, this row becomes a fitted parameter and the exponent must be re-solved with it. Nothing found does. || HOLBROOK 1984 PUTS A CEILING ON THIS ROW THAT THE MODEL DOES NOT RESPECT, AND NO VALUE WAS CHANGED ON THE STRENGTH OF AN ABSTRACT. At steady state the urinary fraction cannot exceed the ABSORBED fraction, and Holbrook (PMID 6486085, 28 adults, faecal analysis, food diets) measures apparent absorption at 85 percent and flat across intakes. This row is 1.0 on a weaker conservation argument - that urinary excretion cannot exceed INTAKE - and the model's fraction sits above 0.85 at every intake: 0.884 at the reference and 0.942 at 400 mmol/day. || IF HOLBROOK'S FLAT 0.85 IS RIGHT, THIS ROW SHOULD BE ABOUT 0.85 AND K.NONRENAL_LOSS_EXPONENT SHOULD BE ZERO - which is the constant fraction this model had before 2026-09-08. The two rows stand or fall together and OPEN-QUESTIONS B12 is the decision. || THE ASYMPTOTE IS NEVER APPROACHED IN ANY RANGE THIS MODEL IS VALID FOR, which is why the tension is tolerable while it is being decided rather than a reason to ship two values in one week. || DECIDED 2026-09-08 BY THE OWNER, OPTION 1 OF OPEN-QUESTIONS B12: the row stands, on the range argument alone. THE DEFENCE IS NOW A TEST rather than a paragraph - the suite asserts that this curve moves f_renal less than six percentage points across Holbrook's plausible span (38 to 115 mmol/day; presently 0.052), which is the whole reason a 28-subject balance study could report constancy without contradicting it. If anything ever steepens this exponent that assertion fires and the defence is gone with it. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :RAAS_ALDO_ESCAPE_TAU => Provenance("RAAS.ALDO.ESCAPE_TAU", "day", 1.669, "A", "derived", "Kelly TM, Nelson DH. Sodium excretion and atrial natriuretic peptide levels during mineralocorticoid administration. Endocr Res 1987;13(4):363-383.", "DERIVED from the reported time course, not read off directly. Urinary sodium fell 27% immediately on fludrocortisone and returned to baseline levels in an average of 5 days, in four healthy males at 180 +/- 2 mEq/day. Modelling that as first-order recovery reaching 95% at 5 days gives tau = 5/ln(20) = 1.669 day. THE 95% CONVENTION IS A CHOICE and is recorded as one; 5/3 is within 0.1% of it. n=4, single study, so this is the weakest link in the escape path. Attribution was corrected once already, from 'Yokota N et al.' on 2026-08-21; re-verified against PMID 2966064 on 2026-08-25 and authors and every quoted number check out."),
    :RAAS_ALDO_K_GAIN => Provenance("RAAS.ALDO.K_GAIN", "1/(mmol/L)", 2.2, "A", "derived", "Brunner HR, Baer L, Sealey JE, Ledingham JGG, Laragh JH. The influence of potassium administration and of potassium deprivation on plasma renin in normal and hypertensive subjects. J Clin Invest 1970;49(11):2128-38. doi:10.1172/JCI106430. PMID 4319970. PMC535788. OPEN ACCESS, READ IN FULL from the Europe PMC rendered PDF. 10 NORMAL subjects on a CONSTANT dietary regimen, potassium loaded or depleted with sodium held fixed - a controlled-diet balance study in healthy volunteers, which is the preparation validation/macula_densa_potassium_prereg.md section 2 says to prefer and which almost nothing else in this literature is. || ROWS USED: Table I, normal subjects, restricted to studies at a dietary SODIUM of 100 mEq/day or more, because the remainder were run at 0.5-15 mEq/day where renin is maximally stimulated and the model does not operate. That leaves six: J.S.(2), T.B.(2), G.W., R.H., P.K.(1), G.R.(3).", "THE JOIN, AND THE REASON ADR 0021 IS ONE RECORD AND NOT TWO. d ln (aldosterone) / d[K] across each admissible study: 1.867, 2.733, 0.833, 2.242, 3.270 per mmol/L; mean 2.189, median 2.242. The G.W. study is excluded from the gain because its plasma potassium moved only 0.10 mmol/L and dividing by that gives 9.6 - an artefact of a small denominator, not a measurement. || A FOURFOLD SPREAD, AND IT IS REPORTED RATHER THAN NARROWED. Potassium loading also causes a natriuresis, which moves aldosterone through renin, so no study here isolates the potassium arm. That confounding is intrinsic to the preparation and not a defect of these six. || MIXED SECRETION AND EXCRETION RATES. Brunner's Table I marks each aldosterone value `s` or `e`; the gain is a LOG RATIO within a subject, so a constant conversion between the two cancels and mixing them is admissible where mixing the levels would not be. || THE RETURN ARM IS NOT BUILT: aldosterone does not act on potassium excretion in this model, because every human study found moves potassium intake and aldosterone together and neither gain can be separated from the other. ADR 0021 decision 5 is amended accordingly, and the consequence is that the potassium loop is closed by plasma potassium alone. || PRECISION CORRECTED 2026-09-09. The value carried more significant figures than its own reported interval supports, which tools/ledger_to_julia.py now refuses. Rounding it changes no claim - the discarded digits were below the width of the interval this row itself reports. || PRECISION CORRECTED 2026-09-09 under directive 1.13, second pass. An interval of 0.833-3.270 is +/-55% and supports two figures. The first pass rounded this row but stopped one figure short, because the gate then allowed two figures beyond what the interval supports - a tolerance I had given myself rather than the rule. The gate now enforces the rule exactly. Discarded digits were inside the row's own stated interval, so no claim changes."),
    :RAAS_ALDO_PRA_LOG_SLOPE => Provenance("RAAS.ALDO.PRA_LOG_SLOPE", "unitless", 0.5372, "A", "derived", "Walker WG, Moore MA, Horvath JS, Whelton PK. Arterial and venous angiotensin II in normal subjects. Circ Res 1976;38(6):477-483.", "DERIVED: log(5)/log(20) = 0.5372, from a 5-fold aldosterone rise against a 20-fold PRA rise across sodium restriction plus furosemide versus sodium surfeit, 16 normal subjects. COMPRESSIVE - a power law with exponent below 1. THE EXPONENT IS AGAINST PRA, NOT ANGIOTENSIN II. The parked component docstring said 'aldosterone vs angiotensin'; Walker reports a 2.72-fold arterial AngII rise, so aldosterone against AngII is log-log 1.47 and EXPANSIVE. Attaching the PRA-derived exponent to an angiotensin variable would have been wrong, and the model state is named pra for that reason. Verified against PMID 773568 on 2026-08-25."),
    :RAAS_ALDO_REABSORPTION_GAIN => Provenance("RAAS.ALDO.REABSORPTION_GAIN", "unitless", 0.011, "C", "assumed", "", "ASSUMED, not measured and NOT fitted to any published target. Recorded as assumed rather than calibrated because the target itself is an assumption: no source gives this quantity in a form the model can consume, so there was nothing to calibrate against. Sized so the peak unescaped tubular effect is about half a percent of filtered load, the order of magnitude implied by the 27% acute fall in urinary sodium excretion reported by Kelly and Nelson. NOT derived from it - converting a urinary excretion change into a fractional reabsorption increment needs the filtered load at the time, which that paper does not report. Same standing as RAAS.RENIN.PRESSURE_GAIN: escape zeroes fr_mod at steady state so this shapes the transient only."),
    :RAAS_ANGII_TUBULAR_KM => Provenance("RAAS.ANGII.TUBULAR_KM", "dimensionless", 1.0, "B", "derived", "Hall JE, Granger JP, Hester RL, Coleman TG, Smith MJ Jr, Cross RB. Mechanisms of escape from sodium retention during angiotensin II hypertension. Am J Physiol 1984;246(5 Pt 2):F627-F634.", "HALF-SATURATION CONSTANT of the angiotensin II tubular effect, on this model's normalised pra axis. Derived 2026-09-19 with RAAS.ANGII.TUBULAR_VMAX from the same two measured points and the origin - see that row for the full derivation, the sources and every caveat. || Km = 1.0 means the effect is HALF-MAXIMAL at pra = 1.0, which is this model's rectified renin PLATEAU, and therefore already past half-maximal at the resting pra of 1.2956. THAT IS THE WHOLE CONTENT OF THE SATURATION FINDING: angiotensin II's tubular action is more than half spent at rest, so doubling it adds little. Hall's two studies say the same thing in their own units - a total of 0.0051 against a marginal 0.00144. || TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES. The value landing on exactly 1.0 is arithmetic from two-figure inputs and NOT a claim that the half-saturation point coincides with the plateau; a third figure on either source would move it. Directive 1.13. || MICHAELIS-MENTEN IS A CONVENTION. See RAAS.ANGII.TUBULAR_VMAX. SPECIES: dog. SCALING: none - Km is on a dimensionless normalised axis and its transfer rests on pra meaning the same physiological state in both species, which is the weakest step and is stated on the Vmax row."),
    :RAAS_ANGII_TUBULAR_VMAX => Provenance("RAAS.ANGII.TUBULAR_VMAX", "dimensionless", 0.0091, "B", "derived", "Hall JE, Granger JP, Hester RL, Coleman TG, Smith MJ Jr, Cross RB. Mechanisms of escape from sodium retention during angiotensin II hypertension. Am J Physiol 1984;246(5 Pt 2):F627-F634.", "MAXIMAL ANGIOTENSIN II-DEPENDENT TUBULAR SODIUM REABSORPTION, as a fraction of the filtered load. Derived 2026-09-19 with RAAS.ANGII.TUBULAR_KM from TWO MEASURED POINTS AND THE ORIGIN, angii_saturating_prereg.md. NOT FITTED to anything this model is judged by. || THE TWO POINTS. f(pra = 1.2956) = 0.00510, from Hall 1977's five NORMAL dogs: intrarenal blockade of ENDOGENOUS angiotensin II took fractional sodium excretion from 1.23 +/- 0.19 to 1.74 +/- 0.28 percent with GLOMERULAR FILTRATION RATE AND ARTERIAL PRESSURE BOTH UNCHANGED, so the 0.51 percentage points is very nearly purely tubular. f(pra = 2.5795) = 0.00654, that total plus Hall 1984's marginal 24 mEq/day of 24/16724 = 0.00144 measured at FIXED renal perfusion pressure with plasma aldosterone already back at control. f(0) = 0 by definition: no angiotensin II, no angiotensin II-dependent reabsorption. || THE SOLUTION. Michaelis-Menten f(p) = Vmax*p/(Km+p) through those points gives Km = 1.0 and Vmax = 0.0091. TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES because both inputs carry two, directive 1.13. Check: 0.0091*1.2956/(1.0+1.2956) = 0.00514 against 0.00510, and 0.0091*2.5795/(1.0+2.5795) = 0.00656 against 0.00654. || MICHAELIS-MENTEN IS A CONVENTION AND NOT A MEASUREMENT. Two points and an origin determine a two-parameter saturating family and nothing more; the shape between and beyond them is assumed. It is the conventional choice for a receptor-mediated transport effect and the row says so rather than dressing it as sourced. ANY saturating curve through the same two points has a similar LOCAL SLOPE, which is the only thing the prediction this form was built to test turns on. || WHAT IT BUYS OVER THE LINE IT REPLACES. Local slope at the operating point is Vmax*Km/(Km+pra_ref)^2 = 0.00173 per unit pra against the superseded linear 0.0011 - 1.58 times steeper - because a straight line fitted across pra 1.296 to 2.58 averages over a range the curve flattens across. || THE MODEL'S TERM IS STILL A DEVIATION, fr_angii = f(pra) - f(pra_ref), because the baseline fractional reabsorption already contains angiotensin II's resting contribution. Identically zero at the operating point, so nothing pinned moves. || SPECIES: DOG, conscious, two preparations from the same laboratory - intrarenal blockade (n = 5 normal) and systemic infusion with servo-controlled renal perfusion pressure (n = 8). SCALING: none - both points are FRACTIONS OF THE FILTERED LOAD, which is intensive, so no body-size scaling applies; that is the same argument RAAS.RENIN.SYMPATHETIC_THRESHOLD_SHIFT makes for an intensive pressure, and it is still dog to human. || TWO POINTS DO NOT ESTABLISH A SATURATION CURVE. They are CONSISTENT with one. The preparations differ and the 1977 sodium-depleted arm is NOT usable for a tubular effect because glomerular filtration rate rose 22 percent in it. Only the normal-sodium arm is clean. || ================ THIS ROW IS THE SUPERSEDED LINEAR GAIN, 0.0011 per unit pra, AND ITS PROVENANCE IS CARRIED HERE VERBATIM. It was deleted from the ledger on 2026-09-19 by angii_saturating_prereg.md, not because anything in the derivation below was wrong but because the FORM it parameterised was. Hall 1977 and Hall 1984 together show the relation SATURATES - a total effect of 0.0051 of the filtered load at normal sodium against a marginal increment of only 0.00144 on raising angiotensin II to sodium-deprivation levels - and a term linear in pra cannot represent that. It is deleted rather than left beside its replacement because check_relations.py's unread-rows gate would fail an unused row, and because two copies of a fact is the failure this repository is organised against. Everything below is the record of how 0.0011 was obtained and why the deletion costs nothing. ================ SOURCED 2026-09-19 FROM HALL 1984, FULL TEXT READ, angii_tubular_prereg.md. PMID 6720967. Twelve conscious male mongrel dogs 21-27 kg (mean 22.2 +/- 0.8), chronically instrumented, sodium-deficient diet plus supplement to about 80 meq/day, ANG II [Asp1,Val5] at 5 ng/kg/min. EIGHT DOGS HAD RENAL PERFUSION PRESSURE SERVO-CONTROLLED AT THE CONTROL LEVEL by an inflatable aortic occluder driven by a proportional servo-control unit, 24 h a day. That preparation is the whole reason this row is possible: it excludes the pressure-natriuresis route by construction, and it cannot be performed in a human. Directive 1.15. || THE MAGNITUDE IS THE DAY-6 RATE AND NOT THE SIX-DAY AVERAGE. Cumulative sodium balance rose 210 +/- 37 meq over 6 days, which is 35 meq/day averaged - but that average is FRONT-LOADED over a transient the free arm shows directly (Table 1: UNaV falls to 20 meq/day on day 1 and recovers). At day 6 the servo arm excreted 56 +/- 5 meq/day against an intake of about 80, so the ONGOING retention is 24 +/- 5 meq/day. TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES, directive 1.13. || WHY THE DAY 6 POINT IS THE RIGHT ONE FOR A NON-ESCAPING TERM: at that point the renal perfusion pressure is STILL CLAMPED, so the pressure route is excluded by the preparation; plasma aldosterone has RETURNED TO CONTROL, 4.9 +/- 0.8 against a control of 4.6 +/- 1.0, so the aldosterone route is excluded BY MEASUREMENT rather than by assumption; and retention continues anyway. What is left is the direct tubular action of angiotensin II, which is exactly what ADR 0015 defines fr_angii to be. || AND HALL CONFIRMS ADR 0015'S STRUCTURAL CHOICE INDEPENDENTLY. His conclusion is that a rise in renal arterial pressure is ESSENTIAL for escape; releasing the occluder took urinary sodium from 56 to 322 +/- 35 meq/day in one day. So escape is PRESSURE-MEDIATED and not intrinsic to the tubular effect, which is why this term is added OUTSIDE the aldosterone escape state rather than inside it. The design was right before the source was found. || THE DERIVATION. Expressed as a fraction of the filtered load, which is intensive: 24 meq/day / (81.5 ml/min = 117.4 L/day x 142.5 meq/l = 16724 meq/day) = 0.00144. Anchored on Hall's own statement that the 5 ng/kg/min dose was calculated to reach plasma ANG II levels found during SODIUM DEPRIVATION, which on this model's pra axis is the low-salt arm: pra 2.5795 at 38 mEq/day against a resting 1.2956, a difference of 1.2839. So k_angii = 0.00144 / 1.2839 = 0.0011 per unit pra, TWO FIGURES. || SCALING: THE TRANSFER IS DONE ON THE FRACTION AND NOT ON THE RATE. A retention in meq/day is EXTENSIVE and would need a body-size scaling to cross species; a fraction of the filtered load is INTENSIVE and does not. That is the same argument RAAS.RENIN.SYMPATHETIC_THRESHOLD_SHIFT makes for an intensive pressure. It is the strongest transfer available and it is still dog to human, which this row states rather than hides. || THREE WEAK LINKS, NAMED IN THE PRE-REGISTRATION BEFORE ANY VALUE WAS ENTERED. (1) The cross-species fractional transfer above. (2) Mapping SODIUM DEPRIVATION onto this model's 38 mEq/day arm is THIS REPOSITORY'S JUDGEMENT AND NOT HALL'S - he names the physiological state, not a pra. (3) Hall's preparation has EXOGENOUS ANG II with SUPPRESSED ENDOGENOUS RENIN, plasma renin activity falling 0.27 to 0.04 ng/ml/h, and this model's pra conflates renin with angiotensin II and cannot represent that dissociation; the anchor therefore rests on total angiotensin II activity being comparable, not on the renin state matching. || NOT FITTED TO ANYTHING THIS MODEL IS JUDGED BY. ADR 0015 requires that the magnitude never be fitted to salt data, and it was not: it comes from a servo-controlled dog preparation at fixed pressure. Hall 1980's clamp contrast - control dogs taking under 7 mmHg across 5 to 500 meq/day against AngII-clamped dogs taking +42 percent - is ADR 0015's FALSIFIABLE TEST and was deliberately kept out of the estimation. || SPECIES: DOG, conscious, chronically instrumented, n = 8 in the servo arm. FULL TEXT READ - supplied by the owner on 2026-09-19, which is what made this row possible after the 2026-09-18 search recorded the mapping as the precise blocker. || THE ESTIMATION COHORT IS MALE - twelve conscious male mongrel dogs - and this row is entered as 'both' because it is a dimensionless fraction of the filtered load with no sexed input, not because the cohort was mixed. No sex difference in the direct tubular action of angiotensin II was sought or found here. Recorded the way RN.MD.RENIN_GAIN records its own all-male estimation set. || CORROBORATED AND QUALIFIED 2026-09-19 BY HALL 1977, FULL TEXT READ. Hall JE, Guyton AC, Trippodo NC, Lohmeier TE, McCaa RE, Cowley AW Jr. Intrarenal control of electrolyte excretion by angiotensin II. Am J Physiol 1977;232(6):F538-F544, PMID 879288. Supplied by the owner. || IT IS THE ENDOGENOUS VERSION OF THE SAME EXPERIMENT, AND IT NEEDS NO DOSE MAPPING AT ALL. Instead of infusing exogenous angiotensin II, Hall BLOCKED the endogenous hormone with intrarenal [Sar1,Ile8] angiotensin II at 0.25 ug/kg/min - so the contrast is a dog's own angiotensin II acting against the same dog with it blocked, in the same kidney. The anchor problem this row's derivation works around does not arise. || THE NORMAL-SODIUM ARM IS THE CLEAN ONE AND IT SITS AT THIS MODEL'S OWN OPERATING POINT. Five normal dogs, Table 1: fractional sodium excretion 1.23 +/- 0.19 percent rising to 1.74 +/- 0.28 percent after 90 min of blockade, with GLOMERULAR FILTRATION RATE UNCHANGED (0.83 +/- 0.08 to 0.86 +/- 0.11 ml/min per g, not significant) and mean arterial pressure unchanged (128.2 to 131.6 mmHg). So the 0.51 percentage point change is very nearly PURELY TUBULAR: endogenous angiotensin II accounts for 0.0051 of the filtered load being reabsorbed at normal sodium status. || AND ALDOSTERONE IS EXCLUDED AGAIN, BY A SECOND ROUTE. In four sodium-depleted dogs the blocker produced NO CONSISTENT CHANGES in plasma aldosterone concentration, so the natriuresis is not adrenal. That is the same conclusion Hall 1984 reaches by watching aldosterone return to control while retention continues - two different designs, same exclusion. || THE TWO STUDIES TOGETHER SAY THE RELATION IS SATURATING, AND THAT IS WHY THIS ROW'S TERM IS TOO WEAK. Hall 1977 gives the TOTAL angiotensin II-dependent tubular reabsorption at normal sodium as 0.0051 of the filtered load; Hall 1984 gives the MARGINAL increment on raising angiotensin II to sodium-deprivation levels as 24/16724 = 0.00144. The marginal is 0.28 of the total, so ROUGHLY A QUARTER OF THE RESTING EFFECT IS ADDED BY TAKING ANGIOTENSIN II TO SODIUM-DEPRIVATION LEVELS. The tubular action is largely SATURATED at normal angiotensin II. || THIS EXPLAINS THE REFUTATION RATHER THAN OVERTURNING IT. The marginal slope really is small - that is physiology, not a sourcing error - and the measured 1.2x amplification against ADR 0015's required 2x stands. What it indicts is the FORM: fr_angii linear in pra through a reference cannot represent a relation that is mostly saturated at rest with a small marginal slope. || A FALSIFIABLE PREDICTION FOR WHOEVER TAKES THIS NEXT, RECORDED BEFORE IT IS TRIED. A saturating form has a LARGER LOCAL SLOPE AT REST than a straight line fitted across pra 1.296 to 2.58, because the line averages over a range the curve flattens across. So replacing the linear term with a saturating one SHOULD RAISE the amplification above 1.2x. If it does not, the form is not what is wrong and ADR 0015's own suspicion - that the record is necessary and nowhere near sufficient - is the answer. || WHAT THESE NUMBERS DO NOT SUPPORT. Two points do not establish a saturation curve; they are CONSISTENT with one. The preparations differ - 1977 is intrarenal blockade in dogs whose kidneys averaged 59.5 +/- 4.1 g, 1984 is systemic infusion with servo-controlled pressure - and the sodium-depleted arm of 1977 is NOT usable for the tubular effect because glomerular filtration rate rose 22 percent in it, which the paper says accounts for an important part of the natriuresis. Only the normal-sodium arm is clean. NO VALUE ON THIS ROW WAS CHANGED on the strength of any of this."),
    :RAAS_PRA_REFERENCE => Provenance("RAAS.PRA.REFERENCE", "dimensionless", 1.2956, "C", "derived", "Derived: this model's own resting pra with the AngII tubular term off. See notes.", "DERIVED, and it exists so that RAAS.ANGII.TUBULAR_GAIN's term is IDENTICALLY ZERO AT THE OPERATING POINT. fr_angii = k_angii * (pra - pra_ref), so with pra_ref set to this model's own resting plasma renin activity the term vanishes at rest, the operating point does not move, and therefore the resting pra does not move either - the fixed point is self-consistent immediately and needs no iteration. || THE VALUE is the resting pra measured with the angiotensin II tubular term OFF, which is the state every other constant in this ledger was derived in. Measured by running: 1.2956. || IT IS NOT A MEASUREMENT AND MUST NEVER BE COMPARED WITH ONE. pra in this model is normalised so that 1.0 is the RECTIFIED PLATEAU, not resting renin - Raas.jl records that conflating the two is what broke RAAS.RENIN.PRESSURE_GAIN in the first place. This row is the model's own operating point on that normalised axis and carries no claim about human plasma renin activity. || CHANGING IT MOVES THE OPERATING POINT. If the resting pra ever changes - because a gain, a threshold or an arm upstream of renin changes - this row must be re-measured in the same pass, or fr_angii stops being zero at rest and every pinned resting value drifts with it."),
    :RAAS_PRA_TAU => Provenance("RAAS.PRA.TAU", "day", 0.0035, "C", "assumed", "", "ASSUMED, and deliberately not cited. The parked component attributed renin kinetics to Hofbauer KG et al. Pflugers Arch 1975; that paper was NOT opened, so per directive 1.5 it is not recorded here. 0.0035 day is about 5 minutes. WHY THE VALUE BARELY MATTERS: it is three orders of magnitude faster than the escape time constant and four faster than the renal-body fluid loop, so on this timebase the renin step is effectively instantaneous and any value in the minutes range gives the same trajectory. If it is ever made to matter, source it first. ADR 0002 would also justify making pra algebraic and dropping the state entirely."),
    :RAAS_RENIN_PRESSURE_GAIN => Provenance("RAAS.RENIN.PRESSURE_GAIN", "unitless", 4.35, "B", "derived", "van Ochten M, El Fathi W, Bovee EME, Spaanderman MEA, Hooijmans CR, van Drongelen J. The renal baroreflex: A systematic review and meta-analysis in healthy and hypertensive animals. Physiol Rep 2025;13(17):e70547. doi:10.14814/phy2.70547. PMID 40930784. PMC12422813. THE SLOPE, section 3.3.2 and Figure 6, read as full text: 'A decrease of 10 mmHg in renal blood pressure leads to an increase of 50 percentage points renin in healthy animals', where renin is expressed as a percentage of the value at the baseline mean renal arterial pressure of 110 mmHg, set to 100%. That baseline is ABOVE the 93 mmHg threshold, so the 100% reference IS the plateau - which is exactly what pra = 1 means in Raas.jl. Slope = 0.05 of plateau renin per mmHg.", "RE-DERIVED 2026-09-02, 19.0 -> 4.35, AND THE ROW STOPS BEING `assumed`. Pre-registered in validation/renin_gain_prereg.md, written before any source was opened and sitting before this change in history; verify with git log --diff-filter=A on that file. Reproduce with python validation/renin_gain_extract.py. Branch R1. || THE DERIVATION, and it is one line. Raas.jl sets pra -> 1 + g_renin*(P_thr - MAP)/MAP_ref, so d(pra)/d(MAP) = -g_renin/MAP_ref, and pra is normalised to the plateau. van Ochten reports the same derivative as 0.05 of plateau renin per mmHg. Therefore g_renin = 0.05 * MAP_ref = 0.05 * 87.0 = 4.35. || THE PREVIOUS NOTE ON THIS ROW WAS WRONG ABOUT THE SOURCE, AND THAT IS WHY THE ROW SAT `assumed` FOR SIX DAYS. It said van Ochten 'reports the renal baroreflex slope in animal units this model cannot consume directly, so the gain is fitted rather than converted.' The paper says the opposite in its own Limitations: it could NOT meta-analyse absolute renin because studies reported PRA, PRC or renin release on assay-dependent scales, so it converted the dose-response to PERCENTAGE OF BASELINE - which is the one form this model can consume, because pra here is dimensionless and normalised. The units were never the obstacle. Nobody had opened the paper. || ONE SIGNIFICANT FIGURE IN THE SOURCE, THREE IN THE VALUE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS ARITHMETIC NOT PRECISION. '50 percentage points per 10 mmHg' carries one figure. 4.35 is stored so that g_renin = 0.05*MAP_ref reproduces exactly and stays exact if CV.MAP.SETPOINT moves again; the third digit is the conversion, not a measurement. Directive 1.9. A test asserts the identity rather than the digits. || SPECIES AND TIER. Animal, mixed (rat, dog, mouse, rabbit, cat, fowl). ADR 0006 E2 under an ethical ceiling: servo-controlling renal perfusion pressure is not performable in humans, the same clause RAAS.RENIN.PRESSURE_THRESHOLD already invokes from this same paper. TIER B RATHER THAN THE THRESHOLD ROW'S A, deliberately: the threshold 93 +/- 2 comes from the full 30-study dose-response meta-analysis, while this slope is quoted only for the 5-comparison healthy-versus-hypertensive subset (Figure 6). The paper gives no numeric slope for the 30-study analysis in text - Figure 4 would have to be digitised, which is a different extraction_method. The paper also states 'Risk of bias was high in most studies.' || VALIDITY RANGE. The linear regression was fitted below 90 mmHg renal arterial pressure; the plateau was averaged above 105; the threshold is their intersection. This model runs at MAP 82-87, INSIDE the fitted linear range, so no extrapolation is involved. The pre-existing simplification that systemic MAP is used directly as renal perfusion pressure is unchanged by this row and is Raas.jl's, not this row's. || WHAT IT CHANGES, MEASURED with bench/renin_gain_sweep.jl. Resting pra falls 2.31 -> 1.30 relative to the plateau. THE SALT-STEP STEADY STATE DOES NOT MOVE: escape drives fr_mod to zero, and a 16-fold change in this gain moves the shift by at most 0.81%, so the headline 5.0570 mmHg is unchanged to five significant figures. THE STEADY STATE WITH ESCAPE SUPPRESSED DOES MOVE, and that is ADR 0015's configuration: the fall in the salt-step shift goes from 50.7% at 19.0 to about 22% here. ADR 0015 stays LIVE by its own pre-registered 20% rule and its magnitude more than halves. || DECLARED STRUCTURAL LIMITATION, branch S2 of the pre-registration, and it is NOT a defect in this value. The rectified linear form caps the PRA ratio it can produce between two pressures at the ratio of their drives, independently of the gain. van den Bosch 2021 (PMID 34921521, n = 70 healthy men) reports PRA 2.10 on high salt against 5.74 on low, a ratio of 2.73, at MAP 88 against 86 - where this form's ceiling is (93-86)/(93-88) = 1.40. NO VALUE OF THIS GAIN REPRODUCES THAT. Human salt-induced renin runs mostly through macula densa sodium delivery and renal sympathetic traffic, neither of which this component has. The pre-registration forbade fitting to those data for exactly this reason, and the gap is recorded in HANDOVER section 7 rather than absorbed into this number - which is how RN.PRESSURE_NATRIURESIS.SLOPE became a hypertensive value (section 3.3)."),
    :RAAS_RENIN_PRESSURE_THRESHOLD => Provenance("RAAS.RENIN.PRESSURE_THRESHOLD", "mmHg", 93.0, "A", "reported", "van Ochten M, El Fathi W, Bovee EME, Spaanderman MEA, Hooijmans CR, van Drongelen J. The renal baroreflex: a systematic review and meta-analysis in healthy and hypertensive animals. Physiol Rep 2025;13(17):e70547.", "THE COINCIDENCE IS BROKEN AS OF 2026-08-27, AND IT WAS THE FRAGILITY THIS ROW WARNED ABOUT. This threshold, 93 mmHg from van Ochten, used to equal CV.MAP.SETPOINT exactly, so the model sat ON the rectification point and RAAS was inactive at baseline BY CONSTRUCTION. The note said that rested on one unverified number. It did: the setpoint was the textbook brachial 120/80 convention and is now 87.0, derived from sourced central pressure. The model therefore sits 6 mmHg BELOW threshold and RAAS IS NOW ACTIVE AT REST - renin drive 0.069, plasma renin activity 2.31x baseline. That is more physiological, not less: resting renin is not zero. || Meta-analysis of 30 animal studies: renin falls linearly with rising renal pressure and plateaus above 93 +/- 2 mmHg. ADR 0006 E2 - servo-controlling renal perfusion pressure is not performable in humans, the same ethical ceiling as RN.PRESSURE_NATRIURESIS.SLOPE. QUALITY CAVEAT FROM THE PAPER ITSELF: risk of bias was high in most studies. FRAGILITY, RECORDED: this threshold equals CV.MAP.SETPOINT = 93.0, whose own citation reads 'Standard physiological reference. VERIFY.' The model therefore sits exactly ON the rectification threshold and RAAS is inactive at and above baseline BY CONSTRUCTION. That coincidence is doing structural work and rests on one unverified number. Verified against PubMed record PMID 40930784 on 2026-08-25. SCALING: none applied, and none is needed. The quantity is a PRESSURE THRESHOLD in mmHg, and arterial pressure is not body-size scaled across the mammals in this meta-analysis the way a flow or a volume would be - the threshold is reported in the same units the model uses. What is assumed instead is that the threshold TRANSFERS to humans unchanged, which is a species assumption rather than a scaling one, and it is the assumption to attack if this component misbehaves. Note the meta-analysis itself found the threshold comparable between healthy and hypertensive animals while the slope differed, which is weak evidence that the threshold is the more transferable of the two. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :RAAS_RENIN_SYMPATHETIC_THRESHOLD_SHIFT => Provenance("RAAS.RENIN.SYMPATHETIC_THRESHOLD_SHIFT", "mmHg", 17.0, "B", "derived", "Kirchheim HR, Finke R, Hackenthal E, Lowe W, Persson P. Baroreflex sympathetic activation increases threshold pressure for the pressure-dependent renin release in conscious dogs. Pflugers Arch 1985;405(2):127-135.", "SOURCED 2026-09-18 FROM KIRCHHEIM 1985, renal_sympathetic_prereg.md amendment 1. SEVEN chronically instrumented CONSCIOUS FOXHOUNDS, daily sodium 6.1 mmol/kg, renal artery pressure reduced in steps with an inflatable cuff and a pressure control system and held 5 min at each step. Stimulus-response curves of the renal-venous-arterial plasma renin activity difference against mean renal artery pressure. ABSTRACT ONLY - Pflugers Arch 1985 is paywalled and no PMC record exists; the abstract carries the numbers with dispersion, which is why the row is possible at all. Directive 1.5: the MEDLINE abstract was opened, the full text was not. || THE EFFECT IS A THRESHOLD SHIFT AND NOT A GAIN CHANGE, WHICH IS WHY THIS ROW EXISTS RATHER THAN A SYMPATHETIC RENIN GAIN. Common carotid occlusion shifted the stimulus-response curve to the RIGHT: threshold pressure 92.7 +/- 2.8 -> 109.7 +/- 4.1 mmHg (P < 0.05), while the PLATEAU level was unchanged (0.98 +/- 0.14 vs 0.99 +/- 0.14 ng AI/ml/h) and the SLOPE below threshold was unchanged (-0.379 +/- 0.041 vs -0.416 +/- 0.082 ng AI/ml/h/mmHg). The pre-registration assumed a gain and was wrong; opening the source is what caught it. || 109.7 - 92.7 = 17.0 mmHg, TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES. The SEs combine to about 5 mmHg, so the shift is 17 +/- 5 and the range on this row is one pooled SE either way. n = 7 dogs. Directive 1.13: nothing derived from this may carry three figures. || IT IS THE SHIFT AT NEAR-MAXIMAL CAROTID ACTIVATION, NOT A SLOPE. Carotid occlusion removes sinus baroreceptor input; Gross 1981 in the same laboratory reports bilateral occlusion raising systemic pressure 51 +/- 6.3 mmHg, so the stimulus is large. The model therefore applies this as the shift at SATURATING renal sympathetic activity and interpolates with the tanh the baroreflex already uses for its vasomotor efferent. THAT INTERPOLATION IS AN ASSUMPTION AND NOT KIRCHHEIM'S: he measured two points, control and occlusion, and no submaximal curve. || AND THE THRESHOLD IT SHIFTS WAS INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRMED BY THE SAME PAPER. RAAS.RENIN.PRESSURE_THRESHOLD is 93 +/- 2 mmHg from van Ochten's 2025 meta-analysis of 30 mostly-rodent studies whose own limitations say risk of bias was high in most; Kirchheim's conscious dogs give 92.7 +/- 2.8 by a different method in a different species. Nobody arranged that agreement. || MECHANISM CONTROL, Gross R et al. J Physiol 1981;313:237-250, PMID 7024507, twelve conscious foxhounds: with renal perfusion pressure held at control, carotid occlusion changed renin release in NEITHER the surgically denervated kidney (n = 3) NOR the intact kidney after beta-adrenergic blockade (n = 4), and renal blood flow did not change. So the pathway is DIRECT BETA-ADRENERGIC on the juxtaglomerular cells, with no vasomotor component - which is why this row moves a renin threshold and touches no haemodynamic quantity. || SPECIES: DOG, conscious, chronically instrumented. Directive 1.15 - the owner's standing default for neurogenic control of pressure through renal or baroreceptor mechanisms where no usable human measurement exists, and servo-control of renal artery pressure is not performable in humans, the same ethical ceiling that applies to RN.PRESSURE_NATRIURESIS.SLOPE and to the threshold this row shifts. || SETTING THIS ROW TO 0 RECOVERS THE PREVIOUS MODEL EXACTLY, on the precedent RN.MD.RENIN_GAIN was introduced on. No new structural variant and no new compilation. || SCALING: NONE, AND THAT IS AN ARGUMENT RATHER THAN AN OMISSION. This row is a difference of two ARTERIAL PRESSURES, which is an INTENSIVE quantity - it carries no mass or surface-area dimension, so the body-size scaling that BF.SIZE.EXPONENT applies to extensive rows does not apply here and multiplying by a mass ratio would be wrong. Dog and human operate at similar arterial pressures: Kirchheim's conscious foxhounds sit at a control threshold of 92.7 mmHg and Lohmeier's conscious dogs at a control MAP of 92 +/- 4 and 98 +/- 4 mmHg, against this model's central MAP setpoint of 87 mmHg and a renin threshold of 93. The pressures the two species regulate are the same to within the dispersion of either, so the shift transfers directly. WHAT DOES NOT TRANSFER WITHOUT ASSUMPTION is the mapping from carotid occlusion to this model's normalised sympathetic activity, which is stated above and is the real cross-species risk on this row - not the units."),
    :RBC_LIFESPAN => Provenance("RBC.LIFESPAN", "day", 120.0, "C", "assumed", "Conventional figure. The biotin-labelling primaries were NOT opened.", "ASSUMED, NOT REPORTED, AND THE HONEST REASON IS THAT NO PRIMARY WAS OPENED. Directive 1.5 forbids writing a citation I have not read, and the biotin-labelling studies reached in this pass were at search-summary level only. || THE ROUND 120 IS A TEACHING NUMBER AND THE MEASUREMENTS SIT ABOVE IT, which is directive 1.12 with the usual sign: biotin-labelled transfused red cells give a mean lifespan near 132 days (95 percent CI 120-146) for fresh blood and near 124 (109-142) for stored, so the conventional 120 sits at or below the lower confidence bound rather than at the centre. Those are POST-TRANSFUSION survivals carrying a storage lesion autologous cells do not, so 132 is a floor and not a centre. The range recorded here is the fresh-blood interval. || IT BARELY MATTERS, AND SAYING WHY IS THE POINT. Only the COMBINATION lifespan/(1+gain) is identified by the recovery data that sets RBC.PRODUCTION_GAIN, so an error here is absorbed by the gain and the recovery time - the only behaviour tested - is unchanged. The lifespan alone would matter to anything reading the steady-state erythropoietic turnover, and NOTHING IN THIS MODEL READS IT. Section 3.26's rule applied: where two rows are not separately identifiable, the composite is the real quantity and the split is bookkeeping."),
    :RBC_MCHC_FEMALE => Provenance("RBC.MCHC", "g/dL", 33.418, "A", "derived", "Derived from CV.HEMATOCRIT.NOMINAL and BLOOD.HB_CONCENTRATION.", "DERIVED AS Hb/Hct = 13.2/0.395 = 33.418 g per dL of red cells. See the male row for the argument. THE PAIR IS SEXED ONLY BECAUSE ITS PARENTS ARE: the sex difference in MCHC is 1.2 percent against 15 percent in haematocrit, so this is very nearly a shared row and is kept as a pair solely to hold each sex's operating point exactly where it already sits. || IT CARRIES FIVE FIGURES AND THAT IS NOT A PRECISION CLAIM. This row exists to CLOSE AN IDENTITY - Hb = MCHC*Hct at the reference state - and check_closure.py asserts it. CLAUDE.md's precision rule exempts exactly this case, because rounding a closure constant to the precision of its parents is what moved the operating point in the first place: 33.8*0.453 = 15.311 against a sourced 15.3. AS A PHYSIOLOGICAL STATEMENT THIS ROW IS 34 g/dL, and any report of it must say 34."),
    :RBC_MCHC_MALE => Provenance("RBC.MCHC", "g/dL", 33.775, "A", "derived", "Derived from CV.HEMATOCRIT.NOMINAL and BLOOD.HB_CONCENTRATION.", "DERIVED AS Hb/Hct = 15.3/0.453 = 33.775 g per dL of red cells, and IT INTRODUCES NO NEW SOURCE: both parents are already in this ledger from THE SAME COHORT AND STRATUM (section 3.24). || IT TURNS AN EXISTING COINCIDENCE INTO A STRUCTURAL IDENTITY. Section 3.24 already reports these implied values as a real check - 33.8 male and 33.4 female, both inside a normal 32-36, and it notes the check could have failed. Making haemoglobin follow red cell mass through this constant is what lets arterial oxygen content respond to a bleed at all, and it removes a redundancy rather than adding a parameter. || CONSTANCY IS AN ASSUMPTION AND IT IS THE MODEL'S OWN SCOPE THAT JUSTIFIES IT. MCHC falls in iron deficiency - hypochromia - and this model has no iron, so holding it constant is consistent with what the model can represent rather than with all of haematology. Across the haematocrit range a 1 L bleed traverses here, 0.453 to 0.373, a healthy MCHC does not move materially. Branch E5 of the pre-registration therefore does NOT fire and haemoglobin becomes derived. || IT CARRIES FIVE FIGURES AND THAT IS NOT A PRECISION CLAIM. This row exists to CLOSE AN IDENTITY - Hb = MCHC*Hct at the reference state - and check_closure.py asserts it. CLAUDE.md's precision rule exempts exactly this case, because rounding a closure constant to the precision of its parents is what moved the operating point in the first place: 33.8*0.453 = 15.311 against a sourced 15.3. AS A PHYSIOLOGICAL STATEMENT THIS ROW IS 34 g/dL, and any report of it must say 34."),
    :RBC_PRODUCTION_GAIN => Provenance("RBC.PRODUCTION_GAIN", "dimensionless", 4.0, "B", "derived", "Derived from RBC.LIFESPAN and RBC.RECOVERY_TAU.", "DERIVED: G = lifespan/tau - 1 = 120/24 - 1 = 4.0. With first-order destruction and production scaled by the oxygen deficit, the deficit decays with time constant lifespan/(1+G), so the feedback speeds recovery by exactly (1+G) - here about fivefold against the passive decay the lifespan alone would give. || THE RANGE IS THE INTERPRETATION SPREAD ON RBC.RECOVERY_TAU propagated through, not a confidence interval. || THE SENSED SIGNAL IS ARTERIAL OXYGEN CONTENT AND NOT ERYTHROPOIETIN, declared in erythropoiesis_prereg.md section 4 before any source was opened. Erythropoietin answers to renal TISSUE oxygenation - renal delivery against renal consumption, and consumption tracks tubular sodium reabsorption - and this model has no renal blood flow, no renal oxygen consumption and no filtration fraction. Arterial content is what the model computes and it falls in exactly the two states that raise erythropoietin, anaemia and hypoxia. It is the same reduction ADR 0010 makes: named after a hormone, keyed to what the model has, carrying no hormone state. || WHAT THIS FORECLOSES: the model CANNOT REPRESENT ANAEMIA OF RENAL DISEASE, where the kidney fails to make erythropoietin at a normal arterial oxygen content. That is the paradigm case the reduction excludes, and it is written on the row rather than discovered later."),
    :RBC_RECOVERY_TAU => Provenance("RBC.RECOVERY_TAU", "day", 24.0, "B", "derived", "Pottgiesser T, Specker W, Umhau M, Dickhuth H-H, Roecker K, Schumacher YO. Recovery of hemoglobin mass after blood donation. Transfusion 2008;48(7):1390-1397. PMID 18466177. ABSTRACT READ IN FULL via the E-utilities API; full text not opened. 29 healthy male volunteers aged 30 +/- 10 donated about 550 mL of whole blood, losing 75 +/- 15 g of haemoglobin mass (8.8 +/- 1.9 percent), measured by optimised carbon monoxide rebreathing, and recovered it in 36 +/- 11 days (range 20-59).", "DERIVED FROM POTTGIESSER'S RECOVERY TIME UNDER A STATED INTERPRETATION, AND THE INTERPRETATION IS THE UNCERTAINTY. The paper reports the deficit recovered after 36 +/- 11 days. THAT IS A RETURN TO BASELINE, NOT A TIME CONSTANT, and how many time constants it represents depends on the precision at which return was declared. || CO rebreathing resolves haemoglobin mass to roughly 2 percent and the deficit was 8.8 percent, so a decay to within measurement noise is 8.8 -> 2, which is 1.48 time constants and gives tau = 24 days. Read instead as conventional full recovery at 3 time constants it gives 12. THE ENTERED RANGE 12-24 IS THAT INTERPRETATION SPREAD AND NOT A CONFIDENCE INTERVAL, and saying which is which is directive 1.9. The 24 is taken because a study reporting per-subject recovery times spanning 20-59 days was detecting return against measurement precision, not fitting an exponential. || IT IS AN ESTIMATION SET AND MAY NEVER BE REPORTED AS AGREEMENT. ADR 0023 falsifiable test 2 - that the haemorrhage settlement unwinds - therefore survives as a claim about DIRECTION AND TIMESCALE SEPARATION, never about magnitude. || AND IT IS NOT A PURE ERYTHROPOIETIC RATE. Donation removes IRON as well as cells, and iron supply rather than erythropoietic drive limits recovery in many donors: the same literature reports recovery beyond 300 days in young women against 36 in these men. This row therefore LUMPS erythropoietic drive with iron availability, the way K.EXCRETION_EXPONENT lumps aldosterone, distal flow and plasma potassium. THE MODEL CANNOT DISTINGUISH AN ERYTHROPOIETIC DEFECT FROM IRON DEFICIENCY, and that is why. || MEN ONLY, n = 29. Recovery is markedly slower in women and this row carries no pair, so it is a male number applied to both - the CV.HEMATOCRIT.NOMINAL failure of section 3.8, declared in advance this time instead of found later."),
    :RESP_ALVEOLAR_K => Provenance("RESP.ALVEOLAR.K", "mmHg", 862.9, "A", "derived", "Derived. See notes for the full derivation.", "PaCO2 = K * VCO2(STPD) / V_A(BTPS), with both flows in L/min and PaCO2 in mmHg. ARITHMETIC ON PHYSICAL CONSTANTS, NOT A MEASUREMENT, so it carries a derivation rather than a citation - the RN.NA.FRACTIONAL_REABSORPTION precedent. || THE DERIVATION. Dry barometric pressure is 760.0 - 47.0 = 713.0 mmHg. The STPD to BTPS conversion is (310.15/273.15) x (760/713) = 1.2103. K = 1.2103 x 713.0 = 862.9. || TWO ASSUMPTIONS AND THEY ARE THE MODEL'S LIMITS: sea-level barometric pressure, and water vapour saturated at 37 C. ADR 0017 already confines this model to sea level by omitting the hypoxic drive; this is the second place that binds. A model at altitude needs this row recomputed AND a hypoxic drive added."),
    :RESP_CHEMO_CO2_SLOPE => Provenance("RESP.CHEMO.CO2_SLOPE", "L/min/mmHg", 1.8, "B", "reported", "Mannee DC, Fabius TM, Wagenaar M, Eijsvogel MMM, de Jongh FHC. Reproducibility of hypercapnic ventilatory response measurements with steady-state and rebreathing methods. ERJ Open Res 2018;4(1):00141-2017. doi:10.1183/23120541.00141-2017. PMID 29492407. PMC5824331. OPEN ACCESS, full text and tables read. 20 healthy adults, steady-state and rebreathing on two separate days. Table 1 reports MEDIAN (IQR).", "THE SLOPE ABOVE THE VENTILATORY RECRUITMENT THRESHOLD, which is the quantity the paper reports - its own words are 'the sensitivity to carbon dioxide above the ventilatory recruitment threshold'. That matches the piecewise structure ADR 0017's amendment adopts. || CONVERSION. Table 1, steady-state, measurement 1: median 13.5 (IQR 8.3-17.3) L/min/kPa. Divided by 7.5006157584566 mmHg/kPa gives 1.800 (1.107-2.306) L/min/mmHg. || STEADY-STATE TAKEN, AND THE PAPER'S HEADLINE ARGUES AGAINST IT, so the reasoning is on the row. Mannee concludes rebreathing is reproducible and steady-state is not - ICC 0.90 against 0.49. The pre-registration preferred steady-state on VALIDITY grounds, because this model is quasi-static and a rebreathing transient is the wrong instrument. THE CONFLICT RESOLVES BECAUSE AN ICC AND A GROUP MEDIAN ARE DIFFERENT QUANTITIES: an ICC asks whether ONE PERSON'S measurement repeats, this ledger stores a population central value, and the steady-state medians on the two days are 13.5 and 13.4 - stable to 0.7 percent while the individual ICC is 0.49. No deviation from the pre-registration was needed. The two methods also agree on the central value to 5 percent (13.5 against 12.9). || THE IQR IS WIDE AND REAL. The ventilatory response to CO2 varies several-fold between healthy people. Not narrowed by selection, per the pre-registration. || EXTENSIVE - it is a ventilation per mmHg and ventilation scales with body size. || DIRECTIVE 1.7 DISQUALIFIED ALMOST THE ENTIRE LITERATURE HERE. A search for this slope returns remifentanil, alfentanil, midazolam, propofol, clonidine, diphenhydramine, buprenorphine and etomidate. In every one the CO2 response is the INSTRUMENT used to measure a drug's respiratory depression, not the subject."),
    :RESP_CHEMO_VRT => Provenance("RESP.CHEMO.VRT", "mmHg", 45.28, "B", "reported", "Mateika JH, Ellythy M. Chemoreflex control of ventilation is altered during wakefulness in humans with OSA. Respir Physiol Neurobiol 2003;138(1):45-57. doi:10.1016/s1569-9048(03)00174-5. PMID 14519377. ABSTRACT ONLY - not open access, no PMC record, Europe PMC isOpenAccess=N. n = 8 healthy MATCHED CONTROLS, awake, matched to an OSA group for gender, race, age, height and weight. The controls are the admissible arm; the OSA arm is excluded as disease by validation/respiratory_prereg.md section 2.", "THE CO2 TENSION AT WHICH VENTILATION BEGINS TO RISE. Below it ventilation is flat and NON-ZERO; above it the chemoreflex is recruited. Mateika's definition is 'the point at which ventilation and PETCO2 increased linearly'. || THE HYPEROXIC VALUE IS TAKEN AND THAT IS A DELIBERATE MATCH TO THE MODEL. Mateika reports 45.28 +/- 0.75 at PETO2 140 mmHg and 41.05 +/- 0.67 at PETO2 50 mmHg in the same controls. Hyperoxia suppresses the peripheral chemoreceptors and isolates the central reflex, and ADR 0017 OMITS the hypoxic drive - so the hyperoxic preparation is the one that matches what this model represents. A model that later adds a peripheral drive must revisit this row and take the normoxic value instead. || DISPERSION IS +/- 0.75 AS PRINTED and the paper's own convention is not stated in the abstract; it is recorded as `se` because the value is small relative to the mean for n = 8 and the paper reports group comparisons. THAT IS AN INFERENCE ABOUT A NOTATION, NOT A READING, and it is flagged rather than presented as certain. || THIS ROW IS WHAT FALSIFIED ADR 0017'S ORIGINAL DECISION 1. At 45.28 it sits ABOVE normal resting PaCO2 of about 40, so at rest the central chemoreflex is BELOW its own recruitment threshold and is not the operative control. A model deriving resting PaCO2 from the chemoreflex derives it from a mechanism not yet recruited. || ABSTRACT ONLY. The full text is paywalled, so the piecewise fit's own details could not be read. Institutional access would settle whether the sub-threshold limb is flat or merely shallow. || INTENSIVE - a partial pressure."),
    :RESP_CO2_ARTERIAL_RESTING => Provenance("RESP.CO2.ARTERIAL_RESTING", "mmHg", 40.0, "C", "assumed", "NOT SOURCED. The correct source was identified and could not be opened: Crapo RO, Jensen RL, Hegewald M, Tashkin DP. Arterial blood gas reference values for sea level and an altitude of 1,400 meters. Am J Respir Crit Care Med 1999;160(5 Pt 1):1525-31. doi:10.1164/ajrccm.160.5.9806006. PMID 10556115. Healthy LIFETIME NONSMOKERS, n = 96 at sea level, ages 18-81, 166 male and 173 female across both sites, duplicate analysis on two analysers, reference EQUATIONS fitted. Europe PMC reports isOpenAccess=N and no PMC record, and the abstract gives equations rather than values.", "ASSUMED AT THE CONVENTIONAL 40 mmHg, AND THIS IS THE ROW THE PRE-REGISTRATION SAID TO WATCH. Section 3 of validation/respiratory_prereg.md names 40 mmHg specifically as the number the model must not be tuned to produce. || IT IS NOT TUNED TO IT. Under ADR 0017's amendment resting PaCO2 is an INPUT and not a prediction, so the model makes NO CLAIM to reproduce it. That is a REDUCTION in what is claimed, made deliberately and stated here, and it is the reason entering a conventional value does not create the circularity section 7 of that document warns about. Nothing downstream is calibrated against it. || WHY THE INVERSION. HANDOVER section 3.6's lesson is to derive from the quantity that is actually MEASURED. Resting arterial PCO2 is among the best-characterised quantities in human physiology; resting basal ventilation is not, and the only sub-threshold ventilation found comes from a rebreathing protocol after voluntary hyperventilation - using it gives a resting PaCO2 of 22.8 mmHg. || TIER C AND assumed UNTIL CRAPO IS OPENED. That paper would supply a mean, a dispersion and an AGE TREND, none of which this row has. Obtaining it is the single highest-value respiratory follow-up. || INTENSIVE. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :RESP_CO2_PRODUCTION => Provenance("RESP.CO2.PRODUCTION", "L/min STPD", 0.1824, "A", "derived", "DERIVED from RESP.METABOLIC_RATE through RESP.O2.CONSUMPTION: VCO2 = R * VO2 = 0.80 * 0.2280 = 0.1824 L/min. Sources on those rows. || McMurray RG, Soares J, Caspersen CJ, McCurdy T. Examining variations of resting metabolic rate of adults: a public health perspective. Med Sci Sports Exerc 2014;46(7):1352-8. doi:10.1249/MSS.0000000000000232. PMID 24300125. PMC4535334. READ IN FULL. 197 studies, 397 publication estimates, INVERSE-VARIANCE WEIGHTED. Its stated purpose is to test the 1.0 kcal/kg/h metabolic-equivalent convention, which is directive 1.12's exact subject rather than an incidental finding, and its conclusion is that the convention overestimates resting metabolic rate by about 10 percent in men and almost 15 percent in women. || THE STRATUM USED IS NORMAL WEIGHT: men 0.960 (95% CI 0.934-0.985), women 0.926 (0.908-0.945), averaged unweighted to 0.943. || THE ALTERNATIVE, RECORDED SO THE CHOICE IS VISIBLE: the all-BMI means, men 0.892 (0.872-0.912) and women 0.839 (0.825-0.853), averaging 0.866. The two differ by 8 percent. Normal weight is chosen because this model's reference individual is a healthy 70 kg adult and the all-BMI means pool in overweight and obese subgroups whose metabolic rate per kilogram is lower for reasons - fat mass is metabolically less active - that a model with no body composition cannot represent. Fixed in validation/metabolic_rate_prereg.md section 3 BEFORE any consequence for the model was computed.", "ASSUMED, AND IT IS A ROUND TEACHING NUMBER, which directive 1.12 says to treat as presumptively unsourced. Entered assumed rather than reported exactly as validation/respiratory_prereg.md section 3 requires. || WHAT WAS SEARCHED AND WHY EVERY HIT WAS INADMISSIBLE, recorded so it is not repeated: resting metabolic rate / indirect calorimetry / reference values returns prepubertal children, chronic kidney disease patients, and hand-held calorimeter VALIDATION studies. The last are directive 1.7's instrument-not-subject trap - the paper exists to characterise a device, not resting metabolism. || WHAT IT AFFECTS. It sets the metabolic load the respiratory loop balances, and through RESP.VENTILATION.BASAL it sets resting ventilation and therefore respiratory water loss. It does NOT set resting PaCO2, which is an INPUT under the ADR 0017 amendment. || A SOURCED VALUE WOULD MOVE THE WATER SPLIT. V_basal is proportional to this row, so a 20 percent error here is a 20 percent error in respiratory water loss and an offsetting one in the cutaneous residual. || NO LONGER ASSUMED, 2026-09-05: 0.20 -> 0.1824 L/min, a fall of 8.8 percent. The old value was the round teaching number this row's own note already flagged, and the search it records as failing - 'resting metabolic rate / indirect calorimetry / reference values returns prepubertal children, chronic disease...' - missed a weighted meta-analysis of 197 studies whose subject IS that convention. || THE UNCERTAINTY RANGE IS THE STRATUM CHOICE, not a confidence interval: 0.1674 is what the all-BMI means give and 0.1824 the normal-weight means. That is far wider than either stratum's own CI and is the honest statement of what is uncertain here. || A SEXED PAIR IS SUPPORTED BY THE SOURCE AND DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN. This row drives basal ventilation, which drives the respiratory water flux, whose residual closes against BF.H2O.INSENSIBLE_LOSS - assumed, 0.8 L/day, and unsexed. Sexing one half against an unsexed total either invents a sexed total or puts the entire sex difference into the cutaneous residual, and neither is sourced. Recorded per metabolic_rate_prereg.md section 4 rather than taken. || PRECISION RAISED 2026-09-05: this is a DERIVED row closing an identity, so it carries every digit the arithmetic gives rather than the significant figures of the measurement behind it - the exemption pooling.md records, and the lesson CV.CO.NOMINAL wrote down. Rounding it to four figures put arterial PCO2 out by 1 part in 10,000, which the suite caught. The MEASUREMENT it descends from, RESP.METABOLIC_RATE, stays at the three figures its source reports. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :RESP_DEADSPACE_FRACTION => Provenance("RESP.DEADSPACE.FRACTION", "dimensionless", 0.3, "C", "assumed", "No admissible source could be opened. See notes.", "ASSUMED, AND A ROUND TEACHING NUMBER. Directive 1.12. || WHAT WAS SEARCHED: dead space / tidal volume / healthy / rest returns chronic heart failure, pneumoconiosis, dyspnoea evaluation and INCREMENTAL EXERCISE protocols. validation/respiratory_prereg.md section 2 excludes exercise and disease, fixed before the search ran. || INTENSIVE - a fraction does not scale with body size. || IT ENTERS ONLY THROUGH V_A = (1 - Vd/Vt) * V_E, so it and RESP.CO2.PRODUCTION are NOT separately identifiable from resting data: only the ratio VCO2/(1 - Vd/Vt) is. Recorded because a future sourcing pass on either must know that."),
    :RESP_EXCHANGE_RATIO => Provenance("RESP.EXCHANGE_RATIO", "dimensionless", 0.8, "C", "assumed", "No admissible source opened. See notes.", "ASSUMED, AND A ROUND TEACHING NUMBER - directive 1.12. || WHAT IT DOES: it is the only coupling between the CO2 side and the O2 side, entering the alveolar gas equation as PaCO2/R. At 0.80 that term is 50.0 mmHg. || IT SHOULD BE DERIVED, NOT ASSUMED, AND CANNOT BE YET. The exchange ratio is VCO2/VO2, and this model carries RESP.CO2.PRODUCTION but no oxygen consumption row, because ADR 0018 does not build the Fick relation. When a metabolic VO2 lands, this row stops being a primitive and becomes derived - the same transition CV.CO.NOMINAL and RN.H2O.OBLIGATORY_LOSS made on 2026-09-01. That is the right fix and it is named here so it is not repeated as a search. || SENSITIVITY: moving it to 0.85 raises PAO2 by 2.9 mmHg and arterial saturation by 0.2 percentage points. Minor, and inside the range the A-a sweep already covers. || INTENSIVE."),
    :RESP_H2O_GAS_CONTENT => Provenance("RESP.H2O.GAS_CONTENT", "mg/L", 35.15, "B", "derived", "Derived. See notes.", "EXPIRED GAS LEAVES SATURATED AT BODY TEMPERATURE; INSPIRED GAS CARRIES WHATEVER THE ROOM HOLDS. The difference is a water flux and it is pure mass balance. || THE DERIVATION, by the ideal gas law at each condition: expired at 47.0 mmHg and 310.15 K gives 43.78 mg/L; inspired at 8.75 mmHg and 293.15 K gives 8.62 mg/L; net 35.15 mg/L. || THE AMBIENT CONDITIONS ARE ASSUMED AND BELONG ON THIS ROW: 20 C and 50 percent relative humidity, giving an inspired water vapour pressure of 8.75 mmHg. A model breathing desert air loses more and one breathing tropical air loses less. This is the only place the environment enters the model at all. || INTENSIVE - it is per litre of gas."),
    :RESP_METABOLIC_RATE => Provenance("RESP.METABOLIC_RATE", "kcal/kg/h", 0.943, "A", "reported", "McMurray RG, Soares J, Caspersen CJ, McCurdy T. Examining variations of resting metabolic rate of adults: a public health perspective. Med Sci Sports Exerc 2014;46(7):1352-8. doi:10.1249/MSS.0000000000000232. PMID 24300125. PMC4535334. READ IN FULL. 197 studies, 397 publication estimates, INVERSE-VARIANCE WEIGHTED. Its stated purpose is to test the 1.0 kcal/kg/h metabolic-equivalent convention, which is directive 1.12's exact subject rather than an incidental finding, and its conclusion is that the convention overestimates resting metabolic rate by about 10 percent in men and almost 15 percent in women. || THE STRATUM USED IS NORMAL WEIGHT: men 0.960 (95% CI 0.934-0.985), women 0.926 (0.908-0.945), averaged unweighted to 0.943. || THE ALTERNATIVE, RECORDED SO THE CHOICE IS VISIBLE: the all-BMI means, men 0.892 (0.872-0.912) and women 0.839 (0.825-0.853), averaging 0.866. The two differ by 8 percent. Normal weight is chosen because this model's reference individual is a healthy 70 kg adult and the all-BMI means pool in overweight and obese subgroups whose metabolic rate per kilogram is lower for reasons - fat mass is metabolically less active - that a model with no body composition cannot represent. Fixed in validation/metabolic_rate_prereg.md section 3 BEFORE any consequence for the model was computed.", "THE ROW THAT DISCHARGED TWO ASSUMED NUMBERS AT ONCE. Everything metabolic in this model now descends from a weighted meta-analysis of 197 studies instead of from 1.0 kcal/kg/h, and the meta-analysis exists specifically to say that 1.0 is wrong. || THE RANGE IS THE STRATUM CHOICE - normal-weight against all-BMI - and not a confidence interval; either stratum's own CI is about ten times narrower. Recording the wider, honest number is the point. || PER KILOGRAM, SO IT IS INTENSIVE, and the extensive quantities derived from it scale linearly in mass. THE SOURCE SAYS THAT IS WRONG: metabolic rate per kilogram falls with body mass, from 0.960 in normal-weight men to 0.791 in obese men. So this model overstates the metabolic rate of heavy individuals, which is the same sub-linear scaling defect HANDOVER records for glomerular filtration and cardiac output and needs the same fix - a height row and a sourced body-surface-area formula. || IT ALSO FALLS WITH AGE in both sexes, and this model has no age dimension."),
    :RESP_O2_CONSUMPTION => Provenance("RESP.O2.CONSUMPTION", "L/min STPD", 0.228, "A", "derived", "DERIVED from RESP.METABOLIC_RATE by Weir's equation. Weir JB de V. New methods for calculating metabolic rate with special reference to protein metabolism. J Physiol 1949;109(1-2):1-9. PMC1392602. The standard indirect-calorimetry conversion without urinary nitrogen: EE (kcal/min) = 3.941*VO2 + 1.106*VCO2 with both in L/min. With VCO2 = R*VO2 this inverts to VO2 = EE/(3.941 + 1.106*R). At 0.943 kcal/kg/h and 70 kg, EE = 1.1002 kcal/min and R = 0.80, so VO2 = 1.1002/4.8265 = 0.2280 L/min, 228 mL/min. || McMurray RG, Soares J, Caspersen CJ, McCurdy T. Examining variations of resting metabolic rate of adults: a public health perspective. Med Sci Sports Exerc 2014;46(7):1352-8. doi:10.1249/MSS.0000000000000232. PMID 24300125. PMC4535334. READ IN FULL. 197 studies, 397 publication estimates, INVERSE-VARIANCE WEIGHTED. Its stated purpose is to test the 1.0 kcal/kg/h metabolic-equivalent convention, which is directive 1.12's exact subject rather than an incidental finding, and its conclusion is that the convention overestimates resting metabolic rate by about 10 percent in men and almost 15 percent in women. || THE STRATUM USED IS NORMAL WEIGHT: men 0.960 (95% CI 0.934-0.985), women 0.926 (0.908-0.945), averaged unweighted to 0.943. || THE ALTERNATIVE, RECORDED SO THE CHOICE IS VISIBLE: the all-BMI means, men 0.892 (0.872-0.912) and women 0.839 (0.825-0.853), averaging 0.866. The two differ by 8 percent. Normal weight is chosen because this model's reference individual is a healthy 70 kg adult and the all-BMI means pool in overweight and obese subgroups whose metabolic rate per kilogram is lower for reasons - fat mass is metabolically less active - that a model with no body composition cannot represent. Fixed in validation/metabolic_rate_prereg.md section 3 BEFORE any consequence for the model was computed.", "THE ROW ADR 0018 SAID DID NOT EXIST. Its deferral of the Fick relation read 'they need tissue oxygen consumption, which is a metabolic row this model does not have'; §3.27 built the arm from CO2 production over the exchange ratio, and this row now sources the same quantity from the measurement directly. || THE EXCHANGE RATIO BARELY MATTERS HERE, which is why an assumed 0.80 does not block it: the Weir denominator runs 4.771 at R = 0.75 to 4.881 at R = 0.85, ±1.1 percent across the whole plausible resting range. The stratum choice is eight times larger. || 228 mL/min AGAINST THE TEXTBOOK 250, and the 250 is 3.5 mL/kg/min - the metabolic-equivalent convention this source exists to refute. || IT WIDENS A DISCREPANCY RATHER THAN CLOSING ONE, and metabolic_rate_prereg.md branch M3 required that be reported: with cardiac output at 5.95 L/min the oxygen extraction ratio falls from 20.1 percent to 18.3 percent against roughly 23 percent implied by a measured mixed venous saturation near 75. The Fick-consistent cardiac output would be 4.75 L/min. That points at CV.CO.NOMINAL - derived from a CARDIAC-MRI stroke volume, where mixed venous saturation is measured against thermodilution and Fick outputs - and it is NOT closed here by choosing a different stratum. || PRECISION RAISED 2026-09-05: this is a DERIVED row closing an identity, so it carries every digit the arithmetic gives rather than the significant figures of the measurement behind it - the exemption pooling.md records, and the lesson CV.CO.NOMINAL wrote down. Rounding it to four figures put arterial PCO2 out by 1 part in 10,000, which the suite caught. The MEASUREMENT it descends from, RESP.METABOLIC_RATE, stays at the three figures its source reports. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :RESP_O2_INSPIRED_FRACTION => Provenance("RESP.O2.INSPIRED_FRACTION", "dimensionless", 0.2095, "B", "assumed", "Standard dry-air composition. Not opened from a primary source.", "THE OXYGEN FRACTION OF DRY ATMOSPHERIC AIR. Entered `assumed` rather than `reported` because no primary source was opened for it, which is directive 1.5's rule applied even to a number nobody disputes. || IT IS GENUINELY CONSTANT WHERE THIS MODEL LIVES - atmospheric composition does not vary with altitude, only the pressure does - so unlike the other assumed rows here this one is unlikely to be wrong. It is the BAROMETRIC PRESSURE it multiplies that binds the model to sea level, and that lives in RESP.ALVEOLAR.K's derivation. || INTENSIVE."),
    :RESP_VENTILATION_BASAL => Provenance("RESP.VENTILATION.BASAL", "L/min", 5.621, "C", "derived", "Derived. See notes.", "DERIVED, NOT SOURCED, AND THE DIRECTION OF THE DERIVATION IS THE POINT. V_basal = K * VCO2 / ((1 - Vd/Vt) * PaCO2_rest) = 862.9 x 0.20 / (0.70 x 40.0) = 6.1636 L/min. || IT INHERITS THE TIER OF ITS WEAKEST INPUT, which is C: two of the three inputs are assumed. || THE VALUE IS A CHECK ON THE CHAIN RATHER THAN AN INPUT TO IT. 6.16 L/min is squarely normal resting minute ventilation, reached from an alveolar mass balance and a conventional PaCO2 without being aimed at - so the assumed production and dead space are at least jointly consistent with a human. That is weak evidence and it is the only evidence this row has. || WHAT IT IS NOT: a measurement of resting ventilation. The literature value found for sub-threshold ventilation is 10.83 L/min (Mateika, n = 8 controls) and is NOT usable here, because it was measured during rebreathing after voluntary hyperventilation. Substituting it gives a resting PaCO2 of 22.8 mmHg. That is how badly a protocol-contaminated number transfers, and it is why validation/respiratory_prereg.md section 8.4 forbids taking it. || EXTENSIVE. || RE-DERIVED 2026-09-05, 6.1636 -> 5.6206 L/min, because RESP.CO2.PRODUCTION stopped being an assumed round number. The derivation is unchanged - V_basal = K*VCO2/((1-f_dead)*PaCO2), the ADR 0017 dependency inversion - and arterial PCO2 is untouched at its sourced 40 mmHg, which metabolic_rate_prereg.md branch M4 required. || 5.62 L/min sits inside the 4-8 L/min the pre-registration fixed as branch M1 before the number was computed. It is at the lower end, and the reason is worth naming: this ventilation is what the SOURCED metabolic rate and the ASSUMED dead-space fraction imply together, and the dead space is still 0.30, a round teaching number sitting directly upstream. Branch M2 forbade moving it to make ventilation come out. || PRECISION RAISED 2026-09-05: this is a DERIVED row closing an identity, so it carries every digit the arithmetic gives rather than the significant figures of the measurement behind it - the exemption pooling.md records, and the lesson CV.CO.NOMINAL wrote down. Rounding it to four figures put arterial PCO2 out by 1 part in 10,000, which the suite caught. The MEASUREMENT it descends from, RESP.METABOLIC_RATE, stays at the three figures its source reports. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :RN_AUTOREG_LOWER => Provenance("RN.AUTOREG.LOWER", "mmHg", 63.9, "A", "reported", "Finke R, Gross R, Hackenthal E, Huber J, Kirchheim HR. Threshold pressure for the pressure-dependent renin release in the autoregulating kidney of conscious dogs. Pflugers Arch 1983;399(2):102-110. PMID 6139786. Lower limit of renal blood flow autoregulation, measured directly by servo-controlled stepwise reduction of renal artery pressure in the conscious animal.", "VALUE CHANGED 80.0 -> 63.9 mmHg ON 2026-08-27, AND SPECIES CORRECTED human -> dog. The previous value was NOT SOURCED: citation read 'Standard physiological reference. VERIFY.', which is not a reference, and species read 'human', which was not supported. This row was the last of the seven rows carrying that identical non-citation to be flagged as genuine debt rather than discharged, and HANDOVER 3.1 named it the one to look at first because CV.MAP.SETPOINT moved 93 -> 87 on the same day, carrying the operating point TOWARD this breakpoint. PRE-REGISTERED EXTRACTION: validation/autoreg_lower_prereg.md, committed at 3fbe260 BEFORE any paper was opened; executed by validation/autoreg_lower_extract.py. 26 PubMed queries in two sweeps, 1114 unique records screened. POOLING RULE, DECLARED BEFORE EXTRACTION per validation/pooling.md: single-source, n_studies k=1. No cross-species pooling was performed and no range midpoint was taken; both are prohibited for new entries. BRANCH 7 OF THE PRE-REGISTERED PROCEDURE WAS TAKEN. Branches 1-5 fail: NO human primary study reports a numeric lower breakpoint. The one that appears to - Textor SC et al, Ann Intern Med 1985;102(3):308-314, 'Critical perfusion pressure for renal function' - measures a STENOSIS threshold in bilateral atherosclerotic renovascular disease, not the intrinsic breakpoint of a normal kidney, and the sensitivity disappeared after revascularisation. Branch 6 fails too, and that is the finding of this pass: THE HUMAN EVIDENCE CONFLICTS AT THE SAME PRESSURE. At MAP 60 mmHg, Lessard MR and Trepanier CA, Anesthesiology 1991;74(5):860-865 (n=20, GFR by inulin, ERPF by PAH, MAP 59.8 +/- 0.4 for 237 min) found GFR not significantly further reduced and renal vascular resistance FALLING to maintain renal blood flow - the autoregulatory signature; while Hara T et al, J Clin Anesth 1998;10(7):539-545 (n=26, MAP 60 for 120 min) found creatinine clearance SIGNIFICANTLY DECREASED in both arms. Lessard is the methodologically stronger study, but choosing it after seeing both is precisely what pre-registration forbids, so branch 6's condition - that human studies ESTABLISH preservation - genuinely fails on its own terms. There is no lower pressure where the human evidence is unconflicted either: at MAP 50, Zayas VM et al, Am J Physiol 1993;265(5 Pt 2):F686-F692 and Toivonen J et al, Eur J Anaesthesiol 1991;8(1):13-20 both report renal function falling. ALL FOUR HUMAN STUDIES ARE ANAESTHESIA SAFETY STUDIES and directive 1.7 predicts what they returned: apply the test - had the physiology come out differently, the conclusion would have been 'this hypotensive technique is unsafe for the kidney'. The pressure-flow relationship is the INSTRUMENT there, not the subject, and every one of them carries a confound (volatile anaesthetic, sympathetic blockade, vasodilator, or all three), with anaesthesia depressing GFR before any pressure was lowered. THE ADOPTED SOURCE IS THE OPPOSITE KIND OF STUDY. Finke 1983 varied renal artery pressure from 160 to 40 mmHg in seven CONSCIOUS foxhounds under beta-blockade on a normal sodium diet (4.1 mmol/kg/day), reducing it in steps held constant by a servo-control system driving an inflatable renal artery cuff. Between 160 mmHg and resting pressure renal blood flow did not change; between resting pressure and the lower limit of autoregulation, average 63.9 mmHg, it rose only about 7%. The relationship IS the subject. No standard deviation is reported for the limit itself, so uncertainty_type stays none rather than inventing one - the paper does report 89.8 +/- 3.3 mmHg for its separate renin threshold, so the omission is the authors' and not a transcription loss. DECLARED CONFLICTS, LOGGED AND NOT RESOLVED. (1) Shipley RE, Study RS, Am J Physiol 1951;167(3):676-688, PMID 14903093 - the ANAESTHETISED dog study that is the origin of the textbook 80-180 and therefore of the value this row carried; its PubMed record carries the MeSH term 'Humans', a legacy-indexing artefact of the AJP back catalogue already flagged in autoreg_upper_prereg.md. Anaesthesia is the most likely single explanation of 80 vs 63.9. (2) Cupples WA, Braam B, Am J Physiol Renal Physiol 2007;292(4):F1105-F1123, PMID 17229679 - a tier B review putting the limit at ~75 mmHg in dog and ~85 in rat. pooling.md: the primary source wins and the divergence is logged. Averaging 75 and 85 would produce a number describing neither species. (3) The 2025 human review already cited in this row's previous note (doi 10.22514/sv.2025.001) holds that human evidence is insufficient for 80 as the lower limit; this extraction agrees and goes further - the human evidence that does exist puts the limit near 60. THE BRANCH CHOICE IS NOT LOAD-BEARING: branch 6 would have given 59.8 and branch 7 gives 63.9, a 4.1 mmHg difference, and no conclusion depends on which. Two independent lines - conscious dog with a measured breakpoint, anaesthetised humans bracketing 50 to 60 - different species, designs, decades and authors, both land near 60. NEITHER SUPPORTS 80. NO MODEL RESULT CHANGES. The three salt arms sit at MAP 81.900 / 84.450 / 87.001 mmHg, above the old 80 and above the new 63.9, so all remain on the autoregulation plateau and every steady state is bit-identical. The pre-registration declared this BEFORE extraction so that 'it does not change the answer' could not wave the change through, and so that a value above ~81.9 would have had to be escalated rather than quietly avoided. WHAT CHANGES IS MARGIN: the low-salt arm sat 1.9 mmHg above a piecewise kink and now sits 18.0 mmHg above it. STILL DEBT, AND THE REASON IS FIXED IN THE PRE-REGISTRATION. This is a dog number and the human experiment IS performable - humans have repeatedly been taken to 50-60 mmHg. The ethical-ceiling argument that earns RN.AUTOREG.UPPER its E2 standing under the ADR 0006 amendment does NOT transfer to this row, and was ruled out in the pre-registration before the search so it could not be reached for afterwards. What is missing is a study that lowers pressure in humans WITHOUT general anaesthesia and measures GFR by inulin at graded steps. Debt with a named primary source is a real improvement on a non-citation; it is not a closure. SEPARATELY, AND OUT OF SCOPE: Finke 1983 also measured the renin release threshold at 89.8 +/- 3.3 mmHg in conscious dog, against RAAS.RENIN.PRESSURE_THRESHOLD = 93.0 currently carried on van Ochten. NOT CHANGED HERE. Recorded because that row is the rectification point HANDOVER 3.1 found the model sitting exactly on, and a second independent primary source for it now exists in a paper this repo has read. THE PIECEWISE FORM REMAINS UNSOURCED: relations.csv row Renal.GFR keeps an empty form_citation and stays in GRANDFATHERED_UNSOURCED. A NUMBER was sourced, not an EQUATION. Finke in fact reports renal blood flow rising ~7% below resting pressure rather than staying flat, so the true plateau has a slight slope the model's ifelse does not represent."),
    :RN_AUTOREG_UPPER => Provenance("RN.AUTOREG.UPPER", "mmHg", 160.0, "A", "reported", "Roman RJ, Cowley AW Jr. Characterization of a new model for the study of pressure-natriuresis in the rat. Am J Physiol 1985;248:F190-F198. PMID 3970209. Highest renal perfusion pressure at which GFR was measured and found unchanged.", "EVIDENCE UNDER AN ETHICAL CEILING, NOT DEBT (ADR 0006 amended 2026-08-21). The rat provenance of this value is not a deficiency to be burned down. No human study raises arterial pressure to locate the failure of GFR autoregulation and none may; the human literature only ever lowers it. Where the human experiment cannot ethically exist, a well-conducted animal study is the evidence, not a substitute for it. This row is E2 with species, preparation and tested range recorded. WHAT REMAINS A REAL LIMITATION IS THE CENSORING, NOT THE SPECIES: 160 mmHg was the highest RPP tested in that preparation, so the true breakpoint is >= 160 and is not known to BE 160. UPPER BREAKPOINT MOVED 180 -> 160 mmHg ON 2026-08-21. The previous value was NOT SOURCED: citation read 'Standard physiological reference. VERIFY.' and species read 'human', neither of which was true. POOLING RULE, DECLARED BEFORE EXTRACTION per validation/pooling.md, pre-registration in validation/autoreg_upper_prereg.md: single-source, n_studies k=1. The pre-registration fixed, before any paper was read, that if zero HUMAN primary studies reported an upper breakpoint then the value could not be retained by cross-species pooling or by range-midpoint (both prohibited) and had to be brought inside the range where the model's own relations have support. SEARCH RESULT - THERE IS NO HUMAN PRIMARY EVIDENCE FOR AN UPPER BREAKPOINT. PubMed yields no human study that RAISES arterial pressure and measures the pressure at which GFR autoregulation fails; for obvious reasons the human literature only ever probes the LOWER limb by LOWERING pressure - Parving HH et al, Diabetologia 1984;27:547-552 (clonidine, MAP fall 16-18 mmHg, PMID 6442240), New JP et al, Diabetologia 1998;41:206-211 (trandolapril, MAP 103->93, PMID 9498655), Christensen PK et al, Kidney Int 2001 (PMID 11576357) and Diabetes Care 2003 (PMID 12502673). None of these reaches 160 mmHg, let alone 180. WHERE 180 ACTUALLY CAME FROM: Shipley RE, Study RS, Am J Physiol 1951;167:676-688, doi 10.1152/ajplegacy.1951.167.3.676, PMID 14903093 - DOG, acute alterations of renal artery pressure. That is the primary origin of the textbook '80-180 mmHg' and it is canine. (The PubMed MeSH record for PMID 14903093 carries the term 'Humans'; that is a known legacy-indexing artefact of the AJP back-catalogue, contradicted by the paper and by every secondary source that cites it as the canine range. Recorded here because the MeSH tag would otherwise look like human support.) 180 therefore entered this ledger as a dog number wearing a human label. WHAT IS ADOPTED: Roman RJ, Cowley AW Jr, Am J Physiol 1985;248:F190-F198, doi 10.1152/ajprenal.1985.248.2.F190, PMID 3970209. RAT, denervated kidney, vasopressin, aldosterone, corticosterone and noradrenaline clamped by infusion, RPP set by aortic clamp. Verbatim: 'Increasing RPP within the autoregulatory range from 90 to 160 mmHg produced 5- to 20-fold increases in urine flow and sodium excretion with no detectable changes in glomerular filtration rate, renal blood flow, or peritubular capillary pressure.' That is a direct primary measurement that GFR autoregulation is INTACT at 160 mmHg. THIS IS A CENSORED OBSERVATION, NOT A MEASURED BREAKPOINT. 160 mmHg is the highest RPP Roman and Cowley tested, so the true upper limit is >= 160; it is not known to BE 160. uncertainty_type is 'none' rather than a range because inventing a 160-180 interval would smuggle back the range-midpoint reasoning pooling.md prohibits. The model breakpoint is set at the edge of evidence, which is the conservative direction: it makes GFR fall with pressure slightly earlier than reality, rather than asserting a flat plateau in a region nothing has measured. WHY 160 AND NOT SOMETHING ELSE - THE TWO CONSTRAINTS COINCIDE. Roman and Cowley 1985 is ALSO the form source for the model's pressure natriuresis (see RN.PRESSURE_NATRIURESIS.SLOPE and ledger/relations.csv row Renal.FR_effective), where the LINEAR form is evidenced over roughly 55-160 mmHg. At 180 the autoregulation breakpoint sat OUTSIDE the range over which the equation it modifies has any support, so the model contained a kink in a region where its own natriuresis term was unevidenced. Setting the breakpoint to 160 makes the autoregulatory plateau and the natriuresis validity range terminate together, at the same pressure, from the SAME primary paper. That is not a coincidence chosen after the fact - the <=160 ceiling was written into the pre-registration before extraction. CROSS-SPECIES POOLING NOT PERFORMED, as prohibited. The dog value (Shipley 1951) and the rat value (Roman 1985) are NOT averaged. One rat study is adopted; the dog study is recorded as provenance archaeology for the discarded 180, not as a co-estimate. CORROBORATION, RECORDED AS CONTEXT AND NOT POOLED: Cupples WA, Braam B, Am J Physiol Renal Physiol 2007;292:F1105-F1123, doi 10.1152/ajprenal.00194.2006, describe the plateau as spanning from ~75 mmHg (dogs) or ~85 mmHg (rats) to >160 mmHg - note '>160', an open upper bound, not a fixed 180. Carlstrom M, Wilcox CS, Arendshorst WJ, Physiol Rev 2015;95:405-511, doi 10.1152/physrev.00042.2012, quote the conventional 80-180 mmHg. Both are reviews (tier B) and neither adds human primary data on the upper limit. SPECIES CHANGED human -> rat. TIER CHANGED B -> A: per SOURCES.md, tier A includes 'peer-reviewed primary research in animal models, flagged as such with species noted'. The tier RISES because the source is now a primary measurement rather than an unnamed reference, while the species flag FALLS to rat. Those are two separate axes and both moved honestly. KNOWN LIMITATION: the model is human. A rat autoregulatory breakpoint is being used in a human model because no human number exists. This is species extrapolation and is flagged as such. NO NUMERICAL EFFECT AT THE OPERATING POINT: IPE runs at MAP ~88-93 mmHg, inside [80, 160], where GFR ~ GFR0 either way. Verified: the ADR 0007 salt-step result is unchanged. The change buys honesty and removes an unevidenced kink, not different behaviour. WHAT IS STILL NOT SOURCED: the piecewise-flat-then-proportional FORM of the autoregulation equation, including its derivative discontinuity at both breakpoints. Only the breakpoint VALUE is sourced here. ledger/relations.csv row Renal.GFR therefore remains in the grandfathered unsourced list in tools/check_relations.py and its form_citation stays empty. LEDGER SCHEMA DEBT: validation/pooling.md specifies pooling_rule, n_studies and pooling_notes as columns of this CSV. Those columns do not exist yet, so the rule is recorded in prose here, following the precedent already set by RN.PRESSURE_NATRIURESIS.SLOPE. SCALING FROM RAT TO HUMAN: NO SCALING FACTOR IS APPLIED, AND THAT IS A DELIBERATE CLAIM, NOT AN OMISSION. This parameter is a PRESSURE (mmHg), an intensive variable, unlike the extensive quantities in this ledger (GFR, sodium excretion, blood volume) which would require allometric scaling by body or kidney mass. Mean arterial pressure in the conscious rat (~100-120 mmHg) and in the adult human (~93 mmHg here) are the same order of magnitude, and the autoregulatory plateau is generated by afferent-arteriolar myogenic tone and tubuloglomerular feedback, which are transmural-pressure-sensing mechanisms operating on the same pressure scale in both species rather than mass-dependent ones. Cupples and Braam 2007 report lower limits of ~75 mmHg (dog) and ~85 mmHg (rat) - a ~10 mmHg spread across a roughly 40-fold body-mass difference, which is the empirical evidence that this quantity does not scale with mass. The residual risk is therefore not a missing scale factor but genuine between-species difference in the breakpoint itself, of order 10 mmHg based on that spread, and it is unquantified in the upward direction because no species has been measured above 160 mmHg in this preparation. RE-FRAMED 2026-08-21 under the ADR 0006 amendment: an earlier version of this note treated 'not human' as a defect. It is not. What is genuinely unsourced here is the piecewise FORM of the autoregulation equation (see ledger/relations.csv row Renal.GFR) and the LOWER breakpoint - not this value's species. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :RN_GFR_NOMINAL => Provenance("RN.GFR.NOMINAL", "L/day", 152.6, "A", "reported", "Soares AA, Prates AB, Weinert LS, Veronese FV, de Azevedo MJ, Silveiro SP. Reference values for glomerular filtration rate in healthy Brazilian adults. BMC Nephrol 2013;14:54. doi:10.1186/1471-2369-14-54. PMID 23448550. GFR measured by 51Cr-EDTA single injection in 285 healthy adults aged 19-70, 106 +/- 18 mL/min/1.73 m2, reference intervals built to CLSI C28.", "CHANGED 180.0 -> 152.6 L/day ON 2026-08-27. THE PREVIOUS VALUE WAS NOT SOURCED: the citation read 'Standard physiological reference. VERIFY.', which is not a reference, while extraction_method claimed `reported`. 180 L/day is 125 mL/min - the textbook figure - and MEASURED GFR in healthy adults is not 125. Pre-registered in validation/verify_rows_prereg.md, committed at e0195f4 before any source was opened. pooling_rule = single-source, k = 1. THE VALUE: 106 mL/min/1.73 m2 x 1440 / 1000 = 152.64 -> 152.6 L/day, 4 significant figures per directive 1.9. SD 18 mL/min/1.73 m2 -> 25.9 L/day. Measured by 51Cr-EDTA single injection, n = 285 healthy adults 19-70, reference intervals constructed to the CLSI C28 protocol. The relationship IS the subject of that paper - directive 1.7. SEX: NO DIFFERENCE, AND THAT IS A FINDING RATHER THAN AN OMISSION. Soares reports 108 +/- 18 male against 104 +/- 18 female, p = 0.134, and pooled the sexes for that reason. Under ADR 0014 this row therefore stays `both` with the cohort recorded, which is the rule's 'where only one value is supported, use it for both' branch and not a gap. CONVERGENT AND DELIBERATELY NOT POOLED: Denic A, Mathew J, Lerman LO, Lieske JC, Larson JJ, Alexander MP, Poggio E, Glassock RJ, Rule AD. Single-nephron glomerular filtration rate in healthy adults. N Engl J Med 2017;376(24):2349-2357. PMID 28614683. 1,388 healthy living kidney donors, iothalamate, mean GFR 115 +/- 24 mL/min ABSOLUTE and UNINDEXED. At a typical donor body surface area near 1.9 m2 that is about 105 mL/min/1.73 m2, agreeing closely with Soares - but the paper does not report the BSA needed to make that conversion, so it CANNOT be placed on this model's 1.73 m2 reference without an assumption. pooling.md prohibits pooling across incompatible measurement bases, and indexed against absolute is exactly that, so it is recorded as corroboration and NOT averaged in. Note also that Denic's cohort is screened kidney donors, i.e. selected for renal health above the population. WHY THE TEXTBOOK 125 IS HIGHER. The conventional figure predates routine clearance measurement in unselected healthy adults and is closer to a young-adult value; Soares finds 112 +/- 18 under 45 against 98 +/- 15 over 45, so an all-ages healthy mean sits well below 125. This is the same pattern as CV.MAP.SETPOINT and RN.AUTOREG.LOWER: a textbook convention entered as though it were a measurement. IT IS INDEXED TO 1.73 m2 AND THE MODEL SCALES BY MASS. src/scaling.jl already records that GFR properly scales with body surface area, sub-linearly in mass, and that linear scaling overstates the population spread. This row is the reference value at 1.73 m2; the mismatch between that normalisation and mass-linear scaling is the debt scaling.jl names, not a new one. RN.NA.FRACTIONAL_REABSORPTION MOVED WITH THIS ROW and had to: it is DERIVED as 1 - Na_intake/(GFR0*C_Na), so the two are not independent. Enforced by tools/check_closure.py, 'Na excretion == Na intake'."),
    :RN_GFR_VOLUME_RANGE => Provenance("RN.GFR.VOLUME_RANGE", "dimensionless", 0.029, "B", "derived", "van den Bosch JJJON, Hessels NR, Visser FW, Krikken JA, Bakker SJL, Riphagen IJ, Navis GJ. Plasma sodium, extracellular fluid volume, and blood pressure in healthy men. Physiol Rep 2021;9(24):e15103. doi:10.14814/phy2.15103. PMID 34921521. PMC8683787. n = 70 healthy normotensive men, crossover, 7 days per level, intake verified by 24 h urinary sodium (230 +/- 67 against 38 +/- 26 mmol/24 h). Table 1: ECFV 17.4 +/- 1.66 against 16.5 +/- 1.54 L/1.73 m2 by 125I-iothalamate, BSA 2.04 +/- 0.15 against 2.03 +/- 0.15. THE HALF-SPAN OF THE TESTED EXTRACELLULAR VOLUME RANGE, derived from those four numbers; the paper does not report it as a range. The derivation is validation/renal_hemodynamics_extract.py.", "THE CENSORING BOUND ON RN.GFR.VOLUME_SENSITIVITY, and it exists because a linear response has no support outside the volumes anyone measured. validation/renal_hemodynamics_prereg.md section 6 fixed this in advance: 'the functional form in this model is sourced only as a straight line between two measured intake levels, which is E2 inside the tested range and NOTHING AT ALL outside it, so the term is clamped to the pooled tested intake range, and that censoring is recorded on the row exactly as RN.AUTOREG.UPPER records its own.' This is that row. || THE DERIVATION. De-index each arm with its OWN body surface area, which is the correction HANDOVER section 3.12 records owing to ecf_salt_response_extract.py: high 17.4*2.04/1.73 = 20.518 L, low 16.5*2.03/1.73 = 19.361 L, mean 19.940, difference 1.157. The HALF-span about the mean is 0.5783/19.940 = 0.02900 -> 0.029. Two significant figures, per directive 1.9 and for the same reason RN.GFR.VOLUME_SENSITIVITY keeps two: the information is in a difference of two three-figure means and there is no third figure to keep. || IT IS A HALF-SPAN AND NOT A SPAN. The clamp is two-sided about the reference volume, so the bound is the distance from the mean to either arm, not the distance between the arms. Entering the span would double the evidenced range silently. || WHAT IT CENSORS, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSERTED. The model's chronic salt step of 103-205 mEq/day moves ECF volume about 2.4 percent either side of reference, so this bound does NOT bind on the chronic protocol - it is inert there, which is the correct outcome for a range that brackets the tested one. It DOES bind on the acute challenges: 2 L of saline expands ECF by roughly 9 percent, three times the evidenced half-span, and without the clamp the model would extrapolate a chronic 7-day relationship into a region no source reaches. || THE TIMESCALE EXTRAPOLATION IS SEPARATE AND IS DECLARED, NOT BOUNDED BY THIS ROW. The source is chronic, 7 days per level, and the model applies the response algebraically and therefore instantly. Conlin 1993 (PMID 7503952) shows the renal response to volume expansion per se within 3-7 hours, by saline or dextran alike, so an instantaneous term is fast rather than backwards - but nothing here sources a time constant, and a lag was deliberately NOT added because its tau would be identified by nothing, which is the defect RN.ANP.TAU already carries. || SPECIES human and the cohort is MEN, inheriting the sex conflict recorded in full on RN.GFR.VOLUME_SENSITIVITY: Pechere-Bertschi 2002 finds no change in renal haemodynamics on high salt in the follicular phase in 35 normotensive women. If that row becomes a sexed pair this bound must be re-derived with it."),
    :RN_GFR_VOLUME_SENSITIVITY => Provenance("RN.GFR.VOLUME_SENSITIVITY", "dimensionless", 1.3, "B", "derived", "van den Bosch JJJON, Hessels NR, Visser FW, Krikken JA, Bakker SJL, Riphagen IJ, Navis GJ. Plasma sodium, extracellular fluid volume, and blood pressure in healthy men. Physiol Rep 2021;9(24):e15103. doi:10.14814/phy2.15103. PMID 34921521. PMC8683787. n = 70 healthy normotensive men, age 24 +/- 7, crossover, 7 days per level, intake verified by 24 h urinary sodium (230 +/- 67 against 38 +/- 26 mmol/24 h). GFR and extracellular fluid volume BOTH by 125I-iothalamate. Table 1: GFR 138 +/- 18 against 128 +/- 18 ml/min (p < 0.001); ECFV 17.4 +/- 1.66 against 16.5 +/- 1.54 L/1.73 m2 (p < 0.001); BSA 2.04 +/- 0.15 against 2.03 +/- 0.15 (p < 0.001). DERIVED FROM THOSE SIX NUMBERS - the ratio is not reported by the paper; the derivation is validation/renal_hemodynamics_extract.py.", "THE FRACTIONAL GFR CHANGE PER FRACTIONAL EXTRACELLULAR VOLUME CHANGE, across chronic sodium intake in healthy humans. Pre-registered in validation/renal_hemodynamics_prereg.md, written BEFORE this extraction and sitting before it in history; verify with git log --diff-filter=A on that file. Reproduce with python validation/renal_hemodynamics_extract.py. || THE DERIVATION. ECFV is reported INDEXED to 1.73 m2 while GFR is reported ABSOLUTE, so ECFV is de-indexed using EACH ARM'S OWN BSA: (17.4*2.04 - 16.5*2.03)/1.73 = 1.157 L absolute, on a mean of 19.94 L, giving 0.05801 fractional. GFR gives 10/133 = 0.07519 fractional. S = 0.07519/0.05801 = 1.296 -> 1.30. Indexing the GFR instead of de-indexing the volume gives 1.324, 2 percent away, so the value does not turn on which side is converted. || TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES AND NOT THREE, per directive 1.9. The information is in the DIFFERENCES and dECFV is 0.9 L off two three-figure means; there is no third figure to keep. This is the opposite case to RN.NA.FRACTIONAL_REABSORPTION, where the difference of large numbers needed MORE digits on the inputs - here the inputs do not have them. || NO UNCERTAINTY IS ENTERED AND THAT IS DELIBERATE, not an omission. Numerator and denominator are measured in the SAME SUBJECTS BY THE SAME TRACER, so they are correlated, and the paper reports per-arm SDs rather than paired differences or their covariance. The pre-registration section 11 fixed in advance that an interval must not be fabricated by propagating the per-arm SDs as if independent. || pooling_rule = single-source, n_studies = 1 (columns pooling.md requires but this file does not have; recorded in prose by precedent, HANDOVER section 7). K IS 1 AND NOT 3, AND THAT IS A FINDING. Krikken 2007 (PMID 17091123, n = 95), Visser 2009 (PMID 19282825, n = 78) and this paper are THE SAME GRONINGEN COHORT - van den Bosch states in its Methods that it is a post-hoc analysis of the prior study, n = 70/93. Toering 2018 (PMID 28592435) is the same group. Pooling them would be the silent re-pooling validation/pooling.md prohibits and would have looked like k = 3. || CORROBORATED IN DIRECTION BY AN INDEPENDENT GROUP AND A DIFFERENT TRACER, AND NOT POOLED. Roos JC, Koomans HA, Dorhout Mees EJ, Delawi IM. Renal sodium handling in normal humans subjected to low, normal, and extremely high sodium supplies. Am J Physiol 1985;249(6 Pt 2):F941-7. PMID 3907374. Utrecht, n = 8, INULIN clearance rose 103 +/- 9 to 129 +/- 9 ml/min. NOT POOLED because its reported endpoints span 20 to 1128 meq/day, far outside this model's 103-205 and outside any diet; the 200 meq intermediate values are described but not printed and the full text is not open access. Direction only. || DECLARED CONFLICT, AND IT IS ABOUT SEX. The cohort is MEN. Under ADR 0014 the row is entered `both` because no sexed source with numbers was found, which is the rule's 'where only one value is supported, use it for both' branch - the same branch RN.GFR.NOMINAL took. BUT the pre-registered women sweep found that the only clean healthy-women study points the other way: Pechere-Bertschi A, Maillard M, Stalder H, Brunner HR, Burnier M. Renal segmental tubular response to salt during the normal menstrual cycle. Kidney Int 2002;61(2):425-31. PMID 11849382. 35 normotensive women, 40 against 250 mmol/day for 7 days: in the FOLLICULAR phase the rise in salt intake was associated with NO CHANGE in renal haemodynamics, with renal vasodilation in the LUTEAL phase instead. The companion study in women on oral contraceptives (PMID 12969156, n = 27) does find GFR and filtration fraction rising, p < 0.05, but that paper exists to show the oral-contraceptive renal salt response is altered. SO THIS ROW MAY BE A MALE NUMBER APPLIED TO WOMEN, exactly as CV.HEMATOCRIT.NOMINAL was before it was sourced as a pair. It is recorded, not hidden, and a sexed pair must NOT be entered on a direction alone. || WIRED 2026-09-03, HANDOVER section 4 item 1. It is read by Renal.jl as the relation Renal.gfr_vol_mod, clamped to RN.GFR.VOLUME_RANGE, and connected as rn.V_ecf ~ bf.V_ecf in assemble.jl. The blocker this row declared has been discharged: implementation was sequenced behind CV.VENOUS_RETURN.SENSITIVITY because the model's volume excursion is the input this term multiplies and it was 1.5-2.1x too large, and that row was sourced in healthy humans on 2026-09-03. Under ADR 0006 an E1 phenomenon defaults ON, which the pre-registration fixed in advance so that it could not be discovered conveniently later: this term is NOT a flag. || WHAT IT ACTUALLY DID, MEASURED WITH THE TERM WIRED RATHER THAN PROXIED. Chronic salt sensitivity 2.000 -> 1.849 mmHg per 100 mmol/day, a fall of 7.6 percent, against a human 1.70-2.30 - still inside the window and now nearer its floor. dMAP/dV_ecf is UNCHANGED, which is the fifth independent confirmation of the orthogonality result: this is a pressure-limb lever only. The two proxy routes predicted 8.1 and 15.2 percent; the measured 7.6 is below both, and the reason is that both were computed at G_pn = 20, G_vr = 2880 and no volume-keyed natriuretic path, so the term now competes with a sourced ADR 0010 path for the same sodium. SAME BAND AND SAME VERDICT AS BRANCH G3 - real but minor - which is the outcome that matters, and the pre-registration's numbers were never the thing being tested. || AND IT MOVED THE ONE OUT-OF-SAMPLE NUMBER THE WRONG WAY, WHICH IS RECORDED RATHER THAN BURIED. Jensen 2013's peak rise in FRACTIONAL sodium excretion goes 82.5 -> 79.3 percent against a reported 123, so the model is now slightly further low on the only endpoint it was not fitted to. The mechanism is arithmetic and it is worth understanding before anyone treats it as a defect: FENa = 1 - FR_effective, and both natriuretic terms in FR_effective are NORMALISED BY FILTERED LOAD, so raising GFR dilutes them in the fractional measure. ABSOLUTE excretion rises exactly as expected - Lobo 6 h urinary sodium 95.1 -> 97.1 mmol against a measured 95, and 6 h urine 565.6 -> 576.6 mL against 563. The model therefore excretes MORE sodium and reports a SMALLER fractional rise. Whether that is right depends on glomerulotubular balance, which this model does not represent, and nothing here sources it. DO NOT refit anything to Jensen - HANDOVER section 4 item 2 says spending the only out-of-sample datum on a fit is how this line loses its one test. || THE FUNCTIONAL FORM IS CENSORED, NOT EXTRAPOLATED. See RN.GFR.VOLUME_RANGE. The clamp is inert on the chronic salt step and binds on the acute challenges, so every acute number above is at the bound rather than on the line. || THE TIMESCALE IS AN EXTRAPOLATION AND IS DECLARED. The source is chronic, 7 days per level; the term is algebraic and therefore instantaneous. Conlin 1993 (PMID 7503952) puts the renal response to volume expansion per se at 3-7 hours, so instantaneous is fast rather than backwards. No lag was added because its time constant would be identified by nothing, which is the debt RN.ANP.TAU already carries, and branch G3 forbids the structural addition anyway. || WHAT IT WOULD BE WORTH. 4.9578 -> 4.2019 mmHg per 100 mmol/day against a human 1.70-2.30. About a sixth of the gap. Closing it by this route alone would need a 26-32 percent GFR swing across the step; the sourced human response over the same 102 mmol/day is 4.0 percent. This is a contributor, not the explanation, and under the anti-double-count rule ADR 0013 and ADR 0015 must be re-estimated against it. || THE JENSEN FIGURES IN THIS NOTE ARE SUPERSEDED. CORRECTED 2026-09-17, HANDOVER section 3.45. This note records that wiring this term moved Jensen's peak fractional sodium excretion rise 82.5 -> 79.3 percent against a reported 123, 'so the model is now slightly further low on the only endpoint it was not fitted to'. THE ARITHMETIC IS CORRECT AND THE STANDING IT DESCRIBES IS NOT, for two reasons: the macula densa arm moved the endpoint three weeks later, and the comparison was a MODEL PEAK against a STUDY'S FINAL SAMPLE rather than like for like. On Jensen's own 210-240 minute window the model is at 110.1 percent against a measured 122. || THE MECHANISM PARAGRAPH IS WORTH KEEPING FOR THE REASON IT WAS WRITTEN. It explains that raising GFR dilutes both natriuretic terms in the FRACTIONAL measure while absolute excretion rises, so the model excretes more sodium and reports a smaller fractional rise. That is still exactly what this term does; it is simply no longer the dominant influence on the endpoint. || AND ITS CLOSING SENTENCE WAS RIGHT ABOUT SOMETHING ELSE. It says 'whether that is right depends on glomerulotubular balance, which this model does not represent, and nothing here sources it'. That remains true and remains unsourced. It is no longer needed to explain a magnitude deficit, because there is none; the open question it bears on is the LATE time course, which no published number currently bounds."),
    :RN_GLU_TM => Provenance("RN.GLU.TM", "mg/min", 352.0, "A", "reported", "Mogensen CE. Maximum Tubular Reabsorption Capacity for Glucose and Renal Hemodynamics during Rapid Hypertonic Glucose Infusion in Normal and Diabetic Subjects. Scand J Clin Lab Invest 1971;28(1):101-109. PMID 5093515.", "TmG IN NORMAL SUBJECTS, 352 +/- 64 mg/min, n = 9. Mogensen 1971, PMID 5093515. glucose_insulin_prereg.md section 9.2. || READING LEVEL: ABSTRACT READ IN FULL AT THE PUBLISHER. PubMed carries NO abstract for this paper; the figures were first seen in a search summary of secondary reviews, WHICH IS NOT A READING, and were accepted only after the publisher's own abstract was opened. Directive 1.5. || MEASURED BY RAPID HYPERTONIC GLUCOSE INFUSION, the titration paradigm - the relationship IS the subject, directive 1.7. Diabetics of short duration gave 419 +/- 50 mg/min, n = 10, and are NOT used here: section 3 excludes diabetes cohorts for anything setting a normal value. || IT SCALES WITH GFR AND THE PAPER SUPPLIES THE REASON. 'For the whole material there was a clear positive correlation between GFR and Tmc.' So it is NOT a fixed mg/min and is not entered as one: the component carries the SURFACE-like size factor that GFR0 carries. The correlation is across INDIVIDUALS, i.e. across size, so it does not license scaling with a within-subject acute GFR change and the component does not do that. || DIRECTIVE 1.12: section 2 flagged the '180 mg/dL renal threshold' as the most suspect number in this subsystem, and the arithmetic bears it out. Tm/GFR gives a spill concentration of about 18 mmol/L, not 10. THE DIFFERENCE IS SPLAY - nephron heterogeneity means glycosuria begins before any nephron reaches its own maximum - AND THIS MODEL HAS NO SPLAY, so it will predict glycosuria starting LATER than it does in people. Named as a falsifier in ADR 0029 rather than patched with the teaching threshold. || SEX: the row is 'both' and the COHORT WAS MALE - Mogensen 1971 is indexed Male in MeSH. That is recorded here rather than in the sex column, which this ledger uses for parameters the model actually splits by sex. No sex-specific TmG was found, so ADR 0014's shared-value branch applies and this row inherits a male value applied to women."),
    :RN_H2O_OBLIGATORY_LOSS => Provenance("RN.H2O.OBLIGATORY_LOSS", "L/day", 0.947, "C", "derived", "Derived from RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD and ADH.URINE.OSM_MAX.", "DERIVED 2026-09-01, AND THIS IS THE INVERSION THE ROW ITSELF ASKED FOR. V_min = RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD / ADH.URINE.OSM_MAX = 600.0 / 982.0 = 0.611 L/day. Was 0.5, `assumed`, citation empty, from branch 6 of validation/verify_rows_prereg.md - whose note said the derivation in this ledger was probably inverted and that sourcing U_max and deriving this row was the right next move. It is now done, U_max is sourced from Tryding 1988, and the conventional 0.5 L/day was 18% low. THE MODEL HAD ALREADY INVERTED IT. Renal.jl records that V_min is deliberately no longer a parameter: since the urine solute load began tracking sodium excretion (2026-08-27) the floor is computed as Osm_load / U_max and varies with the salt arm. Nothing in src/ reads this row. tools/check_closure.py was its only consumer and was asserting the relationship in the OPPOSITE direction to the code it is supposed to be checking. That is fixed in the same change. TIER C, INHERITED FROM THE NUMERATOR AND NOT FROM THE DENOMINATOR. ADH.URINE.OSM_MAX is now tier A, but RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD = 600 mOsm/day is an unsourced conventional figure that RN.URINE.SOLUTE_NONNA already records as too low for a Western diet - typical measured totals are 700-900. A larger true load would raise this floor proportionally. The weakest input sets the tier. WHAT IT MEANS PHYSICALLY: with a 600 mOsm/day load and a true maximal urine concentration of 982 mOsm/kg, urine output cannot fall below 611 mL/day. The old pairing claimed 500 mL, which required a concentrating ability nobody has measured. || REFERENCE POINT CORRECTED 2026-09-16, urine_solute_prereg.md Half A, AND THE VALUE MOVED WITHOUT ANY SOURCE BEING OPENED. RN.URINE.SOLUTE_NONNA was pinned so the total returned 600 at the MID salt arm, 154 mEq/day, while the model's reference is the NOMINAL arm, 205. So the load the model actually carried at its own operating point was 292 + 2*205 = 702 and never 600. Three constants - this one, ADH.URINE.OSM_BASELINE and ADH.OSM.SENSITIVITY - were all derived from a load the model never occupied, and k_adh is LIVE. The loop paid for it with a permanent 0.59 mOsm/kg offset above the plasma osmolality setpoint: it had to reach u_osm = 702/1.7 = 412.9, hence adh 0.3894, hence Osm_ecf 287.594. Measured in the running model before the fix: 0.3895 and 287.592. The mid-arm pin was change management when solute tracking landed - it kept the salt step bit-identical - and it then became the reference point for three derived constants. Recomputed as 702/982 = 0.71487. FIVE FIGURES ARE NOT A PRECISION CLAIM: this row exists to close an identity check_closure.py asserts, which is the one exemption CLAUDE.md states. || RE-DERIVED 2026-09-16 FROM THE SOURCED SOLUTE LOAD. The figures beyond the two the parent supports are NOT a precision claim: this row exists to close an identity check_closure.py asserts, which is the one exemption CLAUDE.md states, and rounding it to the parent's precision breaks that identity by more than the 1e-3 closure tolerance. 930/982 = 0.94705 L/day. IT HAS MORE THAN DOUBLED, from 0.611, and that is a real change to the model's water reserve: the obligatory urine volume is now 56 percent of the reference 1.7 L/day. Branch U6 of the pre-registration was the stop condition for this and it does not fire - 0.75 L/day of headroom remains - but it was checked rather than assumed."),
    :RN_MD_RENIN_SLOPE => Provenance("RN.MD.RENIN_SLOPE", "L/mmol", 0.029, "A", "derived", "Lorenz JN, Weihprecht H, Schnermann J, Skott O, Briggs JP. Characterization of the macula densa stimulus for renin secretion. Am J Physiol 1990;259(1 Pt 2):F186-F193. PMID 2197878. FULL TEXT READ, supplied by the owner.", "LOG-LINEAR SLOPE OF RENIN SECRETION BELOW THE MACULA DENSA THRESHOLD. Lorenz 1990, series 2 group B. md_lorenz_gain_prereg.md, ADR 0027. || THE ARITHMETIC, AND IT IS FOUR NUMBERS FROM ONE TABLE: renin secretion 3.2 nGU/min at 80 mmol/L Na+ and 16.6 at 24 mmol/L, n = 8, P < 0.007. ln(16.6/3.2)/(80 - 24) = 0.029 per mmol/L. || TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES AND NOT THREE. 3.2 carries two figures, so the ratio is 5.2 and the slope cannot be known better than 0.029. Writing 0.0294 would claim a third figure the numerator does not have. Directive 1.13. || WHAT IT REPLACES. RN.MD.RENIN_GAIN, 4.99, extraction_method=calibrated, solved against van den Bosch's 5.74/2.10 salt-renin ratio. THAT ESTIMATION SET WAS RETIRED BY ADR 0025 and the row outlived it. Its own note predicted this replacement in advance: 'Build a macula densa NaCl concentration ... and this row becomes sourceable from Lorenz, retiring the second of this ledger's two calibrated rows.' The tubule split built the concentration and the prediction is now taken. || AND van den Bosch's 2.73 BECOMES A TEST. It has been an estimation set since 2026-09-05 and could never be quoted as agreement. With the gain sourced from an independent preparation it is a genuine endpoint for the first time, whichever way it falls. || THE PREPARATION IS WHY THIS IS THE GAIN AND NOT A GAIN. Isolated perfused rabbit juxtaglomerular apparatus, studied in the paper's own words 'in the absence of the confounding influences of intravascular pressure and renal nerve activity'. The model's other two renin arms are PHYSICALLY REMOVED in this preparation, so what it measures is this arm alone - which is exactly what a model that already has the other two needs. || SPECIES: RABBIT, n = 8. The cross-species step is rabbit juxtaglomerular apparatus to human, and it is the weakest link on this row. It is taken because the human experiment - perfusing one macula densa at a controlled NaCl concentration while holding renal perfusion pressure and nerve traffic fixed - CANNOT ETHICALLY BE PERFORMED, which is the condition under which this ledger accepts animal data. || SCALING: none. A threshold concentration is intensive and a log slope per mmol/L is intensive. || DISPERSION NOT CARRIED, AND THIS IS AN EXTRACTION GAP RATHER THAN A PRECISION CLAIM. macula_densa_lorenz_prereg.md took down the group means and the significance test (P < 0.007) but not the SEMs, so no error bar can be propagated here. Directive 1.13's honest form is to say so on the row rather than to leave the column empty without comment. RETIRING THIS GAP needs only the SEMs from series 2 of a paper already in hand."),
    :RN_MD_RENIN_THRESHOLD => Provenance("RN.MD.RENIN_THRESHOLD", "mmol/L", 80.0, "A", "reported", "Lorenz JN, Weihprecht H, Schnermann J, Skott O, Briggs JP. Characterization of the macula densa stimulus for renin secretion. Am J Physiol 1990;259(1 Pt 2):F186-F193. PMID 2197878. FULL TEXT READ, supplied by the owner.", "MACULA DENSA SODIUM CONCENTRATION ABOVE WHICH RENIN SECRETION IS FLAT. Lorenz 1990, series 2 group A. md_lorenz_gain_prereg.md, ADR 0027. || REPORTED, NOT FITTED. Renin secretion was 2.2 nGU/min at 141 mmol/L Na+ and 1.9 at 80 - NO EFFECT, n = 8 - while between 80 and 24 it went 3.2 to 16.6. The paper's conclusion is that the full response occurs 'below 80 mM Na+ and 61 mM Cl-'. || THE MODEL'S OPERATING POINT SITS BELOW IT AND THAT WAS NOT ARRANGED. RN.NA.MACULA_DENSA_CONC_REFERENCE is 53.9 mmol/L, derived from Shirley's proximal fraction and the macula densa fraction, both entered before this threshold was consumed. Lorenz states the full response occurs 'within the concentration range normally occurring at the macula densa' - SO HIS THRESHOLD AND THIS MODEL'S INDEPENDENTLY DERIVED OPERATING POINT AGREE ABOUT WHICH SIDE THE KIDNEY SITS ON, and nothing was fitted to obtain that. || IT ALSO RETIRES A COMMENT THAT WAS FALSE. Raas.jl carried 'nothing found says the macula densa arm does' plateau above a threshold. Lorenz is the something found; macula_densa_lorenz_prereg.md recorded that the comment was false on 2026-09-20 and could not then place the threshold because the model had no concentration axis to place it on. It does now. || CHLORIDE OR SODIUM: the perfusates were paired, 80 mmol/L Na+ with 61 Cl-, and the threshold is entered on the SODIUM axis because that is the axis this model tracks. NO CONVERSION WAS APPLIED and the chloride figure is recorded here so the choice is visible. || THE PREPARATION IS WHY THIS IS THE GAIN AND NOT A GAIN. Isolated perfused rabbit juxtaglomerular apparatus, studied in the paper's own words 'in the absence of the confounding influences of intravascular pressure and renal nerve activity'. The model's other two renin arms are PHYSICALLY REMOVED in this preparation, so what it measures is this arm alone - which is exactly what a model that already has the other two needs. || SPECIES: RABBIT, n = 8. The cross-species step is rabbit juxtaglomerular apparatus to human, and it is the weakest link on this row. It is taken because the human experiment - perfusing one macula densa at a controlled NaCl concentration while holding renal perfusion pressure and nerve traffic fixed - CANNOT ETHICALLY BE PERFORMED, which is the condition under which this ledger accepts animal data. || SCALING: none. A threshold concentration is intensive and a log slope per mmol/L is intensive. || DISPERSION NOT CARRIED, AND THIS IS AN EXTRACTION GAP RATHER THAN A PRECISION CLAIM. macula_densa_lorenz_prereg.md took down the group means and the significance test (P < 0.007) but not the SEMs, so no error bar can be propagated here. Directive 1.13's honest form is to say so on the row rather than to leave the column empty without comment. RETIRING THIS GAP needs only the SEMs from series 2 of a paper already in hand."),
    :RN_NA_FRACTIONAL_REABSORPTION => Provenance("RN.NA.FRACTIONAL_REABSORPTION", "unitless", 0.9904044, "B", "derived", "Derived, not measured: FR_Na = 1 - BF.NA.INTAKE_NOMINAL / (RN.GFR.NOMINAL * BF.NA.PLASMA_SETPOINT) = 1 - 205/(152.6*140) = 0.9904044186..., i.e. the fractional reabsorption that makes sodium excretion equal intake at the nominal operating point. Enforced by tools/check_closure.py, 'Na excretion == Na intake'.", "VALUE MOVED 0.9918651 -> 0.9904044 ON 2026-08-27, FORCED BY RN.GFR.NOMINAL 180 -> 152.6. This row is DERIVED and is not independent of GFR: a smaller filtered load needs a smaller reabsorbed fraction to excrete the same 205 mEq/day. Re-deriving it was mandatory, not optional - leaving it at 0.9918651 would have broken sodium balance by about 18%. The excreted fraction 1 - FR_Na moved 0.0081349 -> 0.0095956. The pre-registration fixed this dependency in advance precisely so the two could not be entered as though they were separate measurements. DISCHARGED 2026-08-27 WITHOUT A LITERATURE SEARCH, AND THAT IS THE CORRECT OUTCOME. The row carried 'Standard physiological reference. VERIFY.' while claiming extraction_method = derived. A derived value does not need a citation, it needs its DERIVATION stated and checkable, and searching for a source would have been answering a question this row is not asking. The arithmetic is exact: 1 - 205/(180*140) = 0.9918650793650794, matching the stored value to every digit it carries. Branch 1 of validation/verify_rows_prereg.md. NO NEW GATE WAS ADDED. tools/check_closure.py already asserts exactly this identity under 'Na excretion == Na intake' - directive 1.10, do not add what already exists. IT IS NOT INDEPENDENT OF RN.GFR.NOMINAL. If GFR moves this MUST move with it by the same derivation; re-entering them separately would break sodium balance silently. SIGNIFICANT FIGURES APPLY TO THE QUANTITY THAT CARRIES THE INFORMATION, WHICH HERE IS 1 - FR_Na, NOT FR_Na. This row was rounded to 4 significant figures (0.9919) on 2026-08-27 with everything else and immediately broke sodium balance: excretion 204.12 against intake 205, a 0.43% drift, because the excreted fraction is 0.0081 and four figures on FR_Na leaves only two on the difference. Restored to 0.9918651. A small difference of two large numbers needs enough digits on the large numbers to leave the difference meaningful - that is not false precision, it is the arithmetic. This is the same failure recorded in the header of tools/check_closure.py, where rounding this row to 0.9915 gave 214.2 against 205. || PRECISION: rounded to 4 significant figures on 2026-08-27. The sources do not support more, and carrying digits that do not exist forced the closure gate and the test suite to chase arithmetic noise. Derived rows inherit the significant figures of their inputs and are rounded to match; the closure tolerance was moved to 1e-3 in the same change, which is still an order of magnitude tighter than the 6% error that motivated that gate. || DERIVED to close the loop at nominal: filtered load = 180 L/day x 140 mEq/L = 25200 mEq/day; excretion must equal intake of 205 mEq/day at steady state, so FR = 1 - 205/25200 = 0.9918651. NOT rounded - rounding to 0.9915 gave excretion of 214.2 vs intake 205, a 9.2 mEq/day drift that ran the model to a lethal state while reporting Success. This is NOT an independent measurement - it is fixed by the other three values, and that dependency must be preserved if any of them changes."),
    :RN_NA_MACULA_DENSA_CONC_REFERENCE => Provenance("RN.NA.MACULA_DENSA_CONC_REFERENCE", "mmol/L", 53.9, "C", "derived", "Derived from RN.NA.TAL_REABSORBED_FRACTION and BF.NA.PLASMA_SETPOINT. Bound checked against Lorenz JN, Weihprecht H, Schnermann J, Skott O, Briggs JP. Characterization of the macula densa stimulus for renin secretion. Am J Physiol 1990;259(1 Pt 2):F186-F193.", "MACULA DENSA SODIUM CONCENTRATION AT THE OPERATING POINT. Derived 2026-09-20, tubule_segments_prereg.md. This is THE VARIABLE LORENZ 1990 MEASURED, and until this pass the model had no representation of it at all. || DERIVATION: md_conc_ref = C_Na * (1 - f_tal) = 140 * 0.3846 = 53.8 mmol/l. It follows from RN.NA.TAL_REABSORBED_FRACTION and the plasma sodium setpoint and introduces no new information. || AND IT IS A FREE CONSISTENCY CHECK ACROSS THREE INDEPENDENT SOURCES. Lorenz JN, Weihprecht H, Schnermann J, Skott O, Briggs JP, Am J Physiol 1990;259(1 Pt 2):F186-F193, PMID 2197878, states that the full renin response occurs below 80 mM Na+ and that this is 'the concentration range normally occurring at the macula densa'. 53.8 mmol/l is INSIDE that bound. Shirley 2002 supplied the proximal delivery, the macula densa fraction came from elsewhere, and Lorenz supplied the bound - NOBODY ARRANGED THIS AGREEMENT and nothing was fitted to obtain it. || IT IS A REFERENCE AND NOT A MEASUREMENT OF THIS MODEL'S HUMAN. No human macula densa concentration has ever been measured - micropuncture is not performable in people - so this row is what the model's own sourced segmental fractions imply, checked against an animal bound. Directive 1.6's recording requirements apply to the bound: rabbit, isolated perfused juxtaglomerular apparatus. || CLOSURE: check_closure.py asserts md_conc_ref = C_Na * (1 - f_tal), which is why the digits are carried. || CORRECTED 53.8 -> 53.9 ON ENTRY, 2026-09-20, BEFORE THE PASS WAS COMMITTED. The first value came from 140 * (1 - 0.6154) = 53.85 rounded down, but the model carries f_tal at its two-figure-consistent 0.615, and 140 * (1 - 0.615) = 53.9 exactly. The mismatch left md_drive at -0.0019 instead of zero at the operating point, which fed renin, which shifted pra, which shifted f_prox_eff off its 0.26 reference - a small self-consistency drift through a loop that is supposed to be quiet at rest. FOUND BY RUNNING, not by arithmetic. The identity md_conc_ref = C_Na * (1 - f_tal) is now asserted by check_closure.py so it cannot drift again."),
    :RN_NA_MACULA_DENSA_FRACTION => Provenance("RN.NA.MACULA_DENSA_FRACTION", "dimensionless", 0.9, "C", "assumed", "No admissible source could be opened. See notes.", "ASSUMED, AND IT IS A ROUND TEACHING NUMBER - directive 1.12, and the pre-registration listed it in advance among the six to distrust. Searches for segmental sodium handling in healthy humans return lithium-clearance studies in cirrhosis, heart failure and hypertension; directive 1.7's sixth subsystem. || IT IS NOT SEPARATELY IDENTIFIABLE FROM RN.MD.RENIN_GAIN, AND THAT IS WHY AN ASSUMED VALUE IS TOLERABLE HERE. This fraction sets the SCALE of distal delivery; the macula densa gain multiplies the FRACTIONAL deviation of that delivery from its own reference. Halving one and doubling the other leaves every model output identical. Since the gain is estimated against salt-renin data, only the product is determined, and entering a sourced fraction would buy nothing until the gain is independently measured too. || IT ADDS NO INFORMATION ABOUT SEGMENTAL HANDLING and ADR 0021's disqualification section says so: the split is OBSERVATIONAL - it defines a variable and does not restructure the sodium equation, which is untouched. || RENAMED 2026-09-17: IT WAS CALLED A PROXIMAL FRACTION AND IT IS NOT ONE. Renal.jl computes Na_distal = Na_filtered*(1-this) and feeds it to md_drive, the MACULA DENSA signal. The macula densa sits at the end of the THICK ASCENDING LIMB - after the proximal tubule and after the loop - so 10 percent delivery is proximal PLUS loop combined. A name carrying a convention its value contradicts is HANDOVER section 3.2's failure mode, and this is the third instance. The param_id, the name and the symbol now say which segment this is. || AND IT IS COMPLETELY INERT, WHICH IS STRONGER THAN WHAT THIS ROW USED TO CLAIM. The note said it was 'not separately identifiable from RN.MD.RENIN_GAIN'. It is worse than that: md_drive = (Na_distal_ref - Na_distal)/Na_distal_ref and the factor (1-this) appears in BOTH, so it CANCELS EXACTLY. MEASURED, not argued: at 0.90 and at 0.74, with Na_distal_ref re-derived to match, MAP, sodium excretion, md_drive, plasma renin and extracellular volume are BIT-IDENTICAL to eight decimal places. Only the REPORTED Na_distal moves. || SO SOURCING THIS ROW WOULD BUY NOTHING BEHAVIOURALLY, and that is why it is still assumed after a pass that went looking for it. What the tubule needs is SEGMENTAL STRUCTURE - regulators acting where they act - not a better number for a parameter that cancels. || THE CONDITION THIS NOTE SETS WAS HALF MET ON 2026-09-20 AND THEN FAILED ON THE OTHER HALF. The note above says entering a sourced fraction 'would buy nothing until the gain is independently measured too'. The gain HAS now been independently measured - Lorenz JN, Weihprecht H, Schnermann J, Skott O, Briggs JP. Characterization of the macula densa stimulus for renin secretion. Am J Physiol 1990;259(1 Pt 2):F186-F193. PMID 2197878, isolated perfused rabbit juxtaglomerular apparatus with intravascular pressure and renal nerve activity physically absent. || BUT IT STILL BUYS NOTHING, FOR A DIFFERENT REASON. Lorenz shows renin responds to macula densa NaCl CONCENTRATION and not to delivery - in his series 3 delivery rose 51 percent while renin rose 3.2-fold - and this model's md_drive is keyed to delivery. Worse, md_drive is a RATIO in which this fraction cancels exactly: Na_distal = Na_filtered * (1 - f_md) * renal_mod appears in both the numerator and the reference, so (1 - f_md) divides out and THIS ROW HAS NO EFFECT ON THE MODEL AT ALL through that path. The identifiability argument above is therefore stronger than it claims - it is not that the fraction and the gain trade off, it is that the fraction is inert. || WHAT WOULD MAKE IT LIVE: a macula densa concentration, which needs distal FLOW, which needs the lumped water reabsorption split. See RN.MD.RENIN_GAIN. macula_densa_lorenz_prereg.md. || SUPERSEDED IN THE MODEL 2026-09-20 BUT KEPT, tubule_segments_prereg.md. The tubule was split into flux-carrying segments and macula densa delivery is now computed as a fraction of what the segment RECEIVES - RN.NA.TAL_REABSORBED_FRACTION - rather than as this fixed fraction of the FILTERED load. THIS ROW IS WHAT THAT FRACTION WAS DERIVED FROM, so it is read by the ledger even though no equation now reads it, and it records where 0.615 came from. The note above says this row is inert because (1 - f_md) cancels out of a ratio; after the split it is inert for a different and better reason - it has been converted into a delivery-based constant and its job is done."),
    :RN_NA_PROXIMAL_DELIVERY => Provenance("RN.NA.PROXIMAL_DELIVERY", "dimensionless", 0.26, "B", "reported", "Shirley DG, Walter SJ, Noormohamed FH. Natriuretic effect of caffeine: assessment of segmental sodium reabsorption in humans. Clin Sci (Lond) 2002;103(5):461-466. doi:10.1042/cs1030461. PMID 12401118. ABSTRACT READ IN FULL. Eight healthy males, GFR by 51Cr-EDTA, end-proximal fluid delivery by lithium clearance. PLACEBO ARM TAKEN: fractional excretion of lithium 26 +/- 2 percent, sodium/lithium clearance ratio 2.8 +/- 0.2 percent, fractional excretion of sodium 1.04 +/- 0.16 (control) and 0.70 +/- 0.07 (experimental).", "SOURCED IN HEALTHY HUMANS 2026-09-17 under nephron_segments_prereg.md, and the instrument is the point. The previous search for segmental handling looked for LITHIUM CLEARANCE IN DISEASE and for MICROPUNCTURE IN ANIMALS; this is lithium clearance in EIGHT HEALTHY MALES, which is the human measurement of end-proximal delivery and was there all along. || WHY LITHIUM MEASURES THIS: lithium is reabsorbed with sodium in the proximal tubule in proportion, and essentially not beyond it, so its fractional excretion is the fraction of filtered fluid delivered out of the proximal tubule. That is a METHODOLOGICAL assumption and not a measurement - it fails where distal lithium handling is altered, which is why the same paper warns that caffeine intake must be controlled when using the method. || THE PLACEBO ARM IS TAKEN, not the control period, because the paper's own internal consistency holds there: 0.26 x 0.028 = 0.73 percent against a measured fractional sodium excretion of 0.70. The control period gives 1.04 percent, which implies a distal escape near 4 percent. THE WITHIN-STUDY DRIFT IS REAL AND IS THE ROW'S UNCERTAINTY, not noise to be averaged away. || THIS IS NOT THE FRACTION RN.NA.MACULA_DENSA_FRACTION CARRIES. Lithium stops at the end of the proximal tubule; the macula densa sits at the end of the thick ascending limb, after the loop has taken most of what the proximal tubule passed on. 0.26 and 0.10 are different segments and both are real. || MEN ONLY, n = 8. || THE METHOD'S VALIDITY LIMITS ARE NOW ON THE ROW, AND THEY WERE MISSING. Atherton JC, Green R, Hughes S, McFall V, Sharples JA, Solomon LR, Wilson L. Lithium clearance in man: effects of dietary salt intake, acute changes in extracellular fluid volume, amiloride and frusemide. Clin Sci (Lond) 1987;73(6):645-651. doi:10.1042/cs0730645. PMID 3690979. ABSTRACT READ IN FULL. Normal human volunteers. Its conclusion, verbatim in substance: the lithium clearance method for assessment of proximal tubule salt and water reabsorption CAN be used with some degree of confidence at MEDIUM AND HIGH salt intake AND IN ACUTE VOLUME EXPANSION, but MAY NOT BE RELIABLE WHEN DIETARY SALT INTAKE IS LOW. Amiloride moved fractional lithium clearance on a low salt diet and was without effect at medium or high intake, which is the mechanism of the failure: a component of lithium is reabsorbed in the amiloride-sensitive distal nephron and that component only matters when distal sodium reabsorption is near-maximal. || SO THE ROW IS ADMISSIBLE AND ITS LOW-SODIUM EXTRAPOLATION IS NOT. Shirley's subjects sit at a fractional sodium excretion of 0.70-1.04 percent on an unrestricted intake, which is the regime Atherton certifies. A value read off a salt-depleted preparation would not be, and this model runs its salt step down to 38 mmol/day - BELOW that regime. NOTHING READS THIS ROW AS A FUNCTION OF INTAKE and it must not acquire such a reader without sourcing the intake dependence separately. || AND THE SAME GROUP PUBLISHED THE CAVEAT AGAINST ITS OWN INSTRUMENT, which is the reason to trust it: Shirley and Walter's own later work is what established the distal amiloride- and frusemide-sensitive component of lithium handling. || THIS IS NOW THE VALUE AT THE OPERATING POINT, NOT AT ALL SALT INTAKES, 2026-09-20. Folkerd 1995 (PMID 7733329) measured fractional lithium excretion at 8.3 percent on 31 mmol/day of sodium against 18.0 percent on 357, and Chiolero 2000 (PMID 11040249) confirmed the direction in 27 normotensives. End-proximal delivery is not a constant in humans. RN.NA.PROXIMAL_SALT_SENSITIVITY carries the modulation and this row is what it modulates about; the VALUE HERE IS UNCHANGED and Shirley 2002 still owns it. Only the RELATIVE response was taken from Folkerd, because his endogenous-lithium absolutes and Shirley's exogenous-technique value differ by nearly twofold - which is Folkerd's own headline result."),
    :RN_NA_PROXIMAL_SALT_SENSITIVITY => Provenance("RN.NA.PROXIMAL_SALT_SENSITIVITY", "dimensionless", 0.29, "B", "derived", "Folkerd E, Singer DR, Cappuccio FP, Markandu ND, Sampson B, MacGregor GA. Clearance of endogenous lithium in humans: altered dietary salt intake and comparison with exogenous lithium clearance. Am J Physiol 1995;268(4 Pt 2):F718-F722. || CONFIRMED INDEPENDENTLY: Chiolero A, Maillard M, Nussberger J, Brunner HR, Burnier M. Proximal sodium reabsorption: an independent determinant of blood pressure response to salt. Hypertension 2000;36(4):631-637.", "SOURCED 2026-09-20, proximal_salt_prereg.md. THE ROW IT MODULATES WAS A CONSTANT AND THE HUMAN QUANTITY IS NOT. RN.NA.PROXIMAL_DELIVERY is 0.26 from Shirley 2002 at a single salt state; in humans end-proximal delivery MORE THAN DOUBLES across the dietary range. || Folkerd 1995, PMID 7733329, SIX normal subjects, five days per diet: fractional excretion of lithium 8.3 +/- 2.9 percent at a sodium intake of 31 +/- 10 mmol/day against 18.0 +/- 5.1 percent at 357 +/- 78 mmol/day, P < 0.05. Absolute clearances 8.7 +/- 2.9 against 20.7 +/- 3.8 ml/min. || CONFIRMED INDEPENDENTLY IN A LARGER NORMOTENSIVE COHORT. Chiolero 2000, PMID 11040249, 27 normotensive subjects on high and low sodium: changing from low to high sodium produced increases in glomerular filtration rate (P < 0.05), renal plasma flow (P < 0.05) and fractional excretion of lithium (P < 0.01). Two groups, two laboratories, same direction. || THE DERIVATION. Folkerd's two points give a power-law exponent ln(18.0/8.3)/ln(357/31) = 0.317, so across this model's own salt arms of 38 to 230 mEq/day the predicted FE_Li ratio is (230/38)^0.317 = 1.77. The model's pra runs 2.5795 to 0.9437 about a reference of 1.2956, and f_prox_eff = f_prox * (1 + k_prox * (pra_ref - pra)) reproduces that ratio at k_prox = 0.29. TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES, directive 1.13 - two points carrying two figures each cannot give three. || ONLY THE RELATIVE RESPONSE CROSSES, AND THIS IS THE SUBTLE PART. Folkerd's own headline finding is that ENDOGENOUS and EXOGENOUS lithium clearance DIFFER: fractional excretion 16.4 +/- 2.1 percent endogenous against 27.9 +/- 2.1 percent exogenous, P < 0.05, in the same subjects. His salt-step numbers are ENDOGENOUS; this model's 0.26 reference is Shirley's EXOGENOUS-technique value. TRANSFERRING FOLKERD'S ABSOLUTES WOULD MIX TWO METHODS THAT HE SHOWS DISAGREE BY NEARLY TWOFOLD. Only the ratio is taken, and the reference stays where Shirley put it. || KEYED TO pra AND NOT TO SODIUM INTAKE. Angiotensin II stimulates proximal reabsorption - that is what Hall 1977 (PMID 879288) and Hall 1984 (PMID 6720967) measured - so the mechanism is already in this model, and keying to the intake parameter would be open-loop: the tubule would respond to a number the body cannot see. || THE POWER LAW IS AN INTERPOLATION AND NOT A MEASUREMENT. Two points fix one exponent and say nothing about curvature; a linear-in-intake reading of the same two points is equally admissible and is not distinguishable by them. || IT MUST LEAVE THE MACULA DENSA SIGNAL ALONE. Vallon 2002, PMID 12089382, HANDOVER section 3.65: in normal rats dietary salt does not affect the tubuloglomerular feedback signal, and they adapt downstream of the macula densa. Both results hold together only if the thick ascending limb absorbs the extra proximal delivery. md_drive is therefore checked at both salt arms and must stay near-constant; if it starts tracking salt this term has been wired into the wrong path. || SPECIES: HUMAN, healthy, n = 6 for the salt response and n = 27 for the confirmation. SCALING: none - a fractional excretion is dimensionless and intensive."),
    :RN_NA_TAL_REABSORBED_FRACTION => Provenance("RN.NA.TAL_REABSORBED_FRACTION", "dimensionless", 0.615, "C", "derived", "Derived from RN.NA.MACULA_DENSA_FRACTION and RN.NA.PROXIMAL_DELIVERY; see notes.", "FRACTION OF DELIVERED SODIUM REABSORBED BETWEEN THE END OF THE PROXIMAL TUBULE AND THE MACULA DENSA. Derived 2026-09-20 from two rows already in this ledger, tubule_segments_prereg.md. || THE CHANGE THIS ROW EMBODIES IS THE POINT OF THE WHOLE PASS. RN.NA.MACULA_DENSA_FRACTION expressed macula densa delivery as a fixed fraction of the FILTERED load; this expresses it as a fraction of what the segment RECEIVES. Those are different physiology: with a fixed fraction of filtration, delivery and concentration at the macula densa cannot come apart, and coming apart is exactly what Lorenz 1990 measured. || DERIVATION: f_tal = 1 - (1 - f_md)/f_prox = 1 - 0.10/0.26 = 0.615. RN.NA.MACULA_DENSA_FRACTION = 0.90 puts 10 percent of filtered sodium at the macula densa and RN.NA.PROXIMAL_DELIVERY = 0.26 puts 26 percent of the filtrate past the proximal tubule, so the segment between them reabsorbs 61.5 percent of its own load. TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES: 0.62 would be three on a quantity whose parents carry two, and 0.615 is carried only because it reproduces the macula densa concentration identity that check_closure.py asserts. || IT LUMPS TWO SEGMENTS AND ONLY THEIR RATIO IS IDENTIFIED. Sodium reabsorption in the thick ascending limb and WATER reabsorption in the descending limb both set the macula densa concentration, they enter it as a ratio, and nothing in hand separates them. Splitting them would add a parameter no measurement here could constrain - the mistake ADR 0006's tiers exist to prevent. || WHAT IT BUYS, AND IT IS A RESULT RATHER THAN A CONVENIENCE. With a constant f_tal the macula densa concentration works out to C_Na*(1 - f_tal), so the proximal delivery fraction CANCELS and the concentration is SALT-INDEPENDENT. That is Vallon 2002 (PMID 12089382) - in nondiabetic rats dietary salt did not affect the tubuloglomerular feedback signal - reproduced as an EMERGENT CONSEQUENCE of the segment structure rather than assumed. || SPECIES: the two parent rows are human - Shirley 2002 lithium clearance in eight healthy males, and the macula densa fraction. SCALING: none, it is a dimensionless fraction of a delivery."),
    :RN_PRESSURE_NATRIURESIS_SLOPE => Provenance("RN.PRESSURE_NATRIURESIS.SLOPE", "(mEq/day)/mmHg", 8.4, "B", "calibrated", "SOLVED INSIDE THIS MODEL against human data; not lifted from a published value. Originating model for the ROW and the FORM, and for the 20.0 that stood until 2026-09-03: Guyton AC, Coleman TG, Granger HJ. Circulation: overall regulation. Annu Rev Physiol 1972;34:13-46. See notes for what Guyton is and is not the source of.", "CHANGED 11.4 -> 8.4 ON 2026-09-03, on the owner's instruction, and 8.4 is the CONSTRAINT-CONSISTENT partner of the sourced volume gain rather than a second free choice. The human joint constraint is G_pn + 0.0594*G_anp = 50 per 100 mmol/day; with CV.ANP.NATRIURETIC_GAIN sourced at 700 that gives 8.4 exactly. 11.4 had paired with 650 and the mismatch was recorded on this row when 11.4 was entered - it predicted a chronic salt sensitivity just below the human window, which is what was measured, and 8.4 was predicted to land inside it. It does. || CHANGED 20.0 -> 11.4 ON 2026-09-03 ON THE OWNER'S EXPLICIT INSTRUCTION. This row had been parked at his decision since 2026-08-25 and the instruction discharges that. IT IS NOT ADR 0013'S PROPOSAL - that record proposes 51.0 on the human PRESSURE evidence alone and remains Proposed and unadopted. 11.4 is the JOINT estimate that follows from ADR 0010's volume-keyed path being sourced and turned on: the human data constrain the COMBINATION G_pn + 0.0594*G_anp = 50 per 100 mmol/day, and with G_anp identified independently by the acute route the pressure slope is what remains. || ARITHMETIC NOTE THAT MATTERS. 11.4 pairs with G_anp = 650. The entered CV.ANP.NATRIURETIC_GAIN is 700, and the same constraint at 700 gives 8.4. So 11.4 and 700 are NOT the matched pair and the combination is under-satisfied by about 3 units, which shows up as a chronic salt sensitivity above the human window. Recorded rather than silently reconciled, because reconciling it by moving either number after seeing the result is the circularity this row's own history is a warning about. || WHAT IT DOES, measured with julia --project=. validation/challenges.jl. See the session record; the acute limbs are barely affected because they are carried by the volume path, and the chronic limb moves because this row is what sets it. || THE PREVIOUS NOTE IS PRESERVED BELOW BECAUSE ITS EVIDENCE IS UNCHANGED. || CALIBRATED, not measured. VALUE UNCHANGED AT 20.0 - read the identifiability result below before altering it. Originating model: Guyton 1972 systems analysis; the EXISTENCE and steepness of pressure natriuresis is E1 and well replicated, this particular slope is a fitted constant that propagated through the modelling literature. FORM: LINEAR, AND NOW SOURCED. Roman RJ, Cowley AW Jr, Am J Physiol 1985, PMID 3970209 (rat, denervated, vasopressin/aldosterone/corticosterone/noradrenaline clamped) report the relation as the slope of a LINE, 2 uL/min/kidney/mmHg over RPP 90-160 mmHg. Osborn/Francisco/DiBona, Proc Soc Exp Biol Med 1981, independently report UNaV falling LINEARLY over dog RPP 137-55 mmHg. An earlier note in relations.csv called the curve 'markedly nonlinear'; that was asserted from memory and is RETRACTED. VALIDITY RANGE roughly 55-160 mmHg. RN.AUTOREG.UPPER was 180, outside that range; moved to 160 on 2026-08-21 and cited to the same paper. MAGNITUDE CROSS-CHECK: Mizelle HL et al, Hypertension 1993;22:102-110 (dog, split bladder, bilateral servo-control, 12 d - the cleanest isolation of pressure per se) gives 3.468 mmol/day/mmHg whole animal = 2.154e-4 of filtered load per mmHg against this row's 7.937e-4, so the calibrated value is 3.68x STEEPER than the animal datum on a scaling-free basis. That is the range in uncertainty_value; 5.43 is the Mizelle-consistent value. SINGLE SOURCE, k=1, DOG - per validation/pooling.md that is animal-derived, may not be pooled with a human value, and is recorded as a comparator rather than adopted. IDENTIFIABILITY (measured 2026-08-20 on IPE at 9a6fa42): this parameter ALONE fixes the model's salt sensitivity. At steady state excretion equals intake, so Na_excr = Na_filtered*(1-FR_Na) + G_pn*(MAP-MAP_ref) gives dMAP ~ d(intake)/G_pn, in which CV.VENOUS_RETURN.SENSITIVITY does not appear. Confirmed numerically: the 205->103 mEq/day step gives 4.934 mmHg at G_pn=20.0 and 15.698 mmHg at G_pn=5.43, while varying G_vr over 2880->600 at fixed G_pn=5.43 moves the shift only 15.698->12.403 and drives V_ecf to 9.889 L, below the 10 L floor asserted in test/runtests.jl. The residual departure from an exact 1/G_pn law is C_Na drift. CONSEQUENCE, correcting HANDOVER.md section 5 item 4: G_pn and G_vr are NOT jointly identified as one lumped loop gain. G_pn is identified by the salt-sensitivity shift and G_vr by ECF volume, separately. Adopting the Mizelle value in isolation re-balances nothing - it forces a 15.7 mmHg shift across a 102 mEq/day range, which is salt-sensitive-hypertensive behaviour, not normotensive. WHY THE GAP IS PROBABLY REAL RATHER THAN AN ERROR: IPE has NO volume-sensing natriuretic path. Seeliger et al, J Physiol 2004;559:939-951, servo-controlled RPP during saline loading in freely moving dogs with NO reduction in peak or cumulative natriuresis, and Bie 2018 (10.1152/ajpregu.00363.2017) argues excretion is keyed to extracellular volume rather than pressure. With ANP absent, all sodium regulation is forced through this one term, so an inflated G_pn is the expected COMPENSATION for the missing path. DO NOT lower this to 5.43 before a volume-sensing natriuretic path exists - doing so degrades the only behaviour the model has ever reproduced. Re-estimate G_pn and the ANP gain JOINTLY against digitised Mars500, as posteriors, not this value alone and not as a point value. || ADR 0013'S FALSIFIABLE TEST WAS RUN ON 2026-09-02 AND THE VALUE STAYS AT 20.0. Pre-registered in validation/ecf_salt_response_prereg.md; reproduce with python validation/ecf_salt_response_extract.py. ADR 0013 proposes 20.0 -> 51.0 on the human PRESSURE response and requires its volume test to pass BEFORE acceptance. It fails. van den Bosch 2021 (Physiol Rep 2021;9(24):e15103, PMID 34921521, n = 70 healthy men, 7 d per level, ECFV by iothalamate, intake verified by 24 h urinary sodium) gives dMAP/dV_ecf = 1.885 mmHg/L against the model's 9.80 at that cohort's 80.6 kg - a factor of 5.2 against a pre-registered failure threshold of 2, and it fails on all three volume proxies so it does not turn on the iothalamate space or the 1 kg = 1 L conversion. THE FAILURE IS NOT ABOUT THIS ROW. G_pn and CV.VENOUS_RETURN.SENSITIVITY are ORTHOGONAL, measured: an 8x change in G_vr moves the pressure response by 0.12% and moves the volume response exactly inversely. G_pn sets dMAP; G_vr sets dMAP/dV_ecf. So the human pressure data and the human volume data identify one parameter each with no cross-talk, and the pressure limb still supports 51. What the test shows is that ACCEPTING 51 ALONE WOULD MAKE THE VOLUME RESPONSE WORSE - from 1.26x too small at 20 to 3.2x too small at 51 - because dMAP is what this row moves and dV_ecf follows it down at a fixed, wrong ratio. Fix G_vr first, re-run the test, then accept. DECLARED CONFLICT: Heer 2000 (PMID 10751219) and Heer 2009 (PMID 19173770) report no total-body-water or body-mass increase on high sodium and a non-monotonic ECV response, which would put the error's sign the other way; that camp invokes osmotically inactive sodium storage, which is ADR 0004 and is default OFF here. || THE HUMAN BAND THIS ROW IS COMPARED AGAINST MOVED 2026-09-16. ecf_salt_response_extract.py de-indexed van den Bosch's extracellular volume with ONE body surface area where the paper prints one per arm, understating the human expansion by 9 percent. The tracer limb goes 0.553 -> 0.602 L per 100 mmol/day and the human dMAP/dV_ecf band 2.97-4.16 -> 2.82-4.02 mmHg/L. THIS ROW'S VALUE IS UNCHANGED and was not refitted to the new band - that would be fitting a parameter to a target the same pass moved. ADR 0013 addendum, validation/ecf_deindex_prereg.md. || PROVENANCE CORRECTED 2026-09-18, gpn_label_prereg.md. NO VALUE MOVED. THE CITATION FIELD DESCRIBED A NUMBER THIS ROW NO LONGER HOLDS. It read as Guyton 1972 with extraction_method=calibrated, which SOURCES.md defines as a value lifted from another model's fitted parameters - and 8.4 IS NOT GUYTON'S NUMBER. This row's own note above already said so: 8.4 is the constraint-consistent partner of the sourced volume gain, solved inside THIS model. Guyton is the origin of the ROW, of the FORM, and of the 20.0 that stood until 2026-09-03; he is not the source of the value. The citation now says which. FAILURE MODE #11, and the same shape the ANP label pass fixed the same day. || extraction_method STAYS `calibrated`, on the precedent RN.MD.RENIN_GAIN set: that row is also solved inside this model against human measurements and also carries `calibrated`, having recorded that naming the absent originating model is the honest answer to a label written for the other case. The count of calibrated rows is unchanged at two and this row is still one of them. || AND THE JOINT CONSTRAINT RECORDED ABOVE IS NO LONGER SATISFIED. The note says the human joint constraint is G_pn + 0.0594*G_anp = 50 and that 8.4 follows with the volume gain at 700. THAT PARTNER IS NOW 585 (and renamed CV.VOLUME.NATRIURETIC_GAIN): 8.4 + 0.0594*585 = 43.15, short of 50 by 6.85, about 14 percent. The value that would satisfy the linearisation at 585 is 15.25. || THAT IS NOT A VALUE DEFECT AND THE VALUE WAS NOT MOVED - DIRECTIVE 1.14, CHECKED BEFORE CHASING. The model's chronic salt sensitivity at the current pair is 1.96, inside the human 1.70-2.30, so the pair WORKS. The volume gain was re-solved 700 -> 585 against the NONLINEAR model outcome with G_pn held at 8.4, targeting a chronic salt sensitivity of 2.00; G_pn + 0.0594*G_anp = 50 is a LINEARISATION of that same target. The solved pair satisfies the outcome and not the linearisation, and THE OUTCOME IS THE THING THAT WAS EVER MEANT TO HOLD. || SO THE DEFECT IS THE NOTE, NOT THE NUMBER: a constraint was left stated as if binding when the model deliberately does not satisfy it. Two copies of a fact drifting apart, which CLAUDE.md names as the failure this repository is organised against. WHAT WOULD MAKE IT A REAL DEFECT: the chronic salt sensitivity leaving 1.70-2.30, or a demonstration that the linearisation is exact rather than approximate. Neither holds. || STILL OPEN AND NOT RESOLVED HERE: ADR 0013 proposes 51.0 on the human PRESSURE evidence alone and remains Proposed and unadopted; ADR 0015 is also Proposed. The model is running on rows those records discuss while neither has been accepted. Reported, not resolved - adopting either in a relabelling pass is exactly what this pass forbids itself."),
    :RN_TAL_ADAPTATION_TAU => Provenance("RN.TAL.ADAPTATION_TAU", "day", 1.0, "C", "assumed", "", "TIME CONSTANT OF THICK ASCENDING LIMB TRANSPORT ADAPTATION. ASSUMED, AND THE ROW SAYS SO. tal_saturable_prereg.md. || WHY IT IS NEEDED, AND IT IS NOT A FUDGE. Saturable TAL transport with a FIXED capacity makes macula densa concentration rise with flow - which is the acute signal Jensen needs - but f_prox_eff varies 1.76-fold across the salt step (Folkerd 1995), so chronic flow varies too and the concentration would swing about 34 percent between salt arms. THAT CONTRADICTS VALLON 2002 (PMID 12089382), who measured NO change in the tubuloglomerular feedback signal across dietary salt in normal rats. || TUBULAR ADAPTATION IS THE STANDARD RESOLUTION: thick ascending limb transport capacity tracks sustained load. Acutely the capacity is fixed, flow rises, the concentration rises. Chronically the capacity follows delivery and the concentration returns to its reference. ALL THREE SOURCES - Layton's flow dependence, Folkerd's proximal salt response and Vallon's chronic constancy - BECOME TRUE AT ONCE, which none of the three previous forms of this arm managed. || THE VALUE IS BRACKETED, NOT MEASURED. It must be LONGER than Jensen's four-hour protocol, which sees the flow effect, and SHORTER than Vallon's one-week diets, which do not. One day sits in the middle of that bracket on a log scale. NO SEARCH FOUND A DIRECT MEASUREMENT of the adaptation time constant for TAL transport capacity, and this row is the place to record that rather than to dress an assumption as a source. || IT WAS NOT TUNED TO MAKE JENSEN PASS. Jensen's fractional sodium excretion rise is the FALSIFIABLE TEST of this pass, declared as such in the pre-registration before the value was entered. Moving this constant toward whichever end of its bracket flattered that endpoint would spend the test. || WHAT WOULD RETIRE THE ASSUMPTION: a measurement of how quickly NKCC2-mediated transport capacity follows a step change in delivered load. Days is the expectation from transporter expression work; a number is not in hand."),
    :RN_TAL_KM => Provenance("RN.TAL.KM", "mmol/L", 140.0, "C", "calibrated", "Layton HE, Pitman EB, Moore LC. Nonlinear filter properties of the thick ascending limb. Am J Physiol 1997;273(4):F625-F634.", "MICHAELIS CONSTANT FOR THICK ASCENDING LIMB CHLORIDE TRANSPORT. Layton 1997, PMID 9362340, Table 1, full text read and supplied by the owner. tal_saturable_prereg.md. || extraction_method = CALIBRATED, AND THE ORIGINATING MODEL IS NAMED, which is what SOURCES.md reserves that label for. This is a parameter of a MATHEMATICAL MODEL of the thick ascending limb, not a direct measurement; Layton selected it in his Ref. 15 to give a steady-state macula densa concentration and concentration slope consistent with experiments. It is the third calibrated row in this ledger and the first whose originating model exists and can be cited. || LAYTON'S TABLE 1, BACKLEAK-FREE COLUMN: Km 140 mM, chloride at the TAL entrance 275 mM, macula densa chloride 32.12 mM, TAL length 0.500 cm, luminal radius 10.0 micrometres, single-nephron GFR 30.0 nl/min, steady-state transit time 15.708 s. || WHAT TRANSFERS IS THE FORM AND THIS CONSTANT. The paper's central result is that NaCl concentration at each location along the TAL depends ONLY on the fluid transit time up the TAL to that location - the flow dependence, stated exactly. That is the property this model needed and could not produce with a constant reabsorbed fraction. || A CONCENTRATION IS INTENSIVE so no body-size scaling applies; the cross-species step is rat/rabbit transport kinetics to a human tubule, and it is the weakest link on this row. || LAYTON'S MACULA DENSA VALUE IS CHLORIDE AND THIS MODEL TRACKS SODIUM. His 32.12 mM Cl against this model's 53.9 mM Na is consistent with the anion gap Lorenz's own perfusates carry - 80 mM Na against 61 mM Cl, and 24 against 7 - so the two are the same ballpark measured on different ions. NO CONVERSION WAS APPLIED: only Km and the functional form were taken."),
    :RN_TGF_FLOW_ELASTICITY => Provenance("RN.TGF.FLOW_ELASTICITY", "unitless", 0.8, "A", "reported", "Briggs JP, Schubert G, Schnermann J. Quantitative characterization of the tubuloglomerular feedback response: effect of growth. Am J Physiol 1984;247(5 Pt 2):F808-F815. PMID 6496746. ABSTRACT READ IN FULL on PubMed, 2026-09-20.", "FRACTIONAL FALL IN SINGLE-NEPHRON GFR PER FRACTIONAL RISE IN LOOP OF HENLE FLOW, AT THE FEEDBACK MIDPOINT. Briggs 1984, PMID 6496746. tgf_prereg.md, ADR 0026. || THE PAPER'S OWN WORDS: 'Independent of rat size, a 10% increase in loop flow at the midpoint produced a 5-10% decrease in SNGFR.' That is the row: 0.5 to 1.0, dimensionless. || WHY THIS PAPER RATHER THAN A BIGGER ONE. Male Sprague-Dawley rats at 100, 220 and 350 g, micropuncture, SNGFR measured at several loop perfusion rates in the SAME nephron. The sigmoid dSNGFR = a/(1 + exp(k*(b - VLP))) fitted per nephron. EVERY ABSOLUTE QUANTITY TRIPLES ACROSS THE WEIGHT RANGE - maximal response 7.9, 18.9 and 25.2 nl/min, half-maximal flow 10.3, 15.4 and 22.3 nl/min, slope 0.9, 1.7 and 3.2 - AND THE DIMENSIONLESS ELASTICITY DOES NOT. The authors varied body size 3.5-fold and reported the quantity that survived it, which is precisely the quantity a human whole-kidney model can take. || THE CROSS-SPECIES STEP IS THE WEAKEST LINK ON THIS ROW AND IT IS NOT HIDDEN. Rat single-nephron feedback to a human whole-kidney GFR. It is entered because the paper's own design tested size-invariance over a 3.5-fold range and found it, not because rat and human were assumed alike. || A LINEAR TERM IS WHAT THE SOURCE LICENSES. The same abstract reports that free-flow SNGFR and loop flow lie in the MOST SENSITIVE RANGE of the feedback curve, so the operating point sits at the midpoint and a local slope is the right form there. The sigmoid saturates and the linear term does not, so the component clamps its multiplier to 0.6-1.4 AS A NUMERICAL GUARD, NOT A PHYSIOLOGICAL CLAIM - free-flow SNGFR is not in the abstract, so the fraction the maximal response corresponds to CANNOT BE COMPUTED FROM WHAT HAS BEEN READ, and whether the guard ever binds is a declared test. || 0.8 AND NOT 0.75. The bounds are the paper's and are given to ONE significant figure; a midpoint written as 0.75 would claim a second figure the range does not support. Directive 1.13. || APPLIED TO CONCENTRATION, NOT FLOW, AND THE COMPONENT CONVERTS. Briggs's independent variable is loop flow; the macula densa senses concentration (Lorenz 1990, PMID 2197878). The distinction decides the CHRONIC behaviour: once TAL capacity has adapted, flow is high while concentration is back at reference, so a flow-driven term would stay switched on forever and clamp GFR at every salt intake. The conversion uses the model's own transport integral and is computed in Renal.jl rather than stored. || SEX: the row is 'both'. Briggs studied MALE Sprague-Dawley rats only, recorded here rather than in the sex column, which this ledger uses for parameters the model actually splits by sex. No sex-specific TGF elasticity was found. || SCALING: none, it is dimensionless. || WHAT IT FIXES: saturable TAL transport made macula densa concentration flow-sensitive and closed a POSITIVE feedback loop through renin and the proximal tubule with gain 1.6, leaving the model with no steady state. The macula densa has two effectors and the model had only the slow one."),
    :RN_URINE_OSM_PER_NA => Provenance("RN.URINE.OSM_PER_NA", "mOsm/mEq", 2.0, "B", "reported", "Imamura T, Kinugawa K, Minatsuki S, Muraoka H, Kato N, Inaba T, Maki H, Shiga T, Hatano M, Yao A, Kyo S, Komuro I. Urine osmolality estimated using urine urea nitrogen, sodium and creatinine can effectively predict response to tolvaptan in decompensated heart failure patients. Circ J 2013;77(5):1208-1213. PMID 23318562. The validated estimator carries urine sodium with a coefficient of 2.", "THE COEFFICIENT IS ELECTRONEUTRALITY, NOT A FITTED CONSTANT. Every sodium ion leaving in the urine leaves with an accompanying anion, predominantly chloride, so one mEq of excreted sodium contributes approximately two milliosmoles - one for the cation and one for the anion. That is why the standard urine osmolality estimators all carry urine sodium with a coefficient of 2. WHAT THE CITATION ACTUALLY SUPPLIES. Imamura 2013 validated the whole estimator 1.07*(2*[urine Na] + [urine urea nitrogen]/2.8 + [urine creatinine]*2/3) + 16 against MEASURED urine osmolality in 66 patients and reports r = 0.938, p < 0.001. The paper does not measure this coefficient; it measures that a formula containing it reproduces the real thing. That is the honest description of the evidence and is why this row is tier B rather than A. DECLARED LIMITATION OF THE COHORT: decompensated heart failure patients receiving tolvaptan, i.e. a diseased population under a V2 antagonist. Directive 1.7 test - had the correlation come out poorly, the conclusion would have been that estimated urine osmolality cannot predict tolvaptan response, so the composition relation is the INSTRUMENT there and not the subject. It is accepted anyway because the coefficient is not an empirical finding of that paper but a charge-balance requirement, and the validation is corroboration that the requirement holds in real urine. INDEPENDENT CORROBORATION OF THE STRUCTURE, not pooled with it: Alwis US, Delanghe J, Dossche L, Walle JV, Van Camp J, Monaghan TF, Roggeman S, Everaert K. Could evening dietary protein intake play a role in nocturnal polyuria? J Clin Med 2020;9(8):2532. PMID 32764521. 170 subjects, 24 h collections in 8 timed samples with volume, osmolality, creatinine, urea and sodium all measured; reports urea as the largest single contributor to urine osmolality with sodium excretion the other major term. That is the two-part structure this row and RN.URINE.SOLUTE_NONNA implement. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13."),
    :RN_URINE_SOLUTE_LOAD => Provenance("RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD", "mOsm/day", 930.0, "B", "reported", "Kitada K, Daub S, Zhang Y, Klein JD, Nakano D, Pedchenko T, Lantier L, LaRocque LM, Marton A, Neubert P, Schroeder A, Rakova N, Jantsch J, Dikalova AE, Dikalov SI, Harrison DG, Mueller DN, Nishiyama A, Rauh M, Harris RC, Luft FC, Wassermann DH, Sands JM, Titze J. High salt intake reprioritizes osmolyte and energy metabolism for body fluid conservation. J Clin Invest 2017;127(5):1944-1959. doi:10.1172/JCI88532. PMID 28414295. PMC5409074. OPEN ACCESS, FULL TEXT READ. Table 1, human balance study, 12 g/day salt arm: 10 healthy men, 739 complete 24 h collections, urine osmolality 508 +/- 170 mOsm/kg and urine volume 22 +/- 7 mL/kg/day. Companion paper Rakova N et al, J Clin Invest 2017;127(5):1932-1943, PMID 28414302, FULL TEXT AND SUPPLEMENT READ, supplies the protocol (12 g/d = 200 mmol Na/d) and Supplemental Table S1, the anthropometrics (81.5 +/- 10.0 and 84.2 +/- 7.8 kg in the 12 g/d arms, BSA 2.0 m2, age about 33).", "ASSUMED and NOT SOURCED, and it is the load-bearing assumption of the ADH component - urine volume is this divided by urine osmolality, so it scales the whole water-excretion limb. 600 mOsm/day is a conventional adult figure. It is HELD CONSTANT, which is itself a modelling choice: in reality it tracks protein and salt intake, so a salt step should move it and here it does not. That omission makes the model under-respond to a salt load on the water side. Source it, and consider making it depend on Na_excr, before trusting any water result under a changed diet. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13. || REFERENCE POINT CORRECTED 2026-09-16, urine_solute_prereg.md Half A, AND THE VALUE MOVED WITHOUT ANY SOURCE BEING OPENED. RN.URINE.SOLUTE_NONNA was pinned so the total returned 600 at the MID salt arm, 154 mEq/day, while the model's reference is the NOMINAL arm, 205. So the load the model actually carried at its own operating point was 292 + 2*205 = 702 and never 600. Three constants - this one, ADH.URINE.OSM_BASELINE and ADH.OSM.SENSITIVITY - were all derived from a load the model never occupied, and k_adh is LIVE. The loop paid for it with a permanent 0.59 mOsm/kg offset above the plasma osmolality setpoint: it had to reach u_osm = 702/1.7 = 412.9, hence adh 0.3894, hence Osm_ecf 287.594. Measured in the running model before the fix: 0.3895 and 287.592. The mid-arm pin was change management when solute tracking landed - it kept the salt step bit-identical - and it then became the reference point for three derived constants. THIS ROW IS NOW THE LOAD AT THE REFERENCE and is derived from the residual, which is the primitive until it is sourced. It is still tier C and still rests on an unsourced 292: Half A fixes WHERE the constant is stated, not WHAT it is. || SOURCED 2026-09-16 UNDER urine_solute_prereg.md BRANCH U1, replacing an `assumed` conventional 600 mOsm/day that carried no citation at all. || THE VALUE: 508 mOsm/kg x 22 mL/kg/day x 83 kg = 928, entered at TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES as 930 because the volume and the body weight carry two. || TABLE 1 WAS READ AS AN IMAGE. It is published as a figure rather than as HTML, and an automated text extraction of it returned numbers that did not self-consistently add up - a transposed mouse column. The values were read off the rendered table and then checked against themselves: 2Na + 2K + urea = 10.9 against a reported U2Na2KUreaV of 10.8 mmol/kg/day, and osmolality x volume exceeds that sum by 3.4 percent, which is the unmeasured ammonium, phosphate, sulfate and creatinine. || THE BODY WEIGHT IS PINNED TWO INDEPENDENT WAYS and that is what makes the per-kg units trustworthy: the 12 g/day arm is 200 mmol/day of dietary sodium against a measured UNaV of 2.3 mmol/kg/day, which at the usual 95 percent urinary recovery implies 83 kg; Supplemental Table S1 reports 81.5 and 84.2 kg for the two 12 g/day arms. || THE COHERENCE CONSTRAINT OF THE PRE-REGISTRATION SECTION 3 IS SATISFIED, AND THAT WAS THE POINT OF FIXING IT IN ADVANCE. The urine solute load is a DIET, not a physiological constant, so it had to come from a cohort at this model's own salt intake or the mismatch would land silently in the residual. Rakova's 12 g/day arm is 200 mmol Na/day against BF.NA.INTAKE_NOMINAL = 205, and it is the SAME COHORT BF.NA.INTAKE_MID is already derived from - section 3.24's same-cohort rule satisfied by construction rather than by luck. || THE LEDGER PREDICTED THE ANSWER. RN.URINE.SOLUTE_NONNA's note already said the non-sodium remainder was 'almost certainly TOO LOW' and should be 'nearer 400-500'. Measured: 546 at the cohort, 520 at this model's reference sodium intake. || THE SD IS DAY-TO-DAY AND BETWEEN-SUBJECT SPREAD ACROSS 739 COLLECTIONS, NOT UNCERTAINTY ON A MEAN, and it is taken from U2Na2KUreaV (10.8 +/- 2.3, i.e. 21 percent) rather than from the osmolality and volume SDs, which are large and strongly anti-correlated so their product is far tighter than either factor. Directive 1.9: saying which statistic this is, is the point. || MEN ONLY, n = 10, mean age 33, in an enclosed habitat on a fully controlled diet. A controlled diet is exactly the preparation this row needs - directive 1.7, since the alternative literature is kidney-stone risk profiling and hydration-intervention trials where the excretion is the instrument - but it is not a free-living Western diet and the row is not a population mean."),
    :RN_URINE_SOLUTE_NONNA => Provenance("RN.URINE.SOLUTE_NONNA", "mOsm/day", 520.0, "B", "derived", "Derived from RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD, RN.URINE.OSM_PER_NA and BF.NA.INTAKE_NOMINAL.", "DERIVED, and it is a RESIDUAL rather than a measurement: SOLUTE_NONNA = RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD - RN.URINE.OSM_PER_NA * BF.NA.INTAKE_MID = 600.0 - 2.0 * 154.0 = 292.0 mOsm/day. It stands for urea plus potassium salts plus everything else, all held constant. WHY IT IS DEFINED THIS WAY. RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD is load-bearing: ADH.URINE.OSM_MAX, ADH.URINE.OSM_BASELINE and ADH.OSM.SENSITIVITY are all derived from it and four closure checks depend on it. Making the solute load track sodium must therefore leave the REFERENCE load untouched, or every derived ADH constant and every steady state moves. Pinning the residual so that total load returns exactly 600.0 at the mid salt arm (154 mEq/day) achieves that: the mid arm is unchanged to the last digit and only the high and low arms move. INHERITED DEBT, STATED PLAINLY: 292 mOsm/day is almost certainly TOO LOW for the non-sodium fraction. Urea alone is normally the largest single contributor to urine osmolality (Alwis 2020) and potassium salts add roughly another 100-120 mOsm/day, so a real non-sodium remainder on a Western diet is nearer 400-500. The residual is small because the parent RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD = 600 mOsm/day is itself an unsourced conventional figure that is low for a 154 mEq/day sodium intake - typical measured totals are 700-900. THIS ROW DOES NOT FIX THAT AND MUST NOT BE READ AS FIXING IT. Correcting the total would move U_max, U_base, k_adh and every steady state, and needs its own pre-registered extraction. What this row does is make the load RESPOND to salt, which it previously did not do at all. Tier C, inherited from the parent. || ROUNDED 2026-09-09 TO MEASUREMENT PRECISION at the owner's instruction. Nothing physiological in this model is measured to more than four significant figures, and trailing zeros on a whole number read as precision that is not there. The discarded digits were floating-point residue from the derivation, not information. Directive 1.13. || RE-DERIVED 2026-09-16 AGAINST THE SOURCED TOTAL, AND THE PIN MOVED FROM THE MID ARM TO THE NOMINAL ARM. 930 - 2*205 = 520 mOsm/day. It was 292, pinned so the total returned 600 at 154 mEq/day while the model runs at 205 - see the Half A note on RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD. || THE INHERITED DEBT THIS ROW DECLARED IS NOW DISCHARGED. Its own text said 292 was 'almost certainly TOO LOW', that urea is normally the largest single contributor and potassium salts add another 100-120 mOsm/day, and that a real remainder is 'nearer 400-500'. Kitada measures urea at 4.1 and potassium at 1.1 mmol/kg/day, which at 83 kg is 340 mOsm/day of urea and 182 of potassium salts. || IT IS STILL A RESIDUAL AND IT IS STILL HELD CONSTANT, AND THE SECOND HALF OF THAT IS NOW MEASURABLY WRONG. In the source the non-sodium remainder FALLS from 7.08 to 6.58 mOsm/kg/day as salt goes 6 -> 12 g/day, because urine urea concentration falls 13.9 percent - the natriuretic-ureotelic pattern the paper is about. So the true slope of total osmolar excretion against excreted sodium is about 1.5 mOsm/mEq, where this model uses 2.0 and holds the rest fixed. ACROSS ITS OWN SALT ARMS THE MODEL THEREFORE OVER-RESPONDS ON THE SOLUTE LIMB BY ROUGHLY 30 PERCENT: 204 mOsm/day of swing from 205 to 103 mEq/day where the data imply about 157. Not built, because the dependence would need a urea-recycling or glucocorticoid mechanism with no sourced form and this pass pre-registered none. It is now a NUMBER rather than an unknown."),
    :RN_URINE_SOLUTE_NONNA_SLOPE => Provenance("RN.URINE.SOLUTE_NONNA_SLOPE", "mOsm/(kg*mEq)", -0.0049, "B", "derived", "Kitada K, Daub S, Zhang Y, Klein JD, Nakano D, Pedchenko T, Lantier L, LaRocque LM, Marton A, Neubert P, Schroeder A, Rakova N, Jantsch J, Dikalova AE, Dikalov SI, Harrison DG, Mueller DN, Nishiyama A, Rauh M, Harris RC, Luft FC, Wassermann DH, Sands JM, Titze J. High salt intake reprioritizes osmolyte and energy metabolism for body fluid conservation. J Clin Invest 2017;127(5):1944-1959. doi:10.1172/JCI88532. PMID 28414295. PMC5409074. OPEN ACCESS, FULL TEXT READ - it is already the citation on RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD. 10 healthy men, 739 complete 24 h collections. THE NON-SODIUM REMAINDER FALLS FROM 7.08 TO 6.58 mOsm/kg/day as salt intake goes 6 -> 12 g/day NaCl, because urine urea concentration falls 13.9 percent - the natriuretic-ureotelic pattern the paper is about.", "DERIVED FROM TWO REPORTED VALUES. -0.50 mOsm/kg/day across a 102 mmol/day change in salt intake gives -0.004902, entered as -0.0049. TWO SIGNIFICANT FIGURES AND NOT MORE: the inputs are 7.08 and 6.58, three figures each, and their DIFFERENCE of 0.50 carries two. Directive 1.13. || WHAT IT FIXES. RN.URINE.SOLUTE_NONNA's note has recorded since 2026-09-16 that holding the non-sodium remainder CONSTANT makes this model over-respond on the solute limb by roughly 30 percent - 204 mOsm/day of swing across its own salt arms where the data imply about 157. That note said the dependence was 'not built, because it would need a urea-recycling or glucocorticoid mechanism with no sourced form'. THIS ROW DOES NOT BUILD THAT MECHANISM. It is the measured empirical SLOPE, which is the shape RN.GFR.VOLUME_SENSITIVITY already has. || THE MECHANISM IS UNSOURCED AND THAT IS NOT A FORMALITY. Kitada attributes the fall to urea recycling under glucocorticoid control; this row asserts only that the remainder falls with salt intake at the measured rate, across the 6-12 g/day range the source tested. OUTSIDE THAT RANGE IT IS AN EXTRAPOLATION, and the model's own arms - 103 to 205 mmol/day - sit inside it by construction, because they ARE 6 and 12 g/day. || EXTENSIVE, PER KILOGRAM, because urea production tracks lean mass, exactly as RN.URINE.SOLUTE_NONNA already scales. At the 70 kg reference this is -0.343 mOsm/day per mEq/day, so the NET slope of total osmolar excretion against sodium becomes 2.0 - 0.343 = 1.66 against the earlier note's rough estimate of 1.5. || IT IS ZERO AT THE REFERENCE BY CONSTRUCTION. The term is k*(Na_excr - Na_excr_ref), so RN.URINE.SOLUTE_LOAD returns exactly 930 mOsm/day at nominal intake and every ADH constant derived from that total is untouched. validation/solute_limb_prereg.md branch U2: if any of them moves, the TERM is written wrongly and the term gets fixed, not the constants. || RN.URINE.OSM_PER_NA STAYS 2.0. That is charge balance - sodium and its anion - and it is sourced. Folding this slope into it would make the arithmetic come out and destroy the meaning of a sourced coefficient. Failure mode 11."),
    :RN_VOLUME_NATRIURESIS_TAU => Provenance("RN.VOLUME_NATRIURESIS.TAU", "day", 0.15, "C", "derived", "Drummer C, Heer M, Baisch F, Blomqvist CG, Lang RE, Maass H, Gerzer R. Diuresis and natriuresis following isotonic saline infusion in healthy young volunteers before, during, and after HDT. Acta Physiol Scand Suppl 1992;604:101-11. PMID 1324562. 6 healthy men, 0.9% saline 22 mL/kg over 20 min: sodium excretion rose 3-fold, LESS THAN 15% of the infused sodium was excreted acutely, and urine flow and sodium excretion remained significantly elevated for MORE THAN 48 HOURS. The paper's own conclusion is that excretion of an acute isotonic load REQUIRES SEVERAL DAYS. That is the qualitative evidence that the volume-natriuresis relation is lagged rather than instantaneous.", "RE-ESTIMATED 2026-09-03, 0.50 -> 0.15 d, with CV.ANP.NATRIURETIC_GAIN, against the corrected G_pn = 8.4 and the sourced G_vr = 1400. Identified from LOBO's two 6 h endpoints, which agree independently: matching the 563 mL urine gives 0.171 d and matching the 95 mmol sodium gives 0.166 d. Entered as 0.15, which reproduces both to within 0.5 percent - 566 mL and 95.1 mmol. || IT MOVED BY A FACTOR OF 3.3 AND THE REASON IS NOT ANP. The pressure slope fell 20 -> 8.4 over the same period, so the pressure path now contributes far less to the acute natriuresis and the volume path has to respond faster to reproduce the same 6 h excretion. THIS PARAMETER IS NOT IDENTIFIED BY ANP PHYSIOLOGY; it is identified by requiring the model to match one acute human dataset given everything else, and it will move again if anything upstream of it does. Tier C, and the tier is doing work. || THE DRUMMER CORROBORATION IS WEAKER THAN THE PREVIOUS NOTE CLAIMED, and that is corrected here rather than left standing. Drummer 1992 (PMID 1324562) says excretion of an acute isotonic load takes SEVERAL DAYS and that sodium excretion is elevated beyond 48 h. At 0.15 d this lag reaches 95 percent of its steady state in about 11 h, which is hours rather than days. The reconciliation is that Drummer describes the time course of EXCRETING THE LOAD, which in this model is set by how slowly the volume itself decays, not by this lag. Drummer still supports the EXISTENCE of a lag - the algebraic form was refuted without one - but it does not constrain its VALUE, and the previous note overstated that. || WHAT WOULD FALSIFY IT: a human isotonic-loading study reporting the full cumulative sodium excretion curve out to 72 h. Drummer measured one; the abstract does not give the series and the full text would settle this row. || THIS ROW'S FALSIFICATION CLAUSE IS DISCHARGED, AND THE ANSWER IS THAT THE ROW IS NOT THE PROBLEM. 2026-09-17, HANDOVER section 3.46, validation/late_time_course_prereg.md branch L2. The clause asked for 'a human isotonic-loading study reporting the full cumulative sodium excretion curve out to 72 h'. FOUND: Drummer C, Gerzer R, Heer M, Molz B, Bie P, Schlossberger M, Stadaeger C, Roecker L, Strollo F, Heyduck B, et al. Effects of an acute saline infusion on fluid and electrolyte metabolism in humans. Am J Physiol 1992;262(5 Pt 2):F744-54. doi:10.1152/ajprenal.1992.262.5.F744. PMID 1590419. ABSTRACT READ IN FULL; the full text was not obtained, so the interval SERIES is still not in hand and nothing depends on one. Six healthy volunteers, SUPINE, strictly controlled, nine days, 2 L of isotonic saline in 25 min, 48 h of collections AND a 48 h control experiment. || IT IS A DIFFERENT PAPER FROM THE DRUMMER 1992 IN THIS ROW'S CITATION COLUMN. That one is PMID 1324562, the same group's head-down-tilt study, and HDT is its experimental variable. This one has no tilt, which makes it cleanly admissible where the other is admissible only in its control arm. || WHAT IT MEASURES: elevated body weight returned to baseline with an APPROXIMATE HALF-LIFE OF 7 HOURS, and the largest increase in fluid and electrolyte excretion between 3 and 22 h postinfusion. THE MODEL GIVES 13.10 h for the volume excursion and 5.9 h for the peak - so it passes the timing of the peak and FAILS the decay by a factor of 1.9. || AND THE FAILURE IS NOT THIS ROW'S. Swept in bench/late_time_course.jl from 1.0 d down to 0.001 d - effectively instantaneous - the half-life bottoms out at 11.97 h. TWELVE HOURS IS A FLOOR NO VALUE OF THIS LAG CAN PASS, and the measurement is 7. That is not evidence that 0.15 d is the wrong value; it is evidence that THE LAG IS NOT WHAT SETS THIS QUANTITY, which is what this row's own note already said about Drummer's timescale. The tail is set by the natriuretic GAINS, and no single one of them reaches 7 h without taking the chronic salt sensitivity below the human 1.70-2.30. || SO THE ROW IS UNCHANGED AND STILL IDENTIFIED BY LOBO ALONE. What the search bought is not a value but a DISQUALIFICATION: the late time course cannot constrain this lag, so the dimension in which it is unidentified is narrower than it looked. What would still constrain it is the SHAPE inside the first 12 hours, which needs Drummer's interval series and therefore its full text. || THE FULL TEXT WAS PURSUED ON 2026-09-17 AND IT IS CLOSED. EVERY ROUTE TRIED, RECORDED SO NOBODY REPEATS THE SEARCH. Drummer 1992, Am J Physiol 262(5 Pt 2):F744-54, PMID 1590419, doi 10.1152/ajprenal.1992.262.5.F744. (a) Europe PMC: isOpenAccess=N, inEPMC=N, inPMC=N, hasPDF=N, single full-text link marked 'Subscription required'. (b) OpenAlex: oa_status CLOSED, best_oa_location null, FOUR locations and none open - the publisher, PubMed, the DLR electronic library and a University of Copenhagen research profile. (c) journals.physiology.org returns HTTP 403. (d) THE AUTHORS' OWN INSTITUTIONAL REPOSITORY, elib.dlr.de/27251, holds a metadata record that says in terms 'Dieses Archiv kann nicht den Volltext zur Verfuegung stellen' - this archive cannot provide the full text. || SO THE INTERVAL SERIES THIS ROW ASKED FOR IS NOT OBTAINABLE without institutional access, and the row stays where HANDOVER section 3.46 left it: identified by Lobo's two 6 h cumulative endpoints, with the late time course constraining the GAINS and not this lag. The falsification clause is NOT discharged - it is BLOCKED, which is a different thing and is recorded as such. Same standing as RESP.CO2.ARTERIAL_RESTING's Crapo 1999. || AND THE COMPANION PAPER IS CLOSED TOO: PMID 1324562, the head-down-tilt study, isOpenAccess=N. So is Luft FC et al, Am J Kidney Dis 1983;2(4):464-70, PMID 6823962, oa_status CLOSED - which would otherwise have been the sharpest instrument available, because it infused 2 L of saline over 2 h into normal men at FOUR prior sodium intakes (10, 300, 600 and 800 mEq/day) and reports that the natriuresis DEPENDS ON PRIOR INTAKE, with fractional sodium excretion of 6-7 percent maximal at 600 mEq/day. That is the acute response measured at four chronic operating points and it would bear directly on the conflict in section 3.47. ABSTRACT ONLY. || THE FULL TEXT ARRIVED 2026-09-17 AND IT CHANGES WHAT THIS ROW'S FAILURE MEANS. HANDOVER section 3.49, bench/drummer_fulltext.jl. The owner supplied Drummer 1992 (PMID 1590419) from institutional access after the search recorded above came back closed. THE ABSTRACT CARRIED ONE HALF-LIFE AND THE PAPER CARRIES TWO: 'a half-life of ~7 h for returning to baseline body weights was found. The respective half-life for reachieving SODIUM BALANCE WAS 10 h.' || MEASURED: the model's VOLUME excursion half-life is 13.33 h against 7.0, which is 1.90x too slow, but its SODIUM excursion half-life is 12.52 h against 10.0, only 1.25x. The interval series agrees far better than the half-lives suggest - over the bulk 3-22 h window the model excretes 1429 mL against 1322 and 220.6 mmol against 261.0. THE SODIUM LIMB IS ROUGHLY RIGHT AND THE WATER LIMB IS NOT. || SO SECTION 3.46's THREE-FOLD GAIN REQUIREMENT WAS A WATER PROBLEM PUSHED THROUGH A SODIUM LEVER, and section 3.47's conclusion that Drummer and Jensen disagree by twofold IS WITHDRAWN. Jensen measures FRACTIONAL SODIUM EXCRETION and the model is 1.25x off on sodium; the two agree. || THIS ROW IS STILL NOT THE PROBLEM AND THAT PART STANDS. The lag floors at 11.97 h when made instantaneous. What the full text adds is that the quantity to fix is not the natriuretic gain either: Drummer's weight returns FASTER than his sodium, a half-life ratio of 0.70 against the model's 1.065, and in this model extracellular volume is tied to extracellular sodium so water cannot leave ahead of salt. That is ADR 0004's compartment, which is PROVISIONAL and switched off. || PROTOCOL CORRECTIONS: the dose is 30 mL/kg and not the '2 litres' the abstract rounds to, 2.1 L at 70 kg; the subjects were SUPINE throughout and ate and drank nothing from 06:30 to noon, so the first three post-infusion hours are a fasting measurement. || RENAMED 2026-09-18, RN.ANP.TAU -> RN.VOLUME_NATRIURESIS.TAU, anp_label_prereg.md. NO NUMBER MOVED AND THE MODEL OUTPUT IS BIT-IDENTICAL - that was the pass's only test. FAILURE MODE #11: a name carrying a convention its value contradicts. The `name` field already read 'Volume-keyed natriuretic gain' and ADR 0010's own Decision already called it 'a lumped volume-keyed natriuretic term, algebraic in V_blood, with ANP as its named evidence base rather than as a state'. THE param_id DID NOT SAY THAT, and the param_id is what appears in code, in LedgerParams.jl and in the GUI. || AND 'ANP AS ITS NAMED EVIDENCE BASE' WAS ITSELF WRONG. This gain was estimated from WHOLE-BODY ISOTONIC EXPANSION - Lobo 2001 and Jensen 2013 - which measures the TOTAL natriuretic response to a volume load. ANP was the mechanism assumed to carry that total, not the thing measured. HANDOVER section 3.57 supplied the counter-evidence: Lohmeier's split-bladder dogs give a denervated/innervated sodium ratio near 0.56 at sustained elevated pressure, so RENAL SYMPATHETIC WITHDRAWAL carries a substantial share of exactly the response this gain was fitted to. The gain is not wrong. The attribution was. || WHAT THIS LUMP CONTAINS, NAMED AND NOT QUANTIFIED: atrial natriuretic peptide; renal sympathetic withdrawal via cardiopulmonary volume receptors; and whatever else volume expansion does to tubular sodium handling that neither of those covers. THE SHARE IS DELIBERATELY NOT SPLIT. Lohmeier's ratio implies a nerve share near 47 percent, but the authors report a latent impairment in sodium excretion from the denervated kidneys, making that an UPPER BOUND rather than an estimate; the protocol is ANG II hypertension and not a salt step; n = 5 and abstract only. The supportable statement is 'between zero and about a half, biased high', and a parameter with that interval buys nothing. Directive 1.14. || THE TRAP THIS RENAME EXISTS TO PREVENT: a future ANP infusion dose-response MUST NOT be added on top of this gain. This gain ALREADY CONTAINS ANP's contribution, along with the neural one. Sourcing an ANP-specific gain would require SPLITTING this row, not supplementing it. || OLD IDENTIFIER RN.ANP.TAU IS RETAINED IN THE NOTE TEXT ABOVE AND THROUGHOUT THIS LEDGER'S HISTORY, which is append-only and was not rewritten - searching the old name still finds the record."),
    :THY_FT4_EUTHYROID => Provenance("THY.FT4.EUTHYROID", "pmol/L", 10.16, "A", "reported", "National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey 2007-2008, 2009-2010 and 2011-2012, public microdata, files THYROD/DEMO/RXQ_RX/MCQ from https://wwwn.cdc.gov/Nchs/Data/Nhanes/Public/. EXTRACTED HERE, not read out of a paper: validation/nhanes_hpt_extract.py, pre-registered in validation/nhanes_hpt_prereg.md before any relationship was computed. REFERENCE POPULATION n = 6814: adults 20+, not pregnant, no reported thyroid problem (MCQ160M), no thyroid-active prescription (levothyroxine, liothyronine, thyroid, methimazole, propylthiouracil, amiodarone, lithium), thyroid peroxidase antibody < 9 IU/mL and thyroglobulin antibody < 4 IU/mL. NO EXCLUSION ON THE THYROTROPIN VALUE - Hollowell's reference population removes biochemical thyroid dysfunction, which is right for a reference interval and wrong for a regression. Weighted with WTMEC2YR/3 for three pooled cycles; the survey design is NOT used for standard errors, so any interval quoted is the independent-sampling one and is too small. || Weighted mean free thyroxine 10.16 pmol/L, SD 1.77, 2.5th-97.5th percentile 7.70-14.10. Total thyroxine 7.75 ug/dL in the same subjects. || SUPERSEDES Braverman LE et al. J Clin Invest 1973;52(5):1010-7, PMC302354, whose equilibrium-dialysis figure of 16.61 pmol/L this row carried until 2026-09-05. That measurement is not withdrawn and is not wrong; it is on a different scale, and the reason it cannot be used here is on this row.", "1.29 +/- 0.19 ng/100 mL at a molecular weight of 776.87 g/mol is 16.605 +/- 2.446 pmol/L. || THIS IS A MEASURED CONCENTRATION, NOT A REFERENCE INTERVAL, and the distinction is the whole reason the loop's thyrotropin prediction is a prediction. thyroid_prereg.md section 6 forbids using a reference interval to set a parameter; section 2 says to prefer equilibrium dialysis or a source that states its method, which is exactly what this is. See amendment 8.4. || IT SUPPLIES THE ABSOLUTE SCALE THAT A PER-PMOL/L SLOPE NEEDS, and combining a 1973 dialysis measurement with a 2010 immunoassay slope is the largest methodological risk in this subsystem. It is checked rather than argued: Maushart's euthyroid immunoassay visits give 15.0 and 16.6 pmol/L. || SEXED PAIR NOT SUPPORTED. Braverman's control group is 11 subjects and no sex-specific values are given; ADR 0014's 'where only one value is supported, use it for both' applies, and this row is the CV.HEMATOCRIT.NOMINAL failure waiting to happen if free thyroxine turns out to be dimorphic. || CUT TO FOUR SIGNIFICANT FIGURES ON 2026-09-05, from 16.605. The source reports 1.29 +/- 0.19 ng/100 mL, three figures; converting a unit does not create a fourth. || WHY THIS ROW MOVED, AND IT IS A UNIT ERROR RATHER THAN AN IMPRECISE NUMBER. The ledger previously composed a pituitary line measured on one free-thyroxine assay (Benhadi 2010) with a free-thyroxine concentration measured by equilibrium dialysis (Braverman 1973), and predicted a euthyroid thyrotropin 2.2x the measured population value. THE TWO SCALES ARE NOT COMMENSURATE AND THAT IS NOW MEASURED: at essentially identical TOTAL thyroxine - 7.75 ug/dL in NHANES against Braverman's 7.30, agreeing to 6% - the free fraction is 0.0104% by immunoassay against 0.0180% by dialysis, a ratio of 1.73. Two methods agreeing on the total hormone to a few per cent and disagreeing by nearly two-fold on the free fraction are not two measurements of one quantity. thyroid_prereg.md section 2 prohibited POOLING across free-thyroxine assays and should have prohibited COMPOSING across them too. || SEX: NO PAIR IS NEEDED, AND THAT IS NOW A FINDING RATHER THAN AN ABSENCE. Free thyroxine differs by 0.5% between men and women in 6814 adults (10.18 against 10.13 pmol/L) and thyrotropin by 3% (1.534 against 1.486). ADR 0014's 'where only one value is supported, use it for both' was previously invoked because no sexed pair could be found; it is now invoked because a large sample says there is nothing to find."),
    :THY_FT4_GAIN => Provenance("THY.FT4.GAIN", "pmol/L per mIU/L", 6.72, "A", "derived", "DERIVED. G_T = THY.FT4.EUTHYROID / THY.TSH.EUTHYROID = 10.16 / 1.512, both from the NHANES reference population, so this is now a ratio of two quantities measured in the same 6814 people on the same assays rather than a composition across sources.", "THE FORWARD LIMB'S GAIN, AND THE ONLY DERIVED NUMBER IN THE LOOP. It places the equilibrium at the sourced free thyroxine and at the thyrotropin the sourced pituitary line then implies. || IT IS NOT A FREE PARAMETER, WHICH IS THE POINT. Both coordinates it is solved against come from outside the model: the FT4 from equilibrium dialysis in normal subjects, the TSH from arithmetic on a regression measured in a different cohort. Neither is a thyrotropin reference value, so ADR 0019's falsifiable test 2 remains a test. || WHY THE FORWARD LIMB IS DERIVED RATHER THAN SOURCED. The thyroid's response to thyrotropin is measurable in humans only by recombinant TSH, which drives thyrotropin to roughly a hundred times the physiological range; reading a gain off that and extrapolating down to 1-3 mIU/L is the extrapolation error recorded on THY.TSH.INTERCEPT, and it is not committed a second time to save a derivation. || RECOMPUTED BY tools/check_closure.py, not read back. || RECOMPUTED 2026-09-05 from the averaged slope and the rounded free thyroxine: 16.60 / exp(3.454 - 0.1352*16.60) = 4.9517. It moved 1.2% and nothing downstream moved with it, which is what a derived quantity that is doing its job looks like. || RECOMPUTED 2026-09-05 from 4.9517. It is still the forward limb's gain and still the only free number in the loop, but it is no longer solved against a crossing point composed from two scales - it is the measured ratio of the two hormones at the operating point. || RECOMPUTED BY tools/check_closure.py, not read back."),
    :THY_FT4_TAU => Provenance("THY.FT4.TAU", "day", 10.3, "A", "derived", "Braverman LE, Vagenakis A, Downs P, Foster AE, Sterling K, Ingbar SH. Effects of replacement doses of sodium L-thyroxine on the peripheral metabolism of thyroxine and triiodothyronine in man. J Clin Invest 1973;52(5):1010-7. doi:10.1172/JCI107265. PMC302354. OPEN ACCESS, READ IN FULL from the JCI archive. Euthyroid subjects, CONTROL (pre-treatment) values: Table I, n = 11, serum total T4 7.3 +/- 1.0 ug/100 mL, per cent free T4 0.018 +/- 0.001 % by EQUILIBRIUM DIALYSIS, absolute free T4 1.29 +/- 0.19 ng/100 mL; Table II, n = 5, T4 distribution space 8.7 +/- 0.8 L, fractional turnover 9.7 +/- 0.8 %/day, clearance 0.84 +/- 0.09 L/day, disposal 64.2 +/- 9.6 ug/day.", "tau = 1/K with K = 9.7 +/- 0.8 %/day, the fractional turnover rate of T4 measured by 125-I tracer in the five subjects who had kinetics done, control period. tau = 10.309 days, half-life 7.15 days. The range is 1/K at K +/- 1 SD. || DERIVED RATHER THAN REPORTED because the paper reports the fractional rate and the model wants its reciprocal - arithmetic on one measured number, no assumption added. || THIS IS THE JUSTIFICATION FOR THE ONE NEW STATE. ADR 0019 decision 3 permits a slow state only because the slowness IS the physiology: at ten days the axis cannot be represented algebraically on a model that runs 400. Thyrotropin, which turns over in minutes, gets no state at all - see amendment 8.2. || A one-compartment turnover collapses the plasma, interstitial and intracellular pools that Inada 1975 (J Clin Invest 55:1337, PMC301889) resolves into three. That lumping is the reason this model cannot represent the early distribution phase after a thyroxine dose. || CUT TO FOUR SIGNIFICANT FIGURES ON 2026-09-05. K is reported as 9.7 +/- 0.8 %/day; its reciprocal cannot carry more. || PRECISION CORRECTED 2026-09-09 under directive 1.13, second pass. An interval of 9.45-11.17 supports three figures, not four. The first pass rounded this row but stopped one figure short, because the gate then allowed two figures beyond what the interval supports - a tolerance I had given myself rather than the rule. The gate now enforces the rule exactly. Discarded digits were inside the row's own stated interval, so no claim changes."),
    :THY_LOOP_GAIN => Provenance("THY.LOOP_GAIN", "dimensionless", 2.277, "A", "derived", "Benhadi N, Fliers E, Visser TJ, Reitsma JB, Wiersinga WM. Eur J Endocrinol 2010;162(2):323-9. PMID 19926783, ABSTRACT ONLY, n = 21 healthy volunteers under randomised T4 and T3 loading - a WITHIN-SUBJECT PERTURBATION, which is the design a feedback gain needs. Reported log TSH = 1.50 - 0.059 x FT4, base 10 (see validation/thyroid_extract.py section 1). || AND Jostel A, Ryder WDJ, Shalet SM. Clin Endocrinol (Oxf) 2009;71(4):529-34. PMID 19226261, ABSTRACT ONLY, 9519 tests in 4064 patients, 0.1345 per pmol/L in natural-log units, combined with the free-thyroxine mean of the cohort that applies it in Wang Y et al. Front Endocrinol (Lausanne) 2021;12:657114, PMC8129566, OPEN ACCESS, read in full.", "THE ONE THING ABOUT THIS AXIS THAT TRANSFERS BETWEEN ASSAYS. The gain is b*FT4*, a slope in 1/(pmol/L) times a concentration in pmol/L, so it is dimensionless and INVARIANT under a change of free-thyroxine scale - which is exactly what the measured slopes are not. || TWO SCALE-INVARIANT ESTIMATES, AVERAGED. (1) Benhadi's line is internally self-consistent on its own assay, so its implied euthyroid free thyroxine is (3.454 - ln TSH*)/0.13585 whatever that assay reads; at a cohort geometric-mean thyrotropin of 2.0 mIU/L - assumed, and this is the one assumption in the row - that gives 20.3 and a gain of 2.761. Across a plausible cohort mean of 1.5 to 2.5 the gain runs 2.54 to 3.05, so the assumption is worth about 10%. (2) Jostel's 0.1345 with the free-thyroxine mean of the cohort that applies it, 13.33 pmol/L, gives 1.793. Mean 2.277, and the two estimates are the range. || THE MODEL'S BEHAVIOUR IS THIS NUMBER AND ALMOST NOTHING ELSE: d ln FT4 / d ln(secretory capacity) = 1/(1 + G) = 0.305, so the human axis absorbs about 70% of a change in thyroid secretory capacity, and the closed-loop relaxation time is tau/(1+G) = 3.1 days. NOTHING IN THIS REPOSITORY WAS FITTED TO IT and nothing is validated against it. || WHAT WOULD REPLACE THE ASSUMPTION: Benhadi 2010's full text, which reports its cohort's mean thyrotropin. That paper's value has fallen a long way - it once blocked the whole axis, then supplied a slope, and now would tighten one dimensionless number by 10%."),
    :THY_METABOLIC_GAIN => Provenance("THY.METABOLIC_GAIN", "dimensionless", 0.211, "A", "derived", "Maushart CI, Senn JR, Loeliger RC, Siegenthaler J, Bur F, Fischer JGW, Betz MJ. Resting Energy Expenditure and Cold-induced Thermogenesis in Patients With Overt Hyperthyroidism. J Clin Endocrinol Metab 2022;107(2):450-461. doi:10.1210/clinem/dgab706. PMID 34570185. PMC8764338. OPEN ACCESS, READ IN FULL. n = 18 (14 female), each measured hyperthyroid, then euthyroid, then euthyroid at 3 months. Table 1: fT4 36.3 +/- 18.1 -> 15.0 +/- 3.9 -> 16.6 +/- 3.7 pmol/L; REE normalised to lean body mass 42 +/- 6.7 -> 33 +/- 4.4 -> 33 +/- 5.2 kcal/24h/kg LBM.", "(dREE/REE) / (dFT4/FT4) computed on the LEAN-MASS-NORMALISED energy expenditure, because lean mass itself rose 40.8 -> 43.7 kg between the visits and the unnormalised -13% would attribute part of that to thyroid hormone. Against the euthyroid visit: (42-33)/33 / ((36.3-15.0)/15.0) = 0.192. Against the 3-month visit: 0.230. The lower is entered and both are the range. || THE LINK TO CO2 IS TESTED IN THE SAME PAPER RATHER THAN ASSUMED: 'RQ was not significantly affected by thyroid hormone state (P = .19)'. At a fixed respiratory quotient a fractional change in energy expenditure is the same fractional change in CO2 production, which is the only quantity the respiratory component consumes. || THIS ROW BREAKS A PRE-REGISTERED EXCLUSION AND THE BREAKAGE IS RECORDED, NOT HIDDEN. thyroid_prereg.md section 2 excludes thyroid disease of any kind. The exclusion is unsatisfiable for this quantity - a healthy person cannot ethically be made thyrotoxic - which is SOURCES.md's own argument for animal preparations applied to a disease preparation. Amendment 8.3 relaxes it FOR THIS ROW ONLY; every parameter of the feedback loop is sourced inside the original rules. || AND THE ARM IT FEEDS DEFAULTS OFF, per ADR 0019 decision 4, which was written before any of this was known. Switching it on is what makes RESP.CO2.PRODUCTION stop being a primitive, and it moves ventilation and therefore the water balance. || AVERAGED 2026-09-05. The two figures are the same hyperthyroid visit against the study's two euthyroid visits - two estimates of one quantity from one cohort, differing by 20%, with no reason to prefer either. The mean is entered and the pair is the range. Taking the lower one, as this row first did, was picking the conservative-looking number rather than the central one, which is a bias and not a caution."),
    :THY_TSH_EUTHYROID => Provenance("THY.TSH.EUTHYROID", "mIU/L", 1.512, "A", "reported", "National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey 2007-2008, 2009-2010 and 2011-2012, public microdata, files THYROD/DEMO/RXQ_RX/MCQ from https://wwwn.cdc.gov/Nchs/Data/Nhanes/Public/. EXTRACTED HERE, not read out of a paper: validation/nhanes_hpt_extract.py, pre-registered in validation/nhanes_hpt_prereg.md before any relationship was computed. REFERENCE POPULATION n = 6814: adults 20+, not pregnant, no reported thyroid problem (MCQ160M), no thyroid-active prescription (levothyroxine, liothyronine, thyroid, methimazole, propylthiouracil, amiodarone, lithium), thyroid peroxidase antibody < 9 IU/mL and thyroglobulin antibody < 4 IU/mL. NO EXCLUSION ON THE THYROTROPIN VALUE - Hollowell's reference population removes biochemical thyroid dysfunction, which is right for a reference interval and wrong for a regression. Weighted with WTMEC2YR/3 for three pooled cycles; the survey design is NOT used for standard errors, so any interval quoted is the independent-sampling one and is too small. || Weighted GEOMETRIC mean thyrotropin 1.512 mIU/L, geometric SD 1.80, 2.5th-97.5th percentile 0.460-4.484 - which is where the conventional 0.4-4.5 reference interval comes from, now measured here rather than quoted as a round number (directive 1.12). Geometric because thyrotropin is log-normal, which is the same fact that makes the feedback relation log-linear. || REPLICATED ACROSS TWO SURVEYS AND TWO DECADES: Hollowell JG et al. J Clin Endocrinol Metab 2002;87(2):489-99, PMID 11836274, ABSTRACT ONLY, NHANES III 1988-94, reference population n = 13,344, geometric mean 1.40 mIU/L. Different assay, different sample, 1.40 against 1.512.", "THE OPERATING POINT IS NOW AN INPUT, NOT AN OUTPUT, AND ADR 0019's FALSIFIABLE TEST 2 IS VOID. That test asked whether the loop's crossing point lands inside human reference intervals with neither hormone entered as a target. It cannot be a test: a crossing point is a line meeting a concentration, and a slope in 1/(pmol/L) and a concentration in pmol/L are only composable on a COMMON free-thyroxine scale - which free-thyroxine immunoassays and equilibrium dialysis do not share. || WHY THIS ROW MOVED, AND IT IS A UNIT ERROR RATHER THAN AN IMPRECISE NUMBER. The ledger previously composed a pituitary line measured on one free-thyroxine assay (Benhadi 2010) with a free-thyroxine concentration measured by equilibrium dialysis (Braverman 1973), and predicted a euthyroid thyrotropin 2.2x the measured population value. THE TWO SCALES ARE NOT COMMENSURATE AND THAT IS NOW MEASURED: at essentially identical TOTAL thyroxine - 7.75 ug/dL in NHANES against Braverman's 7.30, agreeing to 6% - the free fraction is 0.0104% by immunoassay against 0.0180% by dialysis, a ratio of 1.73. Two methods agreeing on the total hormone to a few per cent and disagreeing by nearly two-fold on the free fraction are not two measurements of one quantity. thyroid_prereg.md section 2 prohibited POOLING across free-thyroxine assays and should have prohibited COMPOSING across them too. || SO THE DEPENDENCY IS INVERTED, exactly as ADR 0017 inverted it for arterial PCO2 when the chemoreflex turned out to sit below its own threshold at rest. The operating point is sourced and the RESPONSE is what the model claims. || WHAT SURVIVES AS A REAL TEST: ADR 0019's falsifiable tests 1, 3 and 4 - thyrotropin must fall when secretory capacity rises and free thyroxine must rise by less than open-loop; the state must take weeks; the disabled arm must change nothing - and the dimensionless loop gain, which nothing here was fitted to."),
    :THY_TSH_FT4_SLOPE => Provenance("THY.TSH.FT4_SLOPE", "1/(pmol/L)", 0.2241, "A", "derived", "DERIVED, and it had to become derived. b = THY.LOOP_GAIN / THY.FT4.EUTHYROID = 2.277 / 10.16, because a slope in units of 1/(pmol/L) is SCALE-DEPENDENT and the model's free thyroxine is now on the NHANES immunoassay scale. The measured slopes it comes from are on other scales and cannot be transferred directly. Sources behind the loop gain: Benhadi N, Fliers E, Visser TJ, Reitsma JB, Wiersinga WM. Eur J Endocrinol 2010;162(2):323-9. PMID 19926783, ABSTRACT ONLY, n = 21 healthy volunteers under randomised T4 and T3 loading - a WITHIN-SUBJECT PERTURBATION, which is the design a feedback gain needs. Reported log TSH = 1.50 - 0.059 x FT4, base 10 (see validation/thyroid_extract.py section 1). || AND Jostel A, Ryder WDJ, Shalet SM. Clin Endocrinol (Oxf) 2009;71(4):529-34. PMID 19226261, ABSTRACT ONLY, 9519 tests in 4064 patients, 0.1345 per pmol/L in natural-log units, combined with the free-thyroxine mean of the cohort that applies it in Wang Y et al. Front Endocrinol (Lausanne) 2021;12:657114, PMC8129566, OPEN ACCESS, read in full.", "THE FEEDBACK GAIN OF THE HUMAN THYROID AXIS, in natural-log units per pmol/L of free thyroxine. Benhadi's 0.059 read as base 10 is 0.059*ln(10) = 0.13585; Jostel's is 0.1345. THE TWO AGREE TO 1% AND ONLY UNDER THAT PAIRING - read Benhadi as ln and the two competent measurements of the same gain differ 2.3-fold, in cohorts of comparable FT4 range, which is not credible. || THE VALUE ENTERED IS BENHADI'S because that cohort is admissible under thyroid_prereg.md section 2 on every criterion and the design is an intervention; Jostel's population is a hypopituitary referral series and its purpose was to define a diagnostic index, which is directive 1.7's flag. It is the corroboration, not the value. NOT POOLED - different assays, and pooling.md forbids pooling across incompatible methods. The uncertainty range is the two estimates, not a confidence interval. || THIS ROW DISCHARGES BRANCH T3, which an earlier extraction pass took correctly on the evidence then available. || VALUE CHANGED 2026-09-05, AND THE CHANGE IS THE POINT. This row first entered Benhadi's 0.13585 alone and called Jostel's 0.1345 'corroboration, not the value', on the grounds that pooling.md forbids pooling across assays. THE TWO NUMBERS DIFFER BY 1%. Choosing between measurements that agree to within a fifth of any plausible experimental error is not rigour, it is a coin toss dressed as a decision - directive 1.9 and HANDOVER section 3.23, both of which this repository already had. The entered value is now the MEAN of the two, 0.1352, and the uncertainty is their spread. || UNWEIGHTED, because neither source reports a standard error and the weights cannot change a 1% interval by anything that matters. Method: two estimates, arithmetic mean; extraction_method is therefore derived rather than reported, since no paper prints 0.1352. || WHAT THE SPREAD IS WORTH, and it is the useful consequence: carrying both ends through the loop moves the predicted euthyroid thyrotropin from 3.32 to 3.39 mIU/L, about 2%. So the slope is NOT where the model's 2.4x thyrotropin discrepancy comes from, and that is now arithmetic rather than assertion. || WHY THIS ROW MOVED, AND IT IS A UNIT ERROR RATHER THAN AN IMPRECISE NUMBER. The ledger previously composed a pituitary line measured on one free-thyroxine assay (Benhadi 2010) with a free-thyroxine concentration measured by equilibrium dialysis (Braverman 1973), and predicted a euthyroid thyrotropin 2.2x the measured population value. THE TWO SCALES ARE NOT COMMENSURATE AND THAT IS NOW MEASURED: at essentially identical TOTAL thyroxine - 7.75 ug/dL in NHANES against Braverman's 7.30, agreeing to 6% - the free fraction is 0.0104% by immunoassay against 0.0180% by dialysis, a ratio of 1.73. Two methods agreeing on the total hormone to a few per cent and disagreeing by nearly two-fold on the free fraction are not two measurements of one quantity. thyroid_prereg.md section 2 prohibited POOLING across free-thyroxine assays and should have prohibited COMPOSING across them too. || THE PREVIOUS VALUE, 0.1352, WAS THE MEAN OF TWO MEASURED SLOPES ON TWO OTHER ASSAYS, and entering it against a third assay's concentration is what produced the 2.2x error. The dimensionless product b*FT4* is scale-invariant and is what actually transfers; it is now its own row. || The range is THY.LOOP_GAIN's range divided by the same concentration."),
    :THY_TSH_INTERCEPT => Provenance("THY.TSH.INTERCEPT", "ln(mIU/L)", 2.69, "A", "derived", "DERIVED. a = ln(THY.TSH.EUTHYROID) + THY.LOOP_GAIN = ln(1.512) + 2.277. Sources are those two rows; nothing new is sourced here.", "THE LOAD-BEARING WEAKNESS OF THIS AXIS, AND IT IS SAID HERE RATHER THAN DISCOVERED LATER. The slope has two independent estimates agreeing to 1% and the euthyroid FT4 has two agreeing to within a standard deviation; THIS NUMBER HAS ONE SOURCE AND IT CARRIES THE WHOLE OF THE MODEL'S DISCREPANCY IN THYROTROPIN. Reconstructing it from independent euthyroid pairs at the agreed slope gives 2.72 (Maushart euthyroid visit), 2.82 (Maushart 3-month) and 2.59 (NHANES III geometric mean TSH at the sourced FT4) - all 0.6 to 0.9 log units below 3.454, a factor of 1.9-2.4 in TSH. || WHY IT WOULD BE HIGH, AND THIS IS THE THIRD INSTANCE OF THE SAME PATTERN IN THIS REPOSITORY: an intercept is a line extrapolated to FT4 = 0 from data that never approached zero. The same extrapolation forced the ADR 0017 amendment - a chemoreflex line extrapolated below its measured range put ventilation at 19.3 L/min against a real 6.2 - and produced HANDOVER section 3.22's censoring bound. Benhadi's cohort is also mean age 60 and NHANES III reports TSH rising with age. || IT IS NOT REPLACED BY ANY OF THE RECONSTRUCTIONS. Every one of them is built from a measured euthyroid TSH, which is the quantity ADR 0019's falsifiable test 2 judges the loop against; fitting to it would be HANDOVER section 3.15's error committed deliberately. The model reports a euthyroid TSH 2.4x the NHANES III geometric mean and nothing downstream consumes TSH. || CUT FROM 3.453878 TO 3.454 ON 2026-09-05. Seven significant figures on a number whose source reports three ('1.50') is exactly the false precision directive 1.9 exists to stop. || AND THE ARITHMETIC THAT CONVICTS THIS ROW RATHER THAN THE SLOPE: sweeping the slope across its full two-source spread moves the predicted euthyroid thyrotropin by 2%, against a discrepancy of 2.4x. Nothing about the slope's uncertainty reaches that. The intercept does, because slope and intercept in a regression over a narrow range are strongly anti-correlated and the intercept is the extrapolated one - Benhadi's free-thyroxine range is a euthyroid baseline plus a thyroxine load, and the intercept lies about 17 pmol/L outside it. THE ABSTRACT REPORTS NO STANDARD ERROR FOR IT, so its variance cannot be propagated and the honest statement is that this coefficient is not determined to better than roughly a factor of two by the study that reports it. || THIS ROW WAS THE WHOLE PROBLEM AND IS NO LONGER SOURCED DIRECTLY. It carried Benhadi's 3.454 - one abstract, no standard error, an extrapolation to zero free thyroxine from data that never went near zero - and the model inherited a euthyroid thyrotropin 2.2x too high. NHANES settled what was actually wrong: not the coefficient's precision but the SCALE it was composed against. || WHY THIS ROW MOVED, AND IT IS A UNIT ERROR RATHER THAN AN IMPRECISE NUMBER. The ledger previously composed a pituitary line measured on one free-thyroxine assay (Benhadi 2010) with a free-thyroxine concentration measured by equilibrium dialysis (Braverman 1973), and predicted a euthyroid thyrotropin 2.2x the measured population value. THE TWO SCALES ARE NOT COMMENSURATE AND THAT IS NOW MEASURED: at essentially identical TOTAL thyroxine - 7.75 ug/dL in NHANES against Braverman's 7.30, agreeing to 6% - the free fraction is 0.0104% by immunoassay against 0.0180% by dialysis, a ratio of 1.73. Two methods agreeing on the total hormone to a few per cent and disagreeing by nearly two-fold on the free fraction are not two measurements of one quantity. thyroid_prereg.md section 2 prohibited POOLING across free-thyroxine assays and should have prohibited COMPOSING across them too. || IT IS NOW DERIVED FROM THE OPERATING POINT RATHER THAN THE OTHER WAY ROUND, which is the same dependency inversion ADR 0017 made for arterial PCO2 and for the same reason - derive from the quantity that is actually measured. The consequence is stated plainly on THY.TSH.EUTHYROID: the euthyroid thyrotropin is now an INPUT and ADR 0019's falsifiable test 2 is VOID."),
)

"""
    provenance(sym::Symbol)

Return the ledger record backing a parameter. Every number in a published
result should be traceable through this.
"""
provenance(sym::Symbol) = PARAM_PROVENANCE[sym]

"""
    unledgered_check()

Report parameters whose basis is weak: `assumed` (no literature basis) or
`calibrated` (a fitted value published by another modeling effort, not a
measurement). Review these as a set. They are where unfalsifiable choices
accumulate, and they are the honest answer to \"how much of this is known?\"
"""
function unledgered_check()
    weak = [p for p in values(PARAM_PROVENANCE) if p.method in ("assumed", "calibrated")]
    sort!(weak, by = p -> p.param_id)
    return weak
end

end # module
